// Expanded explanations for JJ QBank (MSK). Generated; edit the source notes, not this file.
window.XP = Object.assign(window.XP || {}, {"a1":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Levator scapulae is the only scapular muscle that arises from cervical TRANSVERSE processes; the other superficial back muscles hang off SPINOUS processes.</p><p>Levator scapulae runs from the posterior tubercles of the transverse processes of C1–C4 down to the superior angle and upper medial border of the scapula. It elevates the scapula and rotates the glenoid downward, and it is supplied by the dorsal scapular nerve (C5) with help from C3–C4. A crush or fracture of the cervical transverse processes therefore tears its origin. Trapezius, the rhomboids and serratus posterior superior all arise along the midline from the ligamentum nuchae and spinous processes, so they are spared by a transverse-process injury.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Trapezius</b> — Trapezius arises from the external occipital protuberance, ligamentum nuchae and the spinous processes of C7–T12, all midline structures, so transverse-process fractures leave it intact.</li><li><b>Rhomboid major</b> — Rhomboid major arises from the spinous processes of T2–T5 and inserts on the medial border of the scapula below the spine.</li><li><b>Serratus posterior superior</b> — Serratus posterior superior arises from the ligamentum nuchae and spinous processes of C7–T3 and inserts on ribs 2–5; it is a respiratory muscle with no transverse-process origin.</li><li><b>Rectus capitis posterior major</b> — Rectus capitis posterior major runs from the spinous process of the axis (C2) to the occipital bone, again a spinous-process attachment.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Transverse processes of the neck → levator scapulae (and scalenes); spinous processes → trapezius, rhomboids, serratus posterior.</p>","w":["Trapezius arises from the external occipital protuberance, ligamentum nuchae and the spinous processes of C7–T12, all midline structures, so transverse-process fractures leave it intact.","Correct — levator scapulae arises from the transverse processes of C1–C4, so cervical transverse-process fractures disrupt its origin.","Rhomboid major arises from the spinous processes of T2–T5 and inserts on the medial border of the scapula below the spine.","Serratus posterior superior arises from the ligamentum nuchae and spinous processes of C7–T3 and inserts on ribs 2–5; it is a respiratory muscle with no transverse-process origin.","Rectus capitis posterior major runs from the spinous process of the axis (C2) to the occipital bone, again a spinous-process attachment."]},"a2":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Dorsal scapular nerve → rhomboids + levator scapulae; spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) → trapezius + sternocleidomastoid.</p><p>Both nerves run along the medial border of the scapula near its superior angle. The dorsal scapular nerve (C5) pierces the middle scalene and descends deep to levator scapulae and the rhomboids, supplying all three. The spinal accessory nerve crosses the posterior triangle of the neck and runs on the deep surface of trapezius. A deep stab wound at the superior angle can cut both, weakening scapular retraction (rhomboids, middle trapezius) and elevation (upper trapezius, levator scapulae). The only answer that pairs one muscle from each nerve correctly is rhomboid major and trapezius.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Trapezius and serratus posterior superior</b> — Trapezius fits (accessory nerve), but serratus posterior superior is supplied by intercostal nerves (ventral rami T2–T5), not the dorsal scapular nerve.</li><li><b>Rhomboid minor and latissimus dorsi</b> — Rhomboid minor fits, but latissimus dorsi is supplied by the thoracodorsal nerve from the posterior cord.</li><li><b>Splenius cervicis and sternocleidomastoid</b> — Sternocleidomastoid is supplied by the accessory nerve, but splenius cervicis is a deep back muscle supplied by dorsal rami.</li><li><b>Levator scapulae and erector spinae</b> — Levator scapulae fits, but erector spinae is supplied by dorsal rami of spinal nerves.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Superficial back muscles that move the upper limb are supplied by ventral rami (or CN XI); true (deep) back muscles are supplied by dorsal rami.</p>","w":["Trapezius fits (accessory nerve), but serratus posterior superior is supplied by intercostal nerves (ventral rami T2–T5), not the dorsal scapular nerve.","Correct — rhomboid major is supplied by the dorsal scapular nerve and trapezius by the spinal accessory nerve, the two nerves cut here.","Rhomboid minor fits, but latissimus dorsi is supplied by the thoracodorsal nerve from the posterior cord.","Sternocleidomastoid is supplied by the accessory nerve, but splenius cervicis is a deep back muscle supplied by dorsal rami.","Levator scapulae fits, but erector spinae is supplied by dorsal rami of spinal nerves."]},"a3":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The rhomboids retract (adduct) the scapula and are supplied by the dorsal scapular nerve (C5).</p><p>Rhomboid major (spines of T2–T5) and rhomboid minor (ligamentum nuchae and spines of C7–T1) pull the medial border of the scapula toward the midline and slightly upward, rotating the glenoid downward. They belong to the superficial (extrinsic) back muscles that attach the upper limb to the trunk, so they are supplied by a branch of the brachial plexus root, not by dorsal rami. Loss of the dorsal scapular nerve gives weak retraction and the medial border can drift laterally. Semispinalis, multifidus, rotatores and iliocostalis are intrinsic (deep) back muscles supplied by dorsal rami that move the vertebral column, not the scapula.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Semispinalis capitis</b> — Semispinalis capitis is a deep transversospinalis muscle that extends and rotates the head; dorsal rami, no scapular attachment.</li><li><b>Multifidus</b> — Multifidus stabilizes and rotates vertebral segments; dorsal rami, no scapular attachment.</li><li><b>Rotator longus</b> — Rotatores rotate adjacent vertebrae; dorsal rami, no scapular attachment.</li><li><b>Iliocostalis</b> — Iliocostalis is the lateral column of the erector spinae, extending the trunk; dorsal rami, no scapular attachment.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> If a muscle moves the scapula or humerus, its nerve comes from ventral rami (brachial plexus or CN XI), never dorsal rami.</p>","w":["Semispinalis capitis is a deep transversospinalis muscle that extends and rotates the head; dorsal rami, no scapular attachment.","Correct — rhomboid major retracts (adducts) the scapula and is supplied by the dorsal scapular nerve.","Multifidus stabilizes and rotates vertebral segments; dorsal rami, no scapular attachment.","Rotatores rotate adjacent vertebrae; dorsal rami, no scapular attachment.","Iliocostalis is the lateral column of the erector spinae, extending the trunk; dorsal rami, no scapular attachment."]},"a4":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Thoracodorsal nerve (C6–C8, posterior cord) → latissimus dorsi → extension, adduction and medial rotation of the arm.</p><p>Latissimus dorsi arises broadly from the spines of T7–T12, the thoracolumbar fascia, iliac crest and lower ribs, and inserts into the floor of the intertubercular sulcus of the humerus. Pulling the humerus down and back makes it a powerful extensor, adductor and medial rotator, used in climbing, rowing, swimming and pushing up out of a chair. The thoracodorsal nerve runs on its deep surface with the thoracodorsal artery along the posterior axillary wall, where it can be injured. Loss weakens arm extension (and adduction), while scapular movements and lateral rotation are untouched.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Adduction of the scapula</b> — Scapular adduction (retraction) is done by the rhomboids and middle trapezius (dorsal scapular and accessory nerves).</li><li><b>Elevation of the scapula</b> — Scapular elevation is done by upper trapezius and levator scapulae (accessory and dorsal scapular nerves).</li><li><b>Abduction of the arm</b> — Arm abduction is done by supraspinatus (first ~15°) and deltoid; latissimus is actually an ADductor.</li><li><b>Lateral rotation of the arm</b> — Lateral rotation is done by infraspinatus, teres minor and posterior deltoid; latissimus is a MEDIAL rotator.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Latissimus dorsi = \"swimmer's muscle\": extend, adduct, medially rotate — think of reaching into your back pocket.</p>","w":["Scapular adduction (retraction) is done by the rhomboids and middle trapezius (dorsal scapular and accessory nerves).","Scapular elevation is done by upper trapezius and levator scapulae (accessory and dorsal scapular nerves).","Arm abduction is done by supraspinatus (first ~15°) and deltoid; latissimus is actually an ADductor.","Correct — the thoracodorsal nerve supplies latissimus dorsi, a major extensor (and adductor, medial rotator) of the arm.","Lateral rotation is done by infraspinatus, teres minor and posterior deltoid; latissimus is a MEDIAL rotator."]},"a5":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Of the superficial back muscles, only levator scapulae attaches to cervical transverse processes.</p><p>Levator scapulae arises from the transverse processes of C1–C4 and runs to the superior angle of the scapula. When the dashboard crushes the transverse processes, the origin of this muscle is the one disrupted, weakening scapular elevation. Every other choice attaches at the posterior midline (spinous processes or ligamentum nuchae), which a frontal dashboard impact on the transverse processes would not involve.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Trapezius</b> — Trapezius arises from the occiput, ligamentum nuchae and spinous processes of C7–T12, all midline.</li><li><b>Latissimus dorsi</b> — Latissimus dorsi arises from spinous processes of T7–T12, the thoracolumbar fascia, iliac crest and lower ribs, far from the cervical transverse processes.</li><li><b>Rhomboid major</b> — Rhomboid major arises from the spinous processes of T2–T5.</li><li><b>Serratus posterior superior</b> — Serratus posterior superior arises from the ligamentum nuchae and spinous processes of C7–T3.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Same concept as the previous question; when a stem names cervical transverse processes, think levator scapulae.</p>","w":["Trapezius arises from the occiput, ligamentum nuchae and spinous processes of C7–T12, all midline.","Latissimus dorsi arises from spinous processes of T7–T12, the thoracolumbar fascia, iliac crest and lower ribs, far from the cervical transverse processes.","Rhomboid major arises from the spinous processes of T2–T5.","Correct — levator scapulae arises from the transverse processes of C1–C4, the bones crushed here.","Serratus posterior superior arises from the ligamentum nuchae and spinous processes of C7–T3."]},"a6":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Long thoracic nerve (C5–C7) → serratus anterior; injury = winged scapula and inability to raise the arm above horizontal.</p><p>The long thoracic nerve runs down the medial wall of the axilla on the superficial surface of serratus anterior, so it is exposed during axillary node dissection, mastectomy and chest-tube placement. Serratus anterior protracts the scapula and holds its medial border against the thoracic wall; together with trapezius it upwardly rotates the glenoid. Without it, the medial border and inferior angle lift off the chest (winging, best seen when pushing against a wall), and the arm can't be abducted much past 90° because the scapula can't rotate upward.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Median nerve</b> — The median nerve supplies forearm flexors and thenar muscles; it doesn't reach the scapula and causes no winging.</li><li><b>Ulnar nerve</b> — The ulnar nerve supplies FCU, the medial half of FDP and most intrinsic hand muscles.</li><li><b>Thoracodorsal nerve</b> — The thoracodorsal nerve (also at risk in axillary surgery) supplies latissimus dorsi; injury weakens extension/adduction, not scapular stability.</li><li><b>Radial nerve</b> — The radial nerve supplies the extensors of the arm and forearm; injury gives wrist drop, not winging.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> \"C5, 6, 7 raise your wings to heaven\" — long thoracic nerve roots; winged scapula after axillary surgery is the classic picture.</p>","w":["The median nerve supplies forearm flexors and thenar muscles; it doesn't reach the scapula and causes no winging.","The ulnar nerve supplies FCU, the medial half of FDP and most intrinsic hand muscles.","The thoracodorsal nerve (also at risk in axillary surgery) supplies latissimus dorsi; injury weakens extension/adduction, not scapular stability.","Correct — the long thoracic nerve supplies serratus anterior; injury causes medial winging and difficulty raising the arm above horizontal.","The radial nerve supplies the extensors of the arm and forearm; injury gives wrist drop, not winging."]},"a7":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Pectoralis minor runs from ribs 3–5 to the CORACOID process; losing that insertion stops it from drawing the scapula forward and downward.</p><p>Pectoralis minor arises from ribs 3–5 near their costal cartilages and inserts on the medial border of the coracoid process. It protracts and depresses the scapula and stabilizes it against the chest wall. It is supplied by the medial pectoral nerve (and partly the lateral pectoral nerve), from the medial and lateral cords. A fracture through the coracoid detaches its insertion, so its pull on the scapula is lost even though the nerve is fine. Pectoralis minor also forms part of the anterior axillary wall and is the landmark that divides the axillary artery into three parts.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Fracture of the clavicle</b> — Pectoralis minor has no clavicular attachment; a clavicle fracture affects deltoid, trapezius, SCM, pectoralis major and subclavius, not pectoralis minor.</li><li><b>Injury to the posterior cord of the brachial plexus</b> — The pectoral nerves come from the medial and lateral cords; the posterior cord gives subscapular, thoracodorsal, axillary and radial nerves.</li><li><b>Axillary nerve injury</b> — The axillary nerve supplies deltoid and teres minor, not pectoralis minor.</li><li><b>Defects in the posterior wall of the axilla</b> — Pectoralis minor lies in the ANTERIOR axillary wall; the posterior wall is subscapularis, teres major and latissimus dorsi.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Coracoid attachments: pectoralis minor, short head of biceps and coracobrachialis.</p>","w":["Pectoralis minor has no clavicular attachment; a clavicle fracture affects deltoid, trapezius, SCM, pectoralis major and subclavius, not pectoralis minor.","The pectoral nerves come from the medial and lateral cords; the posterior cord gives subscapular, thoracodorsal, axillary and radial nerves.","Correct — pectoralis minor inserts on the coracoid process, so a coracoid fracture removes its pull on the scapula.","The axillary nerve supplies deltoid and teres minor, not pectoralis minor.","Pectoralis minor lies in the ANTERIOR axillary wall; the posterior wall is subscapularis, teres major and latissimus dorsi."]},"a8":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The main adductors of the arm are latissimus dorsi, pectoralis major and teres major.</p><p>Latissimus dorsi inserts into the floor of the intertubercular sulcus and pulls the humerus toward the trunk, as well as extending and medially rotating it. With pectoralis major in front and teres major behind, it provides most arm adduction. The other choices act on the shoulder in different directions: rotator cuff muscles rotate or abduct the humerus, and serratus anterior moves the scapula.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Teres minor</b> — Teres minor (axillary nerve) laterally rotates the arm as part of the rotator cuff.</li><li><b>Supraspinatus</b> — Supraspinatus (suprascapular nerve) initiates ABduction, the opposite of adduction.</li><li><b>Infraspinatus</b> — Infraspinatus (suprascapular nerve) is the main lateral rotator of the arm.</li><li><b>Serratus anterior</b> — Serratus anterior (long thoracic nerve) protracts and upwardly rotates the scapula, helping abduction above 90°.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Adductors: \"a lady between two majors\" — latissimus dorsi inserts between pectoralis major and teres major on the intertubercular groove.</p>","w":["Teres minor (axillary nerve) laterally rotates the arm as part of the rotator cuff.","Supraspinatus (suprascapular nerve) initiates ABduction, the opposite of adduction.","Correct — latissimus dorsi is a major adductor of the arm (also extends and medially rotates it).","Infraspinatus (suprascapular nerve) is the main lateral rotator of the arm.","Serratus anterior (long thoracic nerve) protracts and upwardly rotates the scapula, helping abduction above 90°."]},"a9":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Arm abduction = supraspinatus (suprascapular nerve, first ~15°) + deltoid (axillary nerve, 15°–90°); above 90° needs trapezius and serratus anterior.</p><p>Supraspinatus initiates abduction by pulling the humeral head into the glenoid and starting the swing. Once the arm is off the side, the middle fibers of deltoid take over and abduct to about 90°. Beyond that the scapula must upwardly rotate (trapezius and serratus anterior). Losing both the suprascapular and axillary nerves removes both prime abductors, so the arm can't be lifted from the side at all.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Thoracodorsal and upper subscapular</b> — Thoracodorsal (latissimus dorsi) and upper subscapular (subscapularis) nerves supply adductors and medial rotators.</li><li><b>Axillary and musculocutaneous</b> — The axillary nerve fits, but the musculocutaneous nerve supplies the elbow flexors (biceps, brachialis, coracobrachialis).</li><li><b>Radial and lower subscapular</b> — The radial nerve supplies the extensors; the lower subscapular nerve supplies subscapularis and teres major (adduction, medial rotation).</li><li><b>Suprascapular and dorsal scapular</b> — The suprascapular nerve fits, but the dorsal scapular nerve supplies the rhomboids and levator scapulae, which move the scapula, not the arm.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Can't START abduction → suprascapular nerve/supraspinatus; can start but can't continue to 90° → axillary nerve/deltoid; can't go above 90° → long thoracic (serratus) or accessory (trapezius).</p>","w":["Correct — the suprascapular nerve (supraspinatus) and axillary nerve (deltoid) supply the two muscles that abduct the arm.","Thoracodorsal (latissimus dorsi) and upper subscapular (subscapularis) nerves supply adductors and medial rotators.","The axillary nerve fits, but the musculocutaneous nerve supplies the elbow flexors (biceps, brachialis, coracobrachialis).","The radial nerve supplies the extensors; the lower subscapular nerve supplies subscapularis and teres major (adduction, medial rotation).","The suprascapular nerve fits, but the dorsal scapular nerve supplies the rhomboids and levator scapulae, which move the scapula, not the arm."]},"a10":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The rotator cuff is SITS: Supraspinatus, Infraspinatus, Teres minor, Subscapularis. Teres major is not part of it.</p><p>The four rotator cuff tendons blend with the shoulder joint capsule and hold the humeral head in the shallow glenoid during movement. Supraspinatus, infraspinatus and teres minor insert on the greater tubercle; subscapularis inserts on the lesser tubercle. Teres major arises from the inferior angle of the scapula and inserts on the medial lip of the intertubercular sulcus, well away from the joint capsule, so a rotator cuff tear leaves it intact. It adducts and medially rotates the arm and is supplied by the lower subscapular nerve.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Supraspinatus</b> — Supraspinatus is the most commonly torn cuff tendon (it passes under the acromion and impinges).</li><li><b>Subscapularis</b> — Subscapularis is the anterior cuff muscle, inserting on the lesser tubercle.</li><li><b>Teres minor</b> — Teres minor is the small posterior cuff muscle, supplied by the axillary nerve.</li><li><b>Infraspinatus</b> — Infraspinatus is the posterior cuff muscle on the infraspinous fossa, supplied by the suprascapular nerve.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Teres MINOR is in the cuff (axillary nerve); teres MAJOR is not (lower subscapular nerve) — a favorite exam swap.</p>","w":["Supraspinatus is the most commonly torn cuff tendon (it passes under the acromion and impinges).","Subscapularis is the anterior cuff muscle, inserting on the lesser tubercle.","Correct — teres major is not a rotator cuff muscle, so it is unaffected by a cuff tear.","Teres minor is the small posterior cuff muscle, supplied by the axillary nerve.","Infraspinatus is the posterior cuff muscle on the infraspinous fossa, supplied by the suprascapular nerve."]},"a11":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Axillary nerve (posterior cord, C5–C6) wraps the surgical neck through the quadrangular space and supplies deltoid and teres minor; inferior shoulder dislocation stretches it.</p><p>The axillary nerve leaves the posterior cord, passes through the quadrangular space (bounded by teres minor above, teres major below, long head of triceps medially and the humeral surgical neck laterally) with the posterior circumflex humeral artery, and wraps around the surgical neck. Because it lies just below the joint capsule, an inferior (or anterior-inferior) dislocation of the humeral head pushes down on and stretches it. Teres minor is a lateral rotator and deltoid's posterior fibers also laterally rotate, so the boy has weak lateral rotation, along with weak abduction and numbness over the lower deltoid (regimental badge area).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Injury to the lateral cord of the brachial plexus</b> — The lateral cord gives the musculocutaneous nerve, the lateral pectoral nerve and the lateral root of the median; the axillary nerve comes from the posterior cord.</li><li><b>Fracture of the anatomic neck of the humerus</b> — The axillary nerve wraps the SURGICAL neck; the anatomic neck is higher, at the edge of the articular surface, and fractures there rarely injure it.</li><li><b>Knife wound on the teres major muscle</b> — The axillary nerve runs above teres major (through the quadrangular space); a stab of teres major muscle itself would not specifically cut it.</li><li><b>A tumor in the triangular space in the shoulder region</b> — The triangular space contains the circumflex scapular artery; the axillary nerve passes through the QUADRANGULAR space.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Quadrangular space → axillary nerve + posterior circumflex humeral artery; triangular space → circumflex scapular artery; triangular interval → radial nerve + deep brachial artery.</p>","w":["The lateral cord gives the musculocutaneous nerve, the lateral pectoral nerve and the lateral root of the median; the axillary nerve comes from the posterior cord.","The axillary nerve wraps the SURGICAL neck; the anatomic neck is higher, at the edge of the articular surface, and fractures there rarely injure it.","The axillary nerve runs above teres major (through the quadrangular space); a stab of teres major muscle itself would not specifically cut it.","Correct — an inferior dislocation of the humeral head stretches the axillary nerve, weakening teres minor and deltoid (lateral rotation).","The triangular space contains the circumflex scapular artery; the axillary nerve passes through the QUADRANGULAR space."]},"a12":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Subscapularis is the only rotator cuff muscle that inserts on the LESSER tubercle.</p><p>Subscapularis fills the subscapular fossa on the costal surface of the scapula, passes in front of the shoulder joint and inserts on the lesser tubercle of the humerus. It is the main medial rotator of the arm and forms most of the posterior axillary wall; it is supplied by the upper and lower subscapular nerves. An avulsion (chip) fracture of the lesser tubercle detaches its tendon, weakening medial rotation and anterior stability of the joint. The other three cuff muscles insert in order on the greater tubercle (superior, middle and inferior facets: supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Supraspinatus muscle</b> — Supraspinatus inserts on the superior facet of the greater tubercle.</li><li><b>Infraspinatus muscle</b> — Infraspinatus inserts on the middle facet of the greater tubercle.</li><li><b>Teres minor muscle</b> — Teres minor inserts on the inferior facet of the greater tubercle.</li><li><b>Coracohumeral ligament</b> — The coracohumeral ligament runs from the coracoid to the greater (and partly lesser) tubercle; it is a capsular reinforcement, not a muscle, and a lesser tubercle chip doesn't classically damage it.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Greater tubercle: SIT (supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor); lesser tubercle: S (subscapularis).</p>","w":["Supraspinatus inserts on the superior facet of the greater tubercle.","Infraspinatus inserts on the middle facet of the greater tubercle.","Correct — subscapularis inserts on the lesser tubercle, so a chip fracture there damages its tendon and medial rotation.","Teres minor inserts on the inferior facet of the greater tubercle.","The coracohumeral ligament runs from the coracoid to the greater (and partly lesser) tubercle; it is a capsular reinforcement, not a muscle, and a lesser tubercle chip doesn't classically damage it."]},"a13":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> In a midclavicle fracture, the lateral fragment is pulled DOWN by deltoid and the weight of the limb (and medially by pectoralis major); the medial fragment is pulled UP by sternocleidomastoid.</p><p>The clavicle is the only bony strut connecting the upper limb to the axial skeleton, and it most often breaks at the junction of its middle and lateral thirds. Once it breaks, the lateral fragment is no longer held up: the weight of the arm (gravity) and the downward pull of deltoid, which attaches to the lateral third, drop the shoulder. Pectoralis major pulls the arm and lateral fragment medially, causing the overriding. Meanwhile the medial fragment is lifted by sternocleidomastoid. This produces the classic drooping shoulder with a step deformity.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Deltoid and trapezius muscles</b> — Deltoid fits, but trapezius attaches to the lateral third and tends to pull it UP and back, not down.</li><li><b>Pectoralis major and deltoid muscles</b> — Pectoralis major mainly pulls the lateral fragment and arm medially (overriding), not downward; deltoid alone lacks gravity in this pairing.</li><li><b>Pectoralis minor muscle and gravity</b> — Gravity fits, but pectoralis minor has no clavicular attachment (it inserts on the coracoid).</li><li><b>Trapezius and pectoralis minor muscles</b> — Trapezius pulls upward and pectoralis minor isn't attached to the clavicle.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Clavicle fracture: medial fragment UP (SCM), lateral fragment DOWN (deltoid + gravity) and IN (pectoralis major).</p>","w":["Deltoid fits, but trapezius attaches to the lateral third and tends to pull it UP and back, not down.","Pectoralis major mainly pulls the lateral fragment and arm medially (overriding), not downward; deltoid alone lacks gravity in this pairing.","Gravity fits, but pectoralis minor has no clavicular attachment (it inserts on the coracoid).","Trapezius pulls upward and pectoralis minor isn't attached to the clavicle.","Correct — deltoid pull and the weight of the limb (gravity) depress the lateral fragment."]},"a14":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Sternocleidomastoid attaches to the superior surface of the medial third of the clavicle and pulls the medial fragment upward after a fracture.</p><p>The clavicular head of sternocleidomastoid arises from the upper surface of the medial third of the clavicle. When the clavicle breaks at the middle–lateral junction, the medial fragment loses its connection to the shoulder and is elevated by this unopposed pull, producing the visible upward step at the fracture site. The lateral fragment, by contrast, is pulled down by deltoid and gravity.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Pectoralis major</b> — Pectoralis major's clavicular head attaches to the medial half of the anterior clavicle, but its pull is downward/medial toward the arm, not upward.</li><li><b>Deltoid</b> — Deltoid attaches to the lateral third and pulls the lateral fragment down.</li><li><b>Trapezius</b> — Trapezius attaches to the lateral third of the clavicle, not the medial fragment.</li><li><b>Scalenus anterior</b> — Scalenus anterior inserts on the first rib (scalene tubercle), not the clavicle.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Medial fragment up = SCM; lateral fragment down = deltoid + gravity.</p>","w":["Pectoralis major's clavicular head attaches to the medial half of the anterior clavicle, but its pull is downward/medial toward the arm, not upward.","Deltoid attaches to the lateral third and pulls the lateral fragment down.","Trapezius attaches to the lateral third of the clavicle, not the medial fragment.","Correct — sternocleidomastoid's clavicular head pulls the medial fragment upward.","Scalenus anterior inserts on the first rib (scalene tubercle), not the clavicle."]},"a15":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The subclavian vessels and the brachial plexus (especially its LOWER trunk) lie directly under the middle third of the clavicle and can be injured by its fragments.</p><p>Behind the clavicle, the subclavian vein crosses the first rib in front of anterior scalene, with the subclavian artery and brachial plexus just behind it. Sharp fragments can tear or compress the subclavian vein; a damaged vein can thrombose, and a clot that breaks off travels through the brachiocephalic vein, superior vena cava and right heart into the pulmonary arteries as a pulmonary embolism. Arterial injury, by contrast, sends emboli downstream into the arm, and the part of the plexus lying lowest against the first rib (lower trunk, C8–T1) is the one most at risk.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>A fatal hemorrhage from the brachiocephalic vein</b> — The brachiocephalic vein lies behind the sternoclavicular joint and manubrium, medial to the usual fracture site; fatal brachiocephalic hemorrhage is not the expected complication.</li><li><b>Thrombosis of the subclavian artery, causing an embolism in the ascending aorta</b> — Emboli from the subclavian artery travel distally with arterial flow into the axillary and brachial arteries, not backward into the ascending aorta.</li><li><b>Damage to the upper trunk of the brachial plexus</b> — The plexus component classically injured by a clavicle fracture is the LOWER trunk, which lies against the first rib; the upper trunk is higher in the neck.</li><li><b>Damage to the long thoracic nerve, causing the winged scapula</b> — The long thoracic nerve runs down the medial axillary wall on serratus anterior, away from the clavicle; it is injured in axillary surgery, not clavicle fractures.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Venous clot → lungs (PE); arterial clot → downstream (limb ischemia). Clavicle fracture can injure subclavian vessels and the lower trunk.</p>","w":["The brachiocephalic vein lies behind the sternoclavicular joint and manubrium, medial to the usual fracture site; fatal brachiocephalic hemorrhage is not the expected complication.","Correct — injury and thrombosis of the subclavian vein can release an embolus that lodges in the pulmonary arteries.","Emboli from the subclavian artery travel distally with arterial flow into the axillary and brachial arteries, not backward into the ascending aorta.","The plexus component classically injured by a clavicle fracture is the LOWER trunk, which lies against the first rib; the upper trunk is higher in the neck.","The long thoracic nerve runs down the medial axillary wall on serratus anterior, away from the clavicle; it is injured in axillary surgery, not clavicle fractures."]},"a16":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The suprascapular notch (bridged by the superior transverse scapular ligament) transmits the suprascapular nerve, which supplies supraspinatus and infraspinatus.</p><p>On the superior border of the scapula, just medial to the coracoid, is the suprascapular notch. The superior transverse scapular ligament converts it into a foramen. The suprascapular nerve passes UNDER the ligament through the notch, while the suprascapular artery and vein pass OVER it (\"army over the bridge, navy under\"). If the ligament calcifies, the foramen narrows and compresses the nerve (suprascapular nerve entrapment). The nerve supplies supraspinatus, then winds around the spinoglenoid notch to supply infraspinatus, so the patient has weak initiation of abduction and weak lateral rotation, often with infraspinatus wasting.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Deltoid</b> — Deltoid is supplied by the axillary nerve through the quadrangular space, not via the suprascapular notch.</li><li><b>Teres major</b> — Teres major is supplied by the lower subscapular nerve.</li><li><b>Teres minor</b> — Teres minor is supplied by the axillary nerve.</li><li><b>Subscapularis</b> — Subscapularis is supplied by the upper and lower subscapular nerves.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Suprascapular nerve: under the ligament; artery: over it. Lesion → weak abduction initiation (supraspinatus) + weak lateral rotation (infraspinatus).</p>","w":["Deltoid is supplied by the axillary nerve through the quadrangular space, not via the suprascapular notch.","Teres major is supplied by the lower subscapular nerve.","Teres minor is supplied by the axillary nerve.","Correct — a calcified superior transverse scapular ligament compresses the suprascapular nerve, paralyzing infraspinatus (and supraspinatus).","Subscapularis is supplied by the upper and lower subscapular nerves."]},"a17":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The surgical neck of the humerus is encircled by the axillary nerve and the posterior circumflex humeral artery; fractures there injure both.</p><p>The surgical neck is the narrow part below the tubercles and is the most common fracture site of the proximal humerus, especially in older adults falling on an outstretched arm. The posterior circumflex humeral artery (from the third part of the axillary artery) passes with the axillary nerve through the quadrangular space and wraps around the back of the surgical neck, where fracture fragments can tear it. Expect bleeding plus deltoid weakness and numbness over the lower lateral shoulder from the accompanying axillary nerve injury.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Musculocutaneous nerve</b> — The musculocutaneous nerve pierces coracobrachialis and runs between biceps and brachialis in the anterior arm, away from the surgical neck.</li><li><b>Radial nerve</b> — The radial nerve runs in the radial (spiral) groove of the midshaft and is injured by midshaft, not surgical neck, fractures.</li><li><b>Deep brachial artery</b> — The deep brachial (profunda brachii) artery accompanies the radial nerve in the radial groove at the midshaft.</li><li><b>Scapular circumflex artery</b> — The circumflex scapular artery passes through the triangular space to the back of the scapula, not around the humerus.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Surgical neck → axillary nerve + posterior circumflex humeral artery; midshaft → radial nerve + deep brachial artery; supracondylar → median nerve + brachial artery; medial epicondyle → ulnar nerve.</p>","w":["The musculocutaneous nerve pierces coracobrachialis and runs between biceps and brachialis in the anterior arm, away from the surgical neck.","The radial nerve runs in the radial (spiral) groove of the midshaft and is injured by midshaft, not surgical neck, fractures.","The deep brachial (profunda brachii) artery accompanies the radial nerve in the radial groove at the midshaft.","Correct — the posterior circumflex humeral artery wraps around the surgical neck with the axillary nerve.","The circumflex scapular artery passes through the triangular space to the back of the scapula, not around the humerus."]},"a18":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Brachioradialis is the one elbow flexor supplied by the radial nerve; biceps and brachialis are musculocutaneous.</p><p>Although the radial nerve supplies the extensor compartment, brachioradialis is a flexor of the elbow, strongest with the forearm in the mid-prone (thumb-up, \"beer-drinking\") position. It arises from the lateral supracondylar ridge of the humerus and inserts on the styloid process of the radius. Developmentally it belongs to the posterior compartment, which is why it keeps a radial nerve supply despite its function. This also explains why elbow flexion is only weakened, not lost, after a musculocutaneous nerve injury.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Flexor digitorum longus</b> — Flexor digitorum longus is a deep posterior LEG muscle (tibial nerve) that flexes the toes; it doesn't cross the elbow.</li><li><b>Brachialis</b> — Brachialis is the main elbow flexor, but it is supplied mainly by the musculocutaneous nerve (with a small radial contribution to its lateral part).</li><li><b>Extensor digitorum longus</b> — Extensor digitorum longus is an anterior LEG muscle (deep fibular nerve).</li><li><b>Biceps brachii</b> — Biceps brachii flexes the elbow and supinates the forearm but is supplied by the musculocutaneous nerve.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Elbow flexors: biceps + brachialis (musculocutaneous) + brachioradialis (radial).</p>","w":["Flexor digitorum longus is a deep posterior LEG muscle (tibial nerve) that flexes the toes; it doesn't cross the elbow.","Correct — brachioradialis flexes the elbow (best in mid-pronation) and is supplied by the radial nerve.","Brachialis is the main elbow flexor, but it is supplied mainly by the musculocutaneous nerve (with a small radial contribution to its lateral part).","Extensor digitorum longus is an anterior LEG muscle (deep fibular nerve).","Biceps brachii flexes the elbow and supinates the forearm but is supplied by the musculocutaneous nerve."]},"a19":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> FDS flexes the PIP joints; FDP flexes the DIP joints.</p><p>Flexor digitorum superficialis tendons split (at Camper's chiasm) and insert on the sides of the middle phalanges, so they flex the PIP (and MCP) joints. Flexor digitorum profundus tendons pass through that split to insert on the distal phalanges, so they are the only muscles that flex the DIP joints. To test FDS alone, hold the other fingers straight (which immobilizes the shared FDP muscle belly) and ask the patient to bend one finger: flexion at the PIP with a floppy DIP means FDS is working. FDS is supplied entirely by the median nerve.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Palmar interossei</b> — Palmar interossei adduct the fingers toward the middle finger and help flex the MCP and extend the IP joints (ulnar nerve).</li><li><b>Dorsal interossei</b> — Dorsal interossei abduct the fingers away from the middle finger (DAB) and also flex MCP / extend IP joints.</li><li><b>Flexor digitorum profundus</b> — FDP flexes the DIP joints (and secondarily the PIP), but PIP flexion with the DIP spared is the job of FDS; the question isolates PIP flexion.</li><li><b>Lumbricals</b> — Lumbricals flex the MCP joints and EXTEND the IP joints, the opposite of what's lost.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Superficialis splits to insert on the middle phalanx (PIP); profundus goes deeper/further to the distal phalanx (DIP).</p>","w":["Palmar interossei adduct the fingers toward the middle finger and help flex the MCP and extend the IP joints (ulnar nerve).","Dorsal interossei abduct the fingers away from the middle finger (DAB) and also flex MCP / extend IP joints.","FDP flexes the DIP joints (and secondarily the PIP), but PIP flexion with the DIP spared is the job of FDS; the question isolates PIP flexion.","Correct — FDS inserts on the middle phalanges and flexes the PIP joints.","Lumbricals flex the MCP joints and EXTEND the IP joints, the opposite of what's lost."]},"a20":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The anterior interosseous nerve (branch of the median nerve) supplies FPL, the lateral half of FDP and pronator quadratus; it is purely motor.</p><p>The median nerve gives off the anterior interosseous nerve just below the elbow; it runs on the interosseous membrane deep in the forearm. It supplies the deep flexors: flexor pollicis longus (thumb IP flexion), the lateral half of flexor digitorum profundus (index and middle DIP flexion) and pronator quadratus. Injury gives a weak \"OK\" sign: the patient can't flex the tips of the thumb and index finger, so pinching makes a flat square instead of a circle. There is no sensory loss because the nerve has no cutaneous branches.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Flexor pollicis longus and brevis</b> — FPL fits, but flexor pollicis brevis is a thenar muscle supplied by the recurrent (thenar) branch of the median nerve in the hand.</li><li><b>Flexor pollicis longus and opponens pollicis</b> — FPL fits, but opponens pollicis is supplied by the recurrent branch of the median nerve.</li><li><b>Flexor digitorum profundus and superficialis</b> — FDS is supplied directly by the median nerve trunk, not the anterior interosseous branch.</li><li><b>Flexor pollicis brevis and pronator quadratus</b> — Pronator quadratus fits, but flexor pollicis brevis is supplied by the recurrent branch (and partly the ulnar nerve).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Anterior interosseous nerve palsy: can't make the \"OK\" sign, no numbness.</p>","w":["FPL fits, but flexor pollicis brevis is a thenar muscle supplied by the recurrent (thenar) branch of the median nerve in the hand.","FPL fits, but opponens pollicis is supplied by the recurrent branch of the median nerve.","Correct — the anterior interosseous nerve supplies the lateral half of FDP and pronator quadratus (plus FPL).","FDS is supplied directly by the median nerve trunk, not the anterior interosseous branch.","Pronator quadratus fits, but flexor pollicis brevis is supplied by the recurrent branch (and partly the ulnar nerve)."]},"a21":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The radial tuberosity is where biceps brachii inserts; biceps is the most powerful supinator and a strong flexor of the elbow.</p><p>Biceps brachii (long head from the supraglenoid tubercle, short head from the coracoid) inserts by its tendon on the radial tuberosity and by the bicipital aponeurosis into the forearm fascia. Because the tendon wraps around the radius when the forearm is pronated, contraction unwinds it, which is why biceps is the strongest supinator, especially with the elbow flexed (think of driving a screw with the right hand). It also flexes the elbow. Destroying the tuberosity detaches the tendon, weakening both supination and flexion. Supinator (radial nerve) and brachialis/brachioradialis still give some supination and flexion, so the loss is weakness rather than complete paralysis. Biceps is supplied by the musculocutaneous nerve (C5–C6).</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Biceps tendon rupture at the radial tuberosity → weak supination and elbow flexion with a \"Popeye\" bulge (distal) or weak flexion with a balled-up belly (proximal long-head rupture).</p>","w":["","Correct — this is the radial tuberosity, the insertion of biceps brachii (supination and elbow flexion).","","","",""]},"a22":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The medial epicondyle gives origin to pronator teres and the common flexor tendon (FCR, palmaris longus, FCU, FDS).</p><p>The superficial forearm flexors all start from a common tendon on the medial epicondyle of the humerus: pronator teres, flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus, flexor carpi ulnaris and (partly) flexor digitorum superficialis. Destroying this area removes pronator teres (the stronger pronator) and the main wrist flexors at once. Pronation is weakened but not abolished because pronator quadratus arises from the distal ulna, and the wrist can still be flexed weakly by the long finger and thumb flexors that cross it (FDS, FDP, FPL). The ulnar nerve also runs right behind the medial epicondyle, so injuries here often affect it too.</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Medial epicondyle = common flexor-pronator origin (\"golfer's elbow\"); lateral epicondyle = common extensor-supinator origin (\"tennis elbow\").</p>","w":["","","","","Correct — this is the medial epicondyle, origin of pronator teres and the common wrist flexors.",""]},"a23":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The olecranon is the insertion of triceps brachii, the main elbow extensor, supplied by the radial nerve.</p><p>All three heads of triceps (long head from the infraglenoid tubercle, lateral and medial heads from the posterior humerus on either side of the radial groove) converge on a tendon that inserts on the olecranon of the ulna. Anconeus, a small muscle from the lateral epicondyle to the olecranon, also helps extend the elbow and is radial too. Both are supplied by the radial nerve. A radial nerve lesion high in the axilla paralyzes triceps; a lesion at the midshaft (radial groove) usually spares it because its branches leave above the groove.</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Olecranon fracture can be pulled apart (displaced) by the unopposed triceps; triceps reflex tests C7.</p>","w":["","","","Correct — this is the olecranon, the insertion of triceps (and anconeus), both supplied by the radial nerve.","",""]},"a24":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Arm abduction needs supraspinatus (suprascapular nerve, from the UPPER TRUNK) and deltoid (axillary nerve, from the POSTERIOR CORD).</p><p>To answer \"which plexus parts\" questions, trace each muscle to its nerve and each nerve to its origin. Supraspinatus is supplied by the suprascapular nerve, one of only two branches of a trunk (both from the upper trunk; the other is the nerve to subclavius). Deltoid is supplied by the axillary nerve, a terminal branch of the posterior cord. Damage to both levels removes both prime abductors, so the arm cannot be lifted away from the side.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Middle trunk and posterior cord</b> — The middle trunk gives no named branches, so it cannot account for supraspinatus; the posterior cord alone would leave supraspinatus working.</li><li><b>Middle trunk and lateral cord</b> — The middle trunk has no branches, and the lateral cord supplies flexors (musculocutaneous) and pectoralis major, not abductors.</li><li><b>Lower trunk and lateral cord</b> — The lower trunk (C8–T1) mainly feeds the medial cord and hand; the lateral cord gives no abductor nerve.</li><li><b>Lower trunk and medial cord</b> — The lower trunk and medial cord supply forearm flexors and hand intrinsics (ulnar, median), not shoulder abductors.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Only the upper trunk has branches: suprascapular nerve and nerve to subclavius.</p>","w":["The middle trunk gives no named branches, so it cannot account for supraspinatus; the posterior cord alone would leave supraspinatus working.","The middle trunk has no branches, and the lateral cord supplies flexors (musculocutaneous) and pectoralis major, not abductors.","The lower trunk (C8–T1) mainly feeds the medial cord and hand; the lateral cord gives no abductor nerve.","Correct — upper trunk → suprascapular nerve → supraspinatus; posterior cord → axillary nerve → deltoid.","The lower trunk and medial cord supply forearm flexors and hand intrinsics (ulnar, median), not shoulder abductors."]},"a25":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> C5–C6 (upper trunk) injury knocks out the shoulder abductors and lateral rotators and the elbow flexors/supinators; infraspinatus (suprascapular nerve, C5–C6) is a classic casualty.</p><p>The C5 and C6 roots feed the upper trunk, whose fibers supply supraspinatus and infraspinatus (suprascapular nerve), deltoid and teres minor (axillary nerve), and biceps/brachialis (musculocutaneous nerve). Losing them produces Erb–Duchenne palsy: the arm hangs adducted (no deltoid/supraspinatus), medially rotated (no infraspinatus/teres minor), with the elbow extended and forearm pronated (no biceps). Every other muscle offered is supplied by the ulnar nerve, which carries C8–T1, so it is spared.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Flexor carpi ulnaris</b> — Flexor carpi ulnaris is supplied by the ulnar nerve (C7–C8, mostly C8), not C5–C6.</li><li><b>Palmar interossei</b> — Palmar interossei are supplied by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve (C8–T1).</li><li><b>Adductor pollicis</b> — Adductor pollicis is supplied by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve (C8–T1).</li><li><b>Palmaris brevis</b> — Palmaris brevis is supplied by the superficial branch of the ulnar nerve (C8–T1).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Erb palsy (C5–C6) = \"waiter's tip\"; Klumpke palsy (C8–T1) = claw hand.</p>","w":["Correct — infraspinatus is supplied by the suprascapular nerve (C5–C6), so it is paralyzed when those roots are cut.","Flexor carpi ulnaris is supplied by the ulnar nerve (C7–C8, mostly C8), not C5–C6.","Palmar interossei are supplied by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve (C8–T1).","Adductor pollicis is supplied by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve (C8–T1).","Palmaris brevis is supplied by the superficial branch of the ulnar nerve (C8–T1)."]},"a26":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> A Pancoast tumor at the lung apex invades the lower trunk (C8–T1), which carries the ulnar nerve fibers to the hand intrinsics; finger abduction by the DORSAL interossei is lost.</p><p>The lower trunk lies on the first rib directly above the lung apex, so apical (Pancoast) tumors invade it first. Its fibers form most of the medial cord and the ulnar nerve, which supplies the hypothenar muscles, adductor pollicis, all interossei and the medial two lumbricals. Abduction of the index finger is done by the first DORSAL interosseous (remember DAB: dorsal abduct); palmar interossei ADduct (PAD). Note that the source explanation mislabels this muscle; the conclusion (ulnar nerve, lower trunk) is still right. Pancoast tumors may also involve the sympathetic chain, adding Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Lateral rotation of the arm</b> — Lateral rotation of the arm is done by infraspinatus and teres minor (suprascapular and axillary nerves), which carry C5–C6 fibers from the upper trunk.</li><li><b>Extension of the ring finger</b> — Finger extension is done by extensor digitorum (radial nerve, mainly C7), not lower-trunk ulnar fibers.</li><li><b>Flexion of the forearm</b> — Elbow (forearm) flexion is done by biceps and brachialis (musculocutaneous nerve, C5–C6).</li><li><b>Pronation of the forearm</b> — Pronation is done by pronator teres and quadratus (median nerve, C6–C7 for pronator teres); a lower-trunk lesion wouldn't be the best match.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> PAD (palmar adduct) and DAB (dorsal abduct) — all interossei are ulnar nerve. Pancoast tumor = lower trunk ± Horner syndrome.</p>","w":["Lateral rotation of the arm is done by infraspinatus and teres minor (suprascapular and axillary nerves), which carry C5–C6 fibers from the upper trunk.","Finger extension is done by extensor digitorum (radial nerve, mainly C7), not lower-trunk ulnar fibers.","Correct — index finger abduction (first dorsal interosseous, ulnar nerve, C8–T1) is lost with a lower-trunk lesion.","Elbow (forearm) flexion is done by biceps and brachialis (musculocutaneous nerve, C5–C6).","Pronation is done by pronator teres and quadratus (median nerve, C6–C7 for pronator teres); a lower-trunk lesion wouldn't be the best match."]},"a27":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Upper trunk (C5–C6) injury: lateral rotators and abductors are paralyzed, so the arm lies adducted and MEDIALLY rotated (\"waiter's tip\").</p><p>In Erb–Duchenne palsy the unopposed medial rotators (subscapularis, latissimus dorsi, teres major, pectoralis major, which also get C7–T1 fibers) swing the arm inward, while loss of deltoid and supraspinatus leaves it adducted against the trunk. Loss of biceps leaves the elbow extended and forearm pronated. Branches that leave the plexus ABOVE the upper trunk — the dorsal scapular nerve (C5 root) and the long thoracic nerve (C5–C7 roots) — are usually spared in an isolated trunk lesion, and sensory loss is over the lateral (C5–C6) side of the arm and forearm.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Paralysis of the rhomboid major</b> — Rhomboid major is supplied by the dorsal scapular nerve, which leaves the C5 ROOT before the upper trunk forms, so it is spared.</li><li><b>Inability to elevate the arm above the horizontal</b> — Raising the arm above horizontal needs serratus anterior (long thoracic nerve), which also leaves at the root level (C5–C7) above the trunk.</li><li><b>Loss of sensation on the medial side of the arm</b> — The medial side of the arm is supplied by the medial cutaneous nerve of the arm (medial cord, C8–T1); upper trunk injury numbs the LATERAL arm.</li><li><b>Inability to adduct the thumb</b> — Adductor pollicis is supplied by the ulnar nerve (C8–T1, lower trunk).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Root-level branches (dorsal scapular, long thoracic) help localize: if they're spared, the lesion is distal to the roots (trunk level).</p>","w":["Rhomboid major is supplied by the dorsal scapular nerve, which leaves the C5 ROOT before the upper trunk forms, so it is spared.","Raising the arm above horizontal needs serratus anterior (long thoracic nerve), which also leaves at the root level (C5–C7) above the trunk.","Correct — with lateral rotators and abductors paralyzed, the arm lies in medial rotation (waiter's tip).","The medial side of the arm is supplied by the medial cutaneous nerve of the arm (medial cord, C8–T1); upper trunk injury numbs the LATERAL arm.","Adductor pollicis is supplied by the ulnar nerve (C8–T1, lower trunk)."]},"a28":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Winged scapula = serratus anterior paralysis = long thoracic nerve, which arises directly from the ROOTS (C5–C7).</p><p>The long thoracic nerve is formed from the ventral rami (roots) of C5, C6 and C7 before they join into trunks, and descends on the outer surface of serratus anterior. Serratus anterior holds the medial border of the scapula against the chest and, with trapezius, upwardly rotates the scapula. Without it, the medial border lifts off (\"winging\") and the arm cannot be raised above horizontal, because abduction beyond 90° requires the scapula to rotate. The only plexus level that gives this nerve is the roots.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Medial cord</b> — The medial cord gives the medial pectoral, medial cutaneous nerves, ulnar nerve and the medial root of the median nerve — none reach serratus anterior.</li><li><b>Posterior cord</b> — The posterior cord gives the subscapular, thoracodorsal, axillary and radial nerves.</li><li><b>Lower trunk</b> — The lower trunk gives no branches of its own.</li><li><b>Upper trunk</b> — The upper trunk gives the suprascapular nerve and nerve to subclavius, not the long thoracic nerve.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Root branches: dorsal scapular (C5) and long thoracic (C5–C7). \"C5, 6, 7 raise your wings to heaven.\"</p>","w":["The medial cord gives the medial pectoral, medial cutaneous nerves, ulnar nerve and the medial root of the median nerve — none reach serratus anterior.","The posterior cord gives the subscapular, thoracodorsal, axillary and radial nerves.","The lower trunk gives no branches of its own.","Correct — the long thoracic nerve comes directly from the C5–C7 roots.","The upper trunk gives the suprascapular nerve and nerve to subclavius, not the long thoracic nerve."]},"a29":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cell bodies of primary sensory neurons (the ones whose loss causes numbness) sit in the dorsal root ganglia.</p><p>A cutaneous nerve like the medial cutaneous nerve of the arm carries general somatic afferent (GSA) fibers. These are pseudounipolar neurons: one process runs out to the skin, the other into the dorsal horn, and the cell body sits in between in the dorsal root ganglion (DRG), just outside the spinal cord in the intervertebral foramen. The same cutaneous nerve also carries postganglionic sympathetic fibers to sweat glands and blood vessels; those cell bodies are in the sympathetic chain ganglia, but they don't cause numbness when injured.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Sympathetic chain ganglion</b> — The sympathetic chain ganglion holds POSTganglionic sympathetic cell bodies (sweating, vasomotor), not sensory neurons.</li><li><b>Anterior horn of the spinal cord</b> — The anterior (ventral) horn holds lower motor neurons (GSE) that supply skeletal muscle.</li><li><b>Lateral horn of the spinal cord</b> — The lateral horn (T1–L2) holds PREganglionic sympathetic cell bodies.</li><li><b>Posterior horn of the spinal cord</b> — The posterior (dorsal) horn holds second-order neurons and interneurons that receive the sensory input, not the first-order cell bodies.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Sensory = dorsal root ganglion; motor = anterior horn; preganglionic sympathetic = lateral horn (T1–L2); postganglionic sympathetic = chain or prevertebral ganglia.</p>","w":["The sympathetic chain ganglion holds POSTganglionic sympathetic cell bodies (sweating, vasomotor), not sensory neurons.","Correct — the sensory neurons of the medial brachial cutaneous nerve have their cell bodies in the dorsal root ganglia.","The anterior (ventral) horn holds lower motor neurons (GSE) that supply skeletal muscle.","The lateral horn (T1–L2) holds PREganglionic sympathetic cell bodies.","The posterior (dorsal) horn holds second-order neurons and interneurons that receive the sensory input, not the first-order cell bodies."]},"a30":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The lateral cord gives the lateral pectoral nerve (→ pectoralis major), the musculocutaneous nerve and the lateral root of the median nerve (LLM).</p><p>Pectoralis major is supplied by BOTH pectoral nerves: the lateral pectoral nerve (lateral cord, C5–C7) mainly to its clavicular head and the medial pectoral nerve (medial cord, C8–T1) to its sternocostal part. A lateral cord lesion therefore weakens pectoralis major (along with biceps/brachialis via musculocutaneous, and some median-nerve muscles). The other options are all posterior-cord muscles, which sit on the back of the shoulder and form the posterior axillary wall.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Subscapularis</b> — Subscapularis is supplied by the upper and lower subscapular nerves (posterior cord).</li><li><b>Teres major</b> — Teres major is supplied by the lower subscapular nerve (posterior cord).</li><li><b>Latissimus dorsi</b> — Latissimus dorsi is supplied by the thoracodorsal nerve (posterior cord).</li><li><b>Teres minor</b> — Teres minor is supplied by the axillary nerve (posterior cord).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Lateral cord = LLM (Lateral pectoral, Lateral root of median, Musculocutaneous); posterior cord = STAR (Subscapulars, Thoracodorsal, Axillary, Radial).</p>","w":["Subscapularis is supplied by the upper and lower subscapular nerves (posterior cord).","Teres major is supplied by the lower subscapular nerve (posterior cord).","Latissimus dorsi is supplied by the thoracodorsal nerve (posterior cord).","Teres minor is supplied by the axillary nerve (posterior cord).","Correct — pectoralis major receives the lateral pectoral nerve from the lateral cord."]},"a31":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Carpal tunnel syndrome is compression of the median nerve deep to the flexor retinaculum.</p><p>The carpal tunnel is bounded by the carpal bones (floor and sides) and the flexor retinaculum (roof). It carries the median nerve plus nine tendons: four FDS, four FDP and flexor pollicis longus. Anything that crowds the tunnel (repetitive wrist flexion, tenosynovitis, pregnancy, hypothyroidism, rheumatoid arthritis) squeezes the median nerve. The result is numbness/tingling in the lateral 3½ digits (often worse at night), weakness and wasting of the thenar muscles (abductor pollicis brevis, opponens pollicis, superficial head of flexor pollicis brevis) and the lateral two lumbricals. Sensation over the thenar eminence is spared because the palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve passes ABOVE the retinaculum.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Ulnar artery</b> — The ulnar artery enters the palm superficial to the flexor retinaculum through Guyon's canal, outside the carpal tunnel.</li><li><b>Ulnar nerve</b> — The ulnar nerve also runs superficial to the retinaculum in Guyon's canal; it's compressed there, not in the carpal tunnel.</li><li><b>Flexor carpi radialis tendon</b> — The flexor carpi radialis tendon runs in its own groove in the trapezium, separate from the carpal tunnel.</li><li><b>Palmaris longus tendon</b> — The palmaris longus tendon passes superficial to the retinaculum to the palmar aponeurosis.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Tinel sign (tapping the wrist) and Phalen test (sustained wrist flexion) reproduce carpal tunnel symptoms; thenar-eminence skin is spared.</p>","w":["The ulnar artery enters the palm superficial to the flexor retinaculum through Guyon's canal, outside the carpal tunnel.","The ulnar nerve also runs superficial to the retinaculum in Guyon's canal; it's compressed there, not in the carpal tunnel.","Correct — the median nerve runs through the carpal tunnel and is compressed in carpal tunnel syndrome.","The flexor carpi radialis tendon runs in its own groove in the trapezium, separate from the carpal tunnel.","The palmaris longus tendon passes superficial to the retinaculum to the palmar aponeurosis."]},"a32":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The lunate sits in the middle of the proximal row, articulating with the radius above and the scaphoid and triquetrum on either side.</p><p>Proximal carpal row, lateral to medial: scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform. Distal row, lateral to medial: trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, hamate (\"Some Lovers Try Positions That They Can't Handle\"). The radius articulates with the scaphoid and lunate at the radiocarpal joint; the ulna is separated from the carpus by the articular disc. The lunate is the most commonly DISLOCATED carpal bone (usually anteriorly into the carpal tunnel, where it can compress the median nerve) and can undergo avascular necrosis (Kienböck disease).</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Scaphoid = most commonly fractured carpal; lunate = most commonly dislocated.</p>","w":["","Correct — this is the lunate, which articulates with the radius and the triquetrum.","","",""]},"a33":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The hook of the hamate anchors the flexor retinaculum and the hypothenar muscles flexor digiti minimi and opponens digiti minimi.</p><p>The hamate is the most medial bone of the distal carpal row; its hook projects into the palm and forms the lateral wall of Guyon's canal (with the pisiform medially). Flexor digiti minimi brevis and opponens digiti minimi arise from the hook and the flexor retinaculum, so a hook fracture (common in golfers, batters and cyclists) disrupts their origin. The deep branch of the ulnar nerve curves around the hook, so the same fracture can also injure it, weakening the interossei, medial lumbricals and adductor pollicis.</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hook of hamate fracture → ulnar nerve (deep branch) at risk; pain in the hypothenar region after a club or bat strikes the ground.</p>","w":["","","Correct — this is the hook of the hamate, the origin of flexor digiti minimi and opponens digiti minimi.","",""]},"a34":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The short thumb muscles (abductor pollicis brevis, flexor pollicis brevis, adductor pollicis) insert on the base of the proximal phalanx of the thumb; opponens pollicis inserts on the first metacarpal.</p><p>The thenar eminence is formed by abductor pollicis brevis, flexor pollicis brevis and opponens pollicis, all arising from the flexor retinaculum and the scaphoid/trapezium tubercles. Abductor pollicis brevis and flexor pollicis brevis insert on the lateral side of the base of the proximal phalanx of the thumb (adductor pollicis inserts on its medial side), while opponens pollicis inserts along the lateral border of the first metacarpal. The thenar muscles are supplied by the recurrent branch of the median nerve (except the deep head of flexor pollicis brevis, often ulnar). Note: the source explanation's \"adductor pollicis brevis\" means adductor pollicis.</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> The recurrent (thenar) branch of the median nerve is superficial at the base of the thenar eminence — the \"million-dollar nerve\" that can be cut by a superficial laceration.</p>","w":["","","","","Correct — this is the base of the proximal phalanx of the thumb, where the thenar muscles (except opponens) insert."]},"a35":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> FDS inserts on the sides of the MIDDLE phalanges of the four fingers; FDP inserts on the distal phalanges.</p><p>Each FDS tendon splits at the level of the proximal phalanx so the FDP tendon can pass through (Camper's chiasm), then reattaches to both sides of the shaft of the middle phalanx. This is why FDS flexes the PIP joint and FDP flexes the DIP joint. In a radiograph question, the middle phalanx of any finger (here the ring finger) is the FDS insertion.</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Test FDS by holding the other fingers straight and asking for PIP flexion; test FDP by stabilizing the PIP and asking for DIP flexion.</p>","w":["","","","Correct — this is a middle phalanx, the insertion of flexor digitorum superficialis.",""]},"a36":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The scaphoid forms the floor of the anatomic snuffbox; snuffbox tenderness after a fall means scaphoid fracture until proven otherwise.</p><p>The anatomic snuffbox is bounded by the tendons of extensor pollicis longus (medially) and abductor pollicis longus plus extensor pollicis brevis (laterally). Its floor is the scaphoid and trapezium, and the radial artery runs across it. The scaphoid's blood supply enters distally and flows backward to the proximal pole, so a fracture through the waist can leave the proximal fragment without blood, causing avascular necrosis and nonunion. Early X-rays are often normal, so suspected fractures are splinted (thumb spica) and re-imaged.</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Scaphoid = most commonly fractured carpal bone; risk of avascular necrosis of the proximal pole.</p>","w":["Correct — this is the scaphoid, which lies in the floor of the anatomic snuffbox.","","","",""]},"a37":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Palmar interossei ADduct the fingers (PAD) and, like all interossei, flex the MCP joints while extending the IP joints.</p><p>The interossei pass in front of the MCP joint axis and behind the IP joint axis by joining the extensor expansion, so they flex the MCP joints and extend the IP joints (the same as the lumbricals). Palmar interossei (to the index, ring and little fingers, plus the thumb in some descriptions) pull those fingers toward the axis of the middle finger, i.e., adduction. The ring finger is adducted by the third palmar interosseous, so weakness of both MCP flexion and adduction of the ring finger points to that muscle. All interossei are supplied by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Flexor digitorum profundus</b> — FDP flexes the DIP joints (and helps at PIP/MCP), but it cannot adduct the finger.</li><li><b>Extensor digitorum</b> — Extensor digitorum extends the MCP and IP joints; it neither flexes the MCP nor adducts.</li><li><b>Lumbrical</b> — The ring finger's lumbrical flexes the MCP and extends the IP joints, but lumbricals do not adduct.</li><li><b>Dorsal interosseous</b> — Dorsal interossei flex the MCP but ABduct the fingers away from the middle finger (DAB).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> PAD and DAB. Card test: patient grips a card between straightened fingers (palmar interossei); ulnar nerve lesions weaken this.</p>","w":["FDP flexes the DIP joints (and helps at PIP/MCP), but it cannot adduct the finger.","Extensor digitorum extends the MCP and IP joints; it neither flexes the MCP nor adducts.","The ring finger's lumbrical flexes the MCP and extends the IP joints, but lumbricals do not adduct.","Dorsal interossei flex the MCP but ABduct the fingers away from the middle finger (DAB).","Correct — the palmar interosseous to the ring finger flexes its MCP joint and adducts it."]},"a38":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The middle finger has no palmar interosseous: it's the reference line for adduction and abduction, so only DORSAL interossei attach to it.</p><p>Abduction and adduction of the fingers are defined relative to the long axis of the middle finger. Adduction means moving toward it, so the middle finger itself can't be adducted and receives no palmar interosseous. It can be abducted to either side, so it receives two dorsal interossei (the second on its radial side and the third on its ulnar side). It also receives extensor digitorum, FDS, FDP and the second lumbrical. A crush of the middle finger damages the muscles that attach to it, so the one that \"retains function\" is the palmar interosseous, which never attached there.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Extensor digitorum</b> — Extensor digitorum sends a tendon to the middle finger's extensor expansion.</li><li><b>Flexor digitorum profundus</b> — FDP sends a tendon to the distal phalanx of the middle finger.</li><li><b>Dorsal interosseous</b> — Two dorsal interossei (2nd and 3rd) insert on the middle finger.</li><li><b>Lumbrical</b> — The second lumbrical inserts on the extensor expansion of the middle finger.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Middle finger: two dorsal interossei, zero palmar interossei.</p>","w":["Extensor digitorum sends a tendon to the middle finger's extensor expansion.","FDP sends a tendon to the distal phalanx of the middle finger.","Correct — no palmar interosseous attaches to the middle finger, so it isn't affected by the crush.","Two dorsal interossei (2nd and 3rd) insert on the middle finger.","The second lumbrical inserts on the extensor expansion of the middle finger."]},"a39":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Superficial to the flexor retinaculum: ulnar nerve, ulnar artery, palmaris longus tendon and the palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve. Deep (in the carpal tunnel): median nerve and the long flexor tendons.</p><p>At the wrist, the ulnar nerve and artery run lateral to the pisiform and superficial to the flexor retinaculum through Guyon's canal (roofed by the volar carpal ligament). Because they are so superficial, cuts on the medial (ulnar) side of the wrist from broken glass often injure both: the nerve injury causes claw hand and loss of finger abduction/adduction, and the arterial injury bleeds briskly. The median nerve and the FDS, FDP and FPL tendons are protected deep to the retinaculum inside the carpal tunnel.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Ulnar nerve and median nerve</b> — The ulnar nerve is superficial, but the median nerve passes DEEP to the retinaculum through the carpal tunnel.</li><li><b>Median nerve and flexor digitorum profundus</b> — Both the median nerve and FDP pass deep to the retinaculum.</li><li><b>Median nerve and flexor pollicis longus</b> — Both the median nerve and FPL pass deep to the retinaculum.</li><li><b>Ulnar nerve and flexor digitorum superficialis</b> — The ulnar nerve is superficial, but FDS tendons pass deep to the retinaculum.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> \"Guyon's canal\" = ulnar nerve + artery between the pisiform and hook of hamate; superficial to the flexor retinaculum.</p>","w":["The ulnar nerve is superficial, but the median nerve passes DEEP to the retinaculum through the carpal tunnel.","Both the median nerve and FDP pass deep to the retinaculum.","Both the median nerve and FPL pass deep to the retinaculum.","Correct — the ulnar artery and ulnar nerve enter the palm superficial to the flexor retinaculum.","The ulnar nerve is superficial, but FDS tendons pass deep to the retinaculum."]},"a40":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Opponens pollicis is the only intrinsic thumb muscle inserting on the first METACARPAL; the others insert on the proximal phalanx.</p><p>Bennett's fracture is an intra-articular fracture–dislocation of the base of the first metacarpal; abductor pollicis longus (inserting on the base) pulls the main fragment proximally. Opponens pollicis arises from the flexor retinaculum and trapezium and inserts along the whole lateral border of the first metacarpal, rotating it medially so the thumb pad can face the fingers (opposition). Because its insertion is on the metacarpal, a fracture there disturbs opposition. The other short thumb muscles cross the MCP joint to insert on the proximal phalanx, so they aren't directly attached at the fracture.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Abductor pollicis brevis</b> — Abductor pollicis brevis inserts on the lateral base of the proximal phalanx of the thumb.</li><li><b>Flexor pollicis brevis (superficial head)</b> — The superficial head of flexor pollicis brevis inserts on the lateral base of the proximal phalanx.</li><li><b>Adductor pollicis</b> — Adductor pollicis inserts on the medial base of the proximal phalanx (ulnar nerve).</li><li><b>Flexor pollicis brevis (deep head)</b> — The deep head of flexor pollicis brevis inserts on the proximal phalanx (often ulnar-innervated).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Bennett fracture = intra-articular base of first metacarpal; opponens pollicis is the intrinsic muscle attached there.</p>","w":["Abductor pollicis brevis inserts on the lateral base of the proximal phalanx of the thumb.","The superficial head of flexor pollicis brevis inserts on the lateral base of the proximal phalanx.","Correct — opponens pollicis inserts on the first metacarpal, the bone broken in Bennett's fracture.","Adductor pollicis inserts on the medial base of the proximal phalanx (ulnar nerve).","The deep head of flexor pollicis brevis inserts on the proximal phalanx (often ulnar-innervated)."]},"a41":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> In the hand the median nerve supplies only \"LOAF\": Lateral two lumbricals, Opponens pollicis, Abductor pollicis brevis, Flexor pollicis brevis (superficial head). Everything else intrinsic is ulnar.</p><p>After passing through the carpal tunnel, the median nerve gives the recurrent (thenar) branch to the thenar muscles and digital branches that supply the lateral two lumbricals (to the index and middle fingers) and the skin of the lateral 3½ digits. Carpal tunnel compression therefore weakens opposition and thumb abduction and impairs MCP flexion/IP extension of the index and middle fingers — a real problem for fine finger work such as piano playing. All interossei, the medial two lumbricals, adductor pollicis and the hypothenar muscles are supplied by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve, which runs outside the carpal tunnel and is spared.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Palmar interossei and adductor pollicis</b> — Both palmar interossei and adductor pollicis are supplied by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve.</li><li><b>Dorsal interossei and lateral two lumbricals</b> — The lateral lumbricals fit, but dorsal interossei are ulnar nerve.</li><li><b>Abductor pollicis brevis and palmar interossei</b> — Abductor pollicis brevis fits, but palmar interossei are ulnar nerve.</li><li><b>Medial two and lateral two lumbricals</b> — The lateral lumbricals fit, but the medial two lumbricals (ring and little fingers) are ulnar nerve.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> LOAF = median in the hand. Lumbricals follow the FDP split: lateral half median, medial half ulnar.</p>","w":["Both palmar interossei and adductor pollicis are supplied by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve.","The lateral lumbricals fit, but dorsal interossei are ulnar nerve.","Correct — the median nerve supplies the lateral two lumbricals and opponens pollicis, both weakened in carpal tunnel syndrome.","Abductor pollicis brevis fits, but palmar interossei are ulnar nerve.","The lateral lumbricals fit, but the medial two lumbricals (ring and little fingers) are ulnar nerve."]},"a42":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The ulnar bursa (common flexor sheath) wraps the FDS and FDP tendons; the radial bursa wraps flexor pollicis longus.</p><p>In the carpal tunnel the long flexor tendons are wrapped in two synovial sheaths. The ulnar bursa encloses all eight FDS and FDP tendons and usually continues into the digital sheath of the little finger. The radial bursa encloses the FPL tendon and continues to the thumb. A penetrating palm wound that seeds bacteria into the ulnar bursa produces tenosynovitis that can spread along these tendons, and pressure and infection can cause tendon necrosis. Because the two bursae often communicate at the wrist, infection from the little finger can spread to the thumb (a \"horseshoe abscess\").</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Tendon of the flexor carpi ulnaris</b> — Flexor carpi ulnaris inserts on the pisiform at the wrist and has no tendon running in the palmar synovial sheaths.</li><li><b>Tendon of the flexor pollicis longus</b> — FPL lies in the RADIAL bursa, not the ulnar bursa.</li><li><b>Tendon of the flexor carpi radialis</b> — Flexor carpi radialis runs in its own sheath through a groove in the trapezium, outside the carpal tunnel.</li><li><b>Tendon of the palmaris longus</b> — Palmaris longus passes superficial to the flexor retinaculum into the palmar aponeurosis, outside any synovial bursa.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Ulnar bursa → little finger sheath; radial bursa → thumb sheath; index/middle/ring finger sheaths are usually separate.</p>","w":["Flexor carpi ulnaris inserts on the pisiform at the wrist and has no tendon running in the palmar synovial sheaths.","FPL lies in the RADIAL bursa, not the ulnar bursa.","Correct — the FDP (and FDS) tendons lie in the ulnar bursa, so infection there can cause their necrosis.","Flexor carpi radialis runs in its own sheath through a groove in the trapezium, outside the carpal tunnel.","Palmaris longus passes superficial to the flexor retinaculum into the palmar aponeurosis, outside any synovial bursa."]},"a43":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Carpal tunnel syndrome = median nerve at the wrist → weak/wasted thenar eminence + numbness of the lateral 3½ digits.</p><p>The recurrent branch of the median nerve supplies abductor pollicis brevis, opponens pollicis and the superficial head of flexor pollicis brevis. With chronic compression these muscles waste, flattening the thenar eminence (\"ape hand\" in severe cases), and thumb abduction and opposition weaken. Sensory loss affects the palmar side of the thumb, index, middle and half of the ring finger. Findings in the little finger, the ring finger DIP or adductor pollicis point instead to the ulnar nerve, which is outside the tunnel.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Inability to adduct the little finger</b> — Little-finger adduction is done by the third palmar interosseous, supplied by the ulnar nerve.</li><li><b>Inability to flex the DIP joint of the ring finger</b> — DIP flexion of the ring finger is done by the medial half of FDP, supplied by the ulnar nerve in the forearm.</li><li><b>Loss of skin sensation of the medial one and one-half fingers</b> — Skin of the medial 1½ fingers is supplied by the ulnar nerve; carpal tunnel affects the LATERAL 3½.</li><li><b>Atrophied adductor pollicis muscle</b> — Adductor pollicis is supplied by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve; it isn't part of the thenar eminence.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Thenar wasting + lateral 3½ digit numbness, sparing thenar-eminence skin = carpal tunnel.</p>","w":["Little-finger adduction is done by the third palmar interosseous, supplied by the ulnar nerve.","DIP flexion of the ring finger is done by the medial half of FDP, supplied by the ulnar nerve in the forearm.","Correct — a flattened (wasted) thenar eminence reflects median nerve compression in the carpal tunnel.","Skin of the medial 1½ fingers is supplied by the ulnar nerve; carpal tunnel affects the LATERAL 3½.","Adductor pollicis is supplied by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve; it isn't part of the thenar eminence."]},"a44":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Proximal carpal row (lateral → medial): scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform. Distal row: trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, hamate.</p><p>Mnemonic: \"Some Lovers Try Positions That They Can't Handle\" lists all eight from lateral to medial, proximal row first. The proximal row forms the radiocarpal (wrist) joint with the radius and articular disc; the pisiform sits on the front of the triquetrum as a sesamoid in the FCU tendon. Of the choices, only the triquetrum belongs to the proximal row.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Capitate</b> — Capitate is the largest carpal bone and sits in the middle of the distal row.</li><li><b>Hamate</b> — Hamate is the most medial bone of the distal row (with its hook).</li><li><b>Trapezium</b> — Trapezium is the most lateral bone of the distal row, articulating with the first metacarpal.</li><li><b>Trapezoid</b> — Trapezoid is in the distal row between the trapezium and capitate.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> \"Some Lovers Try Positions That They Can't Handle.\"</p>","w":["Capitate is the largest carpal bone and sits in the middle of the distal row.","Hamate is the most medial bone of the distal row (with its hook).","Trapezium is the most lateral bone of the distal row, articulating with the first metacarpal.","Correct — the triquetrum is in the proximal row (with the scaphoid, lunate and pisiform).","Trapezoid is in the distal row between the trapezium and capitate."]},"a45":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The little finger has no dorsal interosseous because it has its own abductor (abductor digiti minimi).</p><p>Dorsal interossei abduct fingers away from the axis of the middle finger. The index finger gets the first, the middle finger gets the second and third, and the ring finger gets the fourth. The little finger is abducted by abductor digiti minimi (a hypothenar muscle) instead, and the thumb by abductor pollicis brevis and longus. Muscles that DO attach to the little finger include extensor digitorum (and extensor digiti minimi), FDS, FDP, the third palmar interosseous and the fourth lumbrical, so a crush of the whole finger would damage those tendons but not a dorsal interosseous.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Flexor digitorum profundus</b> — FDP sends a tendon to the distal phalanx of the little finger.</li><li><b>Extensor digitorum</b> — Extensor digitorum sends a tendon to the little finger's extensor expansion.</li><li><b>Palmar interossei</b> — The third palmar interosseous inserts on the little finger and adducts it.</li><li><b>Lumbricals</b> — The fourth lumbrical inserts on the extensor expansion of the little finger.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Thumb and little finger each have their own abductors, so no dorsal interosseous attaches to them.</p>","w":["FDP sends a tendon to the distal phalanx of the little finger.","Extensor digitorum sends a tendon to the little finger's extensor expansion.","The third palmar interosseous inserts on the little finger and adducts it.","Correct — no dorsal interosseous attaches to the little finger, so it is spared.","The fourth lumbrical inserts on the extensor expansion of the little finger."]},"a46":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Flexor carpi ulnaris inserts on the pisiform and, via the pisohamate and pisometacarpal ligaments, on the hook of the hamate and base of the 5th metacarpal.</p><p>FCU arises from the medial epicondyle (humeral head) and the olecranon/posterior ulna (ulnar head) and runs down the medial forearm to the pisiform, a sesamoid bone in its tendon. Ligaments continue its pull to the hook of the hamate and the base of the fifth metacarpal. It flexes and adducts (ulnar deviates) the wrist and is supplied by the ulnar nerve. Destroying the pisiform/hook region detaches its insertion and weakens wrist flexion and adduction.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Flexor carpi radialis</b> — Flexor carpi radialis inserts on the bases of the 2nd and 3rd metacarpals, on the lateral side.</li><li><b>Palmaris longus</b> — Palmaris longus inserts into the palmar aponeurosis, not a carpal bone.</li><li><b>Brachioradialis</b> — Brachioradialis inserts on the radial styloid process.</li><li><b>Flexor digitorum superficialis</b> — FDS inserts on the middle phalanges of the four fingers.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> FCU is the only wrist flexor supplied by the ulnar nerve; the pisiform is a sesamoid in its tendon.</p>","w":["Flexor carpi radialis inserts on the bases of the 2nd and 3rd metacarpals, on the lateral side.","Palmaris longus inserts into the palmar aponeurosis, not a carpal bone.","Correct — FCU inserts on the pisiform and hook of the hamate, so destroying them weakens it.","Brachioradialis inserts on the radial styloid process.","FDS inserts on the middle phalanges of the four fingers."]},"a47":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The scaphoid forms the floor of the anatomic snuffbox and its tubercle gives origin to abductor pollicis brevis.</p><p>The scaphoid is the lateral bone of the proximal carpal row. Its tubercle on the palmar surface, together with the trapezium tubercle and the flexor retinaculum, provides origin for abductor pollicis brevis and flexor pollicis brevis. Dorsally it forms the floor of the anatomic snuffbox, with the radial artery lying on it. Damage to both locations therefore points to the scaphoid.</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Scaphoid tubercle and trapezium tubercle are the lateral anchors of the flexor retinaculum.</p>","w":["","Correct — this is the scaphoid, floor of the snuffbox and origin of abductor pollicis brevis.","","",""]},"a48":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The middle finger is abducted (in both directions) by the 2nd and 3rd dorsal interossei.</p><p>Dorsal interossei are bipennate muscles filling the spaces between the metacarpals, visible on a transverse MRI of the mid-palm as the muscles between adjacent metacarpal shafts closer to the dorsum. The second dorsal interosseous (between metacarpals 2 and 3) inserts on the radial side of the middle finger and abducts it toward the thumb; the third (between metacarpals 3 and 4) moves it toward the ulnar side. Both are supplied by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve.</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Any movement of the middle finger to either side is \"abduction\" — done only by dorsal interossei.</p>","w":["","","","Correct — this is the second dorsal interosseous, which abducts the middle finger.","","","",""]},"a49":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Flexor pollicis longus is supplied by the anterior interosseous branch of the MEDIAN nerve.</p><p>On a transverse MRI through the palm, the FPL tendon runs alone on the lateral side, between the thenar muscles, in its own radial bursa. FPL flexes the interphalangeal joint of the thumb (the thumb tip). It is innervated by the anterior interosseous nerve, a branch of the median nerve in the forearm, so a proximal median nerve lesion paralyzes it while a carpal tunnel lesion does not (its muscle belly is in the forearm).</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Thumb-tip flexion = FPL = anterior interosseous (median); weak \"OK\" sign.</p>","w":["Correct — this is flexor pollicis longus, supplied by the median nerve (anterior interosseous branch).","","","","","","",""]},"a50":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The little finger is adducted by the third palmar interosseous (ulnar nerve).</p><p>Palmar interossei are unipennate muscles on the palmar side of the metacarpals of the fingers that move toward the middle finger: index (1st), ring (2nd) and little finger (3rd). The third palmar interosseous arises from the fifth metacarpal and inserts on the radial side of the little finger, pulling it toward the middle finger. Loss of it (ulnar nerve palsy) can leave the little finger slightly abducted (Wartenberg sign).</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Wartenberg sign: persistent abduction of the little finger from unopposed extensor digiti minimi when the palmar interosseous is weak.</p>","w":["","","","","","","Correct — this is the third palmar interosseous, which adducts the little finger.",""]},"a51":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Extensor digitorum extends both the MCP and IP joints of the fingers through the extensor expansion (radial nerve).</p><p>Extensor digitorum tendons run on the dorsum of the hand and spread into extensor (dorsal digital) expansions over each finger. The central slip inserts on the middle phalanx and the lateral bands on the distal phalanx, so the muscle extends the MCP, PIP and DIP joints. The interossei and lumbricals also extend the IP joints, but only extensor digitorum (with extensor indicis and digiti minimi) extends the MCP joints. It is supplied by the posterior interosseous branch of the radial nerve.</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> MCP extension is purely the long extensors; IP extension is shared with the lumbricals and interossei.</p>","w":["","","","","Correct — this is extensor digitorum, which extends both MCP and IP joints.","","",""]},"a52":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The intercostobrachial nerve is the lateral cutaneous branch of the 2nd intercostal nerve (T2); it is purely cutaneous to the medial arm and axillary floor.</p><p>The intercostobrachial nerve crosses the axilla to supply skin of the axillary floor and the upper medial arm, often joining the medial cutaneous nerve of the arm. Being a cutaneous nerve, it carries sensory (GSA) fibers with cell bodies in the T2 dorsal root ganglion and postganglionic sympathetic fibers to sweat glands and vessels; it has no motor fibers. It is often injured during axillary node dissection, leaving numbness of the medial upper arm. Cardiac pain referred to the medial arm travels along the same T1–T2 dermatomes.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Inability to move the ribs</b> — Rib movement needs motor fibers to intercostal muscles; the intercostobrachial nerve is purely cutaneous.</li><li><b>Loss of tactile sensation on the lateral aspect of the arm</b> — It supplies the MEDIAL (not lateral) arm; the lateral arm is supplied by the axillary and radial nerves (C5–C6).</li><li><b>Absence of sweating on the posterior aspect of the arm</b> — Its sympathetic fibers serve the medial arm and axilla, not the posterior arm.</li><li><b>Damage to the sympathetic preganglionic fibers</b> — Peripheral cutaneous nerves carry POSTganglionic sympathetic fibers; preganglionic fibers stay in the white rami and chain.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Intercostobrachial (T2) explains medial-arm numbness after axillary surgery and medial-arm referred angina.</p>","w":["Rib movement needs motor fibers to intercostal muscles; the intercostobrachial nerve is purely cutaneous.","It supplies the MEDIAL (not lateral) arm; the lateral arm is supplied by the axillary and radial nerves (C5–C6).","Its sympathetic fibers serve the medial arm and axilla, not the posterior arm.","Correct — the intercostobrachial nerve carries sensory fibers from the lateral cutaneous branch of the 2nd intercostal nerve.","Peripheral cutaneous nerves carry POSTganglionic sympathetic fibers; preganglionic fibers stay in the white rami and chain."]},"a53":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cubital fossa contents from medial to lateral: Median nerve, brachial Artery, biceps Tendon, Radial nerve (\"MATeR\" / \"My Bloody Tendons Rule\").</p><p>The cubital fossa is bounded by pronator teres (medially), brachioradialis (laterally) and a line between the epicondyles (superiorly), with a floor of brachialis and supinator. The median nerve is the most medial content, lying medial to the brachial artery; the biceps tendon is lateral to the artery, and the radial nerve lies most laterally, tucked between brachioradialis and brachialis. A stab wound to the most medial part of the fossa therefore hits the median nerve.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Biceps brachii tendon</b> — The biceps tendon lies lateral to the brachial artery, in the middle–lateral fossa.</li><li><b>Radial nerve</b> — The radial nerve is the most lateral structure, under the edge of brachioradialis.</li><li><b>Brachial artery</b> — The brachial artery lies just lateral to the median nerve, not most medial.</li><li><b>Radial recurrent artery</b> — The radial recurrent artery runs up the lateral side of the fossa alongside the radial nerve.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Medial → lateral: median nerve, brachial artery, biceps tendon, radial nerve.</p>","w":["The biceps tendon lies lateral to the brachial artery, in the middle–lateral fossa.","The radial nerve is the most lateral structure, under the edge of brachioradialis.","The brachial artery lies just lateral to the median nerve, not most medial.","The radial recurrent artery runs up the lateral side of the fossa alongside the radial nerve.","Correct — the median nerve is the most medial content of the cubital fossa."]},"a54":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The floor of the cubital fossa is brachialis (proximally) and supinator (distally); pronator teres and brachioradialis are its walls.</p><p>The cubital fossa is a triangular space in front of the elbow. Its lateral boundary is brachioradialis, its medial boundary is pronator teres, and its roof is skin, fascia and the bicipital aponeurosis (with the median cubital vein on top). The floor is formed by brachialis and supinator. Tearing the floor therefore weakens elbow flexion (brachialis, the main flexor) and supination (supinator).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Brachioradialis and supinator</b> — Supinator fits, but brachioradialis is the LATERAL boundary, not the floor.</li><li><b>Pronator teres and supinator</b> — Supinator fits, but pronator teres is the MEDIAL boundary.</li><li><b>Supinator and pronator quadratus</b> — Supinator fits, but pronator quadratus is at the distal radius and ulna near the wrist.</li><li><b>Brachialis and pronator teres</b> — Brachialis fits, but pronator teres is a boundary, not the floor.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Floor: brachialis + supinator. Walls: brachioradialis (lateral) + pronator teres (medial).</p>","w":["Supinator fits, but brachioradialis is the LATERAL boundary, not the floor.","Correct — brachialis and supinator form the floor of the cubital fossa.","Supinator fits, but pronator teres is the MEDIAL boundary.","Supinator fits, but pronator quadratus is at the distal radius and ulna near the wrist.","Brachialis fits, but pronator teres is a boundary, not the floor."]},"a55":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Breast cancer that infiltrates the suspensory (Cooper's) ligaments shortens them and pulls in the skin, producing dimpling (\"peau d'orange\" when lymphatics are blocked).</p><p>Cooper's ligaments are fibrous bands running from the deep fascia through the breast to the dermis. A carcinoma that invades and fibroses them shortens them, tethering and dimpling the overlying skin, especially when the arm is raised. Infiltration of the lactiferous ducts causes nipple retraction or inversion, and blockage of subdermal lymphatics causes edema with pitted orange-peel skin. Fixation to pectoralis major (via the retromammary space and pectoral fascia) suggests deep invasion.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Elevated nipple</b> — Cancer pulls on the lactiferous ducts and RETRACTS or inverts the nipple; it doesn't elevate it.</li><li><b>Polymastia</b> — Polymastia (supernumerary breasts along the milk line) is a congenital anomaly, unrelated to cancer.</li><li><b>Shortening of the clavipectoral fascia</b> — The clavipectoral fascia lies deep to pectoralis major around pectoralis minor; it isn't the structure that shortens in cancer.</li><li><b>Enlargement of the breast</b> — General enlargement isn't a characteristic sign; a localized hard mass with skin changes is typical.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Skin dimpling = Cooper's ligaments; nipple retraction = lactiferous ducts; peau d'orange = blocked lymphatics.</p>","w":["Cancer pulls on the lactiferous ducts and RETRACTS or inverts the nipple; it doesn't elevate it.","Polymastia (supernumerary breasts along the milk line) is a congenital anomaly, unrelated to cancer.","The clavipectoral fascia lies deep to pectoralis major around pectoralis minor; it isn't the structure that shortens in cancer.","Correct — shortening of the suspensory (Cooper's) ligaments dimples the overlying skin.","General enlargement isn't a characteristic sign; a localized hard mass with skin changes is typical."]},"a56":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> About 75% of breast lymph drains to the axillary nodes, first to the anterior (pectoral) group; most of the rest goes to the parasternal (internal thoracic) nodes.</p><p>Lymph from the lateral and upper parts of the breast follows vessels along the inferior border of pectoralis minor to the anterior (pectoral) axillary nodes, then on to the central and apical nodes, then to the supraclavicular nodes and subclavian lymph trunk. Medial-quadrant lymph drains mainly to the parasternal nodes along the internal thoracic vessels, which is how medial tumors can spread to the opposite breast or mediastinum. The sentinel node biopsied in breast cancer is usually an anterior (pectoral) axillary node.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Apical nodes</b> — Apical nodes receive lymph secondarily from the other axillary groups; they are not the first stop.</li><li><b>Parasternal (internal thoracic) nodes</b> — Parasternal nodes drain mainly the medial quadrants, a minority (~25%) of breast lymph.</li><li><b>Supraclavicular nodes</b> — Supraclavicular nodes are reached after the apical nodes; enlarged ones indicate advanced spread.</li><li><b>Nodes of the anterior abdominal wall</b> — Lower-quadrant lymph can occasionally reach abdominal wall nodes, but this is minor.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Breast lymph: anterior (pectoral) → central → apical → supraclavicular; medial breast → parasternal.</p>","w":["Apical nodes receive lymph secondarily from the other axillary groups; they are not the first stop.","Correct — most breast lymph drains first to the anterior (pectoral) axillary nodes.","Parasternal nodes drain mainly the medial quadrants, a minority (~25%) of breast lymph.","Supraclavicular nodes are reached after the apical nodes; enlarged ones indicate advanced spread.","Lower-quadrant lymph can occasionally reach abdominal wall nodes, but this is minor."]},"a57":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The radial artery crosses the floor of the anatomic snuffbox (on the scaphoid and trapezium) to reach the dorsum of the hand.</p><p>The snuffbox's lateral (palmar) border is formed by the tendons of abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis and its medial (dorsal) border by extensor pollicis longus. Its floor is the scaphoid and trapezium, and the radial artery runs diagonally across that floor (its pulse can be felt there) before passing between the heads of the first dorsal interosseous into the palm. The superficial branch of the radial nerve and the cephalic vein begin over the roof. A destructive injury here can tear the radial artery.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Triquetral bone</b> — The triquetrum is on the medial (ulnar) side of the proximal row, far from the snuffbox.</li><li><b>Trapezoid bone</b> — The trapezoid is in the distal row and isn't part of the snuffbox floor (the trapezium is).</li><li><b>Extensor indicis tendon</b> — Extensor indicis runs to the index finger, medial to the snuffbox; it isn't a boundary.</li><li><b>Abductor pollicis brevis tendon</b> — Abductor pollicis brevis is a thenar muscle on the palm; the snuffbox boundaries are abductor pollicis LONGUS and the extensors.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Snuffbox: floor scaphoid + trapezium; content radial artery; borders APL/EPB and EPL.</p>","w":["The triquetrum is on the medial (ulnar) side of the proximal row, far from the snuffbox.","The trapezoid is in the distal row and isn't part of the snuffbox floor (the trapezium is).","Extensor indicis runs to the index finger, medial to the snuffbox; it isn't a boundary.","Abductor pollicis brevis is a thenar muscle on the palm; the snuffbox boundaries are abductor pollicis LONGUS and the extensors.","Correct — the radial artery lies on the floor of the anatomic snuffbox."]},"a58":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Under the median cubital vein lies the bicipital aponeurosis, and under that, the brachial artery and median nerve.</p><p>The median cubital vein connects the cephalic and basilic veins across the front of the elbow and is the usual venipuncture site. It sits superficial to the bicipital aponeurosis, a fascial sheet from the biceps tendon to the medial forearm fascia, which protects the brachial artery and median nerve underneath. Pushing the needle too deep (through the aponeurosis) punctures the brachial artery, which divides into radial and ulnar arteries only at the apex of the cubital fossa near the neck of the radius.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Radial</b> — The radial artery begins at the bifurcation at the lower end of the cubital fossa and runs down the lateral forearm.</li><li><b>Ulnar</b> — The ulnar artery also begins at the bifurcation and dives deep to pronator teres, away from the median cubital vein.</li><li><b>Common interosseous</b> — The common interosseous artery branches from the ulnar artery in the upper forearm, below the fossa.</li><li><b>Superior ulnar collateral</b> — The superior ulnar collateral artery leaves the brachial artery in the mid-arm and runs with the ulnar nerve behind the medial epicondyle.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> The bicipital aponeurosis is the only layer between the median cubital vein and the brachial artery.</p>","w":["Correct — the brachial artery lies just deep to the bicipital aponeurosis beneath the median cubital vein.","The radial artery begins at the bifurcation at the lower end of the cubital fossa and runs down the lateral forearm.","The ulnar artery also begins at the bifurcation and dives deep to pronator teres, away from the median cubital vein.","The common interosseous artery branches from the ulnar artery in the upper forearm, below the fossa.","The superior ulnar collateral artery leaves the brachial artery in the mid-arm and runs with the ulnar nerve behind the medial epicondyle."]},"a59":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> In the cubital fossa the median nerve lies immediately MEDIAL to the brachial artery.</p><p>Median nerve and brachial artery run together down the medial arm, with the nerve crossing from lateral to medial over the artery at mid-arm. By the time they reach the elbow, the nerve is medial to the artery, both deep to the bicipital aponeurosis. A needle that hits the brachial artery and strays medially hits the median nerve, causing shooting pain into the lateral 3½ digits. The ulnar nerve is not in the cubital fossa at all; it passes behind the medial epicondyle.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Radial</b> — The radial nerve is the most LATERAL structure, deep between brachioradialis and brachialis.</li><li><b>Ulnar</b> — The ulnar nerve runs behind the medial epicondyle, outside the cubital fossa.</li><li><b>Lateral antebrachial</b> — The lateral antebrachial cutaneous nerve (end of musculocutaneous) is superficial on the lateral side, near the cephalic vein.</li><li><b>Medial antebrachial</b> — The medial antebrachial cutaneous nerve is superficial near the basilic vein, not next to the brachial artery.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> In the arm, the median nerve crosses the brachial artery from lateral to medial; at the elbow it is medial.</p>","w":["The radial nerve is the most LATERAL structure, deep between brachioradialis and brachialis.","Correct — the median nerve lies just medial to the brachial artery in the cubital fossa.","The ulnar nerve runs behind the medial epicondyle, outside the cubital fossa.","The lateral antebrachial cutaneous nerve (end of musculocutaneous) is superficial on the lateral side, near the cephalic vein.","The medial antebrachial cutaneous nerve is superficial near the basilic vein, not next to the brachial artery."]},"a60":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Quadrangular space = axillary nerve + posterior circumflex humeral artery.</p><p>The quadrangular space is bounded by teres minor (above), teres major (below), the long head of triceps (medially) and the surgical neck of the humerus (laterally). The axillary nerve (posterior cord, C5–C6) and posterior circumflex humeral artery travel through it together to reach the deltoid and teres minor. A wound that cuts the artery here is likely to cut the nerve, causing weak abduction, weak lateral rotation and numbness over the lower deltoid. Compression of the space (quadrilateral space syndrome) can occur in overhead athletes.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Radial nerve</b> — The radial nerve passes through the triangular INTERVAL (below teres major) with the deep brachial artery.</li><li><b>Thoracodorsal nerve</b> — The thoracodorsal nerve runs on the deep surface of latissimus dorsi along the posterior axillary wall.</li><li><b>Suprascapular nerve</b> — The suprascapular nerve passes through the suprascapular notch (under the superior transverse scapular ligament).</li><li><b>Accessory nerve</b> — The accessory nerve crosses the posterior triangle of the neck to trapezius.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Quadrangular space: axillary nerve + posterior circumflex humeral artery. Triangular space: circumflex scapular artery. Triangular interval: radial nerve + deep brachial artery.</p>","w":["The radial nerve passes through the triangular INTERVAL (below teres major) with the deep brachial artery.","Correct — the axillary nerve accompanies the posterior circumflex humeral artery through the quadrangular space.","The thoracodorsal nerve runs on the deep surface of latissimus dorsi along the posterior axillary wall.","The suprascapular nerve passes through the suprascapular notch (under the superior transverse scapular ligament).","The accessory nerve crosses the posterior triangle of the neck to trapezius."]},"a61":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Radial nerve in the spiral (radial) groove → wrist drop, with triceps usually spared.</p><p>The radial nerve winds around the back of the humerus in the radial groove with the deep brachial artery, between the lateral and medial heads of triceps. A blow or midshaft fracture here damages it after most triceps branches have left, so elbow extension is preserved. Below the injury it supplies brachioradialis, the extensor carpi radialis longus/brevis, and (through the posterior interosseous nerve) the extensors of the wrist, fingers and thumb, so the hand cannot be extended and hangs flexed (wrist drop). Sensory loss is mainly over the dorsum of the lateral hand, especially the first dorsal web space.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Numbness over the medial side of the forearm</b> — The medial forearm is supplied by the medial cutaneous nerve of the forearm (medial cord), not the radial nerve.</li><li><b>Inability to oppose the thumb</b> — Opposition is done by opponens pollicis (median nerve, recurrent branch).</li><li><b>Weakness in pronating the forearm</b> — Pronation is done by pronator teres and pronator quadratus (median nerve).</li><li><b>Weakness in abducting the arm</b> — Arm abduction is done by deltoid and supraspinatus (axillary and suprascapular nerves), which leave the plexus well above the radial groove.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Spiral-groove radial lesion: wrist drop + first-web-space numbness, triceps spared. Axillary radial lesion (crutches): wrist drop + weak triceps.</p>","w":["The medial forearm is supplied by the medial cutaneous nerve of the forearm (medial cord), not the radial nerve.","Opposition is done by opponens pollicis (median nerve, recurrent branch).","Pronation is done by pronator teres and pronator quadratus (median nerve).","Arm abduction is done by deltoid and supraspinatus (axillary and suprascapular nerves), which leave the plexus well above the radial groove.","Correct — radial nerve injury paralyzes the wrist and finger extensors, causing wrist drop."]},"a62":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The ulnar nerve runs behind the medial epicondyle; a fracture there can injure it, causing sensory and sympathetic (sweating) loss over the medial hand.</p><p>Peripheral nerves carry three kinds of fibers to the skin: sensory (touch/pain), and sympathetic postganglionic fibers to sweat glands and blood vessels. So when a mixed nerve like the ulnar nerve is injured, its cutaneous territory (the medial 1½ fingers and medial palm and dorsum) loses both sensation and sweating (anhidrosis). Motor findings would include weak FCU, medial FDP and most intrinsic hand muscles with claw of the ring and little fingers. None of the other answers follow from an ulnar lesion.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Impaired abduction of the hand</b> — \"Abduction of the hand\" (radial deviation of the wrist) is done by flexor carpi radialis and the radial wrist extensors (median and radial nerves).</li><li><b>Carpal tunnel syndrome</b> — Carpal tunnel syndrome is median nerve compression at the wrist.</li><li><b>Wrist drop</b> — Wrist drop results from radial nerve injury.</li><li><b>Thenar atrophy</b> — Thenar atrophy results from median nerve injury (recurrent branch).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Medial epicondyle fracture → ulnar nerve; loss of sweating maps the same territory as loss of sensation.</p>","w":["\"Abduction of the hand\" (radial deviation of the wrist) is done by flexor carpi radialis and the radial wrist extensors (median and radial nerves).","Carpal tunnel syndrome is median nerve compression at the wrist.","Wrist drop results from radial nerve injury.","Thenar atrophy results from median nerve injury (recurrent branch).","Correct — ulnar nerve injury at the medial epicondyle removes sweating (and sensation) over the medial hand."]},"a63":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Finger abduction = dorsal interossei (+ abductor digiti minimi), all supplied by the ULNAR nerve.</p><p>Spreading the fingers depends on the four dorsal interossei (index, middle ×2, ring) and abductor digiti minimi for the little finger, all supplied by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve. A boxer's punch can injure the ulnar nerve at the wrist (Guyon's canal) or around the hook of the hamate, producing weak finger abduction and adduction. Only the thumb has abductors from other nerves (abductor pollicis brevis — median; abductor pollicis longus — radial).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Median nerve</b> — The median nerve abducts only the thumb (abductor pollicis brevis), not the fingers.</li><li><b>Radial nerve</b> — The radial nerve abducts the thumb through abductor pollicis longus, not the fingers.</li><li><b>Musculocutaneous nerve</b> — The musculocutaneous nerve supplies arm flexors and lateral forearm skin, nothing in the hand.</li><li><b>Axillary nerve</b> — The axillary nerve supplies deltoid and teres minor at the shoulder.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Test the ulnar nerve: \"spread your fingers against resistance\" (dorsal interossei) and the card test (palmar interossei).</p>","w":["Correct — the ulnar nerve supplies the dorsal interossei, which abduct the fingers.","The median nerve abducts only the thumb (abductor pollicis brevis), not the fingers.","The radial nerve abducts the thumb through abductor pollicis longus, not the fingers.","The musculocutaneous nerve supplies arm flexors and lateral forearm skin, nothing in the hand.","The axillary nerve supplies deltoid and teres minor at the shoulder."]},"a64":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Median nerve: sensation to the palmar lateral 3½ digits, pronators (teres and quadratus), and thenar muscles.</p><p>This patient has a median nerve lesion proximal to the forearm, because both the forearm (pronators) and hand (thenar) are affected. The median nerve supplies pronator teres in the cubital region and pronator quadratus via the anterior interosseous nerve; in the hand it supplies the thenar muscles through its recurrent branch and gives palmar digital nerves to the lateral 3½ digits (including the dorsal fingertips). The pattern of thenar flattening plus lateral-digit sensory change is the median nerve signature.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Axillary nerve</b> — The axillary nerve supplies deltoid, teres minor and lateral arm skin; it doesn't reach the hand.</li><li><b>Musculocutaneous nerve</b> — The musculocutaneous nerve supplies biceps, brachialis and lateral forearm skin, not the hand.</li><li><b>Radial nerve</b> — The radial nerve supplies extensors and the dorsum of the lateral hand; it causes no thenar wasting or pronation loss.</li><li><b>Ulnar nerve</b> — The ulnar nerve supplies the MEDIAL 1½ digits and the hypothenar muscles.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Pronation weakness + thenar wasting = median lesion ABOVE the wrist (elbow level); thenar wasting alone = carpal tunnel.</p>","w":["The axillary nerve supplies deltoid, teres minor and lateral arm skin; it doesn't reach the hand.","The musculocutaneous nerve supplies biceps, brachialis and lateral forearm skin, not the hand.","Correct — the median nerve supplies the lateral 3½ digits, the pronators and the thenar muscles.","The radial nerve supplies extensors and the dorsum of the lateral hand; it causes no thenar wasting or pronation loss.","The ulnar nerve supplies the MEDIAL 1½ digits and the hypothenar muscles."]},"a65":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Sweat glands are controlled by sympathetic POSTganglionic neurons whose cell bodies lie in the sympathetic chain (paravertebral) ganglia.</p><p>The sympathetic pathway to skin has two neurons. Preganglionic neurons in the lateral horn (T1–L2) send axons out through white rami to the sympathetic chain, where they synapse. Postganglionic neurons in the chain ganglia send axons back through gray rami into every spinal nerve, riding along cutaneous branches such as the lateral antebrachial cutaneous nerve to sweat glands, arrector pili and blood vessels. Cutting the cutaneous nerve severs these postganglionic axons, so the area stops sweating; the sensory loss (numbness) reflects injured DRG neurons.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Collateral ganglia</b> — Collateral (prevertebral) ganglia, such as the celiac ganglion, supply abdominal and pelvic viscera, not the skin of the limbs.</li><li><b>Dorsal root ganglia</b> — Dorsal root ganglia hold the sensory neurons responsible for the numbness, not the sweating.</li><li><b>Lateral horn of spinal cord</b> — The lateral horn holds PREganglionic sympathetic cell bodies; their axons end in the chain ganglia and never reach the forearm.</li><li><b>Anterior horn of spinal cord</b> — The anterior horn holds somatic motor neurons to skeletal muscle.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Skin sympathetics: lateral horn (pre) → chain ganglion (post) → gray ramus → spinal nerve → sweat gland.</p>","w":["Collateral (prevertebral) ganglia, such as the celiac ganglion, supply abdominal and pelvic viscera, not the skin of the limbs.","Dorsal root ganglia hold the sensory neurons responsible for the numbness, not the sweating.","Correct — the postganglionic sympathetic neurons controlling sweating have cell bodies in the sympathetic chain ganglia.","The lateral horn holds PREganglionic sympathetic cell bodies; their axons end in the chain ganglia and never reach the forearm.","The anterior horn holds somatic motor neurons to skeletal muscle."]},"a66":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Holding paper between the index and middle fingers needs the 1st palmar interosseous (adducts the index) and 2nd dorsal interosseous (abducts the middle finger toward it) — both ulnar.</p><p>A cut on the palmar wrist can sever the ulnar nerve where it runs superficially in Guyon's canal. The deep branch supplies all interossei, the medial two lumbricals, adductor pollicis and the hypothenar muscles. To grip a sheet between two fingers, the index finger is pulled toward the middle finger (palmar interosseous) and the middle finger is pushed toward the index (dorsal interosseous). Without them, the sheet slips out. This is the basis of the \"card test\" for ulnar nerve function.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Radial nerve</b> — The radial nerve supplies extensors in the forearm; it doesn't innervate any interossei.</li><li><b>Axillary nerve</b> — The axillary nerve ends at the shoulder (deltoid, teres minor).</li><li><b>Median nerve</b> — The median nerve supplies the thenar muscles and lateral two lumbricals, not the interossei.</li><li><b>Anterior interosseous nerve</b> — The anterior interosseous nerve supplies FPL, lateral FDP and pronator quadratus in the forearm only.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Card test = palmar interossei = ulnar nerve.</p>","w":["The radial nerve supplies extensors in the forearm; it doesn't innervate any interossei.","The axillary nerve ends at the shoulder (deltoid, teres minor).","Correct — the ulnar nerve supplies the interossei that adduct the index and abduct the middle finger.","The median nerve supplies the thenar muscles and lateral two lumbricals, not the interossei.","The anterior interosseous nerve supplies FPL, lateral FDP and pronator quadratus in the forearm only."]},"a67":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The ring finger MCP joint is flexed, abducted and adducted by ulnar-supplied muscles (interossei, medial lumbricals) and extended by radial-supplied extensor digitorum.</p><p>Muscles crossing the 4th MCP: extensor digitorum (radial nerve via posterior interosseous) extends it; the 3rd lumbrical (ulnar) and the 4th dorsal and 2nd palmar interossei (ulnar) flex it and move it sideways; FDS/FDP also flex it secondarily (median/ulnar), but the intrinsic movers of the joint itself are ulnar plus the radial extensor. That makes ulnar + radial the best pair.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Median and ulnar nerves</b> — The median nerve supplies only the lateral two lumbricals (index and middle fingers), not the ring finger's MCP movers; the extensor (radial) is missing.</li><li><b>Radial and median nerves</b> — This pair misses the ulnar-supplied interossei and lumbrical, which are the main flexors/side-movers of the ring MCP.</li><li><b>Musculocutaneous and ulnar nerves</b> — The musculocutaneous nerve has no muscles in the hand.</li><li><b>Radial and axillary nerves</b> — The axillary nerve has no muscles in the hand.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> For any finger MCP: extension = radial; intrinsic flexion/abduction/adduction = mostly ulnar (except lumbricals 1–2 = median).</p>","w":["The median nerve supplies only the lateral two lumbricals (index and middle fingers), not the ring finger's MCP movers; the extensor (radial) is missing.","This pair misses the ulnar-supplied interossei and lumbrical, which are the main flexors/side-movers of the ring MCP.","The musculocutaneous nerve has no muscles in the hand.","Correct — the ulnar nerve (interossei, medial lumbricals) and radial nerve (extensor digitorum) move the ring finger MCP.","The axillary nerve has no muscles in the hand."]},"a68":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Opposition = opponens pollicis = recurrent branch of the MEDIAN nerve; thumb adduction = adductor pollicis = ULNAR nerve.</p><p>A deep cut on the front of the wrist can sever the median nerve just before or as it enters the carpal tunnel. Its recurrent branch supplies abductor pollicis brevis, opponens pollicis and the superficial head of flexor pollicis brevis, so the thumb can't be rotated across the palm to meet the fingers. The ulnar nerve enters the hand superficial to the retinaculum, lateral to the pisiform, and supplies adductor pollicis, which is why the patient can still pull the thumb in against the index finger. The dissociation (adduction preserved, opposition lost) localizes the lesion to the median nerve.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Radial nerve</b> — The radial nerve supplies the long thumb abductor and extensors in the forearm, not opponens pollicis.</li><li><b>Ulnar nerve</b> — The ulnar nerve supplies adductor pollicis, which is still working in this patient.</li><li><b>Anterior interosseous nerve</b> — The anterior interosseous nerve supplies FPL, lateral FDP and pronator quadratus in the forearm — not opponens.</li><li><b>Posterior interosseous nerve</b> — The posterior interosseous nerve supplies the forearm extensors (and APL, EPL, EPB).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Median at the wrist: lose opposition and thumb abduction, keep adduction (ulnar).</p>","w":["The radial nerve supplies the long thumb abductor and extensors in the forearm, not opponens pollicis.","Correct — the median nerve supplies opponens pollicis; adductor pollicis (ulnar) still works.","The ulnar nerve supplies adductor pollicis, which is still working in this patient.","The anterior interosseous nerve supplies FPL, lateral FDP and pronator quadratus in the forearm — not opponens.","The posterior interosseous nerve supplies the forearm extensors (and APL, EPL, EPB)."]},"a69":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Ulnar nerve lesion: numb ring and little fingers, hypothenar wasting, weak finger abduction/adduction and a claw hand.</p><p>The ulnar nerve supplies the medial 1½ digits, the hypothenar muscles, all interossei, the medial two lumbricals, adductor pollicis and (in the forearm) FCU and the medial half of FDP. Losing the medial lumbricals and interossei removes MCP flexion and IP extension of the ring and little fingers, so the unopposed long extensors hyperextend the MCPs while FDP flexes the IPs — the claw hand. (The stem's \"inability to abduct\" is best read as the fingers; thumb abduction itself is median/radial.)</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Axillary nerve</b> — The axillary nerve supplies deltoid and teres minor; it doesn't reach the hand.</li><li><b>Anterior interosseous nerve</b> — The anterior interosseous nerve supplies deep forearm flexors only and is purely motor (no finger numbness).</li><li><b>Radial nerve</b> — Radial nerve injury causes wrist drop and dorsal-hand numbness, not hypothenar wasting or clawing.</li><li><b>Median nerve</b> — Median nerve injury causes thenar wasting (\"ape hand\") and lateral 3½-digit numbness.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Claw hand = ulnar (ring + little); \"hand of benediction\" on making a fist = proximal median; ape hand = median.</p>","w":["The axillary nerve supplies deltoid and teres minor; it doesn't reach the hand.","The anterior interosseous nerve supplies deep forearm flexors only and is purely motor (no finger numbness).","Radial nerve injury causes wrist drop and dorsal-hand numbness, not hypothenar wasting or clawing.","Correct — ulnar nerve injury gives medial-digit numbness, hypothenar wasting and claw hand.","Median nerve injury causes thenar wasting (\"ape hand\") and lateral 3½-digit numbness."]},"a70":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Medial arm skin = medial cutaneous nerve of the arm (medial brachial cutaneous, medial cord C8–T1), helped by the intercostobrachial nerve (T2).</p><p>Each region of the upper limb has its own cutaneous nerve. Arm: lateral = superior lateral cutaneous nerve of arm (axillary) and inferior lateral (radial); posterior = posterior cutaneous nerve of arm (radial); medial = medial cutaneous nerve of arm (medial cord) plus intercostobrachial. Forearm: lateral = lateral cutaneous nerve of forearm (musculocutaneous); medial = medial cutaneous nerve of forearm (medial cord); posterior = posterior cutaneous nerve of forearm (radial). Numbness of the medial arm after a cut therefore points to the medial brachial cutaneous nerve.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Axillary nerve</b> — The axillary nerve supplies the skin over the lower deltoid (lateral arm).</li><li><b>Musculocutaneous nerve</b> — The musculocutaneous nerve ends as the lateral cutaneous nerve of the FOREARM.</li><li><b>Medial antebrachial cutaneous nerve</b> — The medial antebrachial cutaneous nerve supplies the medial FOREARM, not the arm.</li><li><b>Radial nerve</b> — The radial nerve supplies the posterior arm and forearm and the dorsal lateral hand.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> \"Brachial\" = arm, \"antebrachial\" = forearm.</p>","w":["The axillary nerve supplies the skin over the lower deltoid (lateral arm).","The musculocutaneous nerve ends as the lateral cutaneous nerve of the FOREARM.","Correct — the medial brachial cutaneous nerve supplies the skin of the medial arm.","The medial antebrachial cutaneous nerve supplies the medial FOREARM, not the arm.","The radial nerve supplies the posterior arm and forearm and the dorsal lateral hand."]},"a71":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Midshaft humerus fracture → radial nerve in the radial groove → wrist drop.</p><p>The radial nerve and deep brachial artery spiral around the posterior humerus in direct contact with bone at the midshaft, making them vulnerable to fracture fragments. Distal to this point the nerve still has to supply brachioradialis, the radial wrist extensors and (via the posterior interosseous nerve) the finger and thumb extensors, so all extension at the wrist and MCP joints is lost and the hand drops. Sensation is lost over the dorsum of the lateral hand (first dorsal web space). Triceps branches usually arise above the groove, so elbow extension is typically preserved.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Weakness in pronating the forearm</b> — Pronation is done by pronator teres and quadratus (median nerve).</li><li><b>Sensory loss over the ventral aspect of the base of the thumb</b> — The ventral (palmar) skin at the base of the thumb is supplied by the palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve.</li><li><b>Inability to oppose the thumb</b> — Opposition is done by opponens pollicis (median nerve).</li><li><b>Inability to abduct the fingers</b> — Finger abduction is done by dorsal interossei (ulnar nerve).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Midshaft humerus = radial nerve; surgical neck = axillary nerve; supracondylar = median nerve; medial epicondyle = ulnar nerve.</p>","w":["Correct — radial nerve injury at the midshaft paralyzes the wrist extensors, causing wrist drop.","Pronation is done by pronator teres and quadratus (median nerve).","The ventral (palmar) skin at the base of the thumb is supplied by the palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve.","Opposition is done by opponens pollicis (median nerve).","Finger abduction is done by dorsal interossei (ulnar nerve)."]},"a72":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Median nerve injury → \"ape hand\": thenar wasting with the thumb lying flat, adducted and extended in line with the fingers (no opposition or abduction).</p><p>The thenar muscles hold the thumb in a slightly abducted and opposed position at rest. When the median nerve (or its recurrent branch) is lost, they waste, and the unopposed extensors (radial) and adductor pollicis (ulnar) pull the thumb back into the plane of the palm, like an ape's hand. A median injury in the arm also weakens pronation and wrist and finger flexion of the lateral fingers, giving the \"hand of benediction\" when the patient tries to make a fist.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Waiter’s tip hand</b> — Waiter's tip hand is an upper trunk (C5–C6, Erb–Duchenne) lesion.</li><li><b>Claw hand</b> — Claw hand (ring and little fingers) is an ulnar nerve lesion.</li><li><b>Wrist drop</b> — Wrist drop is a radial nerve lesion.</li><li><b>Flattening of the hypothenar eminence</b> — Flattening of the HYPOthenar eminence is an ulnar nerve lesion; median injury flattens the THENAR eminence.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Ape hand = median; claw hand = ulnar; wrist drop = radial; waiter's tip = upper trunk.</p>","w":["Waiter's tip hand is an upper trunk (C5–C6, Erb–Duchenne) lesion.","Claw hand (ring and little fingers) is an ulnar nerve lesion.","Wrist drop is a radial nerve lesion.","Correct — median nerve damage produces the ape hand (wasted thenar eminence, thumb flat beside the fingers).","Flattening of the HYPOthenar eminence is an ulnar nerve lesion; median injury flattens the THENAR eminence."]},"a73":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Two nerves lie directly on the upper half of the humerus: the axillary nerve (surgical neck) and the radial nerve (radial groove).</p><p>Going down the humerus, nerves in contact with bone are: axillary nerve around the surgical neck, radial nerve in the radial groove at the midshaft, median nerve near the supracondylar region (with the brachial artery) and ulnar nerve behind the medial epicondyle. An \"upper portion\" fracture therefore threatens the axillary and radial nerves. The musculocutaneous nerve lies within coracobrachialis and between biceps and brachialis, never on the bone.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Axillary and musculocutaneous</b> — The axillary nerve fits, but the musculocutaneous nerve doesn't contact the humerus.</li><li><b>Radial and ulnar</b> — The radial nerve fits, but the ulnar nerve relates to the medial epicondyle at the LOWER end.</li><li><b>Median and musculocutaneous</b> — Neither the median nor the musculocutaneous nerve lies on the upper humerus.</li><li><b>Median and ulnar</b> — Both the median (supracondylar) and ulnar (medial epicondyle) nerves relate to the lower humerus.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Humerus top to bottom: axillary → radial → median → ulnar.</p>","w":["The axillary nerve fits, but the musculocutaneous nerve doesn't contact the humerus.","The radial nerve fits, but the ulnar nerve relates to the medial epicondyle at the LOWER end.","Correct — the axillary (surgical neck) and radial (radial groove) nerves lie against the upper half of the humerus.","Neither the median nor the musculocutaneous nerve lies on the upper humerus.","Both the median (supracondylar) and ulnar (medial epicondyle) nerves relate to the lower humerus."]},"a74":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Moving the ring finger MCP joint requires ulnar-supplied intrinsics (flexion, abduction, adduction) and the radial-supplied extensor digitorum (extension).</p><p>A deep cut to the ring finger can sever its extensor tendon and the tendons and nerves serving its intrinsic muscles. The MCP joint is flexed by the 3rd lumbrical and the interossei (4th dorsal, 2nd palmar), all supplied by the ulnar nerve, and extended by extensor digitorum (posterior interosseous branch of the radial nerve). To lose all MCP movement, both supplies must be involved, so the pair is ulnar and radial. Same concept as the carpenter question.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Median and ulnar</b> — The median nerve's lumbricals act on the index and middle fingers; this pair also misses the radial extensor.</li><li><b>Radial and median</b> — The median nerve doesn't act on the ring finger MCP intrinsics, and this pair misses the ulnar nerve.</li><li><b>Musculocutaneous and ulnar</b> — The musculocutaneous nerve has no muscles in the hand.</li><li><b>Radial and axillary</b> — The axillary nerve has no muscles in the hand.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Ring finger MCP: flex/abduct/adduct = ulnar; extend = radial.</p>","w":["The median nerve's lumbricals act on the index and middle fingers; this pair also misses the radial extensor.","The median nerve doesn't act on the ring finger MCP intrinsics, and this pair misses the ulnar nerve.","The musculocutaneous nerve has no muscles in the hand.","Correct — ulnar (interossei, 3rd lumbrical) and radial (extensor digitorum) nerves move the ring finger MCP.","The axillary nerve has no muscles in the hand."]},"a75":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Supination = supinator (radial nerve) + biceps brachii (musculocutaneous nerve).</p><p>Supinator wraps around the upper radius from the lateral epicondyle and ulna and supinates in any elbow position; the posterior interosseous branch of the radial nerve pierces it. Biceps brachii is the more powerful supinator, especially with the elbow flexed, and is supplied by the musculocutaneous nerve. Losing all supination therefore requires damage to both the radial and musculocutaneous nerves.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Suprascapular and axillary</b> — The suprascapular and axillary nerves supply shoulder muscles (supraspinatus, infraspinatus, deltoid, teres minor).</li><li><b>Musculocutaneous and median</b> — The musculocutaneous nerve fits, but the median nerve supplies the PRONATORS.</li><li><b>Axillary and radial</b> — The radial nerve fits, but the axillary nerve doesn't supply any supinator.</li><li><b>Median and ulnar</b> — The median and ulnar nerves supply forearm flexors and hand muscles, not supinators.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Biceps supinates best with the elbow flexed; supinator works in all positions.</p>","w":["The suprascapular and axillary nerves supply shoulder muscles (supraspinatus, infraspinatus, deltoid, teres minor).","The musculocutaneous nerve fits, but the median nerve supplies the PRONATORS.","The radial nerve fits, but the axillary nerve doesn't supply any supinator.","Correct — supinator (radial nerve) and biceps brachii (musculocutaneous nerve) produce supination.","The median and ulnar nerves supply forearm flexors and hand muscles, not supinators."]},"a76":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The ulnar nerve (\"funny bone\" nerve behind the medial epicondyle) supplies the medial third of the hand and the medial 1½ fingers, front and back.</p><p>The ulnar nerve is subcutaneous behind the medial epicondyle, so hitting your \"funny bone\" sends tingling into its territory. It gives a palmar cutaneous branch and a dorsal cutaneous branch in the forearm, and superficial digital branches in the hand, together covering the palmar and dorsal surfaces of the little finger, half of the ring finger and the medial part of the hand. The median nerve covers the lateral palm and lateral 3½ digits (palmar side), and the radial nerve covers the dorsum of the lateral hand.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Axillary</b> — The axillary nerve supplies the skin over the lower deltoid.</li><li><b>Radial</b> — The radial nerve supplies the posterior arm and forearm and the dorsum of the lateral hand.</li><li><b>Median</b> — The median nerve supplies the lateral palm and the palmar side of the lateral 3½ fingers.</li><li><b>Musculocutaneous</b> — The musculocutaneous nerve supplies the lateral forearm.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Medial 1½ digits front and back = ulnar; \"funny bone\" = ulnar nerve at the medial epicondyle.</p>","w":["The axillary nerve supplies the skin over the lower deltoid.","The radial nerve supplies the posterior arm and forearm and the dorsum of the lateral hand.","The median nerve supplies the lateral palm and the palmar side of the lateral 3½ fingers.","Correct — the ulnar nerve supplies the skin of the medial hand and medial 1½ fingers.","The musculocutaneous nerve supplies the lateral forearm."]},"a77":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> A supracondylar fracture injures the median nerve, which supplies FDP to the index/middle fingers and the skin of the lateral 3½ digits including the distal index finger.</p><p>The median nerve passes in front of the elbow with the brachial artery and is vulnerable in supracondylar fractures (common in children). A proximal lesion takes out the anterior interosseous branch (FPL, lateral FDP, pronator quadratus) and the median-supplied FDS, so the index finger can't flex at the DIP joint. The palmar digital branches of the median nerve also wrap around to supply the dorsal skin of the distal phalanges of the lateral 3½ digits, so sensation over the distal second digit is lost too.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Inability to flex the DIP joint of the ring finger</b> — The DIP of the ring finger is flexed by the medial half of FDP, supplied by the ulnar nerve.</li><li><b>Atrophy of the hypothenar eminence</b> — The hypothenar muscles are supplied by the ulnar nerve.</li><li><b>Paralysis of all the thumb muscles</b> — Not all thumb muscles are median: adductor pollicis and the deep head of FPB are ulnar, and APL/EPL/EPB are radial.</li><li><b>Loss of supination</b> — Supination is done by supinator (radial) and biceps (musculocutaneous).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Supracondylar fracture: median nerve + brachial artery (watch for Volkmann ischemic contracture).</p>","w":["The DIP of the ring finger is flexed by the medial half of FDP, supplied by the ulnar nerve.","The hypothenar muscles are supplied by the ulnar nerve.","Correct — the median nerve supplies sensation over the distal index finger (second digit).","Not all thumb muscles are median: adductor pollicis and the deep head of FPB are ulnar, and APL/EPL/EPB are radial.","Supination is done by supinator (radial) and biceps (musculocutaneous)."]},"a78":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cubital tunnel syndrome = ulnar nerve compression at the elbow → weakness of ulnar muscles including the two MEDIAL lumbricals.</p><p>The cubital tunnel is formed behind the medial epicondyle by the two heads of FCU and the arcuate ligament. Compression of the ulnar nerve here (leaning on elbows, prolonged flexion) causes numbness of the ring and little fingers and medial hand (front and back, because the dorsal cutaneous branch arises above the wrist), plus weakness of FCU, medial FDP and the intrinsic hand muscles. The medial two lumbricals (to the ring and little fingers) are ulnar, so they're paralyzed, contributing to clawing.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Flexor digitorum superficialis</b> — FDS is supplied entirely by the median nerve.</li><li><b>Opponens pollicis</b> — Opponens pollicis is supplied by the recurrent branch of the median nerve.</li><li><b>Pronator teres</b> — Pronator teres is supplied by the median nerve.</li><li><b>Supinator</b> — Supinator is supplied by the radial nerve (posterior interosseous branch).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Dorsal-hand numbness distinguishes elbow (cubital tunnel) from wrist (Guyon's canal) ulnar lesions — the dorsal branch leaves above the wrist.</p>","w":["FDS is supplied entirely by the median nerve.","Opponens pollicis is supplied by the recurrent branch of the median nerve.","Correct — the ulnar nerve supplies the two medial lumbricals, which are paralyzed in cubital tunnel syndrome.","Pronator teres is supplied by the median nerve.","Supinator is supplied by the radial nerve (posterior interosseous branch)."]},"a79":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Holding paper between the index and middle fingers = palmar interosseous (index adduction) + dorsal interosseous (middle finger abduction), both ulnar.</p><p>Squeezing a sheet between two adjacent fingers requires the fingers to move toward each other. For the index–middle pair, the first palmar interosseous adducts the index finger toward the middle finger, and the second dorsal interosseous abducts the middle finger toward the index. Both are supplied by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve, so an ulnar lesion makes the paper slip (a positive card test).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Radial nerve</b> — The radial nerve supplies extensors; no interossei.</li><li><b>Median nerve</b> — The median nerve supplies thenar muscles and lateral lumbricals; no interossei.</li><li><b>Musculocutaneous nerve</b> — The musculocutaneous nerve has no hand muscles.</li><li><b>Axillary nerve</b> — The axillary nerve has no hand muscles.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> All interossei = ulnar nerve. Card test = palmar interossei.</p>","w":["The radial nerve supplies extensors; no interossei.","The median nerve supplies thenar muscles and lateral lumbricals; no interossei.","Correct — the ulnar nerve supplies the palmar and dorsal interossei that grip the paper.","The musculocutaneous nerve has no hand muscles.","The axillary nerve has no hand muscles."]},"a80":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The musculocutaneous nerve ends as the lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm, which carries sensory and sympathetic (sweat) fibers to the lateral forearm.</p><p>After supplying coracobrachialis, biceps and brachialis, the musculocutaneous nerve emerges lateral to the biceps tendon as the lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm. Like every cutaneous nerve it carries postganglionic sympathetic fibers to sweat glands and vessels of its territory, so cutting it causes absent sweating (and numbness) over the lateral forearm. Motor loss would weaken elbow flexion and supination (biceps, brachialis), not extension.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Inability to extend the forearm</b> — Forearm (elbow) extension is done by triceps and anconeus (radial nerve).</li><li><b>Paralysis of brachioradialis muscle</b> — Brachioradialis is supplied by the radial nerve despite flexing the elbow.</li><li><b>Loss of tactile sensation on the arm</b> — The musculocutaneous nerve supplies FOREARM skin; the arm is supplied by the axillary, radial and medial brachial cutaneous nerves.</li><li><b>Constriction of blood vessels on the hand</b> — Its sympathetic fibers go to vessels of the lateral forearm, not the hand; and loss of sympathetic tone would cause dilation, not constriction.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Every cutaneous nerve carries sympathetic postganglionic fibers — loss of a cutaneous nerve = numbness + anhidrosis in that patch.</p>","w":["Correct — the lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm carries sympathetic fibers to sweat glands of the lateral forearm.","Forearm (elbow) extension is done by triceps and anconeus (radial nerve).","Brachioradialis is supplied by the radial nerve despite flexing the elbow.","The musculocutaneous nerve supplies FOREARM skin; the arm is supplied by the axillary, radial and medial brachial cutaneous nerves.","Its sympathetic fibers go to vessels of the lateral forearm, not the hand; and loss of sympathetic tone would cause dilation, not constriction."]},"a81":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Peripheral nerves of the limb carry somatic motor, somatic sensory, visceral sensory and POSTganglionic sympathetic fibers — never preganglionic sympathetic fibers.</p><p>Preganglionic sympathetic axons leave the spinal cord (T1–L2) and end in the sympathetic chain or prevertebral ganglia; they reach the chain through white rami and stop there. What travels out into a limb nerve such as the axillary nerve is the postganglionic axon, which rejoins the spinal nerve through a gray ramus. So a severed axillary nerve loses GSE fibers (deltoid, teres minor), GSA fibers (lateral arm numbness), GVA fibers from vessels, and postganglionic GVE fibers (sweating, vasomotor), but it had no preganglionic fibers to lose.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Postganglionic sympathetic axons</b> — The axillary nerve does carry postganglionic sympathetic axons to sweat glands and vessels of the lateral arm.</li><li><b>Somatic afferent axons</b> — It carries somatic afferent (GSA) axons from the skin over the deltoid — their loss causes the numbness.</li><li><b>General somatic efferent axons</b> — It carries general somatic efferent (GSE) axons to deltoid and teres minor.</li><li><b>General visceral afferent axons</b> — It carries general visceral afferent (GVA) axons from blood vessels in its territory.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> White rami = preganglionic (T1–L2 only); gray rami = postganglionic (every spinal nerve).</p>","w":["The axillary nerve does carry postganglionic sympathetic axons to sweat glands and vessels of the lateral arm.","It carries somatic afferent (GSA) axons from the skin over the deltoid — their loss causes the numbness.","Correct — limb nerves contain no preganglionic sympathetic axons, so none are lost.","It carries general somatic efferent (GSE) axons to deltoid and teres minor.","It carries general visceral afferent (GVA) axons from blood vessels in its territory."]},"a82":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Losing supination means losing both supinators: supinator (radial nerve) and biceps brachii (musculocutaneous nerve).</p><p>Supinator winds around the upper radius and is pierced by the deep (posterior interosseous) branch of the radial nerve near the elbow, which is why elbow injuries can damage it. Biceps brachii, the stronger supinator when the elbow is flexed, is supplied by the musculocutaneous nerve. Complete inability to supinate after an elbow injury therefore implies both nerves are involved.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Median and ulnar nerves</b> — The median and ulnar nerves supply the pronators and forearm/hand flexors, not supinators.</li><li><b>Axillary and radial nerves</b> — The radial nerve fits, but the axillary nerve supplies deltoid and teres minor at the shoulder.</li><li><b>Ulnar and axillary nerves</b> — Neither the ulnar nor axillary nerve supplies a supinator.</li><li><b>Musculocutaneous and median nerves</b> — The musculocutaneous nerve fits, but the median nerve supplies the pronators (the opposite action).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Supinators: supinator (radial) + biceps (musculocutaneous). Pronators: pronator teres + pronator quadratus (median).</p>","w":["The median and ulnar nerves supply the pronators and forearm/hand flexors, not supinators.","The radial nerve fits, but the axillary nerve supplies deltoid and teres minor at the shoulder.","Correct — the radial nerve (supinator) and musculocutaneous nerve (biceps) are needed for supination.","Neither the ulnar nor axillary nerve supplies a supinator.","The musculocutaneous nerve fits, but the median nerve supplies the pronators (the opposite action)."]},"a83":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The ulnar nerve runs in the groove behind the medial epicondyle and is the nerve injured by medial epicondyle fractures.</p><p>In the arm the ulnar nerve descends medial to the brachial artery, pierces the medial intermuscular septum, and passes behind the medial epicondyle in the ulnar groove, covered only by skin and fascia. It then enters the forearm between the two heads of FCU (cubital tunnel). Because it lies directly on bone here, a fracture of the medial epicondyle (or elbow dislocation) commonly damages it, causing numbness of the medial 1½ fingers and weakness of the intrinsic hand muscles.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Axillary</b> — The axillary nerve is related to the surgical neck of the humerus.</li><li><b>Musculocutaneous</b> — The musculocutaneous nerve runs between biceps and brachialis, away from bone.</li><li><b>Radial</b> — The radial nerve is related to the radial groove at the midshaft and the lateral elbow (in front of the lateral epicondyle).</li><li><b>Median</b> — The median nerve is at risk in supracondylar fractures, in front of the elbow, not at the medial epicondyle.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Medial epicondyle = ulnar nerve (\"funny bone\").</p>","w":["The axillary nerve is related to the surgical neck of the humerus.","The musculocutaneous nerve runs between biceps and brachialis, away from bone.","The radial nerve is related to the radial groove at the midshaft and the lateral elbow (in front of the lateral epicondyle).","The median nerve is at risk in supracondylar fractures, in front of the elbow, not at the medial epicondyle.","Correct — the ulnar nerve passes behind the medial epicondyle and is damaged by its fracture."]},"a84":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Adductor pollicis is supplied by the deep branch of the ULNAR nerve; the other thumb muscles listed are median or radial.</p><p>Of the muscles moving the thumb, only adductor pollicis (and the deep head of flexor pollicis brevis) is ulnar. It arises from the capitate and the 2nd/3rd metacarpals and inserts on the medial base of the proximal phalanx of the thumb, pulling the thumb toward the palm. A medial epicondyle fracture damaging the ulnar nerve paralyzes it, which can be tested with Froment's sign: the patient holding paper between thumb and index flexes the thumb IP joint (using FPL, median) to compensate.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Extensor pollicis brevis</b> — Extensor pollicis brevis is supplied by the posterior interosseous branch of the radial nerve.</li><li><b>Abductor pollicis longus</b> — Abductor pollicis longus is supplied by the posterior interosseous branch of the radial nerve.</li><li><b>Abductor pollicis brevis</b> — Abductor pollicis brevis is supplied by the recurrent branch of the median nerve.</li><li><b>Opponens pollicis</b> — Opponens pollicis is supplied by the recurrent branch of the median nerve.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Froment's sign = weak adductor pollicis = ulnar nerve.</p>","w":["Extensor pollicis brevis is supplied by the posterior interosseous branch of the radial nerve.","Abductor pollicis longus is supplied by the posterior interosseous branch of the radial nerve.","Abductor pollicis brevis is supplied by the recurrent branch of the median nerve.","Correct — adductor pollicis is supplied by the ulnar nerve, which is damaged here.","Opponens pollicis is supplied by the recurrent branch of the median nerve."]},"a85":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Finger adduction is done by the palmar interossei, supplied by the ulnar nerve.</p><p>With the ulnar nerve damaged at the medial epicondyle, all interossei are paralyzed, so the fingers can't be adducted (palmar) or abducted (dorsal). The first palmar interosseous adducts the index finger toward the middle finger. Other functions in the choices are median- or radial-dependent and remain intact: FDS (median) still flexes the PIPs, the median half of FDP still flexes the index DIP, the median nerve still supplies middle-finger skin, and the median and radial nerves still abduct the thumb.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Flex the proximal interphalangeal joint of his ring finger</b> — PIP flexion of the ring finger is done by FDS, supplied by the median nerve.</li><li><b>Flex the DIP joint of his index finger</b> — DIP flexion of the index finger is done by the lateral half of FDP (anterior interosseous, median nerve).</li><li><b>Feel sensation on his middle finger</b> — Skin of the middle finger is supplied by the median nerve.</li><li><b>Abduct his thumb</b> — Thumb abduction is done by abductor pollicis brevis (median) and longus (radial).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Ulnar lesion at the elbow: interossei, medial lumbricals, hypothenar, adductor pollicis, FCU and medial FDP weak.</p>","w":["PIP flexion of the ring finger is done by FDS, supplied by the median nerve.","DIP flexion of the index finger is done by the lateral half of FDP (anterior interosseous, median nerve).","Skin of the middle finger is supplied by the median nerve.","Thumb abduction is done by abductor pollicis brevis (median) and longus (radial).","Correct — the palmar interossei (ulnar nerve) adduct the index finger, so this is lost."]},"a86":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral artery wrap around the surgical neck of the humerus.</p><p>The surgical neck is the most common fracture site of the proximal humerus. The axillary nerve (posterior cord, C5–C6) leaves the axilla through the quadrangular space with the posterior circumflex humeral artery and curls around the back of the surgical neck before entering deltoid. Fracture here can tear the artery (the bleeding seen on angiogram) and injure the nerve, weakening shoulder abduction and lateral rotation and numbing the skin over the lower deltoid.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Musculocutaneous</b> — The musculocutaneous nerve runs through coracobrachialis and between biceps and brachialis, not on the surgical neck.</li><li><b>Radial</b> — The radial nerve contacts the humerus at the radial groove on the midshaft.</li><li><b>Median</b> — The median nerve isn't in contact with the bone except near the supracondylar region.</li><li><b>Ulnar</b> — The ulnar nerve contacts the bone behind the medial epicondyle.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Surgical neck fracture = axillary nerve + posterior circumflex humeral artery.</p>","w":["The musculocutaneous nerve runs through coracobrachialis and between biceps and brachialis, not on the surgical neck.","Correct — the axillary nerve wraps around the surgical neck and is injured by its fracture.","The radial nerve contacts the humerus at the radial groove on the midshaft.","The median nerve isn't in contact with the bone except near the supracondylar region.","The ulnar nerve contacts the bone behind the medial epicondyle."]},"a87":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Motor (GSE) neurons that move the arm have their cell bodies in the anterior (ventral) horn of the spinal cord.</p><p>The axillary nerve's motor fibers to deltoid and teres minor come from lower motor neurons in the anterior horn of C5–C6. Their axons leave through the ventral roots, join the spinal nerves and travel through the upper trunk, posterior divisions and posterior cord into the axillary nerve. When the nerve is cut, these axons degenerate distally and the muscles become flaccid and atrophic (lower motor neuron signs).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Dorsal root ganglion</b> — The dorsal root ganglion holds the cell bodies of sensory neurons (numbness), not motor neurons.</li><li><b>Sympathetic chain ganglion</b> — Sympathetic chain ganglia hold postganglionic sympathetic neurons (sweating, vasomotor).</li><li><b>Lateral horn of the spinal cord</b> — The lateral horn (T1–L2) holds preganglionic sympathetic neurons.</li><li><b>Posterior horn of the spinal cord</b> — The posterior horn holds sensory relay neurons and interneurons.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> GSE = anterior horn; GSA/GVA = DRG; preganglionic sympathetic = lateral horn; postganglionic = chain ganglia.</p>","w":["The dorsal root ganglion holds the cell bodies of sensory neurons (numbness), not motor neurons.","Sympathetic chain ganglia hold postganglionic sympathetic neurons (sweating, vasomotor).","Correct — the motor neurons supplying deltoid and teres minor live in the anterior horn of the spinal cord.","The lateral horn (T1–L2) holds preganglionic sympathetic neurons.","The posterior horn holds sensory relay neurons and interneurons."]},"a88":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The sensory (GSA) fibers that produce numbness have their cell bodies in the dorsal root ganglia.</p><p>The axillary nerve's cutaneous branch (superior lateral cutaneous nerve of the arm) supplies the \"regimental badge\" area over the lower deltoid. Its sensory neurons are pseudounipolar, with cell bodies in the C5–C6 dorsal root ganglia; the peripheral process runs to the skin, the central process enters the dorsal horn. Injury to the axillary nerve cuts the peripheral process, producing numbness over the lateral arm.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Anterior horn of the spinal cord</b> — The anterior horn holds motor neurons (to deltoid and teres minor), not sensory ones.</li><li><b>Posterior horn of the spinal cord</b> — The posterior horn holds second-order neurons that receive the sensory input, not the primary cell bodies.</li><li><b>Lateral horn of the spinal cord</b> — The lateral horn holds preganglionic sympathetic neurons.</li><li><b>Sympathetic chain ganglia</b> — Sympathetic chain ganglia hold postganglionic sympathetic neurons (sweating).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Numbness → DRG; paralysis → anterior horn; anhidrosis → sympathetic chain ganglia.</p>","w":["The anterior horn holds motor neurons (to deltoid and teres minor), not sensory ones.","The posterior horn holds second-order neurons that receive the sensory input, not the primary cell bodies.","The lateral horn holds preganglionic sympathetic neurons.","Correct — the primary sensory neurons of the axillary nerve have their cell bodies in the dorsal root ganglia.","Sympathetic chain ganglia hold postganglionic sympathetic neurons (sweating)."]},"a89":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The posterior circumflex humeral artery runs with the axillary nerve around the surgical neck.</p><p>The posterior circumflex humeral artery arises from the third part of the axillary artery, passes through the quadrangular space with the axillary nerve, and encircles the surgical neck of the humerus, anastomosing with the anterior circumflex humeral artery. Its intimate contact with the bone makes it the vessel torn by surgical-neck fractures, producing the bleeding seen on the angiogram. It also anastomoses with the ascending branch of the profunda brachii.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Axillary</b> — The axillary artery itself lies medial to the humerus in the axilla, proximal to the fracture.</li><li><b>Deep brachial</b> — The deep brachial (profunda brachii) artery runs with the radial nerve in the radial groove at the midshaft.</li><li><b>Superior ulnar collateral</b> — The superior ulnar collateral artery runs with the ulnar nerve toward the medial epicondyle.</li><li><b>Scapular circumflex</b> — The circumflex scapular artery passes through the triangular space to the back of the scapula.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Surgical neck: axillary nerve + posterior circumflex humeral artery. Radial groove: radial nerve + profunda brachii.</p>","w":["The axillary artery itself lies medial to the humerus in the axilla, proximal to the fracture.","The deep brachial (profunda brachii) artery runs with the radial nerve in the radial groove at the midshaft.","Correct — the posterior circumflex humeral artery wraps the surgical neck and is torn by its fracture.","The superior ulnar collateral artery runs with the ulnar nerve toward the medial epicondyle.","The circumflex scapular artery passes through the triangular space to the back of the scapula."]},"a90":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The axillary nerve supplies teres minor and deltoid — both lateral rotators (deltoid's posterior fibers) — so its injury weakens lateral rotation.</p><p>Lateral rotation of the arm is produced by infraspinatus (the strongest, suprascapular nerve), teres minor (axillary nerve) and the posterior fibers of deltoid (axillary nerve). A surgical-neck fracture injures the axillary nerve, paralyzing teres minor and deltoid; infraspinatus still works, so lateral rotation is weak rather than absent. Deltoid loss also weakens abduction (15°–90°) and flexion/extension of the shoulder.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Teres major and teres minor</b> — Teres minor fits, but teres major is a MEDIAL rotator supplied by the lower subscapular nerve.</li><li><b>Infraspinatus and deltoid</b> — Deltoid fits, but infraspinatus is supplied by the suprascapular nerve, not the injured axillary nerve.</li><li><b>Supraspinatus and subscapularis</b> — Supraspinatus (suprascapular nerve) abducts, and subscapularis (subscapular nerves) medially rotates; neither is axillary.</li><li><b>Teres minor and infraspinatus</b> — Teres minor fits, but infraspinatus is suprascapular.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Axillary nerve = deltoid + teres minor (the D and minor \"T\").</p>","w":["Teres minor fits, but teres major is a MEDIAL rotator supplied by the lower subscapular nerve.","Correct — teres minor and deltoid are supplied by the axillary nerve and are paralyzed after this fracture.","Deltoid fits, but infraspinatus is supplied by the suprascapular nerve, not the injured axillary nerve.","Supraspinatus (suprascapular nerve) abducts, and subscapularis (subscapular nerves) medially rotates; neither is axillary.","Teres minor fits, but infraspinatus is suprascapular."]},"a91":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Fracture of the humeral shaft (midshaft) damages the radial nerve in the radial (spiral) groove.</p><p>The radial nerve, with the profunda brachii artery, lies directly on the posterior surface of the humerus in the radial groove between the origins of the lateral and medial heads of triceps. Shaft fractures commonly injure it, producing wrist drop and loss of sensation on the dorsum of the lateral hand, usually with triceps spared because its branches arise higher.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Axillary nerve</b> — The axillary nerve is related to the surgical neck, not the shaft.</li><li><b>Musculocutaneous nerve</b> — The musculocutaneous nerve lies within the anterior muscles, not on the bone.</li><li><b>Median nerve</b> — The median nerve lies anterior to the brachialis and is at risk with supracondylar (distal) fractures.</li><li><b>Ulnar nerve</b> — The ulnar nerve lies against bone only at the medial epicondyle.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Humeral shaft fracture → radial nerve → wrist drop.</p>","w":["The axillary nerve is related to the surgical neck, not the shaft.","Correct — the radial nerve lies in the radial groove of the humeral shaft and is damaged by its fracture.","The musculocutaneous nerve lies within the anterior muscles, not on the bone.","The median nerve lies anterior to the brachialis and is at risk with supracondylar (distal) fractures.","The ulnar nerve lies against bone only at the medial epicondyle."]},"a92":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Sweating is controlled by sympathetic postganglionic neurons in the sympathetic chain ganglia; their axons travel in the radial nerve to the back of the arm and forearm.</p><p>The radial nerve's posterior cutaneous branches (posterior cutaneous nerves of the arm and forearm) supply the skin of the back of the limb. Along with sensory fibers, they carry postganglionic sympathetic axons that arise from the cervicothoracic (stellate) and upper thoracic chain ganglia and join the plexus through gray rami. Cutting the radial nerve in the radial groove therefore causes anhidrosis in that territory.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Anterior horn of the spinal cord</b> — The anterior horn holds somatic motor neurons.</li><li><b>Posterior horn of the spinal cord</b> — The posterior horn holds sensory relay neurons.</li><li><b>Lateral horn of the spinal cord</b> — The lateral horn holds PREganglionic sympathetic neurons, whose axons stop in the chain ganglia.</li><li><b>Dorsal root ganglion</b> — The dorsal root ganglion holds sensory neurons (numbness), not those controlling sweat glands.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Anhidrosis after a nerve injury = cut postganglionic sympathetic axons from the chain ganglia.</p>","w":["The anterior horn holds somatic motor neurons.","The posterior horn holds sensory relay neurons.","The lateral horn holds PREganglionic sympathetic neurons, whose axons stop in the chain ganglia.","Correct — the postganglionic sympathetic neurons controlling sweating are in the sympathetic chain ganglia.","The dorsal root ganglion holds sensory neurons (numbness), not those controlling sweat glands."]},"a93":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The superficial branch of the radial nerve supplies the dorsum of the lateral hand, including the skin over the anatomic snuffbox and first dorsal web space.</p><p>At the elbow the radial nerve divides into the deep branch (posterior interosseous, motor) and the superficial branch (sensory). The superficial branch runs under brachioradialis, emerges in the distal forearm and crosses the roof of the anatomic snuffbox to supply the radial side of the dorsum of the hand and the dorsal skin of the lateral 2½ digits up to the distal phalanges. A radial nerve lesion in the radial groove therefore numbs this area — the purest radial sensory test is the first dorsal web space.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Medial aspect of the arm</b> — The medial arm is supplied by the medial brachial cutaneous and intercostobrachial nerves.</li><li><b>Lateral aspect of the forearm</b> — The lateral forearm is supplied by the lateral antebrachial cutaneous nerve (musculocutaneous).</li><li><b>Palmar aspect of the second and third digits</b> — The palmar surface of the 2nd and 3rd digits is supplied by the median nerve.</li><li><b>Medial one and one-half fingers</b> — The medial 1½ fingers are supplied by the ulnar nerve.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Radial sensory test = first dorsal web space / snuffbox skin.</p>","w":["The medial arm is supplied by the medial brachial cutaneous and intercostobrachial nerves.","The lateral forearm is supplied by the lateral antebrachial cutaneous nerve (musculocutaneous).","The palmar surface of the 2nd and 3rd digits is supplied by the median nerve.","Correct — the superficial radial nerve supplies the skin over the anatomic snuffbox, so it becomes numb.","The medial 1½ fingers are supplied by the ulnar nerve."]},"a94":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The profunda brachii (deep brachial) artery accompanies the radial nerve in the radial groove of the humeral shaft.</p><p>The profunda brachii arises from the brachial artery just below teres major and travels with the radial nerve through the triangular interval and around the back of the humerus in the radial groove. It supplies triceps and divides into middle and radial collateral arteries that join the elbow anastomosis. Its position on the bone makes it vulnerable in shaft fractures alongside the radial nerve.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Brachial artery</b> — The brachial artery runs on the medial side of the arm, separated from the bone by brachialis.</li><li><b>Posterior humeral circumflex artery</b> — The posterior humeral circumflex artery wraps the surgical neck, not the shaft.</li><li><b>Radial artery</b> — The radial artery begins in the cubital fossa and runs in the forearm.</li><li><b>Radial recurrent artery</b> — The radial recurrent artery arises in the cubital fossa and ascends to the lateral elbow anastomosis.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Radial groove = radial nerve + profunda brachii artery.</p>","w":["The brachial artery runs on the medial side of the arm, separated from the bone by brachialis.","The posterior humeral circumflex artery wraps the surgical neck, not the shaft.","Correct — the profunda brachii artery runs in the radial groove with the radial nerve.","The radial artery begins in the cubital fossa and runs in the forearm.","The radial recurrent artery arises in the cubital fossa and ascends to the lateral elbow anastomosis."]},"a95":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> With the radial nerve cut (supinator paralyzed), biceps brachii (musculocutaneous nerve) can still supinate.</p><p>Supination is produced by two muscles: supinator (radial nerve) and biceps brachii (musculocutaneous). After a midshaft humerus fracture damages the radial nerve, supinator is paralyzed, but biceps continues to supinate — strongly when the elbow is flexed. Brachioradialis, also radial, is paralyzed; it isn't a true supinator anyway (it brings the forearm to mid-position from either extreme).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Supinator</b> — Supinator is supplied by the radial nerve, which is damaged.</li><li><b>Pronator teres</b> — Pronator teres pronates the forearm (median nerve).</li><li><b>Brachioradialis</b> — Brachioradialis is radial-supplied (paralyzed) and mainly an elbow flexor.</li><li><b>Supraspinatus</b> — Supraspinatus abducts the arm at the shoulder.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Radial nerve palsy: supination still possible via biceps.</p>","w":["Supinator is supplied by the radial nerve, which is damaged.","Pronator teres pronates the forearm (median nerve).","Brachioradialis is radial-supplied (paralyzed) and mainly an elbow flexor.","Correct — biceps brachii (musculocutaneous nerve) still supinates when the radial nerve is damaged.","Supraspinatus abducts the arm at the shoulder."]},"a96":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Latissimus dorsi borders both the triangle of auscultation and the lumbar triangle; it is supplied by the thoracodorsal artery.</p><p>The triangle of auscultation is bounded by trapezius (medially), latissimus dorsi (inferiorly) and the medial border of the scapula/rhomboid major (laterally); its floor is thin, so lung sounds are heard well there. The lumbar (Petit) triangle is bounded by latissimus dorsi (posteriorly), external oblique (anteriorly) and the iliac crest (inferiorly), and is a site of rare lumbar hernias. Only latissimus dorsi appears in both. Its main blood supply is the thoracodorsal artery (from the subscapular artery), which runs with the thoracodorsal nerve.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Levator scapulae</b> — Levator scapulae runs from the cervical transverse processes to the superior angle of the scapula, away from both triangles.</li><li><b>Rhomboid minor</b> — Rhomboid minor lies above rhomboid major; it doesn't border either triangle.</li><li><b>Trapezius</b> — Trapezius borders the triangle of auscultation but not the lumbar triangle.</li><li><b>Splenius capitis</b> — Splenius capitis is a deep neck muscle unrelated to these triangles.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Latissimus dorsi is the common border of the auscultation and lumbar (Petit) triangles.</p>","w":["Levator scapulae runs from the cervical transverse processes to the superior angle of the scapula, away from both triangles.","Rhomboid minor lies above rhomboid major; it doesn't border either triangle.","Correct — latissimus dorsi forms a boundary of both triangles and would be ischemic.","Trapezius borders the triangle of auscultation but not the lumbar triangle.","Splenius capitis is a deep neck muscle unrelated to these triangles."]},"a97":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Trapezius is supplied by the superficial (ascending) branch of the transverse cervical artery.</p><p>The transverse cervical artery (usually from the thyrocervical trunk) crosses the posterior triangle and divides into a superficial branch that runs on the deep surface of trapezius with the accessory nerve, and a deep (descending) branch — the dorsal scapular artery — that runs with the dorsal scapular nerve to levator scapulae and the rhomboids. Ligating the superficial branch therefore makes trapezius ischemic.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Latissimus dorsi</b> — Latissimus dorsi is supplied by the thoracodorsal artery.</li><li><b>Multifidus</b> — Multifidus, a deep back muscle, is supplied by segmental (posterior intercostal/lumbar) arteries.</li><li><b>Rhomboid major</b> — Rhomboid major is supplied by the deep (descending) branch, the dorsal scapular artery.</li><li><b>Longissimus capitis</b> — Longissimus capitis is supplied by segmental arteries (and occipital/vertebral branches).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Transverse cervical artery: superficial branch → trapezius (with CN XI); deep branch (dorsal scapular) → rhomboids and levator scapulae.</p>","w":["Latissimus dorsi is supplied by the thoracodorsal artery.","Multifidus, a deep back muscle, is supplied by segmental (posterior intercostal/lumbar) arteries.","Correct — the superficial branch of the transverse cervical artery supplies trapezius.","Rhomboid major is supplied by the deep (descending) branch, the dorsal scapular artery.","Longissimus capitis is supplied by segmental arteries (and occipital/vertebral branches)."]},"a98":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The thoracoacromial trunk has four branches (pectoral, clavicular, acromial, deltoid); the superior thoracic artery comes directly off the axillary artery.</p><p>The axillary artery is divided by pectoralis minor into three parts. Part 1 (above it) gives one branch: the superior thoracic artery. Part 2 (behind it) gives two: the thoracoacromial trunk and the lateral thoracic artery. Part 3 (below it) gives three: subscapular, anterior and posterior circumflex humeral arteries (\"1, 2, 3\"). The thoracoacromial trunk pierces the clavipectoral fascia and splits into its four branches, so tying it off affects all four, but not the superior thoracic artery.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Acromial</b> — The acromial branch arises from the thoracoacromial trunk.</li><li><b>Pectoral</b> — The pectoral branch arises from the thoracoacromial trunk.</li><li><b>Clavicular</b> — The clavicular branch arises from the thoracoacromial trunk.</li><li><b>Deltoid</b> — The deltoid branch arises from the thoracoacromial trunk.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Axillary artery branches: \"Screw The Lawyer, Save A Patient\" — Superior thoracic, Thoracoacromial, Lateral thoracic, Subscapular, Anterior and Posterior circumflex humeral.</p>","w":["The acromial branch arises from the thoracoacromial trunk.","The pectoral branch arises from the thoracoacromial trunk.","The clavicular branch arises from the thoracoacromial trunk.","The deltoid branch arises from the thoracoacromial trunk.","Correct — the superior thoracic artery branches directly from the axillary artery, not the thoracoacromial trunk."]},"a99":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The radial artery crosses the scaphoid in the floor of the anatomic snuffbox and can be torn with scaphoid fractures.</p><p>After giving the superficial palmar branch at the wrist, the radial artery winds dorsally around the lateral wrist, crossing the scaphoid and trapezium in the snuffbox, then passes between the two heads of the first dorsal interosseous into the palm to form the deep palmar arch (with the ulnar artery's deep branch). Its position directly on the scaphoid makes it vulnerable when that bone fractures after a fall on an outstretched hand.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Brachial artery</b> — The brachial artery ends at the cubital fossa by dividing into radial and ulnar arteries.</li><li><b>Ulnar artery</b> — The ulnar artery is on the medial side of the wrist (Guyon's canal), far from the scaphoid.</li><li><b>Deep palmar arterial arch</b> — The deep palmar arch lies deep in the palm on the metacarpal bases, beyond the snuffbox.</li><li><b>Princeps pollicis artery</b> — The princeps pollicis artery arises from the radial artery in the palm, after it leaves the snuffbox.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Snuffbox floor (scaphoid, trapezium) carries the radial artery.</p>","w":["The brachial artery ends at the cubital fossa by dividing into radial and ulnar arteries.","The ulnar artery is on the medial side of the wrist (Guyon's canal), far from the scaphoid.","The deep palmar arch lies deep in the palm on the metacarpal bases, beyond the snuffbox.","Correct — the radial artery lies on the scaphoid in the snuffbox and may rupture with its fracture.","The princeps pollicis artery arises from the radial artery in the palm, after it leaves the snuffbox."]},"a100":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> If the brachial artery is tied at its origin, the profunda brachii is refilled by the posterior circumflex humeral artery through its ascending (deltoid) branch.</p><p>Around the shoulder and arm there are collateral loops connecting axillary branches to brachial branches. The profunda brachii gives an ascending (deltoid) branch that anastomoses with the descending branch of the posterior circumflex humeral artery (from the axillary artery). If the brachial artery is ligated just below teres major, blood can flow from the axillary artery through the posterior circumflex humeral into the profunda brachii and on down to the elbow anastomosis. Arteries that arise below the ligation (superior ulnar collateral, radial recurrent) can't supply it from above.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Lateral thoracic</b> — The lateral thoracic artery supplies the lateral chest wall and breast; it doesn't connect with the profunda brachii.</li><li><b>Subscapular</b> — The subscapular artery's branches (circumflex scapular, thoracodorsal) anastomose around the scapula, not with the profunda brachii.</li><li><b>Superior ulnar collateral</b> — The superior ulnar collateral arises from the brachial artery BELOW the profunda brachii and would itself be downstream of the ligature.</li><li><b>Radial recurrent</b> — The radial recurrent artery arises from the radial artery in the cubital fossa and joins the elbow anastomosis from below.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Profunda brachii ↔ posterior circumflex humeral is the collateral that bypasses a proximal brachial occlusion.</p>","w":["The lateral thoracic artery supplies the lateral chest wall and breast; it doesn't connect with the profunda brachii.","The subscapular artery's branches (circumflex scapular, thoracodorsal) anastomose around the scapula, not with the profunda brachii.","Correct — the posterior circumflex humeral artery anastomoses with the ascending branch of the profunda brachii.","The superior ulnar collateral arises from the brachial artery BELOW the profunda brachii and would itself be downstream of the ligature.","The radial recurrent artery arises from the radial artery in the cubital fossa and joins the elbow anastomosis from below."]},"a101":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The radial artery's terminal branches in the hand are the princeps pollicis artery and the deep palmar arch; the superficial arch is mainly ulnar.</p><p>The Allen test compresses both radial and ulnar arteries at the wrist, then releases one to see whether the hand pinks up, testing each vessel's contribution. The radial artery passes through the snuffbox and between the heads of the first dorsal interosseous into the palm, where it immediately gives the princeps pollicis artery (to both sides of the thumb) and the radialis indicis artery before forming the deep palmar arch. Narrowing of the radial artery therefore reduces flow most dramatically to the thumb via the princeps pollicis, causing exertional pain (claudication) of the thumb muscles.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>A marked decrease in the blood flow in the superficial palmar arterial arch</b> — The superficial palmar arch is formed mainly by the ulnar artery (completed by the superficial palmar branch of the radial).</li><li><b>Decreased pulsation in the artery passing superficial to the flexor retinaculum</b> — The artery passing superficial to the flexor retinaculum is the ulnar artery (in Guyon's canal).</li><li><b>Ischemia of the entire extensor muscles of the forearm</b> — The extensor compartment is supplied mainly by the posterior interosseous artery, a branch of the common interosseous from the ULNAR artery.</li><li><b>A low blood pressure in the anterior interosseous artery</b> — The anterior interosseous artery comes from the common interosseous branch of the ulnar artery, so radial stenosis wouldn't lower its pressure.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Radial artery → deep arch + princeps pollicis; ulnar artery → superficial arch + common interosseous.</p>","w":["The superficial palmar arch is formed mainly by the ulnar artery (completed by the superficial palmar branch of the radial).","The artery passing superficial to the flexor retinaculum is the ulnar artery (in Guyon's canal).","The extensor compartment is supplied mainly by the posterior interosseous artery, a branch of the common interosseous from the ULNAR artery.","Correct — the princeps pollicis artery is a direct branch of the radial artery, so its flow drops.","The anterior interosseous artery comes from the common interosseous branch of the ulnar artery, so radial stenosis wouldn't lower its pressure."]},"a102":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The dorsal scapular artery often arises directly from the subclavian artery (3rd part) and joins the scapular anastomosis.</p><p>The scapular anastomosis connects the subclavian and axillary arteries around the scapula, so blood can bypass a block or injury between the first part of the subclavian and the third part of the axillary artery. Contributors from the subclavian side are the suprascapular artery and the dorsal scapular artery (or deep branch of the transverse cervical); from the axillary side, the subscapular artery via its circumflex scapular branch. The suprascapular and transverse cervical arteries arise from the thyrocervical trunk, so the only DIRECT subclavian branch here is the dorsal scapular artery.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Thoracoacromial artery</b> — The thoracoacromial artery arises from the second part of the AXILLARY artery.</li><li><b>Circumflex scapular artery</b> — The circumflex scapular artery is a branch of the subscapular artery (third part of the axillary).</li><li><b>Transverse cervical artery</b> — The transverse cervical artery arises from the thyrocervical trunk, not directly from the subclavian.</li><li><b>Suprascapular artery</b> — The suprascapular artery also arises from the thyrocervical trunk.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Scapular anastomosis: suprascapular + dorsal scapular (subclavian side) ↔ circumflex scapular (axillary side).</p>","w":["Correct — the dorsal scapular artery arises directly from the subclavian artery and joins the scapular anastomosis.","The thoracoacromial artery arises from the second part of the AXILLARY artery.","The circumflex scapular artery is a branch of the subscapular artery (third part of the axillary).","The transverse cervical artery arises from the thyrocervical trunk, not directly from the subclavian.","The suprascapular artery also arises from the thyrocervical trunk."]},"a103":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The hip is supplied by the medial and lateral femoral circumflex, obturator, and superior and inferior gluteal arteries; the deep circumflex iliac artery does not reach it.</p><p>The femoral head and neck receive blood mainly from retinacular branches of the medial femoral circumflex artery, which run along the neck under the synovial membrane; dislocation or neck fracture tears them, causing avascular necrosis. The lateral femoral circumflex, the obturator (via the artery of the ligament of the head) and the gluteal arteries contribute to the capsule and acetabulum. The deep circumflex iliac artery arises from the external iliac artery and runs along the inner surface of the iliac crest to supply the iliacus and lower abdominal wall, so it stays intact.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Lateral femoral circumflex artery</b> — The lateral femoral circumflex artery supplies the anterior hip capsule and neck.</li><li><b>Medial femoral circumflex artery</b> — The medial femoral circumflex artery is the main supply of the femoral head and neck.</li><li><b>Obturator artery</b> — The obturator artery supplies the acetabulum and gives the artery of the ligament of the head.</li><li><b>Inferior gluteal artery</b> — The inferior gluteal artery contributes to the posterior hip capsule and the cruciate anastomosis.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Femoral neck fracture → medial femoral circumflex retinacular branches torn → avascular necrosis of the head.</p>","w":["The lateral femoral circumflex artery supplies the anterior hip capsule and neck.","The medial femoral circumflex artery is the main supply of the femoral head and neck.","The obturator artery supplies the acetabulum and gives the artery of the ligament of the head.","The inferior gluteal artery contributes to the posterior hip capsule and the cruciate anastomosis.","Correct — the deep circumflex iliac artery does not supply the hip joint, so it remains intact."]},"a104":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Iliopsoas (iliacus + psoas major) inserts on the lesser trochanter.</p><p>Iliacus arises from the iliac fossa and psoas major from the lumbar vertebrae; their tendons merge and insert on the lesser trochanter of the femur. Together they are the most powerful flexor of the hip. Iliacus is supplied by the femoral nerve and psoas major by direct branches of L1–L3. An avulsion fracture of the lesser trochanter (sometimes from forceful contraction in adolescents, or a pathologic fracture from metastasis in adults) detaches this tendon and weakens hip flexion.</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Isolated lesser trochanter avulsion in an adult should raise suspicion of a metastatic lesion.</p>","w":["","","","","Correct — this is the iliacus (with psoas major), which inserts on the lesser trochanter."]},"a105":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Superior gluteal nerve → gluteus medius and minimus (and tensor fasciae latae); loss = Trendelenburg (waddling) gait with the pelvis dropping toward the UNSUPPORTED side.</p><p>When you stand on one leg, the gluteus medius and minimus of the stance limb pull on the pelvis to keep the opposite side from sagging. If the superior gluteal nerve is injured (by a misplaced buttock injection or a stab wound above piriformis), these abductors fail, so each time the patient stands on the affected limb the pelvis drops toward the swinging (normal) side (positive Trendelenburg sign). Patients compensate by leaning the trunk over the weak side, producing a waddling or lurching gait.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Obturator nerve</b> — The obturator nerve supplies the thigh adductors; loss causes weak adduction and a mild outward swing of the limb.</li><li><b>Nerve to obturator internus</b> — The nerve to obturator internus supplies obturator internus and superior gemellus, small lateral rotators.</li><li><b>Inferior gluteal nerve</b> — The inferior gluteal nerve supplies gluteus maximus; loss causes difficulty rising from sitting and climbing stairs, not pelvic drop.</li><li><b>Femoral nerve</b> — The femoral nerve supplies the anterior thigh (quadriceps, sartorius, iliacus); loss weakens knee extension and hip flexion.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Trendelenburg sign: pelvis drops on the side OPPOSITE the injured nerve; the lesion is on the stance side.</p>","w":["The obturator nerve supplies the thigh adductors; loss causes weak adduction and a mild outward swing of the limb.","The nerve to obturator internus supplies obturator internus and superior gemellus, small lateral rotators.","Correct — injury to the superior gluteal nerve paralyzes gluteus medius/minimus, causing a waddling (Trendelenburg) gait.","The inferior gluteal nerve supplies gluteus maximus; loss causes difficulty rising from sitting and climbing stairs, not pelvic drop.","The femoral nerve supplies the anterior thigh (quadriceps, sartorius, iliacus); loss weakens knee extension and hip flexion."]},"a106":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Give gluteal injections in the upper lateral (superolateral) quadrant to avoid the sciatic nerve.</p><p>The sciatic nerve leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen below piriformis and curves down through the lower medial quadrant of the buttock, between the greater trochanter and ischial tuberosity. The superolateral quadrant (above and lateral to a line from the posterior superior iliac spine to the greater trochanter) contains only gluteus medius and minimus and their nerve branches, so it's safe for intramuscular injections. Injections that are too medial or too low risk the sciatic nerve (foot drop, sensory loss) or, very medially, the pudendal nerve.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Over the sacrospinous ligament</b> — Over the sacrospinous ligament lie the pudendal nerve and internal pudendal vessels as they leave and re-enter the pelvis.</li><li><b>Midway between the ischial tuberosity and the lesser trochanter</b> — Midway between the ischial tuberosity and the lesser trochanter, the sciatic and posterior femoral cutaneous nerves descend over quadratus femoris.</li><li><b>Midpoint of the gemelli muscles</b> — The gemelli lie directly deep to the sciatic nerve as it crosses them.</li><li><b>Lower medial quadrant of the gluteal region</b> — The lower medial quadrant contains the sciatic nerve and, more medially, the pudendal nerve.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Superolateral quadrant = safe; a medial injection can cause sciatic nerve palsy.</p>","w":["Over the sacrospinous ligament lie the pudendal nerve and internal pudendal vessels as they leave and re-enter the pelvis.","Midway between the ischial tuberosity and the lesser trochanter, the sciatic and posterior femoral cutaneous nerves descend over quadratus femoris.","The gemelli lie directly deep to the sciatic nerve as it crosses them.","Correct — the upper lateral quadrant of the gluteal region avoids the sciatic nerve.","The lower medial quadrant contains the sciatic nerve and, more medially, the pudendal nerve."]},"a107":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Tensor fasciae latae flexes, abducts and medially rotates the thigh and tightens the iliotibial tract.</p><p>Tensor fasciae latae arises from the anterior superior iliac spine and anterior iliac crest and inserts into the iliotibial tract. Because it lies anterior to the hip's axis, it flexes the thigh; because it is lateral and slightly anterior, it abducts and medially rotates it. It is supplied by the superior gluteal nerve and works with gluteus medius and minimus to stabilize the pelvis. In running and climbing, its loss weakens the combined flexion–medial rotation of the swinging limb.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Semimembranosus</b> — Semimembranosus (a hamstring) extends the thigh and flexes and medially rotates the leg at the knee.</li><li><b>Sartorius</b> — Sartorius flexes, abducts and LATERALLY rotates the thigh (cross-legged \"tailor's\" position).</li><li><b>Rectus femoris</b> — Rectus femoris flexes the thigh and extends the knee, but doesn't medially rotate.</li><li><b>Vastus intermedius</b> — Vastus intermedius only extends the knee; it doesn't cross the hip.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> TFL and gluteus medius/minimus are medial rotators; most other hip muscles around the back are lateral rotators.</p>","w":["Semimembranosus (a hamstring) extends the thigh and flexes and medially rotates the leg at the knee.","Sartorius flexes, abducts and LATERALLY rotates the thigh (cross-legged \"tailor's\" position).","Rectus femoris flexes the thigh and extends the knee, but doesn't medially rotate.","Vastus intermedius only extends the knee; it doesn't cross the hip.","Correct — tensor fasciae latae flexes and medially rotates the thigh."]},"a108":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The artery of the ligament of the head of the femur is the acetabular branch of the OBTURATOR artery.</p><p>The ligamentum teres (ligament of the head of the femur) runs from the acetabular notch to the fovea of the femoral head. It carries a small artery from the posterior branch of the obturator artery (sometimes the medial femoral circumflex contributes). In children it is an important supply to the femoral head epiphysis; in adults it supplies only a small area near the fovea. Posterior dislocation of the hip ruptures the ligament and its artery.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Medial circumflex femoral</b> — The medial femoral circumflex is the main supply of the head, through retinacular branches along the neck, not the ligament.</li><li><b>Lateral circumflex femoral</b> — The lateral femoral circumflex supplies the anterior neck and trochanteric region.</li><li><b>Superior gluteal</b> — The superior gluteal artery supplies the gluteal muscles and upper hip capsule, not the ligament.</li><li><b>Inferior gluteal</b> — The inferior gluteal artery supplies gluteus maximus and joins the cruciate anastomosis.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Artery in the round ligament = obturator branch; important in children, minor in adults.</p>","w":["The medial femoral circumflex is the main supply of the head, through retinacular branches along the neck, not the ligament.","The lateral femoral circumflex supplies the anterior neck and trochanteric region.","Correct — the obturator artery's acetabular branch runs in the ligamentum teres.","The superior gluteal artery supplies the gluteal muscles and upper hip capsule, not the ligament.","The inferior gluteal artery supplies gluteus maximus and joins the cruciate anastomosis."]},"a109":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> In adults the femoral head is supplied mainly by the medial femoral circumflex artery's retinacular branches; neck fractures tear them → avascular necrosis.</p><p>The medial femoral circumflex artery winds around the back of the femoral neck and sends retinacular (cervical) arteries up along the neck under the capsule to enter the head. A displaced intracapsular (subcapital) fracture of the neck interrupts these vessels; the small artery in the ligament of the head can't compensate in adults, so the head can die. This is why displaced femoral neck fractures in older adults are often treated with hip replacement rather than fixation.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Obturator</b> — The obturator artery supplies the head only via the small artery of the ligament of the head, insufficient in adults.</li><li><b>Superior gluteal</b> — The superior gluteal artery supplies gluteal muscles and doesn't reach the femoral head.</li><li><b>Inferior gluteal</b> — The inferior gluteal artery supplies gluteus maximus and the cruciate anastomosis, not the head.</li><li><b>Lateral femoral circumflex</b> — The lateral femoral circumflex contributes only through anastomoses, mainly to the neck and trochanter.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Avascular necrosis of femoral head = medial femoral circumflex; intracapsular fractures carry the highest risk.</p>","w":["The obturator artery supplies the head only via the small artery of the ligament of the head, insufficient in adults.","The superior gluteal artery supplies gluteal muscles and doesn't reach the femoral head.","The inferior gluteal artery supplies gluteus maximus and the cruciate anastomosis, not the head.","Correct — the medial femoral circumflex artery is the chief supply of the adult femoral head.","The lateral femoral circumflex contributes only through anastomoses, mainly to the neck and trochanter."]},"a110":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Acetabulum = ilium (superior, ~2/5) + ischium (posteroinferior, ~2/5) + pubis (anteromedial, ~1/5), fused at the Y-shaped triradiate cartilage.</p><p>The three hip bones meet in the acetabulum and fuse at puberty. The superior (and posterosuperior) rim — the weight-bearing roof — is ilium. In a posterior hip dislocation, the femoral head is driven backward and upward, and can break off the posterosuperior acetabular rim, which is iliac. The posteroinferior part is ischium and the anteromedial part is pubis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Pubis</b> — The pubis forms the anteromedial (front) part of the acetabulum.</li><li><b>Ischium</b> — The ischium forms the posteroinferior part, below the region described.</li><li><b>Sacrum</b> — The sacrum articulates with the ilium at the sacroiliac joint and isn't part of the acetabulum.</li><li><b>Head of the femur</b> — The femoral head articulates WITH the acetabulum but isn't part of it.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Triradiate cartilage joins ilium, ischium and pubis in the acetabulum.</p>","w":["The pubis forms the anteromedial (front) part of the acetabulum.","The ischium forms the posteroinferior part, below the region described.","Correct — the ilium forms the superior (posterosuperior) part of the acetabulum.","The sacrum articulates with the ilium at the sacroiliac joint and isn't part of the acetabulum.","The femoral head articulates WITH the acetabulum but isn't part of it."]},"a111":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Gluteus medius and minimus abduct and medially rotate the thigh; most other gluteal muscles are lateral rotators.</p><p>Gluteus medius and minimus arise from the outer surface of the ilium and insert on the greater trochanter. Their anterior fibers medially rotate the thigh, and as a whole they abduct it and stabilize the pelvis during walking (superior gluteal nerve). Damage from the fall (or to their nerve) weakens abduction and medial rotation. The deep short muscles (piriformis, gemelli, obturators, quadratus femoris) and gluteus maximus are lateral rotators.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Piriformis</b> — Piriformis laterally rotates the extended thigh (and abducts the flexed thigh).</li><li><b>Obturator internus</b> — Obturator internus laterally rotates the thigh.</li><li><b>Quadratus femoris</b> — Quadratus femoris laterally rotates the thigh.</li><li><b>Gluteus maximus</b> — Gluteus maximus extends and laterally rotates the thigh.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Medial rotators of the hip: gluteus medius/minimus (anterior fibers), TFL; the rest of the gluteal group rotates laterally.</p>","w":["Piriformis laterally rotates the extended thigh (and abducts the flexed thigh).","Obturator internus laterally rotates the thigh.","Quadratus femoris laterally rotates the thigh.","Gluteus maximus extends and laterally rotates the thigh.","Correct — gluteus minimus abducts and medially rotates the thigh."]},"a112":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cruciate anastomosis = inferior gluteal + transverse branches of the medial and lateral femoral circumflex + ascending branch of the first perforating artery. The superior gluteal artery isn't part of it.</p><p>The cruciate anastomosis lies behind the upper femur at the level of the lesser trochanter, forming a cross: inferior gluteal artery from above, first perforating artery from below, and the circumflex femoral arteries from the sides. It links the internal iliac system to the femoral system, providing a bypass if the external iliac or femoral artery is blocked. The superior gluteal artery runs between gluteus medius and minimus higher up and joins the trochanteric anastomosis instead.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Medial femoral circumflex</b> — The medial femoral circumflex contributes a transverse branch to the cruciate anastomosis.</li><li><b>Lateral femoral circumflex</b> — The lateral femoral circumflex contributes a transverse branch to the cruciate anastomosis.</li><li><b>Inferior gluteal</b> — The inferior gluteal artery forms the top limb of the cross.</li><li><b>First perforating</b> — The first perforating artery forms the bottom limb of the cross.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Trochanteric anastomosis includes the SUPERIOR gluteal; cruciate anastomosis includes the INFERIOR gluteal.</p>","w":["The medial femoral circumflex contributes a transverse branch to the cruciate anastomosis.","The lateral femoral circumflex contributes a transverse branch to the cruciate anastomosis.","Correct — the superior gluteal artery is not part of the cruciate anastomosis, so it keeps flowing.","The inferior gluteal artery forms the top limb of the cross.","The first perforating artery forms the bottom limb of the cross."]},"a113":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Gluteus maximus is the main extensor and a lateral rotator of the thigh (inferior gluteal nerve).</p><p>Gluteus maximus arises from the posterior ilium, sacrum, coccyx and sacrotuberous ligament and inserts into the iliotibial tract and gluteal tuberosity. It extends the thigh powerfully (rising from sitting, climbing stairs, running uphill) and laterally rotates it. Weakness in combined extension + lateral rotation points to it or its nerve, the inferior gluteal nerve. Patients lurch the trunk backward when walking (gluteus maximus gait).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Obturator externus</b> — Obturator externus laterally rotates the thigh but doesn't extend it.</li><li><b>Sartorius</b> — Sartorius flexes the hip and knee and laterally rotates the thigh.</li><li><b>Tensor fasciae latae</b> — Tensor fasciae latae flexes and medially rotates the thigh.</li><li><b>Semitendinosus</b> — Semitendinosus extends the thigh but medially rotates the flexed leg; it doesn't laterally rotate the thigh.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Gluteus maximus is used for climbing and rising; walking on level ground uses it little.</p>","w":["Obturator externus laterally rotates the thigh but doesn't extend it.","Sartorius flexes the hip and knee and laterally rotates the thigh.","Tensor fasciae latae flexes and medially rotates the thigh.","Correct — gluteus maximus extends and laterally rotates the thigh.","Semitendinosus extends the thigh but medially rotates the flexed leg; it doesn't laterally rotate the thigh."]},"a114":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Gluteus maximus inserts on the gluteal tuberosity and iliotibial tract, NOT the greater trochanter.</p><p>Many hip muscles converge on the greater trochanter: gluteus medius (lateral surface), gluteus minimus (anterior surface), piriformis (upper border), and obturator internus with the gemelli (medial surface, near the trochanteric fossa where obturator externus inserts). A trochanteric fracture detaches these abductors and lateral rotators. Gluteus maximus slides over the trochanter (separated by a bursa) and inserts lower down, so it keeps working.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Piriformis</b> — Piriformis inserts on the upper border of the greater trochanter.</li><li><b>Obturator internus</b> — Obturator internus (with the gemelli) inserts on the medial surface of the greater trochanter.</li><li><b>Gluteus medius</b> — Gluteus medius inserts on the lateral surface of the greater trochanter.</li><li><b>Gluteus minimus</b> — Gluteus minimus inserts on the anterior surface of the greater trochanter.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Trochanteric bursitis is irritation of the bursa between gluteus maximus and the greater trochanter.</p>","w":["Piriformis inserts on the upper border of the greater trochanter.","Obturator internus (with the gemelli) inserts on the medial surface of the greater trochanter.","Gluteus medius inserts on the lateral surface of the greater trochanter.","Correct — gluteus maximus inserts on the gluteal tuberosity and iliotibial tract, so it still works.","Gluteus minimus inserts on the anterior surface of the greater trochanter."]},"a115":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The chief flexor of the thigh is iliopsoas, which inserts on the lesser trochanter.</p><p>Iliopsoas (psoas major from the lumbar vertebrae + iliacus from the iliac fossa) crosses in front of the hip joint and inserts on the lesser trochanter on the posteromedial femur. It flexes the hip strongly (and flexes the trunk when the femur is fixed, as in sit-ups). A fracture of the lesser trochanter removes its insertion, so hip flexion becomes weak; rectus femoris, sartorius, TFL and pectineus are much weaker flexors.</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Iliopsoas = chief hip flexor; lesser trochanter = its insertion.</p>","w":["","","","Correct — this is the lesser trochanter, insertion of iliopsoas, the chief flexor of the thigh.",""]},"a116":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Obturator internus leaves the pelvis through the lesser sciatic foramen and inserts on the medial surface of the greater trochanter.</p><p>Obturator internus arises from the inner surface of the obturator membrane and surrounding bone, then its tendon turns 90° around the lesser sciatic notch (which acts like a pulley) to leave through the lesser sciatic foramen. Joined by the superior and inferior gemelli, it inserts on the medial surface of the greater trochanter. It laterally rotates the extended thigh and abducts the flexed thigh. A greater trochanter fracture disrupts this insertion. Its nerve (nerve to obturator internus) also supplies the superior gemellus.</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Lesser sciatic foramen: obturator internus tendon out; pudendal nerve and internal pudendal vessels back in.</p>","w":["","Correct — this is the greater trochanter, where the obturator internus tendon inserts.","","",""]},"a117":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Adductor magnus arises from the ischiopubic ramus (adductor part) and the ischial tuberosity (hamstring part).</p><p>Adductor magnus is a two-part muscle. Its adductor part arises from the inferior pubic and ischial rami and inserts along the linea aspera (supplied by the obturator nerve). Its hamstring part arises from the ischial tuberosity and inserts on the adductor tubercle (supplied by the tibial part of the sciatic nerve). Fracture of the ischiopubic ramus or ischial tuberosity therefore detaches it and weakens adduction (and some hip extension).</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Adductor magnus has dual innervation: obturator (adductor part) and tibial (hamstring part).</p>","w":["","","Correct — this is the ischiopubic ramus/ischial tuberosity region, origin of adductor magnus.","",""]},"a118":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The femoral head depends mainly on the medial femoral circumflex artery; cutting it causes avascular necrosis of the head.</p><p>Retinacular branches of the medial femoral circumflex artery climb the posterior femoral neck under the synovial membrane to supply most of the head; a small area near the fovea is also supplied by the artery of the ligament of the head (obturator artery). If the medial femoral circumflex artery is severed (or its branches torn in a neck fracture or dislocation), the head loses most of its supply and becomes necrotic, collapsing and causing arthritis.</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Femoral head avascular necrosis risk factors: displaced neck fractures, dislocation, steroids, alcohol, sickle cell disease.</p>","w":["Correct — this is the femoral head, which becomes necrotic when the medial femoral circumflex artery is severed.","","","",""]},"a119":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Only the superior gluteal nerve and vessels exit the greater sciatic foramen ABOVE piriformis; everything else exits below.</p><p>Piriformis divides the greater sciatic foramen into two parts. Above it pass the superior gluteal nerve, artery and vein. Below it pass the sciatic nerve, inferior gluteal nerve and vessels, posterior femoral cutaneous nerve, nerves to obturator internus and quadratus femoris, and the pudendal nerve with the internal pudendal vessels. A stab wound entering above piriformis therefore injures the superior gluteal neurovascular bundle, leading to weak abduction (Trendelenburg gait) and possible heavy bleeding.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Sciatic nerve</b> — The sciatic nerve exits BELOW piriformis (occasionally through it).</li><li><b>Internal pudendal artery</b> — The internal pudendal artery exits below piriformis, then re-enters through the lesser sciatic foramen.</li><li><b>Inferior gluteal artery</b> — The inferior gluteal artery exits below piriformis.</li><li><b>Posterior femoral cutaneous nerve</b> — The posterior femoral cutaneous nerve exits below piriformis.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Above piriformis: superior gluteal nerve + vessels only.</p>","w":["The sciatic nerve exits BELOW piriformis (occasionally through it).","The internal pudendal artery exits below piriformis, then re-enters through the lesser sciatic foramen.","Correct — the superior gluteal nerve exits the pelvis above piriformis.","The inferior gluteal artery exits below piriformis.","The posterior femoral cutaneous nerve exits below piriformis."]},"a120":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Quadriceps femoris (rectus femoris + three vasti) is the only extensor of the knee; femoral nerve.</p><p>The four parts of quadriceps join in the quadriceps tendon, which encloses the patella and continues as the patellar ligament to the tibial tuberosity. Contraction straightens the knee; rectus femoris, which also crosses the hip, also flexes the thigh. A fall that ruptures the quadriceps tendon or patellar ligament, fractures the patella, or injures the femoral nerve leaves the patient unable to extend the knee or perform a straight-leg raise.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Semitendinosus</b> — Semitendinosus is a hamstring that extends the thigh and FLEXES the knee.</li><li><b>Sartorius</b> — Sartorius flexes the hip and knee.</li><li><b>Gracilis</b> — Gracilis adducts the thigh and flexes the knee.</li><li><b>Biceps femoris</b> — Biceps femoris is a hamstring that extends the thigh and flexes the knee.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Inability to extend the knee = quadriceps/extensor mechanism or femoral nerve; test with a straight-leg raise and the patellar reflex (L3–L4).</p>","w":["Semitendinosus is a hamstring that extends the thigh and FLEXES the knee.","Sartorius flexes the hip and knee.","Gracilis adducts the thigh and flexes the knee.","Correct — the quadriceps femoris extends the leg at the knee.","Biceps femoris is a hamstring that extends the thigh and flexes the knee."]},"a121":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The adductor canal contains the femoral artery and vein, the saphenous nerve and the nerve to vastus medialis; the great saphenous VEIN is superficial and lies outside it.</p><p>The adductor (subsartorial, Hunter's) canal runs from the apex of the femoral triangle to the adductor hiatus. It is bounded by vastus medialis (anterolaterally), adductor longus and magnus (posteriorly) and sartorius over the roof. Through it pass the femoral artery and vein on their way to becoming the popliteal vessels, the saphenous nerve (which later leaves the canal to run with the great saphenous vein in the leg) and the nerve to vastus medialis. The great saphenous vein stays in the superficial fascia on the medial thigh, outside the deep fascia, so a deep infection in the canal spares it. Don't confuse the saphenous NERVE (in the canal) with the great saphenous VEIN (superficial).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Femoral artery</b> — The femoral artery passes through the adductor canal and becomes the popliteal artery at the adductor hiatus.</li><li><b>Femoral vein</b> — The femoral vein accompanies the artery through the canal.</li><li><b>Saphenous nerve</b> — The saphenous nerve (a branch of the femoral nerve) runs through the canal.</li><li><b>Nerve to the vastus medialis</b> — The nerve to vastus medialis runs in the upper part of the canal.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Adductor canal block (saphenous nerve) is used for knee surgery analgesia because it spares quadriceps strength.</p>","w":["The femoral artery passes through the adductor canal and becomes the popliteal artery at the adductor hiatus.","The femoral vein accompanies the artery through the canal.","The saphenous nerve (a branch of the femoral nerve) runs through the canal.","Correct — the great saphenous vein is superficial and not in the adductor canal, so it remains intact.","The nerve to vastus medialis runs in the upper part of the canal."]},"a122":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Rectus femoris is the only quadriceps head that crosses the hip: it flexes the hip and extends the knee.</p><p>Rectus femoris arises from the anterior inferior iliac spine (straight head) and the ilium above the acetabulum (reflected head), so it crosses both joints. It flexes the thigh at the hip and, through the quadriceps tendon and patellar ligament, extends the leg at the knee — the kicking motion. It is supplied by the femoral nerve. Weakness in exactly this combination points to rectus femoris (or a femoral nerve lesion affecting it).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Sartorius</b> — Sartorius flexes the hip AND flexes the knee (plus abducts and laterally rotates the thigh).</li><li><b>Gracilis</b> — Gracilis adducts the thigh and flexes the knee.</li><li><b>Vastus medialis</b> — Vastus medialis only extends the knee; it arises from the femur and doesn't cross the hip.</li><li><b>Semimembranosus</b> — Semimembranosus extends the hip and flexes the knee — the exact opposite combination.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Kicking a ball = rectus femoris (hip flexion + knee extension). Avulsion of the AIIS occurs in adolescent kickers.</p>","w":["Sartorius flexes the hip AND flexes the knee (plus abducts and laterally rotates the thigh).","Gracilis adducts the thigh and flexes the knee.","Correct — rectus femoris flexes the hip and extends the knee.","Vastus medialis only extends the knee; it arises from the femur and doesn't cross the hip.","Semimembranosus extends the hip and flexes the knee — the exact opposite combination."]},"a123":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Sartorius flexes both the hip (thigh) and the knee (leg), and laterally rotates the thigh.</p><p>Sartorius runs from the anterior superior iliac spine diagonally across the front of the thigh to the medial surface of the upper tibia (as part of the pes anserinus). Because it crosses in front of the hip and behind the knee axis, it flexes both joints; it also abducts and laterally rotates the thigh, producing the cross-legged \"tailor's\" position. Supplied by the femoral nerve.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Rectus femoris</b> — Rectus femoris flexes the thigh but EXTENDS the leg.</li><li><b>Semitendinosus</b> — Semitendinosus extends the thigh (and flexes the leg) — not flexion of both.</li><li><b>Biceps femoris</b> — Biceps femoris extends the thigh (long head) and flexes the leg.</li><li><b>Adductor longus</b> — Adductor longus adducts and helps flex the thigh but doesn't cross the knee.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Pes anserinus (\"Say Grace before Tea\"): Sartorius, Gracilis, semiTendinosus — all flex the knee.</p>","w":["Rectus femoris flexes the thigh but EXTENDS the leg.","Semitendinosus extends the thigh (and flexes the leg) — not flexion of both.","Biceps femoris extends the thigh (long head) and flexes the leg.","Correct — sartorius flexes both the thigh and the leg.","Adductor longus adducts and helps flex the thigh but doesn't cross the knee."]},"a124":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hamstrings that cross both joints (semitendinosus, semimembranosus, long head of biceps) extend the hip and flex the knee.</p><p>The true hamstrings arise from the ischial tuberosity, cross the back of the hip and the back of the knee, and are supplied by the sciatic nerve (tibial part, except the short head of biceps by the common fibular part). Semitendinosus inserts on the medial upper tibia (pes anserinus) and both extends the thigh and flexes the leg, which is exactly the lost combination. Hamstring strains are common in sprinting and jumping sports.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Short head of biceps femoris</b> — The short head of biceps femoris arises from the femur (linea aspera), so it only flexes the leg; it can't extend the hip.</li><li><b>Adductor magnus</b> — Adductor magnus adducts the thigh (its hamstring part extends it) but doesn't cross the knee to flex the leg.</li><li><b>Sartorius</b> — Sartorius flexes both the thigh and leg; it doesn't extend the thigh.</li><li><b>Gracilis</b> — Gracilis adducts the thigh and flexes the leg; it doesn't extend the thigh.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Short head of biceps = the one \"hamstring\" that isn't a true hamstring (femoral origin, common fibular nerve).</p>","w":["The short head of biceps femoris arises from the femur (linea aspera), so it only flexes the leg; it can't extend the hip.","Adductor magnus adducts the thigh (its hamstring part extends it) but doesn't cross the knee to flex the leg.","Correct — semitendinosus extends the thigh and flexes the leg.","Sartorius flexes both the thigh and leg; it doesn't extend the thigh.","Gracilis adducts the thigh and flexes the leg; it doesn't extend the thigh."]},"a125":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The ACL prevents posterior displacement of the femur on the tibia (= anterior displacement of the tibia on the femur) and limits hyperextension; it is taut in extension.</p><p>The anterior cruciate ligament runs from the anterior intercondylar area of the tibia upward, backward and laterally to the medial side of the LATERAL femoral condyle. Its orientation stops the tibia from sliding forward under the femur (or the femur from sliding backward on the tibia), which is what the anterior drawer and Lachman tests check. It is tightest in full extension and helps prevent hyperextension. Torn ACLs, usually from pivoting or landing with the knee near extension, give a pop, rapid swelling (hemarthrosis) and instability.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Becomes taut during flexion of the leg</b> — The ACL becomes taut in EXTENSION and relatively lax in flexion.</li><li><b>Inserts into the medial femoral condyle</b> — The ACL attaches to the LATERAL femoral condyle; the PCL attaches to the medial condyle.</li><li><b>Helps prevent hyperflexion of the knee joint</b> — The ACL limits hyperEXTENSION, not hyperflexion.</li><li><b>Is lax when the knee is extended</b> — The ACL is taut, not lax, when the knee is extended.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> ACL: \"LAMP\" — Lateral condyle, Anterior tibia; Medial condyle = Posterior cruciate. Positive anterior drawer = ACL tear.</p>","w":["The ACL becomes taut in EXTENSION and relatively lax in flexion.","Correct — the ACL resists posterior displacement of the femur on the tibia (anterior tibial translation).","The ACL attaches to the LATERAL femoral condyle; the PCL attaches to the medial condyle.","The ACL limits hyperEXTENSION, not hyperflexion.","The ACL is taut, not lax, when the knee is extended."]},"a126":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The PCL prevents forward (anterior) displacement of the femur on the tibia (= backward sliding of the tibia), especially with the weight-bearing knee flexed.</p><p>The posterior cruciate ligament runs from the posterior intercondylar area of the tibia forward and medially to the lateral side of the MEDIAL femoral condyle. It is the stronger cruciate and becomes taut in flexion. When you walk down stairs or downhill on a flexed knee, the femur tends to slide forward off the tibia, and the PCL is the main restraint. It's tested with the posterior drawer test; tears classically come from a dashboard injury that pushes the tibia backward.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Medial meniscus</b> — The medial meniscus deepens the articular surface and absorbs shock; it isn't the main anteroposterior restraint.</li><li><b>Tibial collateral ligament</b> — The tibial (medial) collateral ligament resists valgus (abduction) stress on the knee.</li><li><b>Fibular collateral ligament</b> — The fibular (lateral) collateral ligament resists varus (adduction) stress.</li><li><b>Anterior cruciate ligament</b> — The ACL prevents BACKWARD displacement of the femur on the tibia (anterior tibial translation).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Dashboard injury → PCL; posterior drawer sign.</p>","w":["The medial meniscus deepens the articular surface and absorbs shock; it isn't the main anteroposterior restraint.","The tibial (medial) collateral ligament resists valgus (abduction) stress on the knee.","The fibular (lateral) collateral ligament resists varus (adduction) stress.","Correct — the PCL prevents forward displacement of the femur on the tibia when the knee is flexed.","The ACL prevents BACKWARD displacement of the femur on the tibia (anterior tibial translation)."]},"a127":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Both menisci are intracapsular but extrasynovial; the lateral meniscus is smaller, nearly circular, more mobile and NOT attached to the fibular collateral ligament.</p><p>The menisci are C-shaped fibrocartilage wedges on the tibial plateaus that deepen the joint and spread load. The synovial membrane lines the capsule but reflects around the menisci and cruciate ligaments, so they lie inside the fibrous capsule yet outside the synovial cavity. The medial meniscus is larger, C-shaped and firmly attached to the tibial (medial) collateral ligament, which makes it less mobile and more often torn. The lateral meniscus is almost circular, smaller and separated from the fibular collateral ligament by the popliteus tendon, which also pulls it backward during flexion, so it escapes injury more often.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Is C-shaped or forms a semicircle</b> — The C-shaped (semicircular) meniscus is the MEDIAL one; the lateral is nearly circular.</li><li><b>Is attached to the fibular collateral ligament</b> — The lateral meniscus is separated from the fibular collateral ligament by the popliteus tendon.</li><li><b>Is larger than the medial meniscus</b> — The lateral meniscus is smaller than the medial meniscus.</li><li><b>Is more frequently torn in injuries than the medial meniscus</b> — The MEDIAL meniscus is torn more often because it's fixed to the tibial collateral ligament.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Medial meniscus = bigger, C-shaped, attached to the MCL, torn more often (unhappy triad).</p>","w":["The C-shaped (semicircular) meniscus is the MEDIAL one; the lateral is nearly circular.","The lateral meniscus is separated from the fibular collateral ligament by the popliteus tendon.","The lateral meniscus is smaller than the medial meniscus.","Correct — the lateral meniscus (like the medial) lies outside the synovial cavity, though inside the capsule.","The MEDIAL meniscus is torn more often because it's fixed to the tibial collateral ligament."]},"a128":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Anterior displacement of the femur on a flexed tibia is prevented by the PCL.</p><p>Same concept as the previous PCL question. Think of it in terms of the tibia: the PCL stops the tibia from sliding backward under the femur, which is the same as stopping the femur from sliding forward. Because it's taut in flexion, it matters most when the knee is bent and bearing weight. A positive posterior drawer test (the tibia sags or can be pushed backward with the knee at 90°) indicates PCL injury.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Anterior cruciate</b> — The ACL prevents POSTERIOR displacement of the femur (anterior tibial translation).</li><li><b>Fibular collateral</b> — The fibular collateral ligament resists varus (adduction) stress.</li><li><b>Patellar</b> — The patellar ligament transmits quadriceps pull to the tibial tuberosity; it isn't a cruciate restraint.</li><li><b>Tibial collateral</b> — The tibial collateral ligament resists valgus (abduction) stress.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> ACL stops the tibia going forward; PCL stops the tibia going backward.</p>","w":["The ACL prevents POSTERIOR displacement of the femur (anterior tibial translation).","The fibular collateral ligament resists varus (adduction) stress.","The patellar ligament transmits quadriceps pull to the tibial tuberosity; it isn't a cruciate restraint.","Correct — the PCL prevents anterior displacement of the femur on the tibia in flexion.","The tibial collateral ligament resists valgus (abduction) stress."]},"a129":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Popliteus \"unlocks\" the fully extended knee by laterally rotating the femur on the fixed tibia (or medially rotating the tibia).</p><p>At the end of extension the femur rotates medially on the tibia, tightening the ligaments and \"locking\" the knee so it can bear weight with little muscle effort (screw-home mechanism). To begin flexion, popliteus — running from the lateral femoral condyle to the posterior tibia above the soleal line — reverses this rotation, unlocking the joint. It also pulls the lateral meniscus backward, protecting it during flexion. Supplied by the tibial nerve.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Rectus femoris</b> — Rectus femoris flexes the hip and extends the knee; it doesn't unlock it.</li><li><b>Semimembranosus</b> — Semimembranosus flexes the knee once it is unlocked, but doesn't produce the unlocking rotation.</li><li><b>Gastrocnemius</b> — Gastrocnemius flexes the knee and plantar flexes the foot, but isn't the unlocking muscle.</li><li><b>Biceps femoris</b> — Biceps femoris flexes the knee and laterally rotates the leg, but doesn't unlock the extended joint.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Screw-home: medial rotation of the femur locks; popliteus laterally rotates the femur to unlock.</p>","w":["Rectus femoris flexes the hip and extends the knee; it doesn't unlock it.","Semimembranosus flexes the knee once it is unlocked, but doesn't produce the unlocking rotation.","Correct — popliteus unlocks the extended knee so flexion can begin.","Gastrocnemius flexes the knee and plantar flexes the foot, but isn't the unlocking muscle.","Biceps femoris flexes the knee and laterally rotates the leg, but doesn't unlock the extended joint."]},"a130":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Of the choices, only vastus lateralis arises from the femur; the others arise from the hip bone. All cross and help stabilize the knee.</p><p>The three vasti arise from the femoral shaft: vastus lateralis from the greater trochanter and lateral lip of the linea aspera, vastus medialis from the intertrochanteric line and medial linea aspera, vastus intermedius from the anterior shaft. They join the quadriceps tendon and act only on the knee, extending it and (especially vastus medialis) keeping the patella tracking. The hamstrings, sartorius and rectus femoris all arise from the coxal bone (ischial tuberosity, ASIS, AIIS).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Semimembranosus</b> — Semimembranosus arises from the ischial tuberosity.</li><li><b>Sartorius</b> — Sartorius arises from the anterior superior iliac spine.</li><li><b>Biceps femoris (long head)</b> — The long head of biceps femoris arises from the ischial tuberosity (only the short head is femoral).</li><li><b>Rectus femoris</b> — Rectus femoris arises from the anterior inferior iliac spine and above the acetabulum.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Vasti (and short head of biceps, popliteus, gastrocnemius) arise from the femur.</p>","w":["Correct — vastus lateralis arises from the femur and stabilizes the knee via the quadriceps tendon.","Semimembranosus arises from the ischial tuberosity.","Sartorius arises from the anterior superior iliac spine.","The long head of biceps femoris arises from the ischial tuberosity (only the short head is femoral).","Rectus femoris arises from the anterior inferior iliac spine and above the acetabulum."]},"a131":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> A blow to the LATERAL knee with the foot planted produces valgus stress → \"unhappy triad\": tibial collateral ligament, medial meniscus and ACL.</p><p>When a tackle strikes the outside of the knee, the joint is forced into valgus (abduction): the medial side opens up and the tibial collateral ligament stretches and tears. Because the medial meniscus is attached to that ligament, it tears with it, and the twisting component ruptures the ACL. The patient has medial knee tenderness, joint effusion (swelling at the front and in the suprapatellar bursa) and instability.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Ruptured fibular collateral ligament</b> — A lateral blow stresses the MEDIAL side; the fibular (lateral) collateral ligament is relaxed.</li><li><b>Tenderness on pressure along the fibular collateral ligament</b> — Tenderness would be along the MEDIAL (tibial) collateral ligament, not the fibular one.</li><li><b>Injury of the posterior cruciate ligament</b> — The cruciate in the triad is the ANTERIOR one; the PCL is usually spared.</li><li><b>Swelling on the back of the knee joint</b> — Effusion fills the front of the joint and suprapatellar bursa; swelling behind the knee suggests a Baker cyst or popliteal problem.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Unhappy triad: MCL + medial meniscus + ACL (some sources now emphasize the lateral meniscus, but exam answer = medial).</p>","w":["Correct — the medial meniscus tears as part of the unhappy triad from a lateral blow.","A lateral blow stresses the MEDIAL side; the fibular (lateral) collateral ligament is relaxed.","Tenderness would be along the MEDIAL (tibial) collateral ligament, not the fibular one.","The cruciate in the triad is the ANTERIOR one; the PCL is usually spared.","Effusion fills the front of the joint and suprapatellar bursa; swelling behind the knee suggests a Baker cyst or popliteal problem."]},"a132":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The fibular (lateral) collateral ligament is EXTRAcapsular — a cord separate from the capsule; menisci and cruciates are intracapsular but extrasynovial.</p><p>The knee's fibrous capsule encloses the menisci and cruciate ligaments, but the synovial membrane wraps around them so they lie outside the synovial cavity. An infection in that space (inside the capsule, outside the synovium) could involve the cruciates and menisci. The fibular collateral ligament runs from the lateral femoral epicondyle to the fibular head, separated from the capsule and lateral meniscus by the popliteus tendon and a bursa, so it lies entirely outside the joint capsule and is spared. (The tibial collateral ligament, by contrast, is a thickening of the capsule fused to the medial meniscus.)</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Anterior cruciate ligament</b> — The ACL is intracapsular but extrasynovial.</li><li><b>Posterior cruciate ligament</b> — The PCL is intracapsular but extrasynovial.</li><li><b>Lateral meniscus</b> — The lateral meniscus is intracapsular but extrasynovial.</li><li><b>Medial meniscus</b> — The medial meniscus is intracapsular but extrasynovial.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> LCL = extracapsular cord; MCL = flat band blended with the capsule and medial meniscus.</p>","w":["The ACL is intracapsular but extrasynovial.","The PCL is intracapsular but extrasynovial.","The lateral meniscus is intracapsular but extrasynovial.","Correct — the lateral collateral ligament lies outside the joint capsule, so it is spared.","The medial meniscus is intracapsular but extrasynovial."]},"a133":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The patella sits in the quadriceps tendon; rupture of the tendon above it abolishes knee extension.</p><p>The quadriceps tendon attaches to the upper border of the patella, and the patellar ligament continues from its lower border to the tibial tuberosity. The patella is a sesamoid bone that increases the lever arm of quadriceps. Rupture of the quadriceps tendon (usually in older adults, often with a palpable gap above the patella) or of the patellar ligament (younger athletes) disconnects the extensor mechanism, so the patient cannot actively extend the knee or do a straight-leg raise.</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Extensor mechanism = quadriceps tendon → patella → patellar ligament → tibial tuberosity; any break → can't extend the knee.</p>","w":["","","Correct — this is the patella; rupture of the quadriceps tendon above it prevents knee extension.","",""]},"a134":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The adductor tubercle receives the hamstring (ischial) part of adductor magnus.</p><p>The adductor tubercle is a small projection above the medial femoral epicondyle. The tendinous hamstring part of adductor magnus (from the ischial tuberosity, tibial nerve) inserts here, and the adductor hiatus lies just above it, where the femoral vessels pass into the popliteal fossa. Adductor magnus as a whole adducts the thigh; its anterior fibers help flex and its hamstring part extends the thigh. A fracture of the tubercle weakens these actions.</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> The adductor hiatus is the gap between the adductor magnus insertions, transmitting the femoral vessels to the popliteal fossa.</p>","w":["Correct — this is the adductor tubercle, insertion of the hamstring part of adductor magnus.","","","",""]},"a135":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The anterior tibial artery enters the anterior compartment through the gap above the interosseous membrane between the upper tibia and the neck of the fibula.</p><p>The popliteal artery divides at the lower border of popliteus into the anterior and posterior tibial arteries. The anterior tibial artery passes forward over the upper edge of the interosseous membrane, between the tibia and fibular neck, then descends with the deep fibular nerve to supply the anterior compartment (tibialis anterior, extensor hallucis longus, extensor digitorum longus, fibularis tertius) and becomes the dorsalis pedis artery. A stab through this gap can cut it, causing ischemia of the anterior compartment muscles.</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Anterior compartment muscles depend on the anterior tibial artery; they're also prone to compartment syndrome.</p>","w":["","Correct — this point marks the gap where the anterior tibial artery enters the anterior compartment.","","",""]},"a136":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The common fibular (peroneal) nerve winds around the neck of the fibula, where fractures or pressure injure it → foot drop.</p><p>The common fibular nerve leaves the popliteal fossa along the medial border of biceps femoris, passes behind the fibular head and wraps around the fibular neck, where it is covered only by skin and fascia. There it divides into the superficial fibular nerve (lateral compartment: fibularis longus and brevis, foot eversion; skin of the dorsum of the foot) and deep fibular nerve (anterior compartment: dorsiflexion and toe extension; skin of the first web space). Injury here (fibular head/neck fracture, tight casts, crossing legs) causes foot drop with a steppage gait and loss of eversion.</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Fibular neck = most commonly injured nerve in the lower limb; foot drop + numb dorsum of foot.</p>","w":["","","","","Correct — this is the fibular head/neck, where fracture injures the common fibular nerve."]},"a137":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Superficial fibular (peroneal) nerve → lateral compartment (fibularis longus and brevis) → eversion; deep fibular nerve → anterior compartment and extensor digitorum brevis.</p><p>After winding around the fibular neck, the common fibular nerve splits. The superficial branch descends in the lateral compartment supplying fibularis longus and brevis, which evert (and weakly plantar flex) the foot, then becomes cutaneous over the dorsum of the foot. The deep branch enters the anterior compartment with the anterior tibial artery, supplying tibialis anterior, extensor hallucis longus, extensor digitorum longus and fibularis tertius, then continues to the dorsum to supply extensor digitorum (and hallucis) brevis and the first web space.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Extensor hallucis longus</b> — Extensor hallucis longus is in the anterior compartment, supplied by the deep fibular nerve.</li><li><b>Extensor digitorum longus</b> — Extensor digitorum longus is in the anterior compartment (deep fibular nerve).</li><li><b>Peroneus tertius</b> — Fibularis (peroneus) tertius is an anterior compartment muscle (deep fibular nerve) despite its name.</li><li><b>Extensor digitorum brevis</b> — Extensor digitorum brevis on the dorsum of the foot is supplied by the deep fibular nerve.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Superficial fibular = eversion + dorsal foot skin; deep fibular = dorsiflexion + first web space skin.</p>","w":["Correct — the superficial fibular nerve supplies fibularis longus (and brevis).","Extensor hallucis longus is in the anterior compartment, supplied by the deep fibular nerve.","Extensor digitorum longus is in the anterior compartment (deep fibular nerve).","Fibularis (peroneus) tertius is an anterior compartment muscle (deep fibular nerve) despite its name.","Extensor digitorum brevis on the dorsum of the foot is supplied by the deep fibular nerve."]},"a138":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Tibialis anterior dorsiflexes AND inverts the foot (deep fibular nerve).</p><p>Tibialis anterior arises from the lateral tibia and interosseous membrane and inserts on the medial cuneiform and base of the first metatarsal. Its pull lifts the foot (dorsiflexion) and its medial insertion turns the sole inward (inversion). It is the main dorsiflexor; weakness causes foot slap/foot drop. Tibialis posterior also inverts but plantar flexes, and the fibular muscles evert.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Peroneus longus</b> — Fibularis longus plantar flexes and EVERTS the foot.</li><li><b>Peroneus brevis</b> — Fibularis brevis plantar flexes and EVERTS the foot.</li><li><b>Extensor digitorum longus</b> — Extensor digitorum longus dorsiflexes the foot and extends the toes, with slight eversion.</li><li><b>Peroneus tertius</b> — Fibularis tertius dorsiflexes and EVERTS the foot.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Inverters: tibialis anterior (with dorsiflexion) and tibialis posterior (with plantar flexion). Everters: fibulares.</p>","w":["Fibularis longus plantar flexes and EVERTS the foot.","Fibularis brevis plantar flexes and EVERTS the foot.","Correct — tibialis anterior dorsiflexes and inverts the foot.","Extensor digitorum longus dorsiflexes the foot and extends the toes, with slight eversion.","Fibularis tertius dorsiflexes and EVERTS the foot."]},"a139":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Swelling of the anterior (extensor) compartment muscles of the leg compresses the anterior tibial artery (anterior compartment syndrome).</p><p>The anterior compartment is a tight space bounded by the tibia, fibula, interosseous membrane and unyielding deep fascia. Trauma, bleeding or overuse makes its muscles swell; pressure rises until it occludes small vessels and compresses the anterior tibial artery and deep fibular nerve. Classic findings are severe pain out of proportion, pain on passive plantar flexion (stretching the anterior muscles), and later numbness of the first web space and foot drop. Treatment is urgent fasciotomy.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Popliteal</b> — The popliteal artery lies behind the knee, outside the anterior compartment.</li><li><b>Deep femoral</b> — The deep femoral (profunda femoris) artery supplies the thigh muscles.</li><li><b>Posterior tibial</b> — The posterior tibial artery runs in the deep posterior compartment.</li><li><b>Peroneal</b> — The fibular (peroneal) artery runs in the deep posterior compartment and supplies the lateral compartment by perforating branches.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Anterior compartment syndrome: anterior tibial artery + deep fibular nerve compressed → first web space numbness.</p>","w":["The popliteal artery lies behind the knee, outside the anterior compartment.","The deep femoral (profunda femoris) artery supplies the thigh muscles.","Correct — swollen extensor (anterior compartment) muscles compress the anterior tibial artery.","The posterior tibial artery runs in the deep posterior compartment.","The fibular (peroneal) artery runs in the deep posterior compartment and supplies the lateral compartment by perforating branches."]},"a140":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Tibialis posterior plantar flexes AND inverts the foot (tibial nerve).</p><p>Tibialis posterior lies deepest in the posterior compartment, arising from the interosseous membrane and adjacent tibia and fibula. Its tendon passes behind the medial malleolus and fans out to the navicular tuberosity, cuneiforms, cuboid and bases of metatarsals 2–4. It plantar flexes and inverts the foot and is the main dynamic support of the medial longitudinal arch. Dysfunction causes acquired flatfoot.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Extensor digitorum longus</b> — Extensor digitorum longus dorsiflexes the foot and extends the toes.</li><li><b>Tibialis anterior</b> — Tibialis anterior inverts but DORSIflexes the foot.</li><li><b>Peroneus longus</b> — Fibularis longus plantar flexes but EVERTS the foot.</li><li><b>Peroneus brevis</b> — Fibularis brevis plantar flexes but EVERTS the foot.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Behind the medial malleolus: \"Tom, Dick And Very Nervous Harry\" — Tibialis posterior, flexor Digitorum longus, Artery, Vein, Nerve, flexor Hallucis longus.</p>","w":["Extensor digitorum longus dorsiflexes the foot and extends the toes.","Tibialis anterior inverts but DORSIflexes the foot.","Correct — tibialis posterior plantar flexes and inverts the foot.","Fibularis longus plantar flexes but EVERTS the foot.","Fibularis brevis plantar flexes but EVERTS the foot."]},"a141":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Tibialis anterior is the prime dorsiflexor of the foot (deep fibular nerve); every other choice plantar flexes.</p><p>Dorsiflexion is produced by the anterior compartment muscles — tibialis anterior, extensor hallucis longus, extensor digitorum longus and fibularis tertius — all supplied by the deep fibular nerve. Tibialis anterior is by far the strongest. Its weakness causes foot drop: the toes catch during the swing phase, so patients lift the knee high (steppage gait) and the foot slaps down at heel strike. Muscles whose tendons pass behind the ankle (posterior compartment) or behind the lateral malleolus (fibulares) plantar flex.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Tibialis posterior</b> — Tibialis posterior (tibial nerve) plantar flexes and inverts.</li><li><b>Flexor digitorum longus</b> — Flexor digitorum longus (tibial nerve) plantar flexes the foot and flexes the lateral four toes.</li><li><b>Peroneus longus</b> — Fibularis longus (superficial fibular nerve) plantar flexes and everts.</li><li><b>Peroneus brevis</b> — Fibularis brevis (superficial fibular nerve) plantar flexes and everts.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Tendons in front of the ankle dorsiflex; tendons behind the malleoli plantar flex.</p>","w":["Tibialis posterior (tibial nerve) plantar flexes and inverts.","Flexor digitorum longus (tibial nerve) plantar flexes the foot and flexes the lateral four toes.","Correct — tibialis anterior dorsiflexes the foot.","Fibularis longus (superficial fibular nerve) plantar flexes and everts.","Fibularis brevis (superficial fibular nerve) plantar flexes and everts."]},"a142":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Inversion is done by tibialis posterior (strongest) and tibialis anterior; the fibulares and extensor digitorum longus evert.</p><p>Inversion and eversion occur mainly at the subtalar and transverse tarsal joints. Muscles that insert on the medial side of the foot invert it: tibialis posterior (navicular and plantar tarsals) and tibialis anterior (medial cuneiform and first metatarsal). Muscles inserting laterally evert it: fibularis longus, brevis and tertius, plus extensor digitorum longus. Losing tibialis posterior (tibial nerve) removes the main inverter and the dynamic support of the medial arch.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Peroneus longus</b> — Fibularis longus everts (and supports the transverse arch).</li><li><b>Peroneus brevis</b> — Fibularis brevis everts (inserts on the base of the 5th metatarsal).</li><li><b>Peroneus tertius</b> — Fibularis tertius everts and dorsiflexes.</li><li><b>Extensor digitorum longus</b> — Extensor digitorum longus dorsiflexes and everts slightly.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> \"Tibialis = inversion; fibularis = eversion.\"</p>","w":["Correct — tibialis posterior is the main inverter of the foot.","Fibularis longus everts (and supports the transverse arch).","Fibularis brevis everts (inserts on the base of the 5th metatarsal).","Fibularis tertius everts and dorsiflexes.","Extensor digitorum longus dorsiflexes and everts slightly."]},"a143":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The superficial fibular nerve supplies the lateral compartment: fibularis longus and fibularis brevis.</p><p>The common fibular nerve divides at the fibular neck. Its superficial branch runs in the lateral compartment between the fibulares, supplying fibularis longus and brevis (eversion), then pierces the deep fascia in the lower leg to supply skin of the lower lateral leg and most of the dorsum of the foot. Cutting it causes weak eversion and a numb dorsum, but dorsiflexion is preserved (deep fibular nerve).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Peroneus tertius</b> — Fibularis tertius is in the anterior compartment, supplied by the deep fibular nerve.</li><li><b>Flexor hallucis longus</b> — Flexor hallucis longus is in the deep posterior compartment, supplied by the tibial nerve.</li><li><b>Tibialis anterior</b> — Tibialis anterior is in the anterior compartment, supplied by the deep fibular nerve.</li><li><b>Tibialis posterior</b> — Tibialis posterior is in the deep posterior compartment, supplied by the tibial nerve.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Lateral compartment = fibularis longus + brevis = superficial fibular nerve = eversion.</p>","w":["Fibularis tertius is in the anterior compartment, supplied by the deep fibular nerve.","Correct — fibularis brevis is supplied by the superficial fibular nerve.","Flexor hallucis longus is in the deep posterior compartment, supplied by the tibial nerve.","Tibialis anterior is in the anterior compartment, supplied by the deep fibular nerve.","Tibialis posterior is in the deep posterior compartment, supplied by the tibial nerve."]},"a144":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Gastrocnemius crosses both the knee and ankle: it flexes the knee and plantar flexes the foot.</p><p>Gastrocnemius arises by two heads from the femoral condyles, so it crosses the back of the knee, then joins soleus in the calcaneal (Achilles) tendon. It therefore flexes the knee and plantar flexes the ankle, and is most effective at plantar flexion when the knee is extended (e.g., jumping, sprinting). Soleus arises below the knee (tibia and fibula), so it plantar flexes only. Both are supplied by the tibial nerve.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Tibialis posterior</b> — Tibialis posterior arises from the leg bones and doesn't cross the knee; it plantar flexes and inverts.</li><li><b>Soleus</b> — Soleus arises from the tibia and fibula below the knee, so it only plantar flexes.</li><li><b>Peroneus longus</b> — Fibularis longus arises from the fibula; it plantar flexes and everts but doesn't act on the knee.</li><li><b>Flexor digitorum longus</b> — Flexor digitorum longus arises from the tibia; it plantar flexes and flexes the toes.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Gastrocnemius = two-joint muscle (knee + ankle); soleus = one-joint (ankle only), the postural muscle.</p>","w":["Tibialis posterior arises from the leg bones and doesn't cross the knee; it plantar flexes and inverts.","Correct — gastrocnemius flexes the knee and plantar flexes the foot.","Soleus arises from the tibia and fibula below the knee, so it only plantar flexes.","Fibularis longus arises from the fibula; it plantar flexes and everts but doesn't act on the knee.","Flexor digitorum longus arises from the tibia; it plantar flexes and flexes the toes."]},"a145":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Extensor hallucis longus dorsiflexes the foot (and extends the big toe) and, because it inserts on the medial side, weakly inverts it.</p><p>EHL arises from the middle fibula and interosseous membrane and inserts on the distal phalanx of the great toe. Its tendon passes in front of the ankle (dorsiflexion) on the medial side of the foot (inversion). Among the anterior compartment muscles, tibialis anterior and EHL invert, while extensor digitorum longus and fibularis tertius, which insert laterally, evert. Fibularis longus and brevis pass behind the lateral malleolus, so they plantar flex and evert.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Peroneus longus</b> — Fibularis longus plantar flexes and everts (its tendon passes behind the lateral malleolus).</li><li><b>Peroneus brevis</b> — Fibularis brevis plantar flexes and everts.</li><li><b>Peroneus tertius</b> — Fibularis tertius dorsiflexes but EVERTS the foot.</li><li><b>Extensor digitorum longus</b> — Extensor digitorum longus dorsiflexes but EVERTS the foot.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Medial anterior tendons (TA, EHL) dorsiflex + invert; lateral anterior tendons (EDL, fibularis tertius) dorsiflex + evert. (The source's note that fibularis longus dorsiflexes is a slip — it plantar flexes.)</p>","w":["Fibularis longus plantar flexes and everts (its tendon passes behind the lateral malleolus).","Fibularis brevis plantar flexes and everts.","Fibularis tertius dorsiflexes but EVERTS the foot.","Correct — extensor hallucis longus dorsiflexes and inverts the foot.","Extensor digitorum longus dorsiflexes but EVERTS the foot."]},"a146":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The spring (plantar calcaneonavicular) ligament supports the head of the talus and is the key passive support of the medial longitudinal arch.</p><p>The spring ligament runs from the sustentaculum tali of the calcaneus to the navicular, forming a sling under the head of the talus, the keystone of the medial arch. When it stretches (with tibialis posterior dysfunction, which normally helps hold the arch), the talar head drops medially and downward, the arch collapses, and the forefoot drifts laterally (abducted) and everted — flat foot (pes planus). The long and short plantar ligaments support the LATERAL arch.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Calcaneofibular</b> — The calcaneofibular ligament is a lateral ankle ligament that resists inversion.</li><li><b>Anterior talofibular</b> — The anterior talofibular ligament is the lateral ankle ligament most often sprained with inversion.</li><li><b>Plantar calcaneocuboid (short plantar)</b> — The plantar calcaneocuboid (short plantar) ligament supports the lateral longitudinal arch.</li><li><b>Anterior tibiotalar</b> — The anterior tibiotalar ligament is part of the deltoid (medial) ankle ligament, not an arch support.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Medial arch supports: spring ligament, tibialis posterior, plantar aponeurosis, FHL. Lateral arch: long and short plantar ligaments, fibularis longus.</p>","w":["Correct — a stretched spring ligament lets the talar head drop, flattening the medial arch.","The calcaneofibular ligament is a lateral ankle ligament that resists inversion.","The anterior talofibular ligament is the lateral ankle ligament most often sprained with inversion.","The plantar calcaneocuboid (short plantar) ligament supports the lateral longitudinal arch.","The anterior tibiotalar ligament is part of the deltoid (medial) ankle ligament, not an arch support."]},"a147":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Lateral longitudinal arch = calcaneus + cuboid (keystone) + 4th and 5th metatarsals.</p><p>The foot has two longitudinal arches. The medial (higher, more flexible) arch is formed by the calcaneus, talus (keystone), navicular, three cuneiforms and the medial three metatarsals. The lateral (lower, flatter) arch is formed by the calcaneus, cuboid and lateral two metatarsals, and rests on the ground in standing. Flattening of the lateral arch after a fall points to displacement of its keystone, the cuboid.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Talus</b> — The talus (head) is the keystone of the MEDIAL arch.</li><li><b>Medial three metatarsals</b> — The medial three metatarsals belong to the medial arch.</li><li><b>Navicular</b> — The navicular belongs to the medial arch.</li><li><b>Cuneiform</b> — The cuneiforms belong to the medial arch.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Keystones: talar head (medial arch), cuboid (lateral arch).</p>","w":["The talus (head) is the keystone of the MEDIAL arch.","The medial three metatarsals belong to the medial arch.","The navicular belongs to the medial arch.","The cuneiforms belong to the medial arch.","Correct — the cuboid forms (and is the keystone of) the lateral longitudinal arch."]},"a148":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The groove on the plantar surface of the cuboid carries the fibularis (peroneus) longus tendon.</p><p>Fibularis longus arises from the upper lateral fibula, its tendon passes behind the lateral malleolus, under the fibular trochlea of the calcaneus, then turns medially into the groove on the underside of the cuboid and crosses the sole to insert on the medial cuneiform and base of the first metatarsal. This sling supports the lateral and transverse arches. Destroying the cuboid groove damages this tendon, weakening eversion and plantar flexion of the first metatarsal.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Flexor hallucis longus</b> — The flexor hallucis longus tendon grooves the posterior talus and the undersurface of the sustentaculum tali.</li><li><b>Peroneus brevis</b> — Fibularis brevis inserts on the tuberosity of the 5th metatarsal; it doesn't run under the cuboid.</li><li><b>Tibialis anterior</b> — Tibialis anterior runs on the front of the ankle to the medial cuneiform.</li><li><b>Tibialis posterior</b> — Tibialis posterior grooves the back of the medial malleolus and inserts on the navicular.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Fibularis longus + tibialis anterior both insert on the medial cuneiform/first metatarsal — a \"stirrup\" under the foot.</p>","w":["The flexor hallucis longus tendon grooves the posterior talus and the undersurface of the sustentaculum tali.","Fibularis brevis inserts on the tuberosity of the 5th metatarsal; it doesn't run under the cuboid.","Correct — the fibularis longus tendon runs in the cuboid groove.","Tibialis anterior runs on the front of the ankle to the medial cuneiform.","Tibialis posterior grooves the back of the medial malleolus and inserts on the navicular."]},"a149":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The flexor hallucis longus tendon runs in the groove under the sustentaculum tali (after grooving the posterior talus).</p><p>The sustentaculum tali is a shelf on the medial calcaneus that supports the talus. On its undersurface is a groove for the FHL tendon, which then continues to the base of the distal phalanx of the great toe. FHL is important in the push-off phase of walking and supports the medial arch. A fall onto the heels that fractures the sustentaculum can tear this tendon. Flexor digitorum longus crosses superficial to FHL higher in the sole, not in this groove.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Flexor digitorum brevis</b> — Flexor digitorum brevis is an intrinsic sole muscle from the calcaneal tuberosity; it has no tendon in this groove.</li><li><b>Flexor digitorum longus</b> — Flexor digitorum longus passes medial to/above the sustentaculum, not in the groove beneath it.</li><li><b>Flexor hallucis brevis</b> — Flexor hallucis brevis is an intrinsic muscle of the sole arising from the cuboid and lateral cuneiform.</li><li><b>Tibialis posterior</b> — Tibialis posterior grooves the back of the medial malleolus and inserts on the navicular.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> FHL grooves: posterior talus → under sustentaculum tali.</p>","w":["Flexor digitorum brevis is an intrinsic sole muscle from the calcaneal tuberosity; it has no tendon in this groove.","Flexor digitorum longus passes medial to/above the sustentaculum, not in the groove beneath it.","Flexor hallucis brevis is an intrinsic muscle of the sole arising from the cuboid and lateral cuneiform.","Correct — the flexor hallucis longus tendon runs in the groove under the sustentaculum tali.","Tibialis posterior grooves the back of the medial malleolus and inserts on the navicular."]},"a150":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The head of the talus is the keystone of the medial longitudinal arch.</p><p>The medial longitudinal arch is formed by the calcaneus, talus, navicular, cuneiforms and medial three metatarsals. The talar head sits at its summit, between the sustentaculum tali and the navicular, cradled by the spring ligament. A fracture of the talar head removes the keystone, so the medial arch can't bear load normally. The talus is also supplied tenuously, so talar fractures risk avascular necrosis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Transverse arch</b> — The transverse arch is formed by the cuneiforms, cuboid and metatarsal bases, not the talar head.</li><li><b>Lateral longitudinal arch</b> — The lateral arch's keystone is the cuboid.</li><li><b>Tendon of the peroneus longus</b> — The fibularis longus tendon supports the lateral and transverse arches; it isn't attached to the talar head.</li><li><b>Long plantar ligament</b> — The long plantar ligament supports the lateral arch from the calcaneus to the cuboid and metatarsals.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Talar head = medial keystone; cuboid = lateral keystone.</p>","w":["The transverse arch is formed by the cuneiforms, cuboid and metatarsal bases, not the talar head.","Correct — the talar head is the keystone of the medial longitudinal arch, which can't function normally.","The lateral arch's keystone is the cuboid.","The fibularis longus tendon supports the lateral and transverse arches; it isn't attached to the talar head.","The long plantar ligament supports the lateral arch from the calcaneus to the cuboid and metatarsals."]},"a151":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The keystone of the lateral longitudinal arch is the cuboid.</p><p>The lateral arch consists of the calcaneus, cuboid and 4th–5th metatarsals and is supported by the long and short plantar ligaments and the fibularis longus tendon running in the cuboid groove. The cuboid sits at the top of this low arch, so its fracture and displacement flatten the arch. The talar head plays the same keystone role medially.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Calcaneus</b> — The calcaneus is part of both arches (their posterior pillar), but not the keystone.</li><li><b>Head of the talus</b> — The head of the talus is the keystone of the MEDIAL arch.</li><li><b>Medial cuneiform</b> — The medial cuneiform is part of the medial and transverse arches.</li><li><b>Navicular bone</b> — The navicular is part of the medial arch.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Lateral arch keystone = cuboid.</p>","w":["The calcaneus is part of both arches (their posterior pillar), but not the keystone.","Correct — the cuboid is the keystone of the lateral longitudinal arch.","The head of the talus is the keystone of the MEDIAL arch.","The medial cuneiform is part of the medial and transverse arches.","The navicular is part of the medial arch."]},"a152":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The posterior process of the talus has a groove for the flexor hallucis longus tendon.</p><p>The posterior surface of the talar body has medial and lateral tubercles separated by a groove; the FHL tendon runs down this groove, then under the sustentaculum tali. A fracture of the posterior talus (or an os trigonum injury in dancers who point their feet) can damage or trap the FHL tendon, causing pain on great-toe flexion and plantar flexion.</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> FHL tendinopathy behind the ankle is \"dancer's tendinitis.\"</p>","w":["Correct — this is the talus, whose posterior groove holds the FHL tendon.","","","",""]},"a153":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The medial (first) cuneiform receives tibialis anterior and fibularis longus (and part of tibialis posterior).</p><p>Tibialis anterior inserts on the medial and plantar surface of the medial cuneiform and base of the first metatarsal; fibularis longus crosses the sole and inserts on the lateral side of the same two bones. Because these two muscles pull from opposite sides, they form a \"stirrup\" supporting the arch. A fracture of the medial cuneiform disrupts both insertions, weakening dorsiflexion/inversion (TA) and eversion/first-ray plantar flexion (FL).</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Medial cuneiform + base of 1st metatarsal = insertion of both tibialis anterior and fibularis longus.</p>","w":["","","","","Correct — this is the medial cuneiform, insertion of tibialis anterior and fibularis longus."]},"a154":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The spring ligament runs from the sustentaculum tali (calcaneus) to the navicular.</p><p>The plantar calcaneonavicular (spring) ligament fills the gap between the sustentaculum tali and the navicular, supporting the head of the talus. If the navicular is fractured, the ligament loses its anterior attachment, the talar head sags, and the medial longitudinal arch flattens. The navicular also receives tibialis posterior, another key arch support.</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Navicular tuberosity = tibialis posterior insertion; navicular + sustentaculum = spring ligament attachments.</p>","w":["","","","Correct — this is the navicular, the anterior attachment of the spring ligament.",""]},"a155":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The cuboid has a groove on its plantar surface for the fibularis (peroneus) longus tendon.</p><p>After passing behind the lateral malleolus and below the fibular trochlea, the fibularis longus tendon turns around the lateral edge of the cuboid and runs in its plantar groove, held by the long plantar ligament, before crossing to the medial cuneiform and first metatarsal. A cuboid fracture can damage the tendon in this groove, weakening eversion.</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cuboid groove = fibularis longus; sustentaculum groove = FHL.</p>","w":["","Correct — this is the cuboid, whose groove carries the fibularis longus tendon.","","",""]},"a156":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Deep fibular (peroneal) nerve = dorsiflexors (anterior compartment); compression → foot drop.</p><p>Sitting with crossed legs presses the common fibular nerve against the fibular neck, temporarily paralyzing it (and especially its deep branch). The deep fibular nerve supplies tibialis anterior, extensor hallucis longus, extensor digitorum longus and fibularis tertius, so dorsiflexion is lost: the toes drag and the patient has a steppage gait. Inversion is only partly affected because tibialis posterior (tibial nerve) still inverts.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Plantar flexion of the foot</b> — Plantar flexion is done by the posterior compartment (tibial nerve) and helped by the fibulares (superficial fibular nerve).</li><li><b>Abduction of the toes</b> — Abduction of the toes is done by dorsal interossei and abductors, supplied by the lateral and medial plantar nerves (tibial).</li><li><b>Adduction of the toes</b> — Adduction of the toes is done by plantar interossei (lateral plantar nerve).</li><li><b>Inversion of the foot</b> — Inversion is mainly tibialis posterior (tibial nerve); only the tibialis anterior component is lost.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Crossed legs, tight casts and fibular neck fractures → common/deep fibular palsy → foot drop.</p>","w":["Plantar flexion is done by the posterior compartment (tibial nerve) and helped by the fibulares (superficial fibular nerve).","Correct — deep fibular nerve compression paralyzes the dorsiflexors.","Abduction of the toes is done by dorsal interossei and abductors, supplied by the lateral and medial plantar nerves (tibial).","Adduction of the toes is done by plantar interossei (lateral plantar nerve).","Inversion is mainly tibialis posterior (tibial nerve); only the tibialis anterior component is lost."]},"a157":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hip flexion (iliopsoas, rectus femoris; femoral nerve and L1–L3) uses upper lumbar segments; hip extension by the hamstrings (sciatic, L5–S2) uses lower segments.</p><p>Spinal levels for lower-limb movements: hip flexion L2–L3, knee extension L3–L4, ankle dorsiflexion L4–L5, hip extension and knee flexion L5–S1(S2), ankle plantar flexion S1–S2. If hip flexion is strong but the hamstrings are weak, the upper lumbar segments are working and the lesion lies lower, around L5. (The source's \"quadratus femoris\" should read the femoral-nerve muscles — iliopsoas/rectus femoris/quadriceps.)</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>T12</b> — A T12 lesion would affect almost all lower-limb segments below, including the hip flexors.</li><li><b>L1</b> — An L1 lesion would weaken hip flexion (L1–L3) too.</li><li><b>L3</b> — An L3 lesion would weaken hip flexion and knee extension.</li><li><b>S5</b> — S5 serves only perianal skin and sphincters; the hamstrings would be spared.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Myotomes: L2–L3 hip flexion, L3–L4 knee extension, L4–L5 dorsiflexion, L5–S1 hip extension/knee flexion, S1–S2 plantar flexion.</p>","w":["A T12 lesion would affect almost all lower-limb segments below, including the hip flexors.","An L1 lesion would weaken hip flexion (L1–L3) too.","An L3 lesion would weaken hip flexion and knee extension.","Correct — an L5-level lesion spares the hip flexors but weakens the hamstrings.","S5 serves only perianal skin and sphincters; the hamstrings would be spared."]},"a158":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Tibial nerve = posterior compartment (plantar flexion, toe flexion) + sole of the foot (via the plantar nerves).</p><p>The tibial nerve runs down the middle of the popliteal fossa, deep to the popliteal vessels, and is stretched or torn in posterior knee dislocations (along with the popliteal artery). It supplies gastrocnemius, soleus, plantaris, popliteus, tibialis posterior, FDL and FHL, then passes behind the medial malleolus to divide into medial and lateral plantar nerves, which supply the intrinsic foot muscles and skin of the sole. Injury abolishes plantar flexion and toe flexion and numbs the sole.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Saphenous nerve</b> — The saphenous nerve is purely sensory to the medial leg and foot; it supplies no muscles.</li><li><b>Deep peroneal nerve</b> — The deep fibular nerve supplies the dorsiflexors and first web space.</li><li><b>Superficial peroneal nerve</b> — The superficial fibular nerve supplies the evertors and dorsum of the foot.</li><li><b>Common peroneal nerve</b> — The common fibular nerve supplies the anterior and lateral compartments; injury causes foot drop, not loss of plantar flexion.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Knee dislocation: check the popliteal artery (ankle-brachial index) and tibial/common fibular nerves.</p>","w":["The saphenous nerve is purely sensory to the medial leg and foot; it supplies no muscles.","Correct — tibial nerve injury removes plantar flexion, toe flexion and sole sensation.","The deep fibular nerve supplies the dorsiflexors and first web space.","The superficial fibular nerve supplies the evertors and dorsum of the foot.","The common fibular nerve supplies the anterior and lateral compartments; injury causes foot drop, not loss of plantar flexion."]},"a159":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The medial sole is supplied by the medial plantar nerve, a branch of the tibial nerve.</p><p>Behind the medial malleolus the tibial nerve divides into the medial plantar nerve (like the median nerve in the hand: abductor hallucis, flexor digitorum brevis, flexor hallucis brevis, first lumbrical; skin of the medial sole and medial 3½ toes) and the lateral plantar nerve (like the ulnar nerve: most other intrinsics; skin of the lateral sole and lateral 1½ toes). Combined motor and sensory loss on the medial plantar foot therefore points to the tibial nerve (or its medial plantar branch, e.g., tarsal tunnel syndrome).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Common peroneal</b> — The common fibular nerve supplies the anterior and lateral leg and dorsum of the foot.</li><li><b>Superficial peroneal</b> — The superficial fibular nerve supplies the lateral leg muscles and the skin of the dorsum of the foot.</li><li><b>Deep peroneal</b> — The deep fibular nerve supplies the anterior compartment and the first dorsal web space.</li><li><b>Sural</b> — The sural nerve supplies skin of the lateral foot and little toe, with no muscles.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Medial plantar ≈ median nerve; lateral plantar ≈ ulnar nerve.</p>","w":["The common fibular nerve supplies the anterior and lateral leg and dorsum of the foot.","Correct — the tibial nerve (medial plantar branch) supplies the medial sole and its muscles.","The superficial fibular nerve supplies the lateral leg muscles and the skin of the dorsum of the foot.","The deep fibular nerve supplies the anterior compartment and the first dorsal web space.","The sural nerve supplies skin of the lateral foot and little toe, with no muscles."]},"a160":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The femoral nerve (L2–L4) supplies the quadriceps (including vastus lateralis), sartorius, pectineus and iliacus, and the skin of the anterior thigh and medial leg.</p><p>The femoral nerve enters the thigh lateral to the femoral artery under the inguinal ligament and quickly branches to the anterior compartment. Loss of quadriceps makes knee extension weak, the patellar reflex is lost, and the knee may buckle when walking. Sensory loss affects the anterior thigh and, via the saphenous nerve, the medial leg and foot. (The source's \"quadratus femoris\" means quadriceps femoris.)</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Paralysis of the psoas major muscle</b> — Psoas major is supplied directly by ventral rami of L1–L3, not the femoral nerve (iliacus is femoral).</li><li><b>Loss of skin sensation on the lateral side of the foot</b> — The lateral side of the foot is supplied by the sural nerve.</li><li><b>Loss of skin sensation over the greater trochanter</b> — The skin over the greater trochanter is supplied by the iliohypogastric (lateral cutaneous branch) and superior clunial nerves.</li><li><b>Paralysis of the tensor fasciae latae</b> — Tensor fasciae latae is supplied by the superior gluteal nerve.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Femoral nerve lesion: weak knee extension, absent patellar reflex, numb anterior thigh and medial leg.</p>","w":["Psoas major is supplied directly by ventral rami of L1–L3, not the femoral nerve (iliacus is femoral).","The lateral side of the foot is supplied by the sural nerve.","The skin over the greater trochanter is supplied by the iliohypogastric (lateral cutaneous branch) and superior clunial nerves.","Correct — the femoral nerve supplies vastus lateralis, which would be paralyzed.","Tensor fasciae latae is supplied by the superior gluteal nerve."]},"a161":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The invertors are tibialis anterior (deep fibular nerve) and tibialis posterior (tibial nerve); losing inversion needs both nerves.</p><p>Inversion turns the sole medially at the subtalar and transverse tarsal joints. Tibialis posterior is the strongest invertor (with plantar flexion) and tibialis anterior inverts with dorsiflexion; extensor hallucis longus helps a little. Tibialis anterior and EHL are anterior compartment muscles (deep fibular nerve); tibialis posterior is a deep posterior compartment muscle (tibial nerve). So a complete loss of inversion requires deep fibular + tibial nerve damage. (The source says \"evertors\" — it means invertors.)</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Superficial and deep peroneal</b> — The deep fibular nerve fits, but the superficial fibular nerve supplies the EVERTORS (fibularis longus and brevis).</li><li><b>Superficial peroneal and tibial</b> — The tibial nerve fits, but the superficial fibular nerve supplies evertors.</li><li><b>Medial and lateral plantar</b> — The medial and lateral plantar nerves supply intrinsic foot muscles, not the invertors.</li><li><b>Obturator and tibial</b> — The tibial nerve fits, but the obturator nerve supplies thigh adductors.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Inversion = tibialis anterior (deep fibular) + tibialis posterior (tibial). Eversion = fibulares (superficial fibular).</p>","w":["The deep fibular nerve fits, but the superficial fibular nerve supplies the EVERTORS (fibularis longus and brevis).","Correct — the deep fibular (tibialis anterior) and tibial (tibialis posterior) nerves supply the invertors.","The tibial nerve fits, but the superficial fibular nerve supplies evertors.","The medial and lateral plantar nerves supply intrinsic foot muscles, not the invertors.","The tibial nerve fits, but the obturator nerve supplies thigh adductors."]},"a162":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Deep fibular nerve: anterior compartment (dorsiflexion) + the skin of the first dorsal web space (adjacent sides of toes 1 and 2).</p><p>The deep fibular nerve runs down the anterior compartment with the anterior tibial artery, supplying the dorsiflexors, then passes under the extensor retinaculum onto the dorsum of the foot. Its lateral branch supplies extensor digitorum and hallucis brevis; its medial branch ends in the first web space, supplying the skin between the great and second toes. This small sensory patch plus foot drop is the signature of a deep fibular nerve lesion (e.g., anterior compartment syndrome).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Superficial peroneal</b> — The superficial fibular nerve supplies most of the dorsum of the foot EXCEPT the first web space, plus the evertors.</li><li><b>Lateral plantar</b> — The lateral plantar nerve supplies the lateral sole and most intrinsic foot muscles.</li><li><b>Sural</b> — The sural nerve supplies the lateral foot and little toe.</li><li><b>Tibial</b> — The tibial nerve supplies the plantar flexors and the sole, not the dorsum.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> First web space numbness = deep fibular nerve.</p>","w":["The superficial fibular nerve supplies most of the dorsum of the foot EXCEPT the first web space, plus the evertors.","The lateral plantar nerve supplies the lateral sole and most intrinsic foot muscles.","Correct — the deep fibular nerve supplies the dorsiflexors and the first web space.","The sural nerve supplies the lateral foot and little toe.","The tibial nerve supplies the plantar flexors and the sole, not the dorsum."]},"a163":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The superficial fibular nerve pierces the deep fascia in the lower third of the leg and runs subcutaneously on the anterolateral leg and dorsum of the foot.</p><p>After supplying fibularis longus and brevis, the superficial fibular nerve becomes cutaneous, emerging through the deep fascia above the ankle and passing SUPERFICIAL to the extensor retinaculum onto the dorsum of the foot. Its position in the subcutaneous tissue of the anterolateral leg makes it the structure lost when the skin is avulsed there. Deeper structures (deep fibular nerve, anterior tibial/dorsalis pedis artery, extensor tendons) pass deep to the retinaculum and are protected by fascia.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Deep peroneal nerve</b> — The deep fibular nerve lies deep in the anterior compartment against the interosseous membrane.</li><li><b>Extensor digitorum longus muscle tendon</b> — The extensor digitorum longus tendon passes deep to the extensor retinaculum.</li><li><b>Dorsalis pedis artery</b> — The dorsalis pedis artery lies deep on the dorsum of the foot, under the extensor tendons.</li><li><b>Great saphenous vein</b> — The great saphenous vein runs on the MEDIAL leg, in front of the medial malleolus.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Superficial fibular nerve emerges about 10–12 cm above the lateral malleolus — at risk in lateral ankle surgery and skin avulsions.</p>","w":["The deep fibular nerve lies deep in the anterior compartment against the interosseous membrane.","The extensor digitorum longus tendon passes deep to the extensor retinaculum.","The dorsalis pedis artery lies deep on the dorsum of the foot, under the extensor tendons.","The great saphenous vein runs on the MEDIAL leg, in front of the medial malleolus.","Correct — the superficial fibular nerve runs superficially on the anterolateral leg and ankle."]},"a164":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The common fibular (peroneal) nerve wraps around the neck of the fibula and is injured by fibular head/neck fractures.</p><p>The common fibular nerve leaves the popliteal fossa along biceps femoris, crosses behind the fibular head and curves around the fibular neck within fibularis longus, where it divides into superficial and deep branches. At the neck it lies directly on bone under skin, making it the most commonly injured nerve of the lower limb. Injury causes foot drop (deep branch), loss of eversion (superficial branch) and numbness of the anterolateral leg and dorsum of the foot.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Sciatic</b> — The sciatic nerve lies in the posterior thigh; it divides above the knee.</li><li><b>Tibial</b> — The tibial nerve runs down the middle of the popliteal fossa and into the posterior leg, away from the fibular neck.</li><li><b>Deep peroneal</b> — The deep fibular nerve is a branch formed AT the neck; a fracture here injures the parent common fibular nerve, affecting both branches.</li><li><b>Superficial peroneal</b> — The superficial fibular nerve is likewise a branch; the classic answer is the common fibular nerve.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Fibular neck = common fibular nerve → foot drop + loss of eversion.</p>","w":["The sciatic nerve lies in the posterior thigh; it divides above the knee.","The tibial nerve runs down the middle of the popliteal fossa and into the posterior leg, away from the fibular neck.","Correct — the common fibular nerve winds around the fibular neck and is damaged by its fracture.","The deep fibular nerve is a branch formed AT the neck; a fracture here injures the parent common fibular nerve, affecting both branches.","The superficial fibular nerve is likewise a branch; the classic answer is the common fibular nerve."]},"a165":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Common fibular nerve injury paralyzes the anterior (deep fibular) and lateral (superficial fibular) compartments — e.g., extensor hallucis longus.</p><p>Because the common fibular nerve gives both the deep and superficial fibular nerves, its injury paralyzes the dorsiflexors (tibialis anterior, EHL, EDL, fibularis tertius) and the evertors (fibularis longus and brevis). The posterior compartment muscles — gastrocnemius, soleus, popliteus, FDL, FHL, tibialis posterior — are supplied by the tibial nerve and keep working, so plantar flexion and inversion (via tibialis posterior) remain.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Gastrocnemius</b> — Gastrocnemius is supplied by the tibial nerve.</li><li><b>Popliteus</b> — Popliteus is supplied by the tibial nerve.</li><li><b>Flexor digitorum longus</b> — Flexor digitorum longus is supplied by the tibial nerve.</li><li><b>Tibialis posterior</b> — Tibialis posterior is supplied by the tibial nerve.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Fibular nerve palsy: can't dorsiflex or evert; can still plantar flex and invert.</p>","w":["Gastrocnemius is supplied by the tibial nerve.","Popliteus is supplied by the tibial nerve.","Correct — extensor hallucis longus (deep fibular nerve) is paralyzed after common fibular nerve injury.","Flexor digitorum longus is supplied by the tibial nerve.","Tibialis posterior is supplied by the tibial nerve."]},"a166":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The fibular (lateral) collateral ligament resists ADduction (varus) of the extended knee; the tibial (medial) collateral resists abduction (valgus).</p><p>The lateral collateral ligament runs from the lateral femoral epicondyle to the fibular head. If the head is fractured or the ligament torn, the leg can be pushed abnormally medially at the knee (varus) when tested with the knee straight — the varus stress test. Abnormal abduction (valgus opening) indicates a medial collateral tear. Cruciate injuries produce anteroposterior instability instead.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Abnormal passive abduction of the extended leg</b> — Abnormal passive abduction (valgus) of the extended leg indicates a torn MEDIAL collateral ligament.</li><li><b>Anterior displacement of the femur on the tibia</b> — Anterior displacement of the femur on the tibia indicates a PCL tear.</li><li><b>Posterior displacement of the femur on the tibia</b> — Posterior displacement of the femur on the tibia indicates an ACL tear.</li><li><b>Maximal flexion of the leg</b> — Maximal flexion isn't limited by the LCL; the collateral ligaments are tight in extension and relax in flexion.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Varus stress → LCL; valgus stress → MCL. Both are taut in extension.</p>","w":["Abnormal passive abduction (valgus) of the extended leg indicates a torn MEDIAL collateral ligament.","Correct — a torn LCL allows abnormal passive adduction (varus) of the extended leg.","Anterior displacement of the femur on the tibia indicates a PCL tear.","Posterior displacement of the femur on the tibia indicates an ACL tear.","Maximal flexion isn't limited by the LCL; the collateral ligaments are tight in extension and relax in flexion."]},"a167":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The anterior tibial artery passes forward over the upper interosseous membrane next to the neck of the fibula, so fibular neck fractures can injure it.</p><p>The popliteal artery ends by dividing into the posterior tibial artery and the anterior tibial artery. The anterior tibial artery immediately passes forward through the gap between the tibia and fibular neck above the interosseous membrane, entering the anterior compartment, where it joins the deep fibular nerve. A fracture of the fibular head/neck can damage both, threatening the anterior compartment's blood supply.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Popliteal</b> — The popliteal artery lies higher, deep in the popliteal fossa behind the knee.</li><li><b>Posterior tibial</b> — The posterior tibial artery continues down the posterior compartment, away from the fibular neck.</li><li><b>Peroneal</b> — The fibular (peroneal) artery branches from the posterior tibial artery lower down, running along the back of the fibula.</li><li><b>Lateral inferior genicular</b> — The lateral inferior genicular artery runs around the lateral knee near the joint line; it's part of the knee anastomosis but not the classic vessel at the fibular neck.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Fibular neck: common fibular nerve + anterior tibial artery nearby.</p>","w":["The popliteal artery lies higher, deep in the popliteal fossa behind the knee.","The posterior tibial artery continues down the posterior compartment, away from the fibular neck.","Correct — the anterior tibial artery passes close to the fibular neck and can be damaged.","The fibular (peroneal) artery branches from the posterior tibial artery lower down, running along the back of the fibula.","The lateral inferior genicular artery runs around the lateral knee near the joint line; it's part of the knee anastomosis but not the classic vessel at the fibular neck."]},"a168":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Damage to the anterior tibial artery (or swelling in the tight anterior compartment) causes anterior tibial compartment syndrome.</p><p>The anterior compartment is enclosed by bone, the interosseous membrane and tough fascia, and receives blood mainly from the anterior tibial artery. If that artery is injured near the fibular neck, or swelling raises compartment pressure, the dorsiflexor muscles become ischemic and can necrose. Patients have severe pain, pain on passive plantar flexion, a tense anterior leg, then foot drop and first-web-space numbness from deep fibular nerve ischemia. Urgent fasciotomy is needed.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Ischemia in the gastrocnemius</b> — Gastrocnemius is supplied by sural branches of the popliteal artery, which are spared.</li><li><b>Loss of plantar flexion</b> — Plantar flexion depends on the posterior compartment (tibial nerve, posterior tibial artery), not involved here.</li><li><b>Trendelenburg’s sign</b> — Trendelenburg sign reflects weak gluteus medius/minimus (superior gluteal nerve).</li><li><b>Flat foot</b> — Flat foot is collapse of the medial longitudinal arch (spring ligament, tibialis posterior).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Compartment syndrome: pain out of proportion + pain on passive stretch — pulses may still be present.</p>","w":["Gastrocnemius is supplied by sural branches of the popliteal artery, which are spared.","Plantar flexion depends on the posterior compartment (tibial nerve, posterior tibial artery), not involved here.","Trendelenburg sign reflects weak gluteus medius/minimus (superior gluteal nerve).","Correct — injury to the anterior tibial artery produces anterior tibial compartment syndrome.","Flat foot is collapse of the medial longitudinal arch (spring ligament, tibialis posterior)."]},"a169":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Adductor magnus has dual innervation: obturator nerve (adductor part) + tibial division of the sciatic nerve (hamstring part). Both must be cut to paralyze it completely.</p><p>Muscles with two nerve supplies are classic exam traps. Adductor magnus's adductor part (from the ischiopubic ramus to the linea aspera) is supplied by the obturator nerve; its hamstring part (from the ischial tuberosity to the adductor tubercle) is supplied by the tibial division of the sciatic nerve. Transecting both nerves therefore paralyzes it completely. Pectineus (femoral ± obturator) and the short head of biceps (common fibular division) would not be completely paralyzed.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Rectus femoris</b> — Rectus femoris is supplied by the femoral nerve.</li><li><b>Biceps femoris, short head</b> — The short head of biceps femoris is supplied by the common fibular (peroneal) division of the sciatic nerve.</li><li><b>Pectineus</b> — Pectineus is supplied mainly by the femoral nerve (sometimes also the obturator), so it isn't completely paralyzed.</li><li><b>Sartorius</b> — Sartorius is supplied by the femoral nerve.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Dual-nerve muscles: adductor magnus (obturator + tibial), pectineus (femoral ± obturator), biceps femoris (tibial + common fibular), brachialis (musculocutaneous + radial).</p>","w":["Rectus femoris is supplied by the femoral nerve.","The short head of biceps femoris is supplied by the common fibular (peroneal) division of the sciatic nerve.","Pectineus is supplied mainly by the femoral nerve (sometimes also the obturator), so it isn't completely paralyzed.","Correct — adductor magnus is supplied by both the obturator and tibial (sciatic) nerves, so cutting both paralyzes it completely.","Sartorius is supplied by the femoral nerve."]},"a170":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Adductor longus is supplied only by the obturator nerve, so it is completely paralyzed when the obturator nerve is cut.</p><p>The obturator nerve (L2–L4) leaves the pelvis through the obturator canal and supplies the medial compartment of the thigh: adductor longus, adductor brevis, gracilis, obturator externus and the adductor part of adductor magnus, plus skin of the medial thigh. Muscles with a second nerve supply survive partially: pectineus (femoral) and adductor magnus (tibial). Adductor longus has no backup, so it's completely paralyzed.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Pectineus</b> — Pectineus is supplied mainly by the femoral nerve, so it keeps function.</li><li><b>Adductor magnus</b> — Adductor magnus also receives the tibial division of the sciatic nerve.</li><li><b>Biceps femoris</b> — Biceps femoris is supplied by the sciatic nerve (both divisions).</li><li><b>Semimembranosus</b> — Semimembranosus is supplied by the tibial division of the sciatic nerve.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Obturator nerve palsy (e.g., pelvic surgery, childbirth) → weak adduction, numb medial thigh, leg swings outward when walking.</p>","w":["Pectineus is supplied mainly by the femoral nerve, so it keeps function.","Adductor magnus also receives the tibial division of the sciatic nerve.","Correct — adductor longus depends only on the obturator nerve, so it is completely paralyzed.","Biceps femoris is supplied by the sciatic nerve (both divisions).","Semimembranosus is supplied by the tibial division of the sciatic nerve."]},"a171":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> A clot from the leg veins travels through the IVC and right heart and lodges in the pulmonary arteries (pulmonary embolism).</p><p>Venous blood from the lower limb returns via the femoral, external and common iliac veins to the inferior vena cava, then to the right atrium and right ventricle, and out through the pulmonary trunk. The pulmonary arterial tree branches into ever-smaller vessels, so a deep-vein thrombus that breaks off becomes trapped there — the first vascular bed it meets. Large emboli lodge at the bifurcation (saddle embolus) and can cause sudden death; smaller ones cause pleuritic pain, dyspnea and hypoxia.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Tributaries of the renal veins</b> — Renal vein tributaries are on the systemic venous side downstream of the kidney; a leg clot never passes through them.</li><li><b>Branches of the coronary arteries</b> — Coronary arteries are systemic arteries; a venous clot reaches them only through a right-to-left shunt (paradoxical embolism).</li><li><b>Sinusoids of the liver</b> — Hepatic sinusoids are part of the portal circulation from the gut, not the route from leg veins.</li><li><b>Tributaries of the pulmonary veins</b> — Pulmonary VEINS come after the pulmonary capillaries, which filter out the embolus first.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Venous emboli → lungs; arterial (left-heart) emboli → brain, gut, limbs, kidneys.</p>","w":["Renal vein tributaries are on the systemic venous side downstream of the kidney; a leg clot never passes through them.","Coronary arteries are systemic arteries; a venous clot reaches them only through a right-to-left shunt (paradoxical embolism).","Hepatic sinusoids are part of the portal circulation from the gut, not the route from leg veins.","Pulmonary VEINS come after the pulmonary capillaries, which filter out the embolus first.","Correct — leg-vein emboli first lodge in branches of the pulmonary arteries."]},"a172":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The great saphenous vein runs in superficial fascia — anterior to the medial malleolus, posterior to the medial condyles of the knee, superficial to the fascia lata — and drains into the femoral vein at the saphenous opening.</p><p>The great saphenous vein starts at the medial end of the dorsal venous arch of the foot, passes in front of the medial malleolus (where it's used for venous cutdowns), ascends along the medial leg with the saphenous nerve, crosses behind the medial condyles of the tibia and femur, then runs up the medial thigh in the superficial fascia. It pierces the cribriform fascia of the saphenous opening to join the femoral vein about 3–4 cm below and lateral to the pubic tubercle. Its length, superficial position and valves make it the usual conduit for coronary artery bypass grafts (reversed so valves don't block flow).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Posterior to the medial malleolus</b> — It runs ANTERIOR to the medial malleolus; the posterior tibial vessels and tibial nerve run behind it.</li><li><b>Into the popliteal vein</b> — The SMALL saphenous vein drains into the popliteal vein; the great saphenous drains into the femoral vein.</li><li><b>Anterior to the medial condyles of the tibia and femur</b> — It passes POSTERIOR to the medial condyles of the tibia and femur at the knee.</li><li><b>Along with the femoral artery</b> — It isn't a venae comitans; the femoral artery is accompanied by the deep femoral vein.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Great saphenous: in front of the medial malleolus, behind the knee condyles, into the femoral vein.</p>","w":["It runs ANTERIOR to the medial malleolus; the posterior tibial vessels and tibial nerve run behind it.","The SMALL saphenous vein drains into the popliteal vein; the great saphenous drains into the femoral vein.","It passes POSTERIOR to the medial condyles of the tibia and femur at the knee.","Correct — the great saphenous vein runs superficial to the fascia lata.","It isn't a venae comitans; the femoral artery is accompanied by the deep femoral vein."]},"a173":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The dorsalis pedis artery (continuation of the anterior tibial artery) gives the lateral and medial tarsal arteries, the arcuate artery and the deep plantar artery; ligating it reduces flow in its branches.</p><p>The anterior tibial artery becomes the dorsalis pedis as it crosses the ankle between the extensor hallucis longus and extensor digitorum longus tendons (where its pulse is felt). Distal to that it gives medial and lateral tarsal branches, then the arcuate artery (which gives dorsal metatarsal arteries) and the deep plantar artery that joins the plantar arch. Ligating the dorsalis pedis proximal to these branches reduces flow in all of them, though anastomoses with the plantar arch eventually help.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Ischemia in the peroneus longus muscle</b> — Fibularis longus is supplied by the anterior tibial and fibular arteries in the leg, above the ligation.</li><li><b>Aneurysm in the plantar arterial arch</b> — The plantar arch is fed mainly by the lateral plantar artery; ligation upstream wouldn't create an aneurysm.</li><li><b>Low blood pressure in the anterior tibial artery</b> — The anterior tibial artery is upstream of the ligature, so its pressure doesn't fall (if anything it rises).</li><li><b>High blood pressure in the arcuate artery</b> — The arcuate artery is downstream of the ligature, so its pressure falls, not rises.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Dorsalis pedis pulse: lateral to the EHL tendon on the dorsum of the foot.</p>","w":["Fibularis longus is supplied by the anterior tibial and fibular arteries in the leg, above the ligation.","The plantar arch is fed mainly by the lateral plantar artery; ligation upstream wouldn't create an aneurysm.","Correct — the medial tarsal artery branches from the dorsalis pedis, so its flow drops.","The anterior tibial artery is upstream of the ligature, so its pressure doesn't fall (if anything it rises).","The arcuate artery is downstream of the ligature, so its pressure falls, not rises."]},"a174":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The small (lesser) saphenous vein runs up the back of the leg with the sural nerve and drains into the popliteal vein.</p><p>The small saphenous vein begins at the lateral end of the dorsal venous arch, passes behind the lateral malleolus, ascends in the midline of the calf in superficial fascia alongside the sural nerve, and pierces the deep fascia in the popliteal fossa to join the popliteal vein. It's the only superficial vein ending in the popliteal vein; the great saphenous vein ends in the femoral vein.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Posterior tibial vein</b> — The posterior tibial vein is a deep vein (venae comitans) that joins with the anterior tibial veins to form the popliteal vein.</li><li><b>Anterior tibial vein</b> — The anterior tibial vein is a deep vein, not superficial.</li><li><b>Peroneal vein</b> — The fibular (peroneal) vein is a deep vein that drains into the posterior tibial vein.</li><li><b>Great saphenous vein</b> — The great saphenous vein drains into the femoral vein at the saphenous opening.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Small saphenous → popliteal vein (with the sural nerve); great saphenous → femoral vein (with the saphenous nerve).</p>","w":["The posterior tibial vein is a deep vein (venae comitans) that joins with the anterior tibial veins to form the popliteal vein.","The anterior tibial vein is a deep vein, not superficial.","The fibular (peroneal) vein is a deep vein that drains into the posterior tibial vein.","The great saphenous vein drains into the femoral vein at the saphenous opening.","Correct — the lesser saphenous vein is the superficial vein ending in the popliteal vein."]},"a175":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> If the proximal popliteal artery is blocked, blood reaches the leg through the genicular anastomosis fed from above — notably the descending branch of the lateral circumflex femoral artery.</p><p>Around the knee, branches from above (descending branch of the lateral circumflex femoral artery, descending genicular artery from the femoral artery) connect with the five genicular branches of the popliteal artery and with recurrent branches from the tibial arteries below. When the popliteal artery is occluded proximally, blood flows from the femoral system through these anastomoses into the lower popliteal artery and on to the tibial arteries and foot. Branches of the popliteal artery itself are below the block and can't bring in new blood.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Anterior tibial</b> — The anterior tibial artery is a terminal branch of the popliteal artery, downstream of the clot.</li><li><b>Posterior tibial</b> — The posterior tibial artery is also a terminal branch of the popliteal artery.</li><li><b>Peroneal</b> — The fibular artery is a branch of the posterior tibial artery, further downstream.</li><li><b>Superior medial genicular</b> — The superior medial genicular artery is a branch of the popliteal artery, part of the receiving end of the anastomosis.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Collateral routes always come from vessels arising ABOVE the block.</p>","w":["The anterior tibial artery is a terminal branch of the popliteal artery, downstream of the clot.","The posterior tibial artery is also a terminal branch of the popliteal artery.","The fibular artery is a branch of the posterior tibial artery, further downstream.","Correct — the descending branch of the lateral circumflex femoral artery joins the knee anastomosis to bypass the clot.","The superior medial genicular artery is a branch of the popliteal artery, part of the receiving end of the anastomosis."]},"a176":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The fibular (peroneal) artery is a branch of the posterior tibial artery, so ligating the posterior tibial at its origin stops it.</p><p>The popliteal artery divides into anterior and posterior tibial arteries. Shortly after its origin, the posterior tibial artery gives off the fibular artery, which runs along the back of the fibula supplying the lateral and posterior compartments. Tying the posterior tibial at its origin cuts off the fibular artery immediately. The anterior tibial artery (and its continuation, dorsalis pedis), the genicular arteries and the descending genicular artery come from other sources.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Dorsalis pedis</b> — The dorsalis pedis is the continuation of the ANTERIOR tibial artery.</li><li><b>Superior medial genicular</b> — The superior medial genicular artery is a branch of the popliteal artery.</li><li><b>Anterior tibial</b> — The anterior tibial artery is the other terminal branch of the popliteal artery.</li><li><b>Descending genicular</b> — The descending genicular artery arises from the femoral artery in the adductor canal.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Fibular artery = branch of posterior tibial; dorsalis pedis = continuation of anterior tibial.</p>","w":["Correct — the fibular (peroneal) artery arises from the posterior tibial artery and loses flow.","The dorsalis pedis is the continuation of the ANTERIOR tibial artery.","The superior medial genicular artery is a branch of the popliteal artery.","The anterior tibial artery is the other terminal branch of the popliteal artery.","The descending genicular artery arises from the femoral artery in the adductor canal."]},"a177":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The saphenous branch of the descending genicular artery supplies skin of the medial knee and is not part of the genicular anastomosis.</p><p>The anastomosis around the knee includes the five genicular arteries of the popliteal artery (superior medial and lateral, middle, inferior medial and lateral), the articular branch of the descending genicular artery, the descending branch of the lateral circumflex femoral artery, and the anterior and posterior tibial recurrent arteries. The descending genicular artery also gives a saphenous branch that travels with the saphenous nerve to the skin of the medial knee and leg — it doesn't join the deep anastomosis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Lateral superior genicular</b> — The lateral superior genicular artery is part of the knee anastomosis.</li><li><b>Medial inferior genicular</b> — The medial inferior genicular artery is part of the knee anastomosis.</li><li><b>Descending branch of the lateral femoral circumflex</b> — The descending branch of the lateral circumflex femoral artery is part of the knee anastomosis.</li><li><b>Anterior tibial recurrent</b> — The anterior tibial recurrent artery joins the anastomosis from below.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Knee anastomosis: genicular branches + lateral circumflex femoral (descending) + descending genicular (articular) + tibial recurrents.</p>","w":["The lateral superior genicular artery is part of the knee anastomosis.","The medial inferior genicular artery is part of the knee anastomosis.","The descending branch of the lateral circumflex femoral artery is part of the knee anastomosis.","Correct — the saphenous branch of the descending genicular artery isn't part of the knee anastomosis, so it's spared.","The anterior tibial recurrent artery joins the anastomosis from below."]},"a178":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The popliteal vein drains into the femoral vein at the adductor hiatus, so popliteal thrombosis reduces flow in the femoral vein.</p><p>The anterior and posterior tibial veins join at the lower border of popliteus to form the popliteal vein, which also receives the small saphenous vein. It ascends through the popliteal fossa and becomes the femoral vein at the adductor hiatus. A clot in the popliteal vein blocks flow into the femoral vein (downstream), while the veins draining into it (tibial veins, small saphenous) become congested. The great saphenous vein joins the femoral vein higher up and is unaffected.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Greater saphenous</b> — The great saphenous vein drains into the femoral vein near the groin, independent of the popliteal vein.</li><li><b>Lesser saphenous</b> — The lesser saphenous vein drains INTO the popliteal vein; it would be congested, not have reduced inflow.</li><li><b>Posterior tibial</b> — The posterior tibial veins are upstream tributaries of the popliteal vein.</li><li><b>Anterior tibial</b> — The anterior tibial veins are upstream tributaries of the popliteal vein.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Downstream of a venous clot: reduced flow; upstream: congestion and edema.</p>","w":["The great saphenous vein drains into the femoral vein near the groin, independent of the popliteal vein.","The lesser saphenous vein drains INTO the popliteal vein; it would be congested, not have reduced inflow.","Correct — the popliteal vein drains into the femoral vein, so femoral flow is reduced.","The posterior tibial veins are upstream tributaries of the popliteal vein.","The anterior tibial veins are upstream tributaries of the popliteal vein."]},"b1":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> McArdle disease (GSD V) = muscle glycogen phosphorylase deficiency → muscle can't use its own glycogen during exercise.</p><p>Muscle glycogen is the main fuel for intense exercise. Glycogen phosphorylase cleaves α-1,4 bonds to release glucose-1-phosphate, which enters glycolysis. Without the muscle isoform (myophosphorylase), exercising muscle runs short of ATP, causing painful cramps, exercise intolerance and muscle fiber damage (high CK, myoglobinuria — red/brown urine after exertion). Glycogen accumulates in muscle. Patients often describe a \"second wind\" when blood glucose and fatty acids take over. Ischemic forearm testing classically shows a flat lactate response with a normal rise in ammonia. Liver phosphorylase is normal, so blood glucose is fine.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Impaired conversion of glucose-6-phosphate to glucose</b> — Glucose-6-phosphatase is a liver/kidney enzyme (absent in muscle); its deficiency is von Gierke disease with fasting hypoglycemia and hepatomegaly.</li><li><b>Decreased function of the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex</b> — PDH deficiency causes congenital lactic acidosis and neurologic problems, not glycogen accumulation.</li><li><b>Decreased function of the TCA cycle</b> — TCA cycle defects don't cause glycogen storage in muscle.</li><li><b>Impaired electron transport chain complex</b> — Electron transport chain defects cause mitochondrial myopathies (ragged red fibers), not glycogen storage.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> McArdle = exercise cramps + myoglobinuria + flat lactate on ischemic exercise + \"second wind.\"</p>","w":["Glucose-6-phosphatase is a liver/kidney enzyme (absent in muscle); its deficiency is von Gierke disease with fasting hypoglycemia and hepatomegaly.","PDH deficiency causes congenital lactic acidosis and neurologic problems, not glycogen accumulation.","TCA cycle defects don't cause glycogen storage in muscle.","Correct — defective muscle glycogen phosphorylase (McArdle disease).","Electron transport chain defects cause mitochondrial myopathies (ragged red fibers), not glycogen storage."]},"b2":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> AMP is the cell's \"energy is low\" signal; it allosterically activates muscle glycogen phosphorylase to mobilize glycogen.</p><p>Glycogen phosphorylase is controlled two ways. Hormonally, epinephrine (and glucagon in liver) → cAMP → PKA → phosphorylase kinase → phosphorylase a (active). Allosterically, in muscle, rising AMP during contraction activates phosphorylase b even without phosphorylation, and Ca²⁺ released during contraction activates phosphorylase kinase. ATP and glucose-6-phosphate inhibit phosphorylase. A failure to respond to AMP means glycogen can't be broken down when energy demand rises.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Inability to deactivate glycogen synthase in response to glucose-6-phosphate</b> — G6P activates glycogen synthase rather than deactivating it, and this concerns storage, not mobilization.</li><li><b>Inability to convert glucose-1-phosphate to glucose-6-phosphate</b> — Phosphoglucomutase converts G1P to G6P after glycogen has already been broken down.</li><li><b>Inability to convert glucose-6-phosphate to glucose in the liver</b> — Glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency (von Gierke) blocks hepatic glucose release, not the response to energy demand.</li><li><b>Inability to activate glycogen synthase in response to glucose-6-phosphate</b> — Activation of glycogen synthase relates to glycogen synthesis, not mobilization.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Muscle phosphorylase activators: AMP, Ca²⁺ (via phosphorylase kinase), epinephrine. Inhibitors: ATP, G6P.</p>","w":["Correct — glycogen can't be mobilized if phosphorylase fails to respond to AMP.","G6P activates glycogen synthase rather than deactivating it, and this concerns storage, not mobilization.","Phosphoglucomutase converts G1P to G6P after glycogen has already been broken down.","Glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency (von Gierke) blocks hepatic glucose release, not the response to energy demand.","Activation of glycogen synthase relates to glycogen synthesis, not mobilization."]},"b3":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Insulin → protein phosphatase 1 → dephosphorylates enzymes: glycogen synthase ON, glycogen phosphorylase OFF.</p><p>Glycogen metabolism is reciprocally regulated by phosphorylation. Glucagon (liver) and epinephrine (liver and muscle) raise cAMP → PKA, which phosphorylates and activates phosphorylase kinase and phosphorylase (breakdown) while phosphorylating and inactivating glycogen synthase. Insulin opposes this: it activates PP1 (and phosphodiesterase to lower cAMP), so synthase is dephosphorylated (active) and phosphorylase is dephosphorylated (inactive). Rule of thumb: phosphorylation favors breakdown; dephosphorylation favors storage.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Glucagon stimulates glycogen breakdown via activation of glycogen synthase</b> — Glucagon stimulates breakdown by activating phosphorylase and inactivating synthase.</li><li><b>AMP activates glycogen synthase to promote glucose storage</b> — AMP activates phosphorylase (breakdown), not synthase.</li><li><b>Cortisol inhibits glycogen phosphorylase to prevent glycogen breakdown</b> — Cortisol doesn't directly inhibit phosphorylase; it promotes gluconeogenesis and supports hepatic glycogen stores.</li><li><b>Glucose-6-phosphate activates glycogen phosphorylase to inhibit glycogen breakdown</b> — G6P activates synthase and inhibits phosphorylase, so this mixes up the targets.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Fed (insulin) = dephosphorylated enzymes; fasting (glucagon) = phosphorylated enzymes.</p>","w":["Glucagon stimulates breakdown by activating phosphorylase and inactivating synthase.","Correct — insulin promotes synthesis and blocks phosphorylase.","AMP activates phosphorylase (breakdown), not synthase.","Cortisol doesn't directly inhibit phosphorylase; it promotes gluconeogenesis and supports hepatic glycogen stores.","G6P activates synthase and inhibits phosphorylase, so this mixes up the targets."]},"b4":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Von Gierke disease (GSD Ia) = glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency → severe fasting hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis, hyperuricemia, hyperlipidemia, hepatomegaly.</p><p>Glucose-6-phosphatase is the final step for both glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis in the liver (and kidney). Without it, the liver can't release free glucose, so hypoglycemia develops within a few hours of fasting. Trapped G6P is pushed into glycolysis (→ lactate), the pentose phosphate pathway (→ purine synthesis → uric acid; lactate also competes with urate excretion), and fatty acid/triglyceride synthesis. Glycogen and fat accumulate in an enlarged liver and kidneys; patients have \"doll-like\" faces. Treatment: frequent feeds and cornstarch, avoid fructose and galactose.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Lysosomal acid α-glucosidase</b> — Lysosomal acid α-glucosidase deficiency is Pompe disease — cardiomegaly and hypotonia with normal glucose.</li><li><b>Phosphorylase</b> — Liver phosphorylase deficiency (Hers disease) is milder, without lactic acidosis or hyperuricemia.</li><li><b>Debranching enzyme</b> — Debranching enzyme deficiency (Cori disease) causes milder hypoglycemia with normal lactate and uric acid (gluconeogenesis is intact).</li><li><b>Branching enzyme</b> — Branching enzyme deficiency (Andersen disease) causes cirrhosis and liver failure.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> GSD I triad: fasting hypoglycemia + lactic acidosis + hyperuricemia/hyperlipidemia.</p>","w":["Lysosomal acid α-glucosidase deficiency is Pompe disease — cardiomegaly and hypotonia with normal glucose.","Correct — glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency (von Gierke disease).","Liver phosphorylase deficiency (Hers disease) is milder, without lactic acidosis or hyperuricemia.","Debranching enzyme deficiency (Cori disease) causes milder hypoglycemia with normal lactate and uric acid (gluconeogenesis is intact).","Branching enzyme deficiency (Andersen disease) causes cirrhosis and liver failure."]},"b5":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Pompe disease (GSD II) = lysosomal acid α-glucosidase (acid maltase) deficiency → glycogen in lysosomes of heart and muscle.</p><p>A small amount of glycogen is normally taken up into lysosomes and degraded by acid maltase. Without it, glycogen builds up inside lysosomes, especially in cardiac and skeletal muscle. The infantile form presents in the first months with massive cardiomegaly (hypertrophic cardiomyopathy), hypotonia (\"floppy baby\"), macroglossia, feeding difficulty and respiratory failure, often fatal in the first year or two without enzyme replacement therapy. Because cytosolic glycogen metabolism is intact, blood glucose is normal. It is the only glycogen storage disease that is also a lysosomal storage disease.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>McArdle disease</b> — McArdle disease presents in adolescents/adults with exercise-induced cramps; no cardiomegaly.</li><li><b>Cori disease</b> — Cori disease (debranching enzyme) causes hepatomegaly and hypoglycemia with abnormal cytosolic glycogen.</li><li><b>Hers disease</b> — Hers disease (liver phosphorylase) causes mild hepatomegaly and hypoglycemia.</li><li><b>Glycogen storage disease type IV</b> — GSD IV (Andersen) causes cirrhosis from abnormal long, unbranched glycogen.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Pompe trashes the Pump (heart) — cardiomegaly + hypotonia in infancy.</p>","w":["Correct — lysosomal glycogen in heart and muscle with infant onset = Pompe disease.","McArdle disease presents in adolescents/adults with exercise-induced cramps; no cardiomegaly.","Cori disease (debranching enzyme) causes hepatomegaly and hypoglycemia with abnormal cytosolic glycogen.","Hers disease (liver phosphorylase) causes mild hepatomegaly and hypoglycemia.","GSD IV (Andersen) causes cirrhosis from abnormal long, unbranched glycogen."]},"b6":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hers disease (GSD VI) = liver glycogen phosphorylase deficiency → hepatomegaly and mild fasting hypoglycemia, without the metabolic derangements of von Gierke.</p><p>When liver phosphorylase is missing, hepatic glycogen can't be broken down, so it accumulates (hepatomegaly) and fasting glucose is somewhat low. However, gluconeogenesis and glucose-6-phosphatase still work, so hypoglycemia is mild and lactate, uric acid and lipids are usually normal. Growth delay and mildly raised liver enzymes are common and typically improve with age. Muscle is unaffected because the muscle isoform is a different gene.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Lysosomal α-glucosidase</b> — Lysosomal α-glucosidase deficiency (Pompe) affects heart and skeletal muscle with normal glucose.</li><li><b>Glucose-6-phosphatase</b> — Von Gierke disease is more severe, with lactic acidosis, hyperuricemia and hyperlipidemia.</li><li><b>Debranching enzyme</b> — Cori disease is possible clinically, but often also involves muscle (weakness, ↑CK) and stores abnormal limit-dextrin glycogen; the key lists liver phosphorylase.</li><li><b>Muscle phosphorylase</b> — Muscle phosphorylase deficiency (McArdle) causes exercise intolerance, not hepatomegaly or hypoglycemia.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> GSDs with hepatomegaly + hypoglycemia: I (most severe), III (± muscle), VI (mildest).</p>","w":["Lysosomal α-glucosidase deficiency (Pompe) affects heart and skeletal muscle with normal glucose.","Von Gierke disease is more severe, with lactic acidosis, hyperuricemia and hyperlipidemia.","Cori disease is possible clinically, but often also involves muscle (weakness, ↑CK) and stores abnormal limit-dextrin glycogen; the key lists liver phosphorylase.","Muscle phosphorylase deficiency (McArdle) causes exercise intolerance, not hepatomegaly or hypoglycemia.","Correct (per key) — liver phosphorylase deficiency (Hers disease)."]},"b7":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Heparin is a highly sulfated GAG that activates antithrombin III, inactivating thrombin (IIa) and factor Xa.</p><p>Heparin is the most negatively charged molecule in the body and is stored in mast cell granules. Its specific pentasaccharide sequence binds antithrombin, changing its shape and accelerating its inhibition of serine proteases ~1000-fold. Unfractionated heparin inhibits both IIa and Xa (monitored by aPTT; reversed by protamine sulfate); low-molecular-weight heparin acts mainly on Xa. Uses: DVT/PE treatment and prophylaxis, acute coronary syndrome, anticoagulation of blood for transfusion and dialysis circuits. Side effect: heparin-induced thrombocytopenia.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Hyaluronic acid</b> — Hyaluronic acid is a non-sulfated GAG that lubricates joints and forms the vitreous; it isn't an anticoagulant.</li><li><b>Chondroitin- 4- Sulfate</b> — Chondroitin-4-sulfate is a structural GAG of cartilage, bone and tendon.</li><li><b>Chondroitin- 6- Sulfate</b> — Chondroitin-6-sulfate is also a structural GAG of cartilage.</li><li><b>Dermatan Sulfate</b> — Dermatan sulfate (skin, vessels, heart valves) has only weak anticoagulant activity (via heparin cofactor II) and isn't used clinically for this.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Heparin → ↑antithrombin activity; reverse with protamine; monitor aPTT.</p>","w":["Hyaluronic acid is a non-sulfated GAG that lubricates joints and forms the vitreous; it isn't an anticoagulant.","Chondroitin-4-sulfate is a structural GAG of cartilage, bone and tendon.","Chondroitin-6-sulfate is also a structural GAG of cartilage.","Correct — heparin is the anticoagulant GAG.","Dermatan sulfate (skin, vessels, heart valves) has only weak anticoagulant activity (via heparin cofactor II) and isn't used clinically for this."]},"b8":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cellulose = glucose linked by β-1,4 bonds; humans lack cellulase, so it's indigestible dietary fiber.</p><p>Human digestive enzymes (salivary and pancreatic amylase, brush border maltase, isomaltase, sucrase, lactase) cleave α-glycosidic bonds (plus the β-1,4 bond of lactose via lactase, which is specific for galactose–glucose). Cellulose's β-1,4 glucose–glucose bonds produce long, straight chains that stack into fibrils; no human enzyme can break them. Undigested, it adds bulk and retains water, which is why cellulose derivatives (methylcellulose) are bulk-forming laxatives. Colonic bacteria ferment some fiber into short-chain fatty acids.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>glycogen</b> — Glycogen has α-1,4 links with α-1,6 branches — digestible.</li><li><b>amylose</b> — Amylose is α-1,4 linked — digested by amylase.</li><li><b>amylopectin</b> — Amylopectin has α-1,4 links with α-1,6 branches — digestible.</li><li><b>lactose</b> — Lactose is galactose β-1,4 glucose; lactase digests it (it isn't glucose–glucose).</li><li><b>sucrose</b> — Sucrose is glucose α1→β2 fructose, digested by sucrase.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Humans digest α-glucosidic bonds; β-glucose polymers (cellulose) = fiber.</p>","w":["Correct — cellulose is glucose β(1→4) glucose and indigestible.","Glycogen has α-1,4 links with α-1,6 branches — digestible.","Amylose is α-1,4 linked — digested by amylase.","Amylopectin has α-1,4 links with α-1,6 branches — digestible.","Lactose is galactose β-1,4 glucose; lactase digests it (it isn't glucose–glucose).","Sucrose is glucose α1→β2 fructose, digested by sucrase."]},"b9":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Antibodies to intrinsic factor (pernicious anemia) → B12 (cobalamin) malabsorption → megaloblastic anemia plus neurologic disease.</p><p>Vitamin B12 must bind intrinsic factor (from gastric parietal cells) to be absorbed in the terminal ileum. Autoimmune destruction of parietal cells or antibodies to IF block this. B12 is a cofactor for two enzymes: methionine synthase (homocysteine → methionine, regenerating THF for DNA synthesis) and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase. Impaired DNA synthesis produces megaloblastic anemia (MCV &gt;100, hypersegmented neutrophils) and often pancytopenia. Elevated methylmalonic acid disrupts myelin, causing peripheral neuropathy, subacute combined degeneration (dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts) and psychiatric changes. Both homocysteine and MMA are high (only homocysteine is high in folate deficiency).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Ascorbate</b> — Ascorbate (vitamin C) deficiency causes scurvy — bleeding gums, poor wound healing, perifollicular hemorrhage.</li><li><b>Folate</b> — Folate deficiency also causes megaloblastic anemia but no neurologic signs and no intrinsic factor antibodies; MMA is normal.</li><li><b>Iron</b> — Iron deficiency causes microcytic (low MCV) anemia.</li><li><b>Niacin</b> — Niacin deficiency causes pellagra (dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> B12 deficiency = ↑homocysteine + ↑MMA + neuro signs; folate deficiency = ↑homocysteine only.</p>","w":["Ascorbate (vitamin C) deficiency causes scurvy — bleeding gums, poor wound healing, perifollicular hemorrhage.","Correct — cobalamin deficiency from pernicious anemia.","Folate deficiency also causes megaloblastic anemia but no neurologic signs and no intrinsic factor antibodies; MMA is normal.","Iron deficiency causes microcytic (low MCV) anemia.","Niacin deficiency causes pellagra (dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia)."]},"b10":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Amylose = unbranched glucose chain with only α-1,4 bonds.</p><p>Starch contains two polymers: amylose (linear α-1,4) and amylopectin (α-1,4 chains with α-1,6 branches every ~24–30 residues). Glycogen is like amylopectin but more highly branched (every ~8–10 residues), giving more ends for rapid mobilization. Salivary and pancreatic α-amylase cleave internal α-1,4 bonds but not α-1,6 bonds, leaving limit dextrins that brush-border isomaltase (α-dextrinase) finishes.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>α-1,6 glycosidic bonds</b> — α-1,6 bonds form the branch points of amylopectin and glycogen.</li><li><b>β-1,4 glycosidic bonds</b> — β-1,4 bonds between glucose units are found in cellulose.</li><li><b>α-1,1 glycosidic bonds</b> — α-1,1 bonds link the two glucoses of trehalose.</li><li><b>α-1→ β-2 glycosidic bonds</b> — α1→β2 is the bond in sucrose (glucose–fructose).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Amylase cleaves α-1,4 only; α-1,6 branches need debranching activity (isomaltase).</p>","w":["Correct — amylose is linked by α-1,4 glycosidic bonds.","α-1,6 bonds form the branch points of amylopectin and glycogen.","β-1,4 bonds between glucose units are found in cellulose.","α-1,1 bonds link the two glucoses of trehalose.","α1→β2 is the bond in sucrose (glucose–fructose)."]},"b11":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> 2,3-BPG binds deoxyhemoglobin, stabilizes the T (taut) state, and right-shifts the O₂ curve → more O₂ unloading to tissues.</p><p>In RBCs, bisphosphoglycerate mutase converts the glycolytic intermediate 1,3-BPG into 2,3-BPG (the Rapoport–Luebering shunt), which binds the central cavity between β-chains of deoxy-Hb. Levels rise in chronic anemia, high altitude, and chronic lung disease, increasing O₂ delivery as an adaptation. Fetal hemoglobin (HbF, γ-chains) binds 2,3-BPG poorly, giving HbF higher O₂ affinity. Stored blood loses 2,3-BPG, so transfused RBCs initially release O₂ poorly. Pyruvate kinase deficiency raises 2,3-BPG (right shift), while hexokinase deficiency lowers it.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>3-phosphoglycerate</b> — 3-phosphoglycerate is a glycolytic intermediate, not a hemoglobin effector.</li><li><b>1,3 bisphosphoglycerate</b> — 1,3-BPG is the precursor of 2,3-BPG and a high-energy intermediate, but it doesn't regulate hemoglobin.</li><li><b>Phosphoenolpyruvate</b> — Phosphoenolpyruvate is the high-energy substrate for pyruvate kinase.</li><li><b>2-phosphoglycerate</b> — 2-phosphoglycerate is the substrate of enolase.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Right shift (\"CADET face right\"): ↑CO₂, Acid, 2,3-BPG, Exercise, Temperature.</p>","w":["3-phosphoglycerate is a glycolytic intermediate, not a hemoglobin effector.","Correct — 2,3-BPG promotes O₂ unloading.","1,3-BPG is the precursor of 2,3-BPG and a high-energy intermediate, but it doesn't regulate hemoglobin.","Phosphoenolpyruvate is the high-energy substrate for pyruvate kinase.","2-phosphoglycerate is the substrate of enolase."]},"b12":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Replacing a charged amino acid with a hydrophobic one (Glu → Val at position 6 of β-globin) is the sickle cell mutation.</p><p>HbS results from a single missense mutation (GAG → GTG) in the β-globin gene. Valine creates a hydrophobic patch on the surface of hemoglobin. When deoxygenated, this patch fits into a hydrophobic pocket on a neighboring HbS molecule, forming long polymers that distort RBCs into sickles. Sickled cells are rigid, causing hemolytic anemia and vaso-occlusion. Conservative substitutions (similar size and charge) usually preserve function. HbC (Glu6Lys) is a milder variant.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Ile-Leu-Val to Ile-Ile-Val</b> — Leu → Ile: both hydrophobic, a conservative change.</li><li><b>Gly-Ile-Gly to Gly-Val-Gly</b> — Ile → Val: both hydrophobic, a conservative change.</li><li><b>Gly-Asp-Gly to Gly-Glu-Gly</b> — Asp → Glu: both acidic, a conservative change.</li><li><b>Val-Val-Val to Val-Leu-Val</b> — Val → Leu: both hydrophobic, a conservative change.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> HbS = Glu6Val (charged → hydrophobic); HbC = Glu6Lys (negative → positive).</p>","w":["Leu → Ile: both hydrophobic, a conservative change.","Correct — Glu → Val, as in sickle hemoglobin.","Ile → Val: both hydrophobic, a conservative change.","Asp → Glu: both acidic, a conservative change.","Val → Leu: both hydrophobic, a conservative change."]},"b13":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Secondary structure = local backbone folding stabilized by hydrogen bonds; α-helices and β-pleated sheets are the main forms.</p><p>Protein structure levels: primary (amino acid sequence, peptide bonds), secondary (α-helix, β-sheet, turns — backbone H-bonds), tertiary (3D fold of one chain, with domains; stabilized by hydrophobic interactions, ionic bonds, H-bonds and disulfide bonds), quaternary (multiple subunits, e.g., hemoglobin α₂β₂). Primary sequence determines all higher levels. Denaturation (heat, pH, solvents) disrupts secondary through quaternary structure but not peptide bonds. Proline disrupts α-helices.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The α-helix is composed of more than one polypeptide chain</b> — An α-helix is formed by a single polypeptide coiling on itself; collagen's triple helix involves three chains but is not an α-helix.</li><li><b>Secondary structure determines the amino acid sequence in the peptide chain</b> — This is reversed: primary sequence determines secondary structure.</li><li><b>The presence of domains is characteristics of secondary structure</b> — Domains are units of tertiary structure.</li><li><b>β sheet is stable during the heat treatment</b> — Heat breaks hydrogen bonds and denatures secondary structure, including β-sheets.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Prion disease = α-helix → β-sheet conversion (misfolding).</p>","w":["An α-helix is formed by a single polypeptide coiling on itself; collagen's triple helix involves three chains but is not an α-helix.","This is reversed: primary sequence determines secondary structure.","Domains are units of tertiary structure.","Correct — α-helix and β-sheet are the main secondary structures.","Heat breaks hydrogen bonds and denatures secondary structure, including β-sheets."]},"b14":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Osteogenesis imperfecta = defective or reduced type I collagen (COL1A1/COL1A2) → brittle bones, blue sclerae, hearing loss, dental problems.</p><p>Type I collagen is the major protein of bone, tendon, skin, dentin and sclera. In the mildest form (type I OI), a null COL1A1 allele produces half the normal amount of structurally normal collagen (quantitative defect). In severe forms, a glycine substitution in the triple helix disrupts collagen structure (dominant negative). Patients have multiple fractures with minimal trauma (can be confused with child abuse), blue sclerae (thin sclera shows choroid), hearing loss (ossicle abnormalities) and dentinogenesis imperfecta.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Ehlers-Danlos syndrome</b> — Ehlers–Danlos syndrome mostly involves type V or III collagen or processing enzymes, causing hyperextensible skin and lax joints.</li><li><b>Marfan's syndrome</b> — Marfan syndrome is caused by fibrillin-1 mutations.</li><li><b>Emphysema</b> — Emphysema relates to α1-antitrypsin deficiency and smoking, not collagen mutations.</li><li><b>Alpha thalassemia</b> — α-thalassemia is reduced α-globin synthesis — a hemoglobin disorder.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> OI: \"can't BITE\" — Bones (fractures), I (eye: blue sclerae), Teeth, Ear (hearing loss).</p>","w":["Ehlers–Danlos syndrome mostly involves type V or III collagen or processing enzymes, causing hyperextensible skin and lax joints.","Correct — type I collagen deficiency causes osteogenesis imperfecta.","Marfan syndrome is caused by fibrillin-1 mutations.","Emphysema relates to α1-antitrypsin deficiency and smoking, not collagen mutations.","α-thalassemia is reduced α-globin synthesis — a hemoglobin disorder."]},"b15":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> α1-antitrypsin is a liver-made serine protease inhibitor (serpin) that protects the lungs from neutrophil elastase.</p><p>Neutrophils in the lung release elastase, which degrades elastin. α1AT normally neutralizes it. In deficiency (most often the PiZZ genotype), elastase breaks down alveolar walls → panacinar emphysema, predominantly in the lower lobes, in young adults (much worse with smoking). The misfolded Z protein polymerizes in the hepatocyte ER instead of being secreted, causing hepatitis and cirrhosis (PAS-positive, diastase-resistant globules) — explaining the abnormal LFTs. Only lung disease improves with IV α1AT augmentation; liver disease may need transplant.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Inhibit action of pancreatic trypsinogen</b> — Trypsinogen is an inactive zymogen; it isn't the physiological target of α1AT.</li><li><b>Initiate blood coagulation in the lung</b> — α1AT has no role in initiating coagulation.</li><li><b>Prevent oxidation of lung proteins</b> — α1AT doesn't protect proteins from oxidation; smoking actually oxidizes and inactivates α1AT itself.</li><li><b>Forms complexes with pancreatic zymogens</b> — α1AT doesn't form complexes with pancreatic zymogens.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Young nonsmoker with lower-lobe panacinar emphysema + liver disease → α1AT deficiency.</p>","w":["Trypsinogen is an inactive zymogen; it isn't the physiological target of α1AT.","α1AT has no role in initiating coagulation.","Correct — α1AT inhibits tissue destruction by proteases such as elastase.","α1AT doesn't protect proteins from oxidation; smoking actually oxidizes and inactivates α1AT itself.","α1AT doesn't form complexes with pancreatic zymogens."]},"b16":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Scurvy = vitamin C deficiency → impaired prolyl/lysyl hydroxylation → weak collagen.</p><p>Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases in the rough ER add hydroxyl groups to proline and lysine in procollagen. These enzymes use Fe²⁺, O₂ and α-ketoglutarate, and need ascorbic acid to keep iron reduced. Hydroxyproline stabilizes the triple helix via hydrogen bonds; hydroxylysine allows glycosylation and cross-linking. Without vitamin C, collagen is unstable, so capillaries bleed (gums, perifollicular hemorrhages, corkscrew hairs), teeth loosen, wounds heal poorly, and bone/joint pain occurs. Dietary vitamin C (citrus, leafy greens) corrects it.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Lysyl oxidase</b> — Lysyl oxidase is an enzyme produced by the body, not a dietary supplement.</li><li><b>Copper</b> — Copper is the cofactor for lysyl oxidase (cross-linking); copper deficiency (Menkes disease) causes brittle, kinky hair and vascular problems, not classic scurvy.</li><li><b>Α-Ketoglutarate</b> — α-Ketoglutarate is a hydroxylation co-substrate but is made endogenously in the TCA cycle.</li><li><b>Glycine</b> — Glycine is non-essential and abundant; it is not the missing factor.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Vitamin C → hydroxylation (RER); copper → lysyl oxidase cross-linking (extracellular).</p>","w":["Lysyl oxidase is an enzyme produced by the body, not a dietary supplement.","Copper is the cofactor for lysyl oxidase (cross-linking); copper deficiency (Menkes disease) causes brittle, kinky hair and vascular problems, not classic scurvy.","α-Ketoglutarate is a hydroxylation co-substrate but is made endogenously in the TCA cycle.","Glycine is non-essential and abundant; it is not the missing factor.","Correct — ascorbic acid restores collagen hydroxylation."]},"b17":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Vitamin C is required for hydroxylation of proline (and lysine) residues in procollagen.</p><p>Collagen's repeating sequence is Gly-X-Y, where X is often proline and Y is often hydroxyproline or hydroxylysine. Hydroxyproline's –OH groups form inter-chain hydrogen bonds that stabilize the triple helix at body temperature. Without vitamin C (no fresh fruits or vegetables), prolyl hydroxylase can't work, so the helix is unstable and degraded, causing loose teeth, bleeding gums and poor wound healing.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Arginine</b> — Arginine isn't hydroxylated in collagen.</li><li><b>Cysteine</b> — Cysteines in the propeptides form disulfide bonds, but this doesn't need vitamin C.</li><li><b>Histidine</b> — Histidine isn't modified in collagen synthesis.</li><li><b>Leucine</b> — Leucine isn't modified in collagen.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hydroxyproline = collagen marker; vitamin C deficiency → scurvy.</p>","w":["Arginine isn't hydroxylated in collagen.","Cysteines in the propeptides form disulfide bonds, but this doesn't need vitamin C.","Histidine isn't modified in collagen synthesis.","Correct — proline hydroxylation is vitamin C dependent.","Leucine isn't modified in collagen."]},"b18":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Lysyl oxidase (copper-dependent) creates allysine aldehydes that form cross-links — in elastin, four lysines condense into desmosine.</p><p>Outside the cell, lysyl oxidase oxidatively deaminates lysine side chains to allysine. In elastin, three allysines and one unmodified lysine join to form desmosine (or isodesmosine), a ring cross-link that ties up to four chains — giving elastin its rubber-like stretch and recoil. The same enzyme cross-links collagen fibrils for tensile strength. Copper deficiency (Menkes disease) or lathyrism (β-aminopropionitrile) impairs cross-linking.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>C-procollagen peptidase</b> — C-procollagen peptidase removes the C-terminal propeptides of procollagen extracellularly.</li><li><b>Lysyl hydroxylase</b> — Lysyl hydroxylase makes hydroxylysine inside the RER (vitamin C dependent).</li><li><b>N-procollagen peptidase</b> — N-procollagen peptidase removes the N-terminal propeptides; its deficiency causes a form of Ehlers–Danlos (dermatosparaxis).</li><li><b>Prolyl hydroxylase</b> — Prolyl hydroxylase makes hydroxyproline (vitamin C dependent).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Desmosine is unique to elastin; urinary desmosine is a marker of elastin breakdown.</p>","w":["C-procollagen peptidase removes the C-terminal propeptides of procollagen extracellularly.","Correct — lysyl oxidase forms desmosine cross-links.","Lysyl hydroxylase makes hydroxylysine inside the RER (vitamin C dependent).","N-procollagen peptidase removes the N-terminal propeptides; its deficiency causes a form of Ehlers–Danlos (dermatosparaxis).","Prolyl hydroxylase makes hydroxyproline (vitamin C dependent)."]},"b19":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Aldose = aldehyde group; ketose = ketone group. Glyceraldehyde is the only aldotriose; dihydroxyacetone is the ketotriose.</p><p>Sugars are classified by number of carbons (triose 3, tetrose 4, pentose 5, hexose 6) and by carbonyl type. In glycolysis, aldolase splits fructose-1,6-bisphosphate into glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate (aldotriose phosphate) and dihydroxyacetone phosphate (ketotriose phosphate). Triose phosphate isomerase converts DHAP to G3P so both halves continue through glycolysis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>dihydroxyacetone</b> — Dihydroxyacetone is a ketotriose (ketone at C2).</li><li><b>erythrose</b> — Erythrose is an aldotetrose (4 carbons).</li><li><b>ribose</b> — Ribose is an aldopentose (5 carbons).</li><li><b>ribulose</b> — Ribulose is a ketopentose.</li><li><b>threose</b> — Threose is an aldotetrose.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Triose phosphate isomerase: DHAP ⇌ G3P (each glucose yields two G3P).</p>","w":["Dihydroxyacetone is a ketotriose (ketone at C2).","Erythrose is an aldotetrose (4 carbons).","Correct — glyceraldehyde is a 3-carbon aldose.","Ribose is an aldopentose (5 carbons).","Ribulose is a ketopentose.","Threose is an aldotetrose."]},"b20":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> GLUT-4 is the insulin-responsive glucose transporter of skeletal muscle, adipose tissue and heart; insulin moves it to the membrane in the fed state.</p><p>After a meal, insulin binds its tyrosine kinase receptor → IRS → PI3K → Akt, which triggers translocation of GLUT-4-containing vesicles to the cell surface, increasing glucose uptake into muscle and fat. When insulin falls (fasting), GLUT-4 is internalized. Exercise also translocates GLUT-4 independently of insulin (via AMPK and Ca²⁺), which is why exercise improves glucose control in type 2 diabetes. Other GLUTs: GLUT-1 (RBCs, brain), GLUT-2 (liver, pancreatic β cells, kidney, intestine basolateral), GLUT-3 (neurons), GLUT-5 (fructose).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Fructose 2,6 bisphosphate in the fasting state</b> — Fructose 2,6-bisphosphate regulates PFK-1 and FBPase-1 inside the cell; it doesn't move GLUT-4.</li><li><b>Fructose 2,6 bisphosphate in the well fed state</b> — Fructose 2,6-bisphosphate isn't a transporter signal, regardless of state.</li><li><b>Glucagon in the fasting state</b> — Glucagon doesn't affect GLUT-4 (and muscle lacks glucagon receptors).</li><li><b>Glucagon in the well-fed state</b> — Glucagon is low in the fed state and doesn't regulate GLUT-4.</li><li><b>Insulin in the fasting state</b> — Insulin is low during fasting.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Insulin-dependent glucose uptake: GLUT-4 (muscle, fat). Insulin-independent: brain, RBCs, liver, kidney, intestine, β cells.</p>","w":["Fructose 2,6-bisphosphate regulates PFK-1 and FBPase-1 inside the cell; it doesn't move GLUT-4.","Fructose 2,6-bisphosphate isn't a transporter signal, regardless of state.","Glucagon doesn't affect GLUT-4 (and muscle lacks glucagon receptors).","Glucagon is low in the fed state and doesn't regulate GLUT-4.","Insulin is low during fasting.","Correct — insulin in the fed state upregulates surface GLUT-4."]},"b21":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Fructose crosses the apical membrane of enterocytes via GLUT-5 (facilitated diffusion); glucose and galactose use SGLT1 (Na⁺ cotransport).</p><p>Sucrase at the brush border splits sucrose into glucose and fructose. Glucose enters via SGLT1, driven by the Na⁺ gradient. Fructose enters only via GLUT-5, down its concentration gradient (no energy). Both then exit the basolateral membrane into portal blood via GLUT-2. If glucose rises in portal blood but fructose doesn't, the defect must be in the step unique to fructose — apical GLUT-5. Unabsorbed fructose is fermented in the colon, causing bloating and diarrhea.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>GLUT-2</b> — Basolateral GLUT-2 exports both glucose and fructose; its loss would block both (and GLUT-2 defects cause Fanconi–Bickel syndrome).</li><li><b>GLUT-7</b> — GLUT-7 is a hepatic ER transporter, not the intestinal fructose carrier.</li><li><b>Glucagon Receptor</b> — The glucagon receptor doesn't affect intestinal sugar absorption.</li><li><b>Insulin Receptor</b> — Intestinal sugar absorption isn't insulin dependent.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Apical: SGLT1 (glucose, galactose), GLUT-5 (fructose). Basolateral: GLUT-2 (all three).</p>","w":["Basolateral GLUT-2 exports both glucose and fructose; its loss would block both (and GLUT-2 defects cause Fanconi–Bickel syndrome).","Correct — GLUT-5 is the apical fructose transporter.","GLUT-7 is a hepatic ER transporter, not the intestinal fructose carrier.","The glucagon receptor doesn't affect intestinal sugar absorption.","Intestinal sugar absorption isn't insulin dependent."]},"b22":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase (GAPDH) is the only glycolytic enzyme that reduces NAD⁺ to NADH.</p><p>GAPDH oxidizes G3P and adds inorganic phosphate, forming the high-energy compound 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate and NADH. Because this step needs NAD⁺, glycolysis depends on continuous NAD⁺ regeneration — by LDH (anaerobic, pyruvate → lactate) or by mitochondrial shuttles (aerobic). Blocking GAPDH halts glycolysis at that point. Arsenate also interferes here, substituting for phosphate and forming an unstable product, so no ATP is made at the next step.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>aldolase</b> — Aldolase splits fructose-1,6-bisphosphate into two trioses; no NAD⁺ is involved.</li><li><b>phosphoglycerate kinase</b> — Phosphoglycerate kinase makes ATP from 1,3-BPG; no NAD⁺ is involved.</li><li><b>pyruvate dehydrogenase</b> — PDH does make NADH, but it links glycolysis to the TCA cycle in mitochondria and isn't a glycolytic enzyme.</li><li><b>triose isomerase</b> — Triose phosphate isomerase interconverts DHAP and G3P; no NAD⁺ is involved.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Arsenate poisons glycolysis at GAPDH → no net ATP; arsenite inhibits lipoic acid enzymes (PDH, α-KGDH).</p>","w":["Aldolase splits fructose-1,6-bisphosphate into two trioses; no NAD⁺ is involved.","Correct — GAPDH is the only glycolytic step that reduces NAD⁺.","Phosphoglycerate kinase makes ATP from 1,3-BPG; no NAD⁺ is involved.","PDH does make NADH, but it links glycolysis to the TCA cycle in mitochondria and isn't a glycolytic enzyme.","Triose phosphate isomerase interconverts DHAP and G3P; no NAD⁺ is involved."]},"b23":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Fasting liver: glucagon → cAMP → PKA phosphorylates PFK-2 (off) and pyruvate kinase (off) → glycolysis slows, gluconeogenesis rises.</p><p>The bifunctional enzyme PFK-2/FBPase-2 controls fructose-2,6-bisphosphate. In the fed state (insulin), it's dephosphorylated: PFK-2 active → high F2,6BP → activates PFK-1, inhibits FBPase-1 → glycolysis. In the fasting state (glucagon), PKA phosphorylates it: FBPase-2 active → low F2,6BP → FBPase-1 freed → gluconeogenesis. PKA also phosphorylates and inactivates liver pyruvate kinase, so newly made PEP isn't wasted converting back to pyruvate (futile cycle).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>adenylyl cyclase</b> — Adenylyl cyclase is activated by glucagon in this state, which is what lowers F2,6BP.</li><li><b>aldolase</b> — Aldolase is not a regulated step.</li><li><b>fructose 1,6-phosphatase</b> — Low F2,6BP relieves inhibition of fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase, so it's activated.</li><li><b>pyruvate carboxylase</b> — Pyruvate carboxylase is active in fasting gluconeogenesis (activated by acetyl-CoA).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Liver PK: inhibited by PKA phosphorylation, ATP, alanine; activated by F-1,6-BP (feed-forward).</p>","w":["Adenylyl cyclase is activated by glucagon in this state, which is what lowers F2,6BP.","Aldolase is not a regulated step.","Low F2,6BP relieves inhibition of fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase, so it's activated.","Pyruvate carboxylase is active in fasting gluconeogenesis (activated by acetyl-CoA).","Correct — liver pyruvate kinase is inhibited in the same fasting state."]},"b24":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Mature RBCs lack mitochondria, so all ATP comes from anaerobic glycolysis: glucose → lactate (net 2 ATP).</p><p>Without mitochondria, RBCs can't use the TCA cycle, β-oxidation or oxidative phosphorylation. Glucose enters via GLUT-1 (insulin-independent). Glycolysis yields 2 ATP; lactate dehydrogenase converts pyruvate to lactate to regenerate NAD⁺, and lactate is released into the blood (recycled by the liver in the Cori cycle). RBCs also run the pentose phosphate pathway to make NADPH, which keeps glutathione reduced to protect hemoglobin from oxidation (defective in G6PD deficiency). Pyruvate kinase deficiency is the commonest glycolytic cause of hemolytic anemia.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Fatty acids to CO2 and H20</b> — Fatty acid oxidation requires mitochondria.</li><li><b>Glucose to ribulose 5-phosphate</b> — The pentose phosphate pathway makes NADPH for antioxidant defense, not ATP.</li><li><b>Glycogen to lactate</b> — RBCs don't store glycogen.</li><li><b>Ketone bodies to CO2</b> — Ketone bodies are oxidized in mitochondria.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> RBC: glycolysis for ATP, HMP shunt for NADPH, Rapoport–Luebering shunt for 2,3-BPG.</p>","w":["Fatty acid oxidation requires mitochondria.","Correct — energy in mature RBCs comes from anaerobic glycolysis to lactate.","The pentose phosphate pathway makes NADPH for antioxidant defense, not ATP.","RBCs don't store glycogen.","Ketone bodies are oxidized in mitochondria."]},"b25":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hexokinase (low Km) is saturated at physiologic glucose; glucokinase (high Km, ~blood glucose) changes its rate with glucose.</p><p>Km is the substrate concentration at half-maximal velocity. Hexokinase (in most tissues) has Km ~0.1 mM, far below blood glucose (~5 mM), so it runs near Vmax and a drop in glucose barely affects it — tissues like brain keep phosphorylating glucose even when glucose is low. Glucokinase (liver, pancreatic β cells) has Km ~10 mM, so its activity tracks glucose: when glucose falls from 120 to 65 mg/dL, the liver phosphorylates far less glucose, leaving it in the blood for the brain. Glucokinase is also induced by insulin and is not inhibited by G6P (hexokinase is). In β cells, glucokinase acts as the glucose sensor (MODY2 mutations).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Catalysed by glucokinase will increase but that of hexokinase will be little affected</b> — Glucokinase falls, not rises, when glucose falls.</li><li><b>Catalysed by both the enzymes will not be affected significantly</b> — Glucokinase is very sensitive to glucose changes in this range.</li><li><b>Catalysed by hexokinase will fall but that of glucokinase will be little affected</b> — This reverses the two enzymes.</li><li><b>Catalysed by both the enzymes will substantially decrease</b> — Hexokinase stays near saturation and changes little.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Glucokinase: high Km, high Vmax, induced by insulin, not inhibited by G6P (GKRP regulates it).</p>","w":["Glucokinase falls, not rises, when glucose falls.","Glucokinase is very sensitive to glucose changes in this range.","Correct — glucokinase activity falls; hexokinase is little affected.","This reverses the two enzymes.","Hexokinase stays near saturation and changes little."]},"b26":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Fed state (2–3 h after a meal): insulin ↑, glucagon ↓ → ↓cAMP → glycogen synthesis in liver and muscle.</p><p>After a high-carbohydrate meal, insulin rises and glucagon falls. Lower glucagon (and epinephrine at rest) means less adenylyl cyclase activity and lower cAMP, so PKA is inactive. Insulin activates PP1 and phosphodiesterase, so glycogen synthase is dephosphorylated (active) and glycogen phosphorylase is dephosphorylated (inactive). Both liver and resting muscle store the incoming glucose as glycogen.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Increased levels of cyclic AMP and increased liver glycogenolysis</b> — Increased cAMP and glycogenolysis describe the fasting state.</li><li><b>Decreased epinephrine levels and increased liver glycogenolysis</b> — Glycogenolysis isn't increased after a large meal.</li><li><b>Decreased AMP in muscle and decreased glycogenesis</b> — Muscle AMP is low at rest, but glycogenesis increases, not decreases.</li><li><b>Decreased Ca++ in muscle and decreased glycogen synthesis</b> — Muscle Ca²⁺ is low at rest, but glycogen synthesis increases, not decreases.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Fed = dephosphorylated enzymes (synthase on); fasting = phosphorylated (phosphorylase on).</p>","w":["Correct — decreased cAMP and increased glycogen synthesis.","Increased cAMP and glycogenolysis describe the fasting state.","Glycogenolysis isn't increased after a large meal.","Muscle AMP is low at rest, but glycogenesis increases, not decreases.","Muscle Ca²⁺ is low at rest, but glycogen synthesis increases, not decreases."]},"b27":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Young adult with early fatigue and cramps during exercise = McArdle disease (GSD V, muscle glycogen phosphorylase deficiency).</p><p>Muscle relies on its glycogen for the early, intense phase of exercise. Without myophosphorylase, it can't mobilize glycogen, causing early fatigue, cramps, and, with strenuous effort, rhabdomyolysis with myoglobinuria. Pain may ease after several minutes (\"second wind\") as blood-borne fuels arrive. Blood glucose is normal because the liver isoform works. Tarui disease (muscle PFK deficiency) is clinically similar but adds hemolytic anemia and no second wind.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Cori's disease</b> — Cori disease (debranching enzyme deficiency) presents in childhood with hepatomegaly and hypoglycemia ± muscle weakness.</li><li><b>Anderson,s disease</b> — Andersen disease (branching enzyme deficiency) causes cirrhosis in infancy.</li><li><b>Tauri,s disease</b> — Tarui disease (muscle PFK-1 deficiency) also causes exercise cramps but adds hemolytic anemia; McArdle is the classic answer.</li><li><b>Pompe's disease</b> — Pompe disease presents in infancy with cardiomegaly and hypotonia.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> McArdle: normal glucose, high CK, myoglobinuria, flat lactate on ischemic forearm test.</p>","w":["Cori disease (debranching enzyme deficiency) presents in childhood with hepatomegaly and hypoglycemia ± muscle weakness.","Correct — McArdle disease.","Andersen disease (branching enzyme deficiency) causes cirrhosis in infancy.","Tarui disease (muscle PFK-1 deficiency) also causes exercise cramps but adds hemolytic anemia; McArdle is the classic answer.","Pompe disease presents in infancy with cardiomegaly and hypotonia."]},"b28":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Insulin stimulates protein phosphatase 1, which shuts off glycogenolysis; in diabetes, PP1 isn't stimulated.</p><p>In the glycogenolysis cascade, hormones (glucagon, epinephrine) → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP → PKA → phosphorylase kinase → phosphorylase (all activated by phosphorylation). The brake is PP1, which insulin activates to dephosphorylate phosphorylase kinase and phosphorylase (inactivating them) and glycogen synthase (activating it). With insulin deficiency or resistance, PP1 activation fails, glycogen breakdown persists, and the liver adds glucose to the blood, worsening hyperglycemia.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Adenylate cyclase</b> — Adenylate cyclase is stimulated by glucagon and epinephrine, not insulin.</li><li><b>cAMP dependent protein kinase</b> — PKA is activated by cAMP, not insulin.</li><li><b>Glycogen synthase a</b> — Glycogen synthase a is a synthesis enzyme, not part of the glycogenolysis cascade.</li><li><b>Active glycogen phosphorylase kinase a</b> — Phosphorylase kinase a is activated by PKA and Ca²⁺, not by insulin.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Insulin's key enzyme actions: ↑PP1, ↑phosphodiesterase (↓cAMP).</p>","w":["Adenylate cyclase is stimulated by glucagon and epinephrine, not insulin.","PKA is activated by cAMP, not insulin.","Glycogen synthase a is a synthesis enzyme, not part of the glycogenolysis cascade.","Phosphorylase kinase a is activated by PKA and Ca²⁺, not by insulin.","Correct — PP1 is the insulin-stimulated enzyme of the cascade."]},"b29":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Andersen disease (GSD IV) = branching enzyme deficiency → can't form α-1,6 branch points → abnormal long-chain glycogen → cirrhosis.</p><p>Branching enzyme (4:6 transferase) removes a block of ~6–8 glucose residues from an α-1,4 chain and reattaches it via an α-1,6 bond, creating branches. Without it, glycogen is poorly branched with long outer chains, resembling amylopectin; it is less soluble and acts as a foreign body in hepatocytes, triggering inflammation and fibrosis. Presentation: infant failure to thrive, hepatosplenomegaly, progressive cirrhosis, and death from liver failure by about age 5 without transplant.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Alpha 1,4 -O-glycosidic linkages</b> — α-1,4 bonds are made by glycogen synthase, which works normally.</li><li><b>Alpha 1 ,Beta 2 -O-glycosidic linkages</b> — α1→β2 is the bond in sucrose.</li><li><b>Beta1,4 -O-glycosidic linkages</b> — β-1,4 bonds are found in cellulose.</li><li><b>Beta1,6 -O-glycosidic linkages</b> — β-1,6 bonds aren't present in glycogen.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Branching = Andersen (IV, cirrhosis); debranching = Cori (III, limit dextrin).</p>","w":["α-1,4 bonds are made by glycogen synthase, which works normally.","Correct — defective formation of α-1,6 branch bonds.","α1→β2 is the bond in sucrose.","β-1,4 bonds are found in cellulose.","β-1,6 bonds aren't present in glycogen."]},"b30":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Ca²⁺ released during contraction binds the calmodulin (δ) subunit of phosphorylase kinase, activating the b form without hormones.</p><p>Phosphorylase kinase has four subunits; the δ subunit is calmodulin. During muscle contraction, Ca²⁺ from the SR binds it, partially activating the unphosphorylated b form. Phosphorylase kinase then phosphorylates glycogen phosphorylase b → a, starting glycogen breakdown. This couples energy supply directly to contraction. Epinephrine (via cAMP/PKA) fully activates phosphorylase kinase by phosphorylation, adding hormonal control.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Amylo- α(1,4 →α-1,6)-transglycosylase</b> — The amylo-α-1,4→α-1,6 transglucosylase is the branching enzyme, used in glycogen synthesis.</li><li><b>Glycogen phosphorylase a</b> — Ca²⁺ doesn't bind glycogen phosphorylase itself; AMP does.</li><li><b>Glycogen phosphorylase kinase a</b> — The a form is already active (phosphorylated by PKA); Ca²⁺'s distinct role is activating the b form.</li><li><b>Protein kinase A</b> — PKA is activated by cAMP, not Ca²⁺.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Muscle glycogenolysis activators: Ca²⁺ (phosphorylase kinase), AMP (phosphorylase), epinephrine (cAMP).</p>","w":["The amylo-α-1,4→α-1,6 transglucosylase is the branching enzyme, used in glycogen synthesis.","Ca²⁺ doesn't bind glycogen phosphorylase itself; AMP does.","Correct — Ca²⁺–calmodulin activates phosphorylase kinase b.","The a form is already active (phosphorylated by PKA); Ca²⁺'s distinct role is activating the b form.","PKA is activated by cAMP, not Ca²⁺."]},"b31":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hypoglycemia + hyperuricemia + hepatomegaly + \"doll face\" = von Gierke disease (glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency).</p><p>Glucose-6-phosphatase in the liver ER is the last step of both glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis. Without it, free glucose can't be released, so patients develop fasting hypoglycemia. Accumulated G6P is diverted to glycolysis (lactic acidosis), the pentose phosphate pathway (more purines → uric acid; lactate also inhibits urate excretion), and lipid synthesis (hypertriglyceridemia, fat deposits in cheeks). Glycogen builds up in liver and kidneys. Management: frequent meals, uncooked cornstarch, avoid fructose and galactose.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>α-1,6-glucosidase</b> — α-1,6-glucosidase is the debranching activity (Cori disease), which lacks hyperuricemia and lactic acidosis.</li><li><b>Glucose 6 phosphate dehydrogenase</b> — G6PD deficiency causes oxidative hemolytic anemia, not glycogen storage.</li><li><b>α-1,4 - α1,6 trans glucosidase</b> — α-1,4→α-1,6 transglucosidase is the branching enzyme (Andersen disease, cirrhosis).</li><li><b>α-1,4- α-1,4 glucan transferase</b> — The 4:4 glucan transferase is the other activity of the debranching enzyme (Cori disease).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> GSD I = most severe hypoglycemia, with lactate, urate and lipids all high.</p>","w":["α-1,6-glucosidase is the debranching activity (Cori disease), which lacks hyperuricemia and lactic acidosis.","G6PD deficiency causes oxidative hemolytic anemia, not glycogen storage.","α-1,4→α-1,6 transglucosidase is the branching enzyme (Andersen disease, cirrhosis).","Correct — glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency.","The 4:4 glucan transferase is the other activity of the debranching enzyme (Cori disease)."]},"b32":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Infant hypotonia + cardiomegaly + normal labs + lysosomal glycogen = Pompe disease (acid maltase / acid α-glucosidase deficiency).</p><p>Acid maltase degrades glycogen inside lysosomes. When it's missing, glycogen accumulates within lysosomes of cardiac, skeletal and smooth muscle, liver and kidney. Infantile Pompe presents in the first months with floppy baby syndrome, macroglossia, feeding difficulty, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (cardiomegaly on X-ray) and respiratory failure. Blood glucose, lactate and urate are normal because cytosolic glycogen metabolism is unaffected. Treatment: enzyme replacement (alglucosidase alfa).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Glycogen phosphorylase</b> — Glycogen phosphorylase deficiency (McArdle or Hers) doesn't store glycogen in lysosomes or cause infant cardiomegaly.</li><li><b>Glycogen synthase</b> — Glycogen synthase deficiency (GSD 0) causes low glycogen stores and fasting hypoglycemia, not storage.</li><li><b>Glucosyl 4:4 glucatransferase</b> — The 4:4 glucan transferase is part of the debranching enzyme (Cori disease).</li><li><b>Glucosyl α-4:6 transferase</b> — The α-4:6 transferase is the branching enzyme (Andersen disease, cirrhosis).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Pompe = the only GSD that is a lysosomal storage disease.</p>","w":["Correct — acid maltase deficiency (Pompe disease).","Glycogen phosphorylase deficiency (McArdle or Hers) doesn't store glycogen in lysosomes or cause infant cardiomegaly.","Glycogen synthase deficiency (GSD 0) causes low glycogen stores and fasting hypoglycemia, not storage.","The 4:4 glucan transferase is part of the debranching enzyme (Cori disease).","The α-4:6 transferase is the branching enzyme (Andersen disease, cirrhosis)."]},"b33":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Glycogenin is the protein primer that starts glycogen synthesis; glycogen synthase can only extend an existing chain.</p><p>Glycogenin autoglucosylates — it transfers glucose from UDP-glucose onto one of its own tyrosine residues and extends it to a short α-1,4 chain of about 8 glucose units. Glycogen synthase then lengthens the chain, and branching enzyme adds α-1,6 branches. Glycogenin remains covalently attached at the core of each glycogen granule, so the number of glycogen particles depends on glycogenin.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>To promote de-branching of glycogen</b> — Debranching is performed by the debranching enzyme (4:4 transferase + α-1,6-glucosidase).</li><li><b>To activate specific enzyme in order to create new branch points</b> — New branch points are made by the branching enzyme.</li><li><b>To stimulate protein kinase A</b> — PKA is activated by cAMP, not glycogenin.</li><li><b>To act as protein cofactor for glycogen synthase</b> — Glycogenin provides a primer chain, not a cofactor.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Glycogen synthesis: UDP-glucose → glycogenin primer → glycogen synthase (rate-limiting) → branching enzyme.</p>","w":["Debranching is performed by the debranching enzyme (4:4 transferase + α-1,6-glucosidase).","New branch points are made by the branching enzyme.","PKA is activated by cAMP, not glycogenin.","Correct — glycogenin initiates glycogen synthesis.","Glycogenin provides a primer chain, not a cofactor."]},"b34":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Mucopolysaccharidoses = inherited deficiencies of lysosomal enzymes that degrade GAGs → GAGs accumulate in lysosomes.</p><p>Proteoglycans are continuously turned over: they are taken up into lysosomes and degraded stepwise by exoglycosidases and sulfatases. A missing enzyme blocks one step, so partially degraded GAGs (dermatan, heparan, keratan sulfate) accumulate in tissues and are excreted in urine. Features: coarse facies, skeletal dysplasia (dysostosis multiplex), hepatosplenomegaly, joint stiffness, and in some types, corneal clouding and intellectual disability. Synthesis is normal. All are autosomal recessive except Hunter (X-linked).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>An increased rate of synthesis of proteoglycans.</b> — The rate of proteoglycan synthesis is normal.</li><li><b>The synthesis of polysaccharides with an altered structure.</b> — The polysaccharides synthesized are structurally normal; the problem is breakdown.</li><li><b>The synthesis of abnormally small amounts of protein cores.</b> — Core proteins are produced in normal amounts.</li><li><b>An insufficient amount of proteolytic enzymes.</b> — The missing enzymes are glycosidases and sulfatases that degrade GAGs, not proteolytic enzymes.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Diagnosis: urinary GAGs, then enzyme assay in leukocytes or fibroblasts.</p>","w":["The rate of proteoglycan synthesis is normal.","The polysaccharides synthesized are structurally normal; the problem is breakdown.","Core proteins are produced in normal amounts.","The missing enzymes are glycosidases and sulfatases that degrade GAGs, not proteolytic enzymes.","Correct — defective degradation of proteoglycans (GAGs)."]},"b35":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hurler syndrome (MPS I) = α-L-iduronidase deficiency → corneal clouding, intellectual disability, coarse facies, hepatosplenomegaly.</p><p>α-L-iduronidase removes iduronic acid residues from dermatan sulfate and heparan sulfate. Its deficiency causes their accumulation in lysosomes. Hurler is autosomal recessive and the most severe MPS, presenting in infancy with gargoyle-like facies, developmental delay, corneal clouding, airway obstruction, hepatosplenomegaly, dysostosis multiplex and cardiac valve disease. Hunter syndrome (iduronate sulfatase) is X-linked and milder, with no corneal clouding and aggressive behavior. (Textbooks list dermatan and heparan sulfate as the accumulating GAGs.)</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Ehlers Danlos syndrome</b> — Ehlers–Danlos syndrome is a collagen disorder, not a lysosomal storage disease.</li><li><b>Rotor syndrome</b> — Rotor syndrome is a benign conjugated hyperbilirubinemia.</li><li><b>Sarcoidosis</b> — Sarcoidosis is granulomatous inflammation.</li><li><b>Paget's disease</b> — Paget disease of bone is disordered bone remodeling.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hurler = corneal clouding; Hunter = no corneal clouding, X-linked (\"hunters need clear eyes to aim for the X\").</p>","w":["Ehlers–Danlos syndrome is a collagen disorder, not a lysosomal storage disease.","Rotor syndrome is a benign conjugated hyperbilirubinemia.","Correct — α-L-iduronidase deficiency is Hurler syndrome.","Sarcoidosis is granulomatous inflammation.","Paget disease of bone is disordered bone remodeling."]},"b36":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> I-cell disease: no mannose-6-phosphate tag → lysosomal enzymes are secreted into the blood instead of reaching lysosomes.</p><p>In the cis-Golgi, N-acetylglucosamine-1-phosphotransferase adds phosphate to mannose on lysosomal enzymes; M6P receptors in the trans-Golgi then direct them to lysosomes. When the phosphotransferase is defective (mucolipidosis II, autosomal recessive), enzymes follow the default secretory pathway and are released. Plasma levels of lysosomal enzymes are high, while lysosomes fill with undegraded material (inclusion bodies). Clinical features resemble Hurler: coarse facies, gingival hyperplasia, restricted joint movement, skeletal abnormalities, clouded corneas; often fatal in childhood.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Hurler's syndrome</b> — Hurler syndrome has similar features, but plasma lysosomal enzymes aren't elevated — one enzyme is low.</li><li><b>Hunter's disease</b> — Hunter syndrome is similar (X-linked, no corneal clouding) without elevated plasma enzymes.</li><li><b>Pompe's disease</b> — Pompe disease is lysosomal glycogen storage with cardiomegaly.</li><li><b>McArdle's disease</b> — McArdle disease is a muscle glycogen storage disease of adolescents/adults.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> I-cell = ↑plasma lysosomal enzymes (missing M6P).</p>","w":["Hurler syndrome has similar features, but plasma lysosomal enzymes aren't elevated — one enzyme is low.","Hunter syndrome is similar (X-linked, no corneal clouding) without elevated plasma enzymes.","Pompe disease is lysosomal glycogen storage with cardiomegaly.","Correct — I-cell disease.","McArdle disease is a muscle glycogen storage disease of adolescents/adults."]},"b37":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> N-linked glycosylation attaches sugars to the amide nitrogen of asparagine (Asn-X-Ser/Thr) in the ER; O-linked attaches to Ser/Thr hydroxyls in the Golgi.</p><p>N-linked: a 14-sugar oligosaccharide is built on dolichol phosphate in the ER membrane, transferred en bloc to asparagine of the nascent protein, then trimmed and modified in the ER and Golgi. Tunicamycin blocks this step. O-linked: sugars are added one at a time to serine, threonine (or hydroxylysine in collagen) in the Golgi — e.g., mucins. Glycosylation affects folding, stability, targeting (M6P), and cell recognition (ABO blood groups).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Serine</b> — Serine forms O-glycosidic bonds via its hydroxyl.</li><li><b>Threonine</b> — Threonine also forms O-glycosidic bonds via its hydroxyl.</li><li><b>Glutamate</b> — Glutamate isn't a glycosylation site.</li><li><b>Valine</b> — Valine has no reactive side-chain group for glycosylation.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> N-linked = Asn (ER, dolichol); O-linked = Ser/Thr (Golgi).</p>","w":["Serine forms O-glycosidic bonds via its hydroxyl.","Threonine also forms O-glycosidic bonds via its hydroxyl.","Correct — asparagine forms N-glycosidic bonds.","Glutamate isn't a glycosylation site.","Valine has no reactive side-chain group for glycosylation."]},"b38":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Anaerobic glycolysis in RBCs: net 2 ATP; the 2 NADH from GAPDH are reoxidized by LDH, so net NADH = 0.</p><p>Investment phase: hexokinase and PFK-1 use 2 ATP. Payoff phase: each glucose gives 2 triose phosphates, so phosphoglycerate kinase and pyruvate kinase each make 2 ATP — 4 ATP gross, 2 net. GAPDH produces 2 NADH, but with no mitochondria, RBCs can't oxidize NADH via the ETC. LDH converts pyruvate to lactate using NADH, regenerating NAD⁺. Net: glucose → 2 lactate + 2 ATP. (Some 1,3-BPG may be diverted to 2,3-BPG, which bypasses phosphoglycerate kinase and lowers ATP yield.)</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>2 ATP and 2 NADH</b> — 2 NADH is the yield in cells with mitochondria (aerobic glycolysis), not the net in RBCs.</li><li><b>4 ATP and 2 NADH</b> — 4 ATP is the gross yield before subtracting the 2 ATP invested.</li><li><b>36 ATP and 0 NADH</b> — Complete oxidation (~30–38 ATP) requires mitochondria, which RBCs lack.</li><li><b>0 ATP and 2 NADH</b> — Glycolysis always yields net ATP.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Rapoport–Luebering shunt (2,3-BPG) costs the RBC ATP.</p>","w":["2 NADH is the yield in cells with mitochondria (aerobic glycolysis), not the net in RBCs.","Correct — net 2 ATP and 0 NADH.","4 ATP is the gross yield before subtracting the 2 ATP invested.","Complete oxidation (~30–38 ATP) requires mitochondria, which RBCs lack.","Glycolysis always yields net ATP."]},"b39":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Substrate-level phosphorylation in glycolysis: phosphoglycerate kinase (1,3-BPG → 3-PG) and pyruvate kinase (PEP → pyruvate).</p><p>Substrate-level phosphorylation transfers a phosphate directly from a high-energy substrate to ADP, without an electron transport chain. In glycolysis, 1,3-BPG (an acyl phosphate) and PEP (an enol phosphate) have high phosphoryl-transfer potential. The only other substrate-level phosphorylation in central metabolism is succinyl-CoA synthetase (makes GTP/ATP) in the TCA cycle.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Hexokinase and phosphofructokinase-1</b> — Hexokinase and PFK-1 consume ATP (the two priming steps).</li><li><b>Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase and enolase</b> — GAPDH makes NADH and 1,3-BPG; enolase makes PEP — neither produces ATP directly.</li><li><b>Aldolase and triose phosphate isomerase</b> — Aldolase and triose phosphate isomerase split and interconvert trioses without ATP production.</li><li><b>Phosphoglycerate mutase and lactate dehydrogenase</b> — Phosphoglycerate mutase and lactate dehydrogenase don't make ATP.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Irreversible glycolytic steps: hexokinase/glucokinase, PFK-1, pyruvate kinase.</p>","w":["Hexokinase and PFK-1 consume ATP (the two priming steps).","Correct — phosphoglycerate kinase and pyruvate kinase.","GAPDH makes NADH and 1,3-BPG; enolase makes PEP — neither produces ATP directly.","Aldolase and triose phosphate isomerase split and interconvert trioses without ATP production.","Phosphoglycerate mutase and lactate dehydrogenase don't make ATP."]},"b40":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> PFK-1 is inhibited by ATP and citrate (energy plentiful) and activated by AMP and fructose-2,6-bisphosphate.</p><p>PFK-1 catalyzes the rate-limiting, committed step of glycolysis (F6P → F-1,6-BP). Citrate accumulates when the TCA cycle is saturated and energy charge is high; it moves into the cytosol (also as the carrier of acetyl-CoA for fatty acid synthesis) and inhibits PFK-1, slowing glycolysis. In the fed liver, F2,6BP (made by insulin-activated PFK-2) overrides this inhibition to keep glycolysis running for lipogenesis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>AMP</b> — AMP is an activator of PFK-1, signaling low energy.</li><li><b>Fructose 2,6-bisphosphate</b> — Fructose-2,6-bisphosphate is the most potent activator of PFK-1.</li><li><b>Fructose 1,6-bisphosphate</b> — Fructose-1,6-bisphosphate is PFK-1's product and a feed-forward activator of pyruvate kinase.</li><li><b>Glucose-6-phosphate</b> — Glucose-6-phosphate inhibits hexokinase, not PFK-1.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Citrate: inhibits PFK-1, activates acetyl-CoA carboxylase.</p>","w":["AMP is an activator of PFK-1, signaling low energy.","Fructose-2,6-bisphosphate is the most potent activator of PFK-1.","Correct — citrate signals TCA saturation and inhibits PFK-1.","Fructose-1,6-bisphosphate is PFK-1's product and a feed-forward activator of pyruvate kinase.","Glucose-6-phosphate inhibits hexokinase, not PFK-1."]},"b41":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Pyruvate kinase deficiency: RBC ATP falls (no mitochondria to compensate) while upstream intermediates, including 2,3-BPG, rise.</p><p>RBCs depend entirely on glycolysis for ATP. Pyruvate kinase is the last ATP-producing step, so its deficiency cuts ATP supply. Without ATP, the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase and membrane maintenance fail; cells lose K⁺ and water, becoming rigid echinocytes that are removed by the spleen (extravascular hemolysis → jaundice, splenomegaly, gallstones). Blockage at PK causes upstream metabolites to accumulate, increasing 2,3-BPG and right-shifting the O₂ curve, so patients often tolerate their anemia relatively well. Autosomal recessive; no Heinz bodies because there's no oxidative injury.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase</b> — G6PD deficiency causes episodic hemolysis with Heinz bodies and bite cells after oxidant stress (fava beans, sulfa drugs, infection).</li><li><b>Hexokinase</b> — Hexokinase deficiency is upstream of 2,3-BPG, so it would lower, not raise, 2,3-BPG (left shift).</li><li><b>Spectrin</b> — Spectrin defects cause hereditary spherocytosis — spherocytes, increased osmotic fragility, normal glycolytic enzymes.</li><li><b>Lactate dehydrogenase</b> — LDH deficiency is rare and mainly causes exercise intolerance, not chronic hemolysis.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> PK deficiency: ↓ATP, ↑2,3-BPG, echinocytes, no Heinz bodies.</p>","w":["G6PD deficiency causes episodic hemolysis with Heinz bodies and bite cells after oxidant stress (fava beans, sulfa drugs, infection).","Correct — pyruvate kinase deficiency.","Hexokinase deficiency is upstream of 2,3-BPG, so it would lower, not raise, 2,3-BPG (left shift).","Spectrin defects cause hereditary spherocytosis — spherocytes, increased osmotic fragility, normal glycolytic enzymes.","LDH deficiency is rare and mainly causes exercise intolerance, not chronic hemolysis."]},"b42":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Fluoride inhibits enolase (2-PG → PEP), stopping RBC glycolysis in collected blood samples.</p><p>After blood is drawn, RBCs and WBCs keep consuming glucose (about 5–7% per hour at room temperature), which would falsely lower the measured glucose. Sodium fluoride in gray-top tubes complexes with Mg²⁺ and phosphate at enolase's active site, halting glycolysis. Fluoride is also why fluoridated toothpaste inhibits oral bacteria's glycolysis. (Other tubes: lavender = EDTA, blue = citrate, green = heparin.)</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Hexokinase</b> — Hexokinase is inhibited by its product, glucose-6-phosphate, not fluoride.</li><li><b>Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase</b> — GAPDH is inhibited by iodoacetamide (and affected by arsenate), not fluoride.</li><li><b>Pyruvate kinase</b> — Pyruvate kinase isn't the fluoride target.</li><li><b>Phosphoglycerate kinase</b> — Phosphoglycerate kinase is bypassed by arsenate, not inhibited by fluoride.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Glycolysis inhibitors: fluoride → enolase; iodoacetamide → GAPDH; arsenate → bypasses PGK.</p>","w":["Hexokinase is inhibited by its product, glucose-6-phosphate, not fluoride.","GAPDH is inhibited by iodoacetamide (and affected by arsenate), not fluoride.","Correct — fluoride inhibits enolase.","Pyruvate kinase isn't the fluoride target.","Phosphoglycerate kinase is bypassed by arsenate, not inhibited by fluoride."]},"b43":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Arsenate replaces phosphate at GAPDH → unstable 1-arseno-3-PG hydrolyzes spontaneously → the phosphoglycerate kinase ATP step is bypassed → no net ATP.</p><p>Normally GAPDH uses Pᵢ to form 1,3-BPG, which phosphoglycerate kinase uses to make ATP. With arsenate, GAPDH forms 1-arseno-3-phosphoglycerate, which falls apart nonenzymatically to 3-PG, skipping the PGK reaction. Glycolysis continues and still produces NADH, and pyruvate kinase still makes 2 ATP — but those just repay the 2 ATP invested, so net ATP = 0. This is \"uncoupling\" of glycolysis. Arsenite (trivalent) is different: it binds lipoic acid, inhibiting PDH and α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Glycolysis stops completely at hexokinase</b> — Hexokinase is unaffected; glycolysis continues.</li><li><b>Lactate production stops while ATP production increases</b> — Lactate production continues, and ATP yield falls rather than rising.</li><li><b>NADH production by GAPDH is blocked</b> — GAPDH still produces NADH; it simply uses arsenate instead of phosphate.</li><li><b>Pyruvate kinase is irreversibly inhibited</b> — Pyruvate kinase still works on PEP.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Arsenic poisoning: garlic breath, rice-water diarrhea, QT prolongation; treat with dimercaprol.</p>","w":["Hexokinase is unaffected; glycolysis continues.","Correct — glycolysis continues without net ATP production.","Lactate production continues, and ATP yield falls rather than rising.","GAPDH still produces NADH; it simply uses arsenate instead of phosphate.","Pyruvate kinase still works on PEP."]},"b44":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Iodoacetamide alkylates the active-site cysteine of GAPDH, blocking glycolysis at the triose stage.</p><p>GAPDH's mechanism uses a cysteine thiol that attacks G3P to form a thiohemiacetal, which is then oxidized by NAD⁺ to a thioester before phosphorolysis yields 1,3-BPG. Iodoacetamide (and iodoacetate) covalently modifies this –SH group, irreversibly inactivating the enzyme. G3P and DHAP accumulate, along with upstream hexose phosphates.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Aldolase</b> — Aldolase is upstream and doesn't depend on an active-site cysteine.</li><li><b>Enolase</b> — Enolase is the fluoride target.</li><li><b>Pyruvate kinase</b> — Pyruvate kinase is downstream of the block.</li><li><b>Lactate dehydrogenase</b> — LDH isn't the classic iodoacetamide target.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> GAPDH: only NADH-producing step of glycolysis; Cys in active site.</p>","w":["Aldolase is upstream and doesn't depend on an active-site cysteine.","Correct — iodoacetamide inhibits GAPDH.","Enolase is the fluoride target.","Pyruvate kinase is downstream of the block.","LDH isn't the classic iodoacetamide target."]},"b45":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> LDH (pyruvate + NADH → lactate + NAD⁺) regenerates NAD⁺ so GAPDH, and therefore glycolysis, can continue anaerobically.</p><p>Glycolysis needs a steady supply of NAD⁺ for GAPDH. With adequate O₂, NADH is reoxidized in mitochondria via the malate–aspartate or glycerol-phosphate shuttles. During a sprint, ATP demand exceeds O₂ delivery, so LDH oxidizes NADH while reducing pyruvate to lactate. Lactate is exported to the liver, where it is converted back to glucose (Cori cycle) — costing hepatic ATP — or oxidized by heart and slow fibers. Lactic acidosis occurs when lactate production exceeds clearance (shock, hypoxia, metformin toxicity in renal failure).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>To produce additional ATP</b> — LDH itself produces no ATP.</li><li><b>To convert lactate into glucose within the muscle</b> — Muscle lacks glucose-6-phosphatase and doesn't do gluconeogenesis; lactate is converted to glucose in the liver.</li><li><b>To produce NADPH for fatty acid synthesis</b> — NADPH comes from the pentose phosphate pathway and malic enzyme.</li><li><b>To transport pyruvate into mitochondria</b> — Pyruvate enters mitochondria via the mitochondrial pyruvate carrier, not LDH.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cori cycle: muscle/RBC lactate → liver glucose → back to tissues (net cost 4 ATP).</p>","w":["LDH itself produces no ATP.","Correct — LDH regenerates NAD⁺ for GAPDH.","Muscle lacks glucose-6-phosphatase and doesn't do gluconeogenesis; lactate is converted to glucose in the liver.","NADPH comes from the pentose phosphate pathway and malic enzyme.","Pyruvate enters mitochondria via the mitochondrial pyruvate carrier, not LDH."]},"b46":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Glucokinase (β-cell glucose sensor): high Km (~10 mM), high Vmax, not inhibited by G6P; heterozygous loss raises the glucose set point (MODY2).</p><p>In β-cells, glucokinase activity rises with glucose across the physiologic range, so ATP production (and thus insulin secretion via K-ATP channel closure) tracks blood glucose. A heterozygous inactivating mutation shifts the threshold upward, giving mild, stable fasting hyperglycemia from childhood that is autosomal dominant (three generations) and rarely needs treatment. Homozygous loss causes permanent neonatal diabetes; activating mutations cause hyperinsulinemic hypoglycemia. In the liver, glucokinase is regulated by glucokinase regulatory protein (sequestered in the nucleus by fructose-6-phosphate) and induced by insulin.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It has a low Km for glucose and is inhibited by glucose-6-phosphate</b> — A low Km with G6P inhibition describes hexokinases I–III.</li><li><b>It is found mainly in skeletal muscle and the brain</b> — Muscle and brain use hexokinase, not glucokinase.</li><li><b>It is activated by glucagon in the fasting state</b> — Glucokinase is induced by insulin and is low in the fasting state.</li><li><b>It phosphorylates fructose but not glucose</b> — Glucokinase phosphorylates glucose; fructose is phosphorylated by fructokinase.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> MODY2 (GCK): mild, stable hyperglycemia, often no treatment. MODY3 (HNF1A): sensitive to sulfonylureas.</p>","w":["A low Km with G6P inhibition describes hexokinases I–III.","Correct — glucokinase has a high Km and isn't inhibited by G6P.","Muscle and brain use hexokinase, not glucokinase.","Glucokinase is induced by insulin and is low in the fasting state.","Glucokinase phosphorylates glucose; fructose is phosphorylated by fructokinase."]},"b47":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> GLUT-2 (liver, β-cells, kidney, basolateral intestine) is a high-Km, high-capacity, bidirectional transporter.</p><p>Because GLUT-2's Km is high (~15–20 mM), transport rises proportionally with glucose, letting hepatocytes take up glucose after meals and release it during fasting, and letting β-cells sense glucose levels. Other transporters: GLUT-1 (RBCs, BBB — basal, low Km), GLUT-3 (neurons, placenta — lowest Km, high affinity), GLUT-4 (muscle, heart, fat — insulin-dependent), GLUT-5 (fructose, intestine and sperm). SGLT1/2 are Na⁺-dependent secondary active transporters (intestine, kidney); SGLT2 inhibitors treat diabetes.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>GLUT-1 — skeletal muscle; insulin-dependent uptake</b> — GLUT-1 is found in RBCs and the BBB and is insulin-independent.</li><li><b>GLUT-3 — intestine; fructose absorption</b> — GLUT-3 is the neuronal transporter; GLUT-5 handles fructose.</li><li><b>GLUT-4 — RBCs and blood–brain barrier; basal uptake</b> — GLUT-4 is the insulin-dependent transporter of muscle and adipose tissue.</li><li><b>GLUT-5 — neurons; high-affinity glucose uptake</b> — GLUT-5 transports fructose, not glucose into neurons.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Insulin-independent glucose uptake: \"BRICK LIPS\" — Brain, RBCs, Intestine, Cornea, Kidney, Liver, Islet β-cells, Placenta, Spermatocytes.</p>","w":["GLUT-1 is found in RBCs and the BBB and is insulin-independent.","Correct — GLUT-2 is high-capacity and bidirectional in liver and β-cells.","GLUT-3 is the neuronal transporter; GLUT-5 handles fructose.","GLUT-4 is the insulin-dependent transporter of muscle and adipose tissue.","GLUT-5 transports fructose, not glucose into neurons."]},"b48":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Contracting muscle translocates GLUT-4 to the membrane via AMPK and Ca²⁺, independent of insulin.</p><p>Insulin moves GLUT-4 through the PI3K–Akt pathway, which is impaired in type 2 diabetes. Muscle contraction uses a separate route: rising AMP activates AMP-activated protein kinase, and Ca²⁺ released from the SR activates CaMK; both trigger GLUT-4 vesicle fusion with the sarcolemma. Glucose uptake therefore increases during and after exercise even with insulin resistance. Over weeks, training also increases GLUT-4 expression and improves insulin sensitivity. Metformin also activates AMPK.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Exercise increases glucagon release, which drives glucose into muscle</b> — Glucagon doesn't drive glucose into muscle; muscle lacks glucagon receptors.</li><li><b>Exercise increases GLUT-2 in skeletal muscle</b> — Skeletal muscle doesn't use GLUT-2.</li><li><b>Exercise inhibits hexokinase in muscle</b> — Hexokinase activity increases as glucose is consumed during exercise.</li><li><b>Exercise increases hepatic glucose-6-phosphatase activity</b> — Increased hepatic glucose-6-phosphatase would release more glucose into blood, worsening hyperglycemia.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Exercise → AMPK → GLUT-4 translocation (insulin-independent).</p>","w":["Glucagon doesn't drive glucose into muscle; muscle lacks glucagon receptors.","Correct — contraction moves GLUT-4 to the membrane independently of insulin.","Skeletal muscle doesn't use GLUT-2.","Hexokinase activity increases as glucose is consumed during exercise.","Increased hepatic glucose-6-phosphatase would release more glucose into blood, worsening hyperglycemia."]},"b49":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Only liver (and kidney) has glucose-6-phosphatase, so only they can release free glucose from glycogen into the blood.</p><p>Glycogenolysis in both tissues: glycogen phosphorylase → glucose-1-phosphate → phosphoglucomutase → glucose-6-phosphate. G6P is charged and can't cross the membrane. Glucose-6-phosphatase in the ER of hepatocytes removes the phosphate, and free glucose exits via GLUT-2. Muscle lacks this enzyme, so its glycogen is a private fuel used for its own glycolysis — it can't directly support blood glucose. (Muscle can indirectly help via lactate and alanine sent to the liver.)</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Muscle lacks glycogen phosphorylase</b> — Muscle does have glycogen phosphorylase (its absence is McArdle disease).</li><li><b>Muscle lacks phosphoglucomutase</b> — Muscle has phosphoglucomutase.</li><li><b>Liver glycogen is stored in lysosomes</b> — Glycogen is a cytosolic store in both tissues; only a small amount is degraded in lysosomes.</li><li><b>Muscle glycogen has no α-1,6 branches</b> — Muscle glycogen is also branched with α-1,6 bonds.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Muscle glycogen serves the muscle; liver glycogen serves the blood.</p>","w":["Muscle does have glycogen phosphorylase (its absence is McArdle disease).","Correct — muscle lacks glucose-6-phosphatase.","Muscle has phosphoglucomutase.","Glycogen is a cytosolic store in both tissues; only a small amount is degraded in lysosomes.","Muscle glycogen is also branched with α-1,6 bonds."]},"b50":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> PKA phosphorylation: glycogen synthase → inactive (b form); glycogen phosphorylase → active (a form).</p><p>Glucagon → Gs → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP → PKA. PKA phosphorylates glycogen synthase directly (inactivating it) and phosphorylates phosphorylase kinase, which then phosphorylates glycogen phosphorylase (activating it). This reciprocal regulation prevents a futile cycle of simultaneous synthesis and breakdown. Insulin activates PP1, which dephosphorylates both enzymes, reversing the effects. Also note that for phosphorylase the active form is \"a\"; for synthase, the active form is also \"a\" — but synthase a is the dephosphorylated form.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Glycogen synthase phosphorylated and active; phosphorylase phosphorylated and inactive</b> — Phosphorylated glycogen synthase is inactive, and phosphorylated phosphorylase is active.</li><li><b>Glycogen synthase dephosphorylated and active; phosphorylase dephosphorylated and inactive</b> — Dephosphorylated synthase active and dephosphorylated phosphorylase inactive describes the fed (insulin) state.</li><li><b>Both enzymes dephosphorylated and active</b> — Phosphorylase is active only when phosphorylated (a form).</li><li><b>Both enzymes phosphorylated and inactive</b> — Phosphorylated phosphorylase is active, not inactive.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Glucagon/epinephrine (PKA) → breakdown; insulin (PP1) → storage.</p>","w":["Phosphorylated glycogen synthase is inactive, and phosphorylated phosphorylase is active.","Correct — synthase inactive and phosphorylase active when phosphorylated.","Dephosphorylated synthase active and dephosphorylated phosphorylase inactive describes the fed (insulin) state.","Phosphorylase is active only when phosphorylated (a form).","Phosphorylated phosphorylase is active, not inactive."]},"b51":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cori disease (GSD III) = debranching enzyme deficiency → abnormal glycogen with short outer branches (limit dextrin) in liver and muscle.</p><p>Glycogen phosphorylase removes glucose from outer chains until it gets within four residues of a branch point. The debranching enzyme (a bifunctional protein with 4:4 transferase and α-1,6-glucosidase activities) then moves three residues to another chain and releases the branch glucose. Without it, glycogen breakdown stops at branch points, leaving limit dextrin. Because gluconeogenesis is intact, hypoglycemia is milder than in von Gierke, and lactate and uric acid are normal. Muscle involvement causes weakness and elevated CK.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Glucose-6-phosphatase</b> — Glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency (von Gierke) causes lactic acidosis and hyperuricemia with more severe hypoglycemia.</li><li><b>Branching enzyme</b> — Branching enzyme deficiency produces long unbranched chains and cirrhosis.</li><li><b>Muscle glycogen phosphorylase</b> — Muscle phosphorylase deficiency (McArdle) doesn't cause hepatomegaly or hypoglycemia.</li><li><b>Lysosomal acid α-glucosidase</b> — Lysosomal acid α-glucosidase deficiency (Pompe) causes lysosomal glycogen storage and cardiomegaly.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cori = von Gierke-like but milder, normal lactate, + muscle involvement, limit dextrin.</p>","w":["Glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency (von Gierke) causes lactic acidosis and hyperuricemia with more severe hypoglycemia.","Correct — debranching enzyme deficiency.","Branching enzyme deficiency produces long unbranched chains and cirrhosis.","Muscle phosphorylase deficiency (McArdle) doesn't cause hepatomegaly or hypoglycemia.","Lysosomal acid α-glucosidase deficiency (Pompe) causes lysosomal glycogen storage and cardiomegaly."]},"b52":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Skeletal muscle has no glucagon receptors; muscle glycogenolysis is driven by epinephrine, Ca²⁺ and AMP.</p><p>Glucagon targets the liver to maintain blood glucose during fasting. Muscle glycogen serves muscle, so its breakdown is controlled by local needs and the fight-or-flight hormone: epinephrine (β₂ → Gs → cAMP → PKA → phosphorylase kinase), Ca²⁺ released during contraction (binds the calmodulin subunit of phosphorylase kinase), and AMP from ATP use (allosterically activates phosphorylase). Insulin promotes storage.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Epinephrine</b> — Epinephrine acts on muscle via β-adrenergic receptors and stimulates glycogenolysis.</li><li><b>Both hormones contribute equally</b> — Only epinephrine (not glucagon) acts on muscle.</li><li><b>Neither — only insulin acts on muscle glycogen</b> — Insulin promotes glycogen storage; adrenergic and Ca²⁺/AMP signals drive breakdown.</li><li><b>Norepinephrine</b> — Circulating norepinephrine can also act via adrenergic receptors (mainly epinephrine on β₂).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Glucagon → liver only; epinephrine → liver and muscle.</p>","w":["Epinephrine acts on muscle via β-adrenergic receptors and stimulates glycogenolysis.","Correct — glucagon doesn't act on muscle glycogen.","Only epinephrine (not glucagon) acts on muscle.","Insulin promotes glycogen storage; adrenergic and Ca²⁺/AMP signals drive breakdown.","Circulating norepinephrine can also act via adrenergic receptors (mainly epinephrine on β₂)."]},"b53":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hunter syndrome (MPS II) = X-linked iduronate-2-sulfatase deficiency; like Hurler but milder, no corneal clouding, aggressive behavior.</p><p>Iduronate-2-sulfatase removes sulfate groups from iduronic acid in dermatan sulfate and heparan sulfate; its deficiency causes their lysosomal accumulation and excretion in urine. Clinical features: coarse facies, hepatosplenomegaly, joint stiffness, dysostosis multiplex, hearing loss, and often behavioral problems; characteristic pebbly skin lesions may appear on the back. It's the only X-linked MPS — explaining an affected boy and his maternal uncle.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>α-L-iduronidase</b> — α-L-iduronidase deficiency is Hurler syndrome — autosomal recessive with corneal clouding.</li><li><b>Acid α-glucosidase</b> — Acid α-glucosidase deficiency is Pompe disease (lysosomal glycogen storage).</li><li><b>β-glucocerebrosidase</b> — β-glucocerebrosidase deficiency is Gaucher disease (glucocerebroside storage).</li><li><b>Hexosaminidase A</b> — Hexosaminidase A deficiency is Tay–Sachs disease (GM2 ganglioside storage).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hunter: X-linked, clear corneas, aggressive behavior.</p>","w":["α-L-iduronidase deficiency is Hurler syndrome — autosomal recessive with corneal clouding.","Correct — iduronate sulfatase deficiency (Hunter syndrome).","Acid α-glucosidase deficiency is Pompe disease (lysosomal glycogen storage).","β-glucocerebrosidase deficiency is Gaucher disease (glucocerebroside storage).","Hexosaminidase A deficiency is Tay–Sachs disease (GM2 ganglioside storage)."]},"b54":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hyaluronic acid: the only GAG that is unsulfated and not attached to a core protein; it forms the backbone of aggrecan aggregates and lubricates joints.</p><p>GAGs are long, unbranched polysaccharides of repeating disaccharides (amino sugar + uronic acid; keratan sulfate uses galactose instead of uronic acid). Their negative charges attract Na⁺ and water, forming hydrated gels that resist compression. Hyaluronic acid is huge, made at the plasma membrane by hyaluronan synthases (not in the Golgi), and extruded directly into the matrix. In cartilage, aggrecan core proteins (bearing chondroitin and keratan sulfate) attach to a hyaluronic acid backbone via link proteins. It also fills the vitreous humor and supports wound healing.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Heparin</b> — Heparin is highly sulfated and attached to a core protein (serglycin).</li><li><b>Keratan sulfate</b> — Keratan sulfate is sulfated, lacks uronic acid, and is bound to a core protein.</li><li><b>Chondroitin sulfate</b> — Chondroitin sulfate is sulfated and bound to the aggrecan core protein.</li><li><b>Dermatan sulfate</b> — Dermatan sulfate is sulfated and bound to core proteins.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hyaluronic acid = no sulfate, no core protein, made at the cell surface.</p>","w":["Heparin is highly sulfated and attached to a core protein (serglycin).","Keratan sulfate is sulfated, lacks uronic acid, and is bound to a core protein.","Correct — hyaluronic acid.","Chondroitin sulfate is sulfated and bound to the aggrecan core protein.","Dermatan sulfate is sulfated and bound to core proteins."]},"b55":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Procollagen peptidases cleave the N- and C-terminal propeptides outside the cell, converting procollagen to tropocollagen.</p><p>Intracellular steps: translation of pre-pro-α chains on RER; hydroxylation of proline and lysine (vitamin C); glycosylation of hydroxylysine; disulfide bonds between C-propeptides; triple helix formation; secretion. Extracellular steps: propeptide cleavage by procollagen N- and C-peptidases (soluble procollagen → insoluble tropocollagen), self-assembly into fibrils, and covalent cross-linking by lysyl oxidase (copper). The propeptides keep collagen soluble inside the cell, preventing premature fibril formation.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Hydroxylation of proline and lysine residues</b> — Hydroxylation of proline and lysine occurs in the RER.</li><li><b>Glycosylation of hydroxylysine residues</b> — Glycosylation of hydroxylysine occurs in the RER and Golgi.</li><li><b>Formation of the procollagen triple helix</b> — The procollagen triple helix forms in the RER.</li><li><b>Formation of interchain disulfide bonds in the propeptides</b> — Interchain disulfide bonds in the propeptides form inside the cell.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> N-peptidase deficiency → Ehlers–Danlos dermatosparaxis type.</p>","w":["Hydroxylation of proline and lysine occurs in the RER.","Glycosylation of hydroxylysine occurs in the RER and Golgi.","The procollagen triple helix forms in the RER.","Correct — propeptide cleavage occurs outside the fibroblast.","Interchain disulfide bonds in the propeptides form inside the cell."]},"b56":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Glycine (side chain = H) is the only residue small enough to occupy the crowded center of the collagen triple helix at every third position.</p><p>Collagen's sequence is (Gly-X-Y)n, with X often proline and Y often hydroxyproline. In the triple helix, every third residue of each chain points into the tightly packed center where the three chains meet. Only glycine fits. A bulkier residue (cysteine, serine, alanine) distorts the helix near the substitution. Because each triple helix contains three chains, a single mutant allele can disrupt most molecules (dominant-negative effect), causing severe osteogenesis imperfecta — more severe than a null allele, which just halves production.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Its side chain forms hydrogen bonds that hold the helix together</b> — Stabilizing hydrogen bonds come mainly from hydroxyproline; glycine has no side chain to form them.</li><li><b>It is the site of hydroxylation by prolyl hydroxylase</b> — Prolyl hydroxylase acts on proline in the Y position, not glycine.</li><li><b>It forms cross-links with lysine in adjacent molecules</b> — Lysine/hydroxylysine residues form cross-links, not glycine.</li><li><b>It carries the carbohydrate attachments</b> — Sugars attach to hydroxylysine residues, not glycine.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Glycine substitutions → dominant-negative → severe OI.</p>","w":["Stabilizing hydrogen bonds come mainly from hydroxyproline; glycine has no side chain to form them.","Correct — glycine is the only residue that fits the helix center.","Prolyl hydroxylase acts on proline in the Y position, not glycine.","Lysine/hydroxylysine residues form cross-links, not glycine.","Sugars attach to hydroxylysine residues, not glycine."]},"b57":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Menkes disease (X-linked ATP7A defect) → copper deficiency → impaired lysyl oxidase cross-linking → fragile connective tissue.</p><p>ATP7A pumps copper out of intestinal cells into blood and into Golgi enzymes. Its defect causes systemic copper deficiency even though copper accumulates in enterocytes. Copper-dependent enzymes fail: lysyl oxidase (collagen/elastin cross-links → tortuous vessels, bladder diverticula, bone changes), tyrosinase (hypopigmentation), cytochrome c oxidase (neurodegeneration, hypotonia, seizures) and others in hair (sparse, brittle, kinky \"steely\" hair). Serum copper and ceruloplasmin are low. Wilson disease (ATP7B) is the opposite: copper accumulates in liver and brain.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Prolyl hydroxylase</b> — Prolyl hydroxylase requires vitamin C and Fe²⁺, not copper.</li><li><b>Lysyl hydroxylase</b> — Lysyl hydroxylase is also iron and vitamin C dependent.</li><li><b>Procollagen N-peptidase</b> — Procollagen N-peptidase isn't copper dependent.</li><li><b>Elastase</b> — Elastase is a protease that degrades elastin, not a synthesis enzyme.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Menkes (ATP7A, ↓copper) vs Wilson (ATP7B, ↑copper in liver, brain, cornea).</p>","w":["Prolyl hydroxylase requires vitamin C and Fe²⁺, not copper.","Lysyl hydroxylase is also iron and vitamin C dependent.","Correct — reduced lysyl oxidase activity due to copper deficiency.","Procollagen N-peptidase isn't copper dependent.","Elastase is a protease that degrades elastin, not a synthesis enzyme."]},"b58":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Elastin: little hydroxyproline, no hydroxylysine, no Gly-X-Y triple helix; cross-linked by desmosine (lysyl oxidase).</p><p>Elastin is secreted as tropoelastin, rich in small nonpolar residues (Gly, Ala, Val, Pro), and assembles on a scaffold of fibrillin microfibrils. Lysyl oxidase converts lysines to allysines, which condense into desmosine/isodesmosine cross-links linking up to four chains. The resulting rubber-like network stretches and recoils, essential in lungs, large arteries (aorta), skin, vocal cords and elastic ligaments (ligamentum flavum). Collagen, by contrast, provides tensile strength.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Elastin has a regular Gly-X-Y repeat and forms a triple helix</b> — A regular Gly-X-Y repeat and triple helix are features of collagen.</li><li><b>Elastin is rich in hydroxylysine and is heavily glycosylated</b> — Hydroxylysine and glycosylation are features of collagen.</li><li><b>Elastin is cross-linked by disulfide bonds made by lysyl hydroxylase</b> — Desmosine cross-links are formed by lysyl oxidase, not disulfides made by lysyl hydroxylase.</li><li><b>Elastin is mainly found in bone and tendon</b> — Elastin is abundant in lungs, arteries, skin and elastic ligaments; bone and tendon are mainly type I collagen.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Elastin is broken down by elastase (inhibited by α1-antitrypsin).</p>","w":["A regular Gly-X-Y repeat and triple helix are features of collagen.","Hydroxylysine and glycosylation are features of collagen.","Correct — elastin has little hydroxyproline, no hydroxylysine and desmosine cross-links.","Desmosine cross-links are formed by lysyl oxidase, not disulfides made by lysyl hydroxylase.","Elastin is abundant in lungs, arteries, skin and elastic ligaments; bone and tendon are mainly type I collagen."]},"b59":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Smoking oxidizes Met358 in α1-antitrypsin's reactive loop, inactivating it, while also recruiting elastase-releasing neutrophils.</p><p>α1-antitrypsin acts as a \"suicide substrate\": neutrophil elastase cleaves its reactive loop at Met358, and the enzyme becomes trapped in an inactive complex. Oxidants from cigarette smoke and activated phagocytes convert Met358 to methionine sulfoxide, which elastase no longer recognizes, so α1AT can't inhibit it. Smoking also increases neutrophils and macrophages in the lungs, adding more protease. The protease–antiprotease imbalance destroys alveolar walls, typically causing centriacinar (upper-lobe) emphysema in smokers, versus panacinar (lower-lobe) emphysema in α1AT deficiency.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It reduces hepatic transcription of the α1-antitrypsin gene</b> — Smoking doesn't significantly reduce hepatic transcription of the α1AT gene.</li><li><b>It activates α1-antitrypsin, increasing elastase inhibition</b> — Smoking inactivates α1AT rather than activating it.</li><li><b>It causes α1-antitrypsin to polymerize in hepatocytes</b> — Polymerization of α1AT in hepatocytes is due to the PiZ mutation, not smoking.</li><li><b>It converts elastin to collagen</b> — Elastin is destroyed, not converted into collagen.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Smoking → centriacinar (upper lobes); α1AT deficiency → panacinar (lower lobes).</p>","w":["Smoking doesn't significantly reduce hepatic transcription of the α1AT gene.","Correct — smoke oxidizes the active-site methionine of α1-antitrypsin.","Smoking inactivates α1AT rather than activating it.","Polymerization of α1AT in hepatocytes is due to the PiZ mutation, not smoking.","Elastin is destroyed, not converted into collagen."]},"b60":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Marfan syndrome = FBN1 (fibrillin-1) mutation; fibrillin forms microfibrils that scaffold elastin and regulate TGF-β.</p><p>Fibrillin-1 microfibrils surround and guide elastin deposition and sequester latent TGF-β in the matrix. Defective fibrillin weakens elastic tissue and increases TGF-β signaling. Features: tall stature with long limbs (arm span &gt; height), arachnodactyly, pectus deformity, joint hypermobility, upward lens subluxation (ciliary zonules), mitral valve prolapse, and aortic root dilation with risk of dissection (cystic medial degeneration). Losartan (an ARB that reduces TGF-β signaling) and β-blockers slow aortic dilation. Autosomal dominant.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It cross-links tropoelastin via desmosine</b> — Desmosine cross-links are made by lysyl oxidase.</li><li><b>It inhibits neutrophil elastase</b> — Inhibition of neutrophil elastase is the role of α1-antitrypsin.</li><li><b>It hydroxylates proline residues in elastin</b> — Proline hydroxylation is done by prolyl hydroxylase.</li><li><b>It is the core protein of aggrecan</b> — Aggrecan's core protein is a separate cartilage proteoglycan protein.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Marfan: lens subluxes upward; homocystinuria: downward.</p>","w":["Desmosine cross-links are made by lysyl oxidase.","Correct — fibrillin microfibrils act as a scaffold for elastin.","Inhibition of neutrophil elastase is the role of α1-antitrypsin.","Proline hydroxylation is done by prolyl hydroxylase.","Aggrecan's core protein is a separate cartilage proteoglycan protein."]},"e1":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Mesenchyme = embryonic (mostly mesodermal) loose connective tissue whose cells are multipotent — they can become fibroblasts, chondroblasts, osteoblasts and more.</p><p>In the embryo, mesoderm (and in the head, neural crest) forms mesenchyme: loosely arranged, star-shaped cells in abundant ground substance. Depending on local signals, mesenchymal cells differentiate into fibroblasts (connective tissue proper), chondroblasts (cartilage), osteoblasts (bone), adipocytes, smooth muscle and others. In adults, mesenchymal stem cells persist in marrow and around vessels. For the skeleton, mesenchyme condenses to form either a cartilage model (endochondral ossification) or a membrane that ossifies directly (intramembranous ossification).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Myoblasts</b> — Myoblasts are already committed to the muscle lineage.</li><li><b>Ectodermal cells</b> — Ectoderm gives rise to the epidermis and nervous system, not general connective tissue.</li><li><b>Neural crest cells</b> — Neural crest cells are a separate population derived from ectoderm (though they form some head mesenchyme).</li><li><b>Osteoclasts</b> — Osteoclasts resorb bone and come from the monocyte/macrophage lineage.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Mesenchyme → connective tissue, cartilage, bone, blood vessels, muscle.</p>","w":["Correct — mesenchymal cells are the multipotent connective tissue precursors.","Myoblasts are already committed to the muscle lineage.","Ectoderm gives rise to the epidermis and nervous system, not general connective tissue.","Neural crest cells are a separate population derived from ectoderm (though they form some head mesenchyme).","Osteoclasts resorb bone and come from the monocyte/macrophage lineage."]},"e2":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cartilage formation sequence (notes): compaction → perichondrium → chondroblasts → matrix → chondrocytes.</p><p>Think of it logically. First, mesenchymal cells must gather (compaction/condensation). A boundary (perichondrium) then forms around this cluster. Inside, mesenchymal cells differentiate into chondroblasts, which secrete matrix (type II collagen and proteoglycans) around themselves. As they become trapped in lacunae within their own matrix, they mature into chondrocytes. Cells can't become chondrocytes before matrix exists, and matrix can't exist before the cells that make it.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Compaction → chondrocytes → matrix → chondroblasts → perichondrium</b> — Chondrocytes are the final, mature stage, so they can't come before matrix and chondroblasts.</li><li><b>Chondrocytes → chondroblasts → perichondrium → compaction → matrix</b> — This sequence runs backwards.</li><li><b>Perichondrium → compaction → chondrocytes → chondroblasts → matrix</b> — The perichondrium forms around a condensation, so compaction must come first.</li><li><b>Matrix → compaction → chondroblasts → perichondrium → chondrocytes</b> — Matrix is laid down by chondroblasts, so they must come before it.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Chondroblast (makes matrix) → chondrocyte (trapped in lacuna, maintains matrix).</p>","w":["Chondrocytes are the final, mature stage, so they can't come before matrix and chondroblasts.","Correct — this is the order of cartilage formation.","This sequence runs backwards.","The perichondrium forms around a condensation, so compaction must come first.","Matrix is laid down by chondroblasts, so they must come before it."]},"e3":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The perichondrium is the boundary of dense connective tissue that forms around a mesenchymal condensation and covers cartilage.</p><p>The perichondrium has an outer fibrous layer (fibroblasts, type I collagen, blood vessels) and an inner chondrogenic layer that can produce new chondroblasts (appositional growth). Because cartilage itself is avascular, the vessels of the perichondrium supply nutrients by diffusion. In endochondral ossification, osteoprogenitor cells appear in this same perichondrium, which then becomes the periosteum once bone forms beneath it. Articular cartilage and fibrocartilage lack a perichondrium.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Epiphyseal line</b> — The epiphyseal line is the adult remnant of the growth plate.</li><li><b>Primary areola</b> — Primary areolae are cavities left by dead chondrocytes during endochondral ossification.</li><li><b>Periosteum</b> — The periosteum surrounds bone, not developing cartilage (it forms later from the perichondrium).</li><li><b>Endosteum</b> — The endosteum lines the marrow cavity of bone.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Perichondrium → periosteum when ossification begins.</p>","w":["The epiphyseal line is the adult remnant of the growth plate.","Primary areolae are cavities left by dead chondrocytes during endochondral ossification.","Correct — the perichondrium forms around the condensation.","The periosteum surrounds bone, not developing cartilage (it forms later from the perichondrium).","The endosteum lines the marrow cavity of bone."]},"e4":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cartilage is avascular; chondrocytes are nourished by diffusion through the hydrated (jelly-like) matrix.</p><p>Nutrients and oxygen diffuse from perichondrial vessels (or synovial fluid in articular cartilage) through the matrix, which is mostly water held by negatively charged proteoglycans. This limits cartilage thickness and explains its poor capacity to heal. In endochondral ossification, once the matrix calcifies, diffusion stops and chondrocytes die — a key step that creates spaces for bone formation.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Through capillaries inside each lacuna</b> — Cartilage has no capillaries within it or within lacunae.</li><li><b>Through canaliculi connecting the cells</b> — Canaliculi are channels connecting osteocytes in bone.</li><li><b>From Haversian canals</b> — Haversian canals are a feature of compact bone.</li><li><b>From bone marrow</b> — Bone marrow is found in bone, not cartilage.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Avascular → poor repair; articular cartilage injuries heal slowly.</p>","w":["Cartilage has no capillaries within it or within lacunae.","Canaliculi are channels connecting osteocytes in bone.","Haversian canals are a feature of compact bone.","Correct — nutrients diffuse through the matrix.","Bone marrow is found in bone, not cartilage."]},"e5":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Intramembranous ossification forms the skull vault (flat bones) — plus the mandible and clavicle; endochondral ossification forms long bones, vertebrae and the skull base.</p><p>Flat bones of the cranial vault (frontal, parietal, parts of occipital and temporal), most facial bones, and much of the clavicle and mandible ossify directly from mesenchymal membranes. The cartilage-based route builds most of the axial and appendicular skeleton. Cleidocranial dysplasia (RUNX2 defect) affects membranous bones — absent clavicles and open fontanelles — while achondroplasia (FGFR3) affects endochondral growth.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Long bones of the limbs</b> — Long bones of the limbs form on a cartilage model.</li><li><b>Vertebrae</b> — Vertebrae form on a cartilage model.</li><li><b>Bones of the skull base</b> — The skull base (chondrocranium) forms on a cartilage model.</li><li><b>Bones of the hands and feet</b> — Bones of the hands and feet are long (tubular) bones formed on cartilage models.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Membranous: skull vault, face, clavicle. Endochondral: long bones, vertebrae, pelvis, skull base.</p>","w":["Long bones of the limbs form on a cartilage model.","Vertebrae form on a cartilage model.","The skull base (chondrocranium) forms on a cartilage model.","Bones of the hands and feet are long (tubular) bones formed on cartilage models.","Correct — the skull vault and flat bones form by membranous ossification."]},"e6":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Membranous ossification: mesenchyme → osteoblasts → osteoid → mineralization, with no cartilage stage.</p><p>Mesenchymal cells condense in a vascular membrane and differentiate directly into osteoblasts (RUNX2-driven). Osteoblasts secrete osteoid (type I collagen-rich unmineralized matrix), which then mineralizes with hydroxyapatite. Trapped osteoblasts become osteocytes. Trabeculae fuse into woven bone, which is remodeled into lamellar bone, and the surrounding mesenchyme becomes periosteum. This forms the flat bones of the skull.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>A hyaline cartilage model is formed first and then replaced by bone</b> — Forming a hyaline cartilage model first describes endochondral ossification.</li><li><b>Osteoid develops into osteoclasts, which lay down new bone</b> — Osteoid doesn't become osteoclasts; osteoclasts come from the monocyte/macrophage line and resorb rather than lay down bone.</li><li><b>Chondrocytes die, leaving primary areolae that are invaded by capillaries</b> — Dying chondrocytes and primary areolae belong to endochondral ossification.</li><li><b>It forms the long bones and the vertebrae</b> — Long bones and vertebrae form by endochondral ossification.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> No cartilage template in membranous ossification.</p>","w":["Correct — this is the sequence of membranous ossification.","Forming a hyaline cartilage model first describes endochondral ossification.","Osteoid doesn't become osteoclasts; osteoclasts come from the monocyte/macrophage line and resorb rather than lay down bone.","Dying chondrocytes and primary areolae belong to endochondral ossification.","Long bones and vertebrae form by endochondral ossification."]},"e7":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Osteoclasts derive from the monocyte/macrophage (hematopoietic) lineage; osteoblasts derive from mesenchyme.</p><p>Osteoclast precursors fuse into large, multinucleated cells under signals from osteoblasts: M-CSF and RANKL (binding RANK on precursors). Osteoprotegerin (OPG), a decoy receptor, blocks RANKL. Active osteoclasts seal onto bone with a ruffled border, secrete H⁺ (via carbonic anhydrase II and proton pumps) to dissolve mineral and cathepsin K to digest collagen, forming resorption pits (Howship lacunae). Osteoid is unmineralized matrix, not a cell.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Osteoid</b> — Osteoid is matrix, not a cell, so it can't develop into osteoclasts.</li><li><b>Osteoblasts</b> — Osteoblasts are mesenchymal bone-building cells; they signal to osteoclasts but don't become them.</li><li><b>Chondrocytes</b> — Chondrocytes form and maintain cartilage.</li><li><b>Osteocytes</b> — Osteocytes are osteoblasts trapped in mineralized matrix.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> RANKL (from osteoblasts) → osteoclast formation; denosumab blocks RANKL.</p>","w":["Osteoid is matrix, not a cell, so it can't develop into osteoclasts.","Correct — osteoclasts come from the monocyte/macrophage line.","Osteoblasts are mesenchymal bone-building cells; they signal to osteoclasts but don't become them.","Chondrocytes form and maintain cartilage.","Osteocytes are osteoblasts trapped in mineralized matrix."]},"e8":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> In endochondral ossification, the osteoprogenitor region of the perichondrium brings in osteoblasts, blood capillaries and osteoclasts.</p><p>Once a cartilage model exists, cells in the perichondrium around the shaft become osteoprogenitors, forming a periosteal bone collar (the perichondrium becomes periosteum). A periosteal bud then invades the calcified cartilage: capillaries (vascular supply), osteoblasts (to lay down bone on cartilage remnants), and osteoclasts (to remove matrix and remodel). This establishes the primary ossification center in the diaphysis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Mesenchymal cells, osteoid and keratinocytes</b> — Keratinocytes are epidermal cells and play no role in bone formation.</li><li><b>Chondroblasts, lymphatics and fibroblasts</b> — Chondroblasts, lymphatics and fibroblasts don't build or remodel bone.</li><li><b>Osteocytes, nerves and adipocytes</b> — Osteocytes appear later as mature bone cells; nerves and adipocytes aren't the key components.</li><li><b>Myoblasts, capillaries and chondrocytes</b> — Myoblasts form muscle; chondrocytes are already present in the cartilage model.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Periosteal bud = blood vessels + osteoprogenitors + osteoclast precursors.</p>","w":["Keratinocytes are epidermal cells and play no role in bone formation.","Chondroblasts, lymphatics and fibroblasts don't build or remodel bone.","Correct — osteoblasts, capillaries and osteoclasts are brought in.","Osteocytes appear later as mature bone cells; nerves and adipocytes aren't the key components.","Myoblasts form muscle; chondrocytes are already present in the cartilage model."]},"e9":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Once cartilage matrix calcifies, nutrients can't diffuse to chondrocytes, so they die, leaving primary areolae.</p><p>In the center of the cartilage model, chondrocytes enlarge (hypertrophy) and the matrix around them calcifies. Because cartilage is avascular and relies on diffusion, the calcified matrix cuts off nutrition, and the chondrocytes undergo apoptosis. The empty lacunae become primary areolae, which are then invaded by capillaries and osteoclasts.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Primary areolae; they are infected</b> — Chondrocytes die from loss of diffusion, not infection.</li><li><b>Secondary areolae; osteoclasts digest them</b> — Secondary areolae form later, when the walls of primary areolae are eroded.</li><li><b>Haversian canals; blood vessels compress them</b> — Haversian canals form later in mature bone.</li><li><b>Lacunae; they become osteocytes</b> — Lacunae in bone contain living osteocytes; these cavities result from chondrocyte death.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hypertrophy → calcification → chondrocyte death → vascular invasion.</p>","w":["Chondrocytes die from loss of diffusion, not infection.","Secondary areolae form later, when the walls of primary areolae are eroded.","Haversian canals form later in mature bone.","Correct — primary areolae form because nutrients can no longer diffuse.","Lacunae in bone contain living osteocytes; these cavities result from chondrocyte death."]},"e10":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Capillaries and osteoclasts invade the primary areolae and erode their walls, merging them into larger secondary areolae.</p><p>After chondrocytes die, the periosteal bud brings in capillaries and osteoclasts. Osteoclasts break down the thin calcified walls between neighboring primary areolae, creating larger spaces (secondary areolae). Osteoblasts line these spaces and deposit osteoid on the remaining calcified cartilage spicules, producing mixed spicules (a calcified cartilage core with a bony covering). The cavities eventually contribute to marrow spaces.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Osteoblasts dissolve the walls with osteoid</b> — Osteoblasts build matrix rather than dissolving it.</li><li><b>The perichondrium contracts</b> — The perichondrium doesn't contract to form cavities.</li><li><b>Calcium is withdrawn from the matrix by osteocytes</b> — Osteocytes don't create these cavities.</li><li><b>Chondrocytes divide and push the walls apart</b> — The chondrocytes have already died.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Mixed spicule = calcified cartilage (basophilic) core + bone (eosinophilic) covering.</p>","w":["Osteoblasts build matrix rather than dissolving it.","The perichondrium doesn't contract to form cavities.","Osteocytes don't create these cavities.","The chondrocytes have already died.","Correct — capillaries and osteoclasts eat away the walls of primary areolae."]},"e11":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> An osteoblast completely surrounded by its own mineralized matrix becomes an osteocyte.</p><p>Osteoblasts line bone surfaces and secrete osteoid. Some become trapped in the matrix they produce and mature into osteocytes, sitting in lacunae and connected to each other by long processes in canaliculi (via gap junctions). Osteocytes are the most abundant bone cells; they sense mechanical strain and regulate remodeling (they secrete sclerostin, which inhibits bone formation, and RANKL). Osteoblasts that are not trapped either become flattened bone-lining cells or undergo apoptosis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It becomes a chondroblast</b> — Chondroblasts make cartilage, not bone.</li><li><b>It dies, forming a new primary areola</b> — Primary areolae come from dead chondrocytes, not osteoblasts.</li><li><b>It returns to the perichondrium</b> — Osteoblasts don't migrate back to the perichondrium.</li><li><b>It becomes an osteoclast</b> — Osteoclasts come from the monocyte/macrophage lineage.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Osteocytes = mechanosensors; sclerostin inhibitor romosozumab treats osteoporosis.</p>","w":["Correct — an entrapped osteoblast becomes an osteocyte.","Chondroblasts make cartilage, not bone.","Primary areolae come from dead chondrocytes, not osteoblasts.","Osteoblasts don't migrate back to the perichondrium.","Osteoclasts come from the monocyte/macrophage lineage."]},"e12":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Osteons (Haversian systems) are built as osteoblasts lay down matrix in concentric lamellae around a central canal, becoming osteocytes layer by layer.</p><p>In compact bone, a tunnel is created (by osteoclasts during remodeling), and osteoblasts line its wall, depositing successive concentric lamellae inward. Each layer traps some osteoblasts as osteocytes. The remaining central space becomes the Haversian canal, containing blood vessels and nerves. Volkmann canals run perpendicular and connect Haversian canals with the periosteum and marrow.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Chondrocytes arrange themselves in columns</b> — Columns of chondrocytes are a cartilage (growth plate) feature.</li><li><b>Osteoclasts deposit layers of osteoid</b> — Osteoclasts resorb bone; they don't deposit osteoid.</li><li><b>Capillaries calcify into concentric rings</b> — Capillaries don't calcify into rings; they occupy the central canal.</li><li><b>The perichondrium folds inward into rings</b> — The perichondrium doesn't fold to form osteons.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Osteon = Haversian canal + concentric lamellae + osteocytes in lacunae + canaliculi.</p>","w":["Columns of chondrocytes are a cartilage (growth plate) feature.","Correct — concentric repetition of osteoblast deposition builds the osteon.","Osteoclasts resorb bone; they don't deposit osteoid.","Capillaries don't calcify into rings; they occupy the central canal.","The perichondrium doesn't fold to form osteons."]},"e13":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Osteoid (unmineralized organic matrix, mostly type I collagen) is where mineralization occurs, turning it into bone.</p><p>Osteoblasts secrete osteoid, then hydroxyapatite crystals [Ca₁₀(PO₄)₆(OH)₂] deposit within it. Alkaline phosphatase from osteoblasts raises local phosphate (by breaking down pyrophosphate, an inhibitor) and matrix vesicles start crystal formation. Normally about 10 days pass between osteoid deposition and mineralization. Without adequate vitamin D, calcium or phosphate, osteoid accumulates unmineralized — osteomalacia (adults) or rickets (children).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Osteoclasts</b> — Osteoclasts resorb bone rather than mineralize.</li><li><b>Cartilage chondrocytes</b> — Chondrocytes are cells; in bone formation it's the osteoid matrix that mineralizes.</li><li><b>Perichondrium</b> — The perichondrium is a connective tissue boundary.</li><li><b>Bone marrow</b> — Bone marrow is soft hematopoietic tissue.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Osteomalacia/rickets = excess unmineralized osteoid.</p>","w":["Osteoclasts resorb bone rather than mineralize.","Chondrocytes are cells; in bone formation it's the osteoid matrix that mineralizes.","Correct — mineralization occurs in osteoid.","The perichondrium is a connective tissue boundary.","Bone marrow is soft hematopoietic tissue."]},"e14":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Epiphysis = ends (secondary ossification center); diaphysis = shaft (primary center); metaphysis = between them (growth region).</p><p>The primary ossification center appears in the diaphysis before birth (around weeks 7–12). Secondary centers appear in the epiphyses, mostly after birth. Between them, a cartilaginous growth plate persists, allowing lengthwise growth until it fuses after puberty. The metaphysis is the flared region next to the growth plate and is highly vascular — a common site of hematogenous osteomyelitis and of primary bone tumors such as osteosarcoma.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Epiphysis — shaft — primary centre</b> — The epiphysis is at the ends, formed by secondary centers.</li><li><b>Diaphysis — ends — secondary centre</b> — The diaphysis is the shaft, formed by the primary center.</li><li><b>Metaphysis — shaft — primary centre</b> — The metaphysis lies between the epiphysis and the diaphysis.</li><li><b>Diaphysis — between epiphysis and metaphysis — secondary centre</b> — The diaphysis is the shaft, formed by the primary center.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Metaphysis: osteomyelitis in children, osteosarcoma.</p>","w":["The epiphysis is at the ends, formed by secondary centers.","The diaphysis is the shaft, formed by the primary center.","The metaphysis lies between the epiphysis and the diaphysis.","Correct — the epiphysis is at the ends and forms from the secondary center.","The diaphysis is the shaft, formed by the primary center."]},"e15":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> In these notes, ossification starts at the center: the dead zone (center) dies first, beside it the active zone, then the mitotic zone above and below.</p><p>The notes simplify the growth plate into three zones arranged from the center outward. Cells in the dead zone, at the center, die first because their matrix has ossified and diffusion fails. The active zone lies directly beside the dead zone. The mitotic zone, outermost, keeps dividing to supply new cells. As ossification spreads from the center outward, the zones shift outward and the bone lengthens. (Standard textbooks describe more zones: resting, proliferative, hypertrophic, calcification, ossification.)</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Calcified zone</b> — The \"calcified zone\" isn't one of the three zones used in these notes.</li><li><b>Mitotic zone</b> — The mitotic zone is the outermost, dividing zone, so its cells don't die first.</li><li><b>Active zone</b> — The active zone lies beside the dead zone; its cells haven't died yet.</li><li><b>Resting zone</b> — The \"resting zone\" isn't one of the three zones used in these notes.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Notes model: dead (center) → active → mitotic (periphery).</p>","w":["The \"calcified zone\" isn't one of the three zones used in these notes.","The mitotic zone is the outermost, dividing zone, so its cells don't die first.","The active zone lies beside the dead zone; its cells haven't died yet.","The \"resting zone\" isn't one of the three zones used in these notes.","Correct — the dead zone's central cells die first."]},"e16":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The mitotic zone lies above and below the active zone; as zones shift outward, the bone lengthens.</p><p>Ossification proceeds from the center toward the periphery. New cells are constantly produced in the outermost mitotic zone, pushing the active and dead zones outward behind them. Bone replaces the dead zone. Because the dividing cells stay ahead of the advancing ossification front, the bone grows in length. Growth in width occurs separately by appositional growth from the periosteum.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>In the periosteum; bone grows only in width</b> — Bone length comes from the shifting zones; the periosteum adds width.</li><li><b>In the centre; the zones shift inward</b> — The dead zone is at the center, and zones move outward, not inward.</li><li><b>Beside the dead zone; the zones stay fixed</b> — The active zone (not the mitotic zone) lies beside the dead zone, and the zones don't stay fixed.</li><li><b>Inside the marrow cavity; osteoclasts add length</b> — Osteoclasts hollow out the marrow cavity; they don't add length.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Length = growth plate (interstitial); width = periosteum (appositional).</p>","w":["Correct — the mitotic zone is above and below the active zone, and zones shift outward.","Bone length comes from the shifting zones; the periosteum adds width.","The dead zone is at the center, and zones move outward, not inward.","The active zone (not the mitotic zone) lies beside the dead zone, and the zones don't stay fixed.","Osteoclasts hollow out the marrow cavity; they don't add length."]},"e17":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The growth plate (epiphyseal plate) is the cartilage growth area; after fusion it remains as the epiphyseal line.</p><p>Longitudinal growth continues as long as the growth plate's cartilage proliferates. At the end of puberty, under estrogen influence (in both sexes), proliferation slows and the plate is replaced by bone, fusing the epiphysis with the diaphysis at the metaphysis. A thin radiodense epiphyseal line remains on X-ray. Radiologists use growth plate status to estimate bone age. Growth plate fractures (Salter–Harris) can disrupt growth.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Metaphysis; marrow cavity</b> — The marrow cavity is created in the diaphysis by osteoclasts; it isn't the growth area.</li><li><b>Mitotic zone; dead zone</b> — The mitotic and dead zones are parts of the growing bone, not the before/after names.</li><li><b>Primary areola; Haversian canal</b> — Primary areolae and Haversian canals are features of ossification and compact bone.</li><li><b>Perichondrium; periosteum</b> — The perichondrium and periosteum are connective tissue coverings.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Estrogen closes growth plates — aromatase deficiency or estrogen receptor defects → tall stature.</p>","w":["The marrow cavity is created in the diaphysis by osteoclasts; it isn't the growth area.","Correct — growth plate before fusion; epiphyseal line after.","The mitotic and dead zones are parts of the growing bone, not the before/after names.","Primary areolae and Haversian canals are features of ossification and compact bone.","The perichondrium and periosteum are connective tissue coverings."]},"e18":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Osteoclasts hollow out the center of the shaft to form the marrow cavity and also remodel the epiphysis.</p><p>As the diaphysis ossifies, bone in the center is resorbed by osteoclasts, creating the medullary (marrow) cavity, which fills with hematopoietic tissue. This keeps bones light while strong. Osteoclasts also reshape the epiphysis and metaphysis as the bone grows (modeling). Failure of osteoclast function (osteopetrosis, e.g., carbonic anhydrase II deficiency) leaves dense bones with no marrow cavity, causing anemia and extramedullary hematopoiesis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Osteocytes dissolve the shaft</b> — Osteocytes maintain bone and sense mechanical strain; they don't dissolve the shaft.</li><li><b>Capillaries alone dilate to form the cavity</b> — Capillaries alone can't hollow out bone.</li><li><b>Osteoblasts lay down marrow</b> — Osteoblasts build bone rather than creating the cavity, and marrow isn't laid down by them.</li><li><b>Chondrocytes die and leave the cavity directly</b> — Chondrocyte death makes primary areolae, not the marrow cavity.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Osteopetrosis: defective osteoclasts → dense brittle bone, no marrow cavity.</p>","w":["Osteocytes maintain bone and sense mechanical strain; they don't dissolve the shaft.","Capillaries alone can't hollow out bone.","Correct — osteoclasts hollow out the marrow cavity.","Osteoblasts build bone rather than creating the cavity, and marrow isn't laid down by them.","Chondrocyte death makes primary areolae, not the marrow cavity."]},"e19":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Metaphysis: no marrow cavity, highly vascular, heavily mineralized, and a storehouse of calcium.</p><p>The metaphysis lies between the epiphysis and diaphysis. It is full of trabecular bone, receives a rich blood supply (metaphyseal arteries with slow-flowing hairpin loops in children), and has a high turnover, making it a calcium reservoir. The sluggish flow explains why childhood hematogenous osteomyelitis (e.g., S. aureus) usually begins here.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It contains the main bone marrow cavity</b> — The main marrow cavity is in the diaphysis.</li><li><b>It is avascular and poorly mineralised</b> — The metaphysis is highly vascular and heavily mineralized.</li><li><b>It is ossified by the secondary ossification centre</b> — The epiphysis, not the metaphysis, is ossified by the secondary center.</li><li><b>It forms the articular surface of the joint</b> — The articular surface is on the epiphysis.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Childhood osteomyelitis → metaphysis (slow blood flow).</p>","w":["The main marrow cavity is in the diaphysis.","The metaphysis is highly vascular and heavily mineralized.","The epiphysis, not the metaphysis, is ossified by the secondary center.","Correct — this describes the metaphysis.","The articular surface is on the epiphysis."]},"e20":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> A typical vertebra ossifies from 3 primary centers: one for the body (centrum) and one for each half of the neural arch.</p><p>The vertebral body ossifies from a central center, and each half of the neural (vertebral) arch from its own center. The two halves of the arch fuse posteriorly in the first years of life, and the arch fuses to the body (neurocentral junction) by about ages 3–6. Secondary centers appear around puberty at the tips of the spinous and transverse processes and as ring epiphyses on the body. Failure of the two arch halves to fuse causes spina bifida.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>1 — the whole vertebra</b> — There is more than one primary center.</li><li><b>2 — one for the body, one for the arch</b> — The neural arch has two centers, one for each half.</li><li><b>4 — body, two transverse processes and spinous process</b> — There are three primary centers, not four.</li><li><b>5 — one for each process and the body</b> — There are three primary centers, not five.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Spina bifida occulta = unfused arch halves, often with a tuft of hair over L5–S1.</p>","w":["There is more than one primary center.","The neural arch has two centers, one for each half.","There are three primary centers, not four.","There are three primary centers, not five.","Correct — three centers: one for the body and one for each half of the arch."]},"e21":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> A typical vertebra = body (anterior, weight-bearing) + neural (vertebral) arch enclosing the neural canal, with transverse and spinous processes.</p><p>The body bears weight and is joined to adjacent bodies by intervertebral discs. The arch consists of two pedicles (attaching to the body) and two laminae (meeting posteriorly). Together with the body, the arch encloses the vertebral foramen; stacked foramina form the neural (vertebral) canal for the spinal cord. Processes: one spinous process (posterior, from the laminae), two transverse processes (lateral), and four articular processes forming facet joints. Notches between adjacent pedicles form intervertebral foramina for spinal nerves.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Head, neck, shaft, condyles</b> — Head, neck, shaft and condyles are parts of a long bone such as the femur.</li><li><b>Diaphysis, epiphysis, metaphysis</b> — Diaphysis, epiphysis and metaphysis are regions of a long bone.</li><li><b>Body, ala, sacral foramina</b> — Ala and sacral foramina are features of the sacrum.</li><li><b>Body, acromion, glenoid, spinous process</b> — Acromion and glenoid are parts of the scapula.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Pedicles + laminae = neural arch; laminectomy removes laminae to decompress the canal.</p>","w":["Correct — these are the parts of a typical vertebra.","Head, neck, shaft and condyles are parts of a long bone such as the femur.","Diaphysis, epiphysis and metaphysis are regions of a long bone.","Ala and sacral foramina are features of the sacrum.","Acromion and glenoid are parts of the scapula."]},"e22":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cervical vertebrae have a foramen transversarium in each transverse process, carrying the vertebral artery (usually C6–C1).</p><p>The vertebral artery arises from the subclavian artery and typically enters the foramen transversarium at C6, ascending through C1 before passing through the foramen magnum to form the basilar artery. C7's foramen usually transmits only vertebral veins. The foramen transversarium is a defining feature of cervical vertebrae. Cervical spine trauma or osteophytes can compress the artery, and neck manipulation can dissect it, causing posterior circulation stroke.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Foramen transversarium; spinal cord</b> — The spinal cord runs through the vertebral foramen (neural canal), not the foramen transversarium.</li><li><b>Intervertebral foramen; spinal nerve</b> — The intervertebral foramen transmits spinal nerves between adjacent vertebrae.</li><li><b>Vertebral foramen; spinal cord</b> — The vertebral foramen holds the spinal cord, not the vertebral artery.</li><li><b>Foramen magnum; medulla</b> — The foramen magnum is in the occipital bone and transmits the medulla.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Vertebral artery enters at C6.</p>","w":["The spinal cord runs through the vertebral foramen (neural canal), not the foramen transversarium.","Correct — the foramen transversarium carries the vertebral artery.","The intervertebral foramen transmits spinal nerves between adjacent vertebrae.","The vertebral foramen holds the spinal cord, not the vertebral artery.","The foramen magnum is in the occipital bone and transmits the medulla."]},"e23":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Spina bifida = failure of the two halves of the neural arch to fuse posteriorly, so the spinous process doesn't form; most common at lumbosacral levels.</p><p>Each half of the neural arch ossifies from its own center and the halves normally fuse in the midline. When they fail to fuse, a posterior bony defect results. Spectrum: spina bifida occulta (bony defect only, skin intact, often a tuft of hair or dimple), meningocele (meninges protrude), myelomeningocele (meninges and spinal cord protrude, with neurological deficits, hydrocephalus and Chiari II malformation). Maternal folate supplementation reduces risk; elevated maternal serum and amniotic AFP suggest open defects.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Chondroma</b> — A chondroma is a benign cartilage tumor.</li><li><b>Scaphocephaly</b> — Scaphocephaly is a skull deformity from premature sagittal suture fusion.</li><li><b>Spina bifida anterior</b> — Spina bifida anterior involves the vertebral body, not the arch, and is rare.</li><li><b>Osteopetrosis</b> — Osteopetrosis is a disorder of osteoclast function.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Neural tube defects: ↑AFP and acetylcholinesterase in amniotic fluid; prevent with folic acid.</p>","w":["A chondroma is a benign cartilage tumor.","Scaphocephaly is a skull deformity from premature sagittal suture fusion.","Correct — spina bifida.","Spina bifida anterior involves the vertebral body, not the arch, and is rare.","Osteopetrosis is a disorder of osteoclast function."]},"e24":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Spina bifida anterior (rare): the vertebral body forms from two centers that fail to fuse, leaving an anterior defect.</p><p>According to the notes, the vertebral body can develop from two lateral halves; if these don't fuse, a sagittal cleft forms in the body, exposing the spinal cord anteriorly. This contrasts with the common (posterior) spina bifida, where the two halves of the neural arch fail to fuse. On imaging, a body formed from two unfused halves gives the \"butterfly vertebra\" appearance.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Osteoclasts fail to form a marrow cavity</b> — Failure of osteoclasts to form a marrow cavity describes osteopetrosis.</li><li><b>The two halves of the neural arch fail to fuse</b> — Failure of the two halves of the neural arch to fuse is (posterior) spina bifida.</li><li><b>The transverse processes fail to develop</b> — Failure of transverse process development isn't the defect described.</li><li><b>The spinous process is duplicated</b> — A duplicated spinous process isn't the defect described.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Posterior defect (arch) = common; anterior defect (body) = rare.</p>","w":["Failure of osteoclasts to form a marrow cavity describes osteopetrosis.","Failure of the two halves of the neural arch to fuse is (posterior) spina bifida.","Failure of transverse process development isn't the defect described.","Correct — unfused body centers cause spina bifida anterior.","A duplicated spinous process isn't the defect described."]},"e25":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Chondroma = benign tumor of hyaline cartilage (enchondroma when inside bone).</p><p>Enchondromas arise in the medullary cavity, most often in the small bones of the hands and feet, and are usually asymptomatic or present with pathological fracture. On X-ray they appear as well-defined lucent lesions with \"rings and arcs\" calcification. Multiple enchondromas occur in Ollier disease and Maffucci syndrome (with hemangiomas), with risk of transformation to chondrosarcoma. Chondrosarcoma is the malignant counterpart, typically in the pelvis and proximal femur of older adults.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>A defect in osteoclast function</b> — A defect in osteoclast function describes osteopetrosis.</li><li><b>A malignant tumour of bone</b> — Chondromas are benign and arise from cartilage, not malignant bone tumors.</li><li><b>A failure of neural arch fusion</b> — Failure of neural arch fusion is spina bifida.</li><li><b>A cyst of the sebaceous gland</b> — A cyst of the sebaceous gland is a sebaceous (epidermoid/pilar) cyst.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> -oma = benign; -sarcoma = malignant mesenchymal tumor.</p>","w":["A defect in osteoclast function describes osteopetrosis.","Chondromas are benign and arise from cartilage, not malignant bone tumors.","Failure of neural arch fusion is spina bifida.","A cyst of the sebaceous gland is a sebaceous (epidermoid/pilar) cyst.","Correct — a chondroma is a benign cartilage tumor."]},"e26":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Osteopetrosis: defective osteoclasts can't resorb bone → dense, brittle bones with no marrow cavity.</p><p>Normally osteoclasts hollow out the diaphysis and continually remodel bone. When they fail (e.g., carbonic anhydrase II deficiency prevents acid secretion; or defects in RANK, chloride channels, proton pumps), bone accumulates. Bones are dense on X-ray (\"bone-in-bone,\" \"marble bone\") but brittle and fracture easily. Without a marrow cavity, hematopoiesis fails, causing pancytopenia and extramedullary hematopoiesis (hepatosplenomegaly). Narrowed foramina compress cranial nerves (blindness, deafness, facial palsy). Severe forms can be treated with bone marrow transplant, since osteoclasts derive from hematopoietic cells.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Abnormal osteoblasts that cannot lay down osteoid</b> — Abnormal osteoblasts that can't make osteoid would produce weak, less dense bone.</li><li><b>Excess resorption from overactive osteoclasts</b> — Overactive osteoclasts would reduce, not increase, bone density.</li><li><b>Failure of neural arch fusion</b> — Failure of neural arch fusion is spina bifida.</li><li><b>Vitamin C deficiency</b> — Vitamin C deficiency impairs collagen synthesis (scurvy).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Osteopetrosis: ↑density, no marrow → anemia; cranial nerve palsies; cure = marrow transplant.</p>","w":["Correct — abnormal osteoclasts fail to hollow out the marrow cavity.","Abnormal osteoblasts that can't make osteoid would produce weak, less dense bone.","Overactive osteoclasts would reduce, not increase, bone density.","Failure of neural arch fusion is spina bifida.","Vitamin C deficiency impairs collagen synthesis (scurvy)."]},"e27":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Osteoporosis: decreased bone mass and increased porosity with normal mineralization; causes include age, hormonal (estrogen loss) and dietary factors.</p><p>Bone mass peaks in the late 20s, then declines. After menopause, estrogen loss increases RANKL and osteoclast activity, accelerating trabecular bone loss. Other causes: low calcium or vitamin D intake, glucocorticoids, hyperthyroidism, hyperparathyroidism, immobilization, smoking and alcohol. Fractures typically affect vertebrae (compression, kyphosis, height loss), hip and distal radius (Colles). Diagnosis: DEXA T-score ≤ −2.5. Labs (calcium, phosphate, ALP, PTH) are usually normal. Treatment: calcium/vitamin D, weight-bearing exercise, bisphosphonates, denosumab, teriparatide.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Abnormal osteoclasts only</b> — Abnormal osteoclasts cause osteopetrosis, which increases density.</li><li><b>Failure of neural arch fusion</b> — Failure of neural arch fusion is spina bifida.</li><li><b>Oligohydramnios</b> — Oligohydramnios is associated with club foot and limb compression deformities.</li><li><b>Thalidomide exposure</b> — Thalidomide exposure is associated with limb defects (phocomelia).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Osteoporosis: normal labs, ↓bone mass; osteomalacia: ↓Ca/PO₄, ↑ALP, ↓mineralization.</p>","w":["Abnormal osteoclasts cause osteopetrosis, which increases density.","Correct — age, hormonal and dietary causes.","Failure of neural arch fusion is spina bifida.","Oligohydramnios is associated with club foot and limb compression deformities.","Thalidomide exposure is associated with limb defects (phocomelia)."]},"e28":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Chondro-osteodystrophy (per the notes): a cartilage abnormality giving a short vertebral column with normal limb length.</p><p>Skeletal dysplasias are classified by which part of the skeleton is short. Short-trunk dwarfism, with normal limbs, results when vertebral cartilage growth is abnormal (as in chondro-osteodystrophy/Morquio-type disorders, which affect the spine more than the limbs). Short-limb dwarfism (e.g., achondroplasia from an activating FGFR3 mutation) has a normal-length trunk but short proximal limbs. Distinguishing them helps narrow the diagnosis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Osteoporosis</b> — Osteoporosis is reduced bone density in adults, not a growth disorder.</li><li><b>Osteopetrosis</b> — Osteopetrosis is increased density with no marrow cavity.</li><li><b>Amelia</b> — Amelia is complete absence of limbs.</li><li><b>Chondroma</b> — A chondroma is a benign cartilage tumor.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Achondroplasia: short limbs, normal trunk (FGFR3, autosomal dominant).</p>","w":["Osteoporosis is reduced bone density in adults, not a growth disorder.","Osteopetrosis is increased density with no marrow cavity.","Correct — chondro-osteodystrophy produces a short vertebral column with normal limbs.","Amelia is complete absence of limbs.","A chondroma is a benign cartilage tumor."]},"e29":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The skull has two parts: the neurocranium (protects the brain) and the viscerocranium (face and jaws).</p><p>The neurocranium has a membranous part (flat bones of the vault) and a cartilaginous part (chondrocranium — skull base). The viscerocranium develops mainly from the pharyngeal arches (largely neural crest-derived mesenchyme) and forms the facial bones and jaws, again with membranous and cartilaginous components. So skull bones may be purely membranous, purely endochondral, or mixed.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Perichondrium and periosteum</b> — Perichondrium and periosteum are connective tissue coverings, not skull divisions.</li><li><b>Sclerotome and dermatome</b> — Sclerotome and dermatome are parts of a somite.</li><li><b>Epiphysis and diaphysis</b> — Epiphysis and diaphysis are parts of a long bone.</li><li><b>Ectoderm and endoderm</b> — Ectoderm and endoderm are germ layers.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Viscerocranium = pharyngeal arch derivatives (mostly neural crest).</p>","w":["Perichondrium and periosteum are connective tissue coverings, not skull divisions.","Sclerotome and dermatome are parts of a somite.","Epiphysis and diaphysis are parts of a long bone.","Correct — neurocranium and viscerocranium.","Ectoderm and endoderm are germ layers."]},"e30":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Purely membranous skull bones (notes): frontal, parietal, lacrimal, vomer and part of the maxilla.</p><p>The flat bones of the skull vault (frontal, parietal) ossify directly in membrane, which allows the sutures and fontanelles that let the skull mold during birth and expand with the growing brain. Purely endochondral: ethmoid, inferior nasal conchae. Mixed: mandible, occipital, sphenoid, temporal.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Inferior nasal concha</b> — The inferior nasal conchae form by endochondral ossification.</li><li><b>Sphenoid</b> — The sphenoid has mixed origin.</li><li><b>Temporal</b> — The temporal bone has mixed origin.</li><li><b>Ethmoid</b> — The ethmoid forms by endochondral ossification.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Vault = membranous; base = endochondral.</p>","w":["The inferior nasal conchae form by endochondral ossification.","The sphenoid has mixed origin.","The temporal bone has mixed origin.","The ethmoid forms by endochondral ossification.","Correct — the parietal bone is entirely membranous."]},"e31":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Purely endochondral skull bones (notes): ethmoid and inferior nasal conchae.</p><p>These bones develop from the cartilaginous nasal capsule of the chondrocranium. The other skull base bones are mixed, with cartilaginous bases and membranous parts (occipital, sphenoid, temporal). Membranous bones include frontal, parietal, lacrimal and vomer.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Lacrimal and vomer</b> — Lacrimal and vomer are membranous.</li><li><b>Mandible and occipital</b> — Mandible and occipital are mixed.</li><li><b>Sphenoid and temporal</b> — Sphenoid and temporal are mixed.</li><li><b>Frontal and parietal</b> — Frontal and parietal are membranous.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Ethmoid + inferior conchae = pure cartilage origin.</p>","w":["Correct — the ethmoid and inferior nasal conchae are endochondral.","Lacrimal and vomer are membranous.","Mandible and occipital are mixed.","Sphenoid and temporal are mixed.","Frontal and parietal are membranous."]},"e32":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The mandible is mixed: the condylar process is cartilaginous (endochondral); the body, ramus and coronoid process are membranous.</p><p>The mandible forms around Meckel's cartilage (first pharyngeal arch), but mostly by intramembranous ossification lateral to it. Secondary cartilage at the condylar process acts as an important growth center for the mandible, and condylar injury in childhood can cause facial asymmetry. Meckel's cartilage itself gives rise to the malleus and incus and the sphenomandibular ligament.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Mental protuberance</b> — The mental protuberance is part of the membranous body.</li><li><b>Coronoid process</b> — The coronoid process is membranous.</li><li><b>Ramus</b> — The ramus is membranous.</li><li><b>Body</b> — The body is membranous.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> First arch (Meckel's cartilage): malleus, incus, sphenomandibular ligament.</p>","w":["The mental protuberance is part of the membranous body.","Correct — the condylar process forms by cartilaginous ossification.","The coronoid process is membranous.","The ramus is membranous.","The body is membranous."]},"e33":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Temporal bone: the mastoid process and petrous part are cartilaginous; the squamous and tympanic parts are membranous.</p><p>The petrous part (housing the inner ear) develops from the cartilaginous otic capsule, and the mastoid process extends from it. The squamous part (forming the side of the skull) and the tympanic ring (around the external acoustic meatus) form in membrane. The styloid process develops from the second pharyngeal arch (Reichert's cartilage). The mastoid is absent at birth and develops as the sternocleidomastoid pulls on it, so the facial nerve is superficial and vulnerable in infants.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The whole bone</b> — The temporal bone has mixed origin.</li><li><b>Squamous and tympanic parts</b> — Squamous and tympanic parts are membranous.</li><li><b>Squamous part only</b> — The squamous part is membranous.</li><li><b>Tympanic part and mastoid process</b> — The tympanic part is membranous.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Mastoid absent at birth → CN VII exposed near stylomastoid foramen in neonates.</p>","w":["The temporal bone has mixed origin.","Squamous and tympanic parts are membranous.","Correct — the mastoid process and petrous part are cartilaginous.","The squamous part is membranous.","The tympanic part is membranous."]},"e34":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Sphenoid: the body and part of the lesser wing are cartilaginous; the rest of the lesser wing, greater wing and pterygoid laminae are membranous.</p><p>The sphenoid's central body (housing the sella turcica and sphenoid sinus) develops from the chondrocranium, as does part of the lesser wings. The greater wings and the medial and lateral pterygoid plates ossify in membrane (the hamulus of the medial plate has a cartilaginous origin in some accounts). This mix fits the general rule: central skull base = cartilage; lateral and facial extensions = membrane.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Medial pterygoid lamina</b> — The medial pterygoid lamina is membranous.</li><li><b>Lateral pterygoid lamina</b> — The lateral pterygoid lamina is membranous.</li><li><b>The whole lesser wing</b> — The lesser wing is only partly cartilaginous; the rest is membranous.</li><li><b>Greater wing</b> — The greater wing is membranous.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Skull base (sphenoid body, petrous temporal, occipital base, ethmoid) = cartilage.</p>","w":["The medial pterygoid lamina is membranous.","The lateral pterygoid lamina is membranous.","The lesser wing is only partly cartilaginous; the rest is membranous.","Correct — the body and part of the lesser wing are cartilaginous.","The greater wing is membranous."]},"e35":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Occipital bone: the base (including the condyles) is cartilaginous; the upper (interparietal) squamous part is membranous.</p><p>The basilar, lateral (condylar) and lower squamous parts develop from the chondrocranium surrounding the foramen magnum. The upper squamous part above the superior nuchal line (interparietal) ossifies in membrane, like the vault bones. Occasionally this part remains separate as an \"Inca bone.\" The pattern mirrors the skull overall: base = endochondral, vault = membranous.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Whole bone cartilaginous</b> — The occipital bone has mixed, not entirely cartilaginous, origin.</li><li><b>Condyles membranous; squama cartilaginous</b> — This is reversed — the base, including the condyles, is cartilaginous.</li><li><b>Upper part cartilaginous; base membranous</b> — This is reversed — the upper part is membranous and the base cartilaginous.</li><li><b>Whole bone membranous</b> — The occipital bone has mixed, not entirely membranous, origin.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Occipital = mixed: cartilaginous base + membranous upper squama.</p>","w":["The occipital bone has mixed, not entirely cartilaginous, origin.","This is reversed — the base, including the condyles, is cartilaginous.","This is reversed — the upper part is membranous and the base cartilaginous.","The occipital bone has mixed, not entirely membranous, origin.","Correct — base cartilaginous, upper part membranous."]},"e36":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Posterior fontanelle: junction of lambdoid and sagittal sutures (lambda); closes by about 3 months.</p><p>Fontanelles are membranous gaps where several skull bones meet. The posterior fontanelle is small and triangular at the meeting of the two parietal bones and the occipital bone. During birth, its position helps determine fetal head orientation on vaginal exam. The anterior fontanelle (bregma) is larger and diamond-shaped and closes much later. Delayed closure suggests hypothyroidism, rickets, raised intracranial pressure or skeletal dysplasias.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Where the lambdoid and sagittal sutures meet; 15th month</b> — The location is correct, but the posterior fontanelle closes by the 3rd month, not the 15th.</li><li><b>Where the coronal and sagittal sutures meet; 15th month</b> — The meeting of coronal and sagittal sutures is the anterior fontanelle.</li><li><b>Where the coronal and lambdoid sutures meet; 3rd month</b> — Coronal and lambdoid sutures don't meet at the midline.</li><li><b>Where the sagittal and metopic sutures meet; at birth</b> — The sagittal and metopic sutures don't define the posterior fontanelle.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Posterior = lambda, ~2–3 months; anterior = bregma, ~18 months (15 months per notes).</p>","w":["Correct — lambdoid + sagittal junction, closes by 3 months.","The location is correct, but the posterior fontanelle closes by the 3rd month, not the 15th.","The meeting of coronal and sagittal sutures is the anterior fontanelle.","Coronal and lambdoid sutures don't meet at the midline.","The sagittal and metopic sutures don't define the posterior fontanelle."]},"e37":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Anterior fontanelle: junction of coronal and sagittal sutures (bregma); the largest, diamond-shaped \"soft spot\"; closes by ~15 months (per notes; often quoted up to 18–24 months).</p><p>The anterior fontanelle lies between the two frontal and two parietal bones. Clinically it's a window on intracranial pressure: bulging suggests raised ICP (meningitis, hydrocephalus), while a sunken fontanelle suggests dehydration. Early closure may indicate craniosynostosis or microcephaly; late closure may indicate hypothyroidism, rickets or achondroplasia.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Sagittal + squamous sutures; 15th month</b> — The squamous suture isn't part of the anterior fontanelle.</li><li><b>Coronal + sagittal sutures; at birth</b> — The anterior fontanelle remains open well after birth.</li><li><b>Lambdoid + sagittal sutures; 3rd month</b> — Lambdoid + sagittal sutures define the posterior fontanelle, which closes by 3 months.</li><li><b>Coronal + lambdoid sutures; 6th month</b> — Coronal and lambdoid sutures don't meet at the midline.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Bulging = ↑ICP; sunken = dehydration.</p>","w":["The squamous suture isn't part of the anterior fontanelle.","Correct — coronal + sagittal junction, closing by about 15 months.","The anterior fontanelle remains open well after birth.","Lambdoid + sagittal sutures define the posterior fontanelle, which closes by 3 months.","Coronal and lambdoid sutures don't meet at the midline."]},"e38":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Premature fusion of the sagittal suture → scaphocephaly (long, narrow, boat-shaped skull) — the most common craniosynostosis.</p><p>When a suture fuses early, growth stops perpendicular to that suture and continues parallel to it (Virchow's law). Sagittal fusion prevents widening, so the skull grows long front-to-back (dolichocephaly/scaphocephaly). Coronal fusion prevents front-to-back growth, causing a tall, pointed (acrocephaly/turricephaly) or short wide skull. Unilateral fusion causes asymmetry (plagiocephaly). Syndromic craniosynostosis (e.g., Crouzon, Apert) is often caused by FGFR2 mutations.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Coronal; acrocephaly</b> — Coronal fusion causes acrocephaly, not a long narrow skull.</li><li><b>Lambdoid; plagiocephaly</b> — Plagiocephaly comes from asymmetrical fusion.</li><li><b>Coronal; scaphocephaly</b> — Coronal fusion with scaphocephaly is a mismatched pair.</li><li><b>Sagittal; acrocephaly</b> — Sagittal fusion with acrocephaly is a mismatched pair.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Virchow's law: growth stops perpendicular to the fused suture.</p>","w":["Coronal fusion causes acrocephaly, not a long narrow skull.","Plagiocephaly comes from asymmetrical fusion.","Correct — sagittal synostosis causes scaphocephaly.","Coronal fusion with scaphocephaly is a mismatched pair.","Sagittal fusion with acrocephaly is a mismatched pair."]},"e39":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Premature fusion of the coronal suture → acrocephaly (tall, pointed \"tower\" skull).</p><p>With the coronal suture fused, the skull can't grow front-to-back, so the brain expands upward, producing a high, peaked skull (acrocephaly/turricephaly; bilateral coronal fusion may give brachycephaly — short and wide). Coronal synostosis is a feature of Crouzon and Apert syndromes. Contrast: sagittal → scaphocephaly; unilateral/asymmetrical → plagiocephaly.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Scaphocephaly</b> — Scaphocephaly results from premature sagittal fusion.</li><li><b>Plagiocephaly</b> — Plagiocephaly results from asymmetrical fusion.</li><li><b>Microcephaly</b> — Microcephaly is a small head from poor brain growth, not a suture defect.</li><li><b>Hydrocephaly</b> — Hydrocephalus is excess CSF, not premature suture fusion.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Coronal → acro-; sagittal → scapho-; asymmetrical → plagio-.</p>","w":["Scaphocephaly results from premature sagittal fusion.","Plagiocephaly results from asymmetrical fusion.","Microcephaly is a small head from poor brain growth, not a suture defect.","Correct — coronal synostosis causes acrocephaly.","Hydrocephalus is excess CSF, not premature suture fusion."]},"e40":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Asymmetrical (one-sided) premature suture fusion → plagiocephaly (lopsided skull).</p><p>When sutures fuse earlier on one side (e.g., unilateral coronal or lambdoid), growth on that side is restricted and the skull becomes asymmetric. Note that most infant plagiocephaly today is positional (deformational) from sleeping on the back, not true synostosis; it improves with repositioning or a helmet.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Symphalangia</b> — Symphalangia is fusion of finger joints.</li><li><b>Scaphocephaly</b> — Scaphocephaly results from sagittal fusion.</li><li><b>Acrocephaly</b> — Acrocephaly results from coronal fusion.</li><li><b>Synophthalmia</b> — Synophthalmia is fusion of the eyes (a severe facial anomaly).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Positional plagiocephaly (common) vs true lambdoid synostosis (rare).</p>","w":["Symphalangia is fusion of finger joints.","Scaphocephaly results from sagittal fusion.","Acrocephaly results from coronal fusion.","Synophthalmia is fusion of the eyes (a severe facial anomaly).","Correct — plagiocephaly."]},"e41":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Upper limb bud appears weeks 4–5 and rotates 90° laterally (so the thumb and extensors face laterally/posteriorly).</p><p>Limb buds arise from lateral plate mesoderm covered by ectoderm, the upper limb slightly earlier than the lower (cranial-to-caudal development). Around week 7, the limbs rotate in opposite directions. The upper limb rotates laterally, so the elbow points backward, the extensor muscles lie posteriorly, and the thumb is lateral. This explains why upper limb dermatomes run relatively straight down the limb.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Weeks 2–3; 180° laterally</b> — Weeks 2–3 is far too early (the embryo is still forming germ layers), and the rotation is 90°, not 180°.</li><li><b>Weeks 4–5; 90° medially</b> — The timing is right, but the upper limb rotates laterally, not medially.</li><li><b>Weeks 8–9; no rotation</b> — Limb buds appear much earlier than weeks 8–9, and they do rotate.</li><li><b>Weeks 5–6; 90° medially</b> — Weeks 5–6 with 90° medial rotation describes the lower limb bud.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Upper limb: lateral rotation → thumb lateral, extensors posterior.</p>","w":["Correct — weeks 4–5 with 90° lateral rotation.","Weeks 2–3 is far too early (the embryo is still forming germ layers), and the rotation is 90°, not 180°.","The timing is right, but the upper limb rotates laterally, not medially.","Limb buds appear much earlier than weeks 8–9, and they do rotate.","Weeks 5–6 with 90° medial rotation describes the lower limb bud."]},"e42":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Lower limb bud appears weeks 5–6 and rotates 90° medially (so the big toe is medial and extensors face anteriorly).</p><p>Because of medial rotation, the knee faces forward, the extensor (quadriceps) compartment is anterior and the flexors are posterior — the opposite of the upper limb. This also twists the lower limb dermatomes into a spiral (e.g., L4 medial leg, L5 dorsum of foot, S1 lateral foot and sole), and explains why the \"extensor\" surface of the leg is anterior.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Weeks 3–4; 90° medially</b> — Weeks 3–4 is too early for the lower limb bud.</li><li><b>Weeks 7–8; 180° medially</b> — Weeks 7–8 is too late, and the rotation is 90°, not 180°.</li><li><b>Weeks 4–5; 90° laterally</b> — Weeks 4–5 with 90° lateral rotation describes the upper limb bud.</li><li><b>Weeks 5–6; 90° laterally</b> — The timing is right, but the lower limb rotates medially, not laterally.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Lower limb: medial rotation → big toe medial, extensors anterior, spiral dermatomes.</p>","w":["Weeks 3–4 is too early for the lower limb bud.","Correct — weeks 5–6 with 90° medial rotation.","Weeks 7–8 is too late, and the rotation is 90°, not 180°.","Weeks 4–5 with 90° lateral rotation describes the upper limb bud.","The timing is right, but the lower limb rotates medially, not laterally."]},"e43":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The apical ectodermal ridge (AER) at the limb bud tip secretes FGFs that drive proximal-to-distal outgrowth of the underlying mesoderm.</p><p>Three signaling centers pattern the limb: the AER (proximodistal axis — FGF-4, FGF-8), the zone of polarizing activity at the posterior margin (anteroposterior axis — Sonic hedgehog, determining the pinky side), and the dorsal ectoderm (dorsoventral axis — Wnt-7a). Removing the AER early truncates the limb (only proximal parts form); removing it later truncates more distally. Limb muscles come from somite myotome cells that migrate into the bud; bones come from lateral plate mesoderm.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It produces the limb muscles</b> — Limb muscles come from somite myotome cells that migrate into the bud.</li><li><b>It ossifies the long bones</b> — Ossification is a later process involving cartilage models.</li><li><b>It causes apoptosis between the digits</b> — Apoptosis between digits is a separate process (involving BMPs).</li><li><b>It forms the skin of the limb</b> — Forming the skin of the limb isn't its role; the AER is a signaling center.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> AER → length (FGF); ZPA → anterior–posterior (Shh); dorsal ectoderm → dorsal–ventral (Wnt-7a).</p>","w":["Limb muscles come from somite myotome cells that migrate into the bud.","Ossification is a later process involving cartilage models.","Correct — the AER stimulates mesoderm to elongate the limb.","Apoptosis between digits is a separate process (involving BMPs).","Forming the skin of the limb isn't its role; the AER is a signaling center."]},"e44":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Syndactyly = fused digits from failure of apoptosis in the interdigital tissue.</p><p>Initially, the hand and foot plates are paddle-shaped. Around weeks 6–8, programmed cell death (driven by BMP signaling) removes the tissue between the digit rays, separating fingers and toes. If apoptosis fails, digits remain joined by skin (simple syndactyly) or by bone (complex syndactyly). It's most common between the 3rd and 4th fingers and 2nd and 3rd toes.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Failure of AER activity; amelia</b> — Failure of AER activity causes limb truncation or amelia (absent limbs), not fused fingers.</li><li><b>Failure of apoptosis; symphalangia</b> — Failure of apoptosis is correct, but symphalangia is fused joints, not fused fingers.</li><li><b>Oligohydramnios; club foot</b> — Oligohydramnios is associated with club foot (inverted feet).</li><li><b>Excess apoptosis; polydactyly</b> — Polydactyly is an extra digit, linked to abnormal Shh signaling, not excess apoptosis.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Apoptosis also shapes joints — failure = symphalangia; between digits — failure = syndactyly.</p>","w":["Failure of AER activity causes limb truncation or amelia (absent limbs), not fused fingers.","Failure of apoptosis is correct, but symphalangia is fused joints, not fused fingers.","Oligohydramnios is associated with club foot (inverted feet).","Correct — failure of apoptosis causes syndactyly.","Polydactyly is an extra digit, linked to abnormal Shh signaling, not excess apoptosis."]},"e45":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Amelia = absence of limbs (or only limb buds); thalidomide is the classic teratogen (also phocomelia — seal-like flippers).</p><p>Thalidomide, used as a sedative and antiemetic in the late 1950s, caused severe limb reduction defects when taken during the critical window for limb development (roughly days 24–36 after conception). It's thought to interfere with angiogenesis and FGF signaling in the developing limb bud. Phocomelia (hands/feet attached close to the trunk) was the most typical defect. Thalidomide is now used for multiple myeloma and leprosy (erythema nodosum leprosum) under strict pregnancy prevention.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Amelia; folic acid</b> — Folic acid doesn't cause amelia; it prevents neural tube defects.</li><li><b>Club foot; thalidomide</b> — Club foot is linked to oligohydramnios, not thalidomide.</li><li><b>Polydactyly; thalidomide</b> — Polydactyly is an extra digit and isn't the classic thalidomide defect.</li><li><b>Syndactyly; folic acid</b> — Syndactyly is fused digits, and folic acid prevents rather than causes defects.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Thalidomide → limb defects; critical exposure weeks 4–7.</p>","w":["Folic acid doesn't cause amelia; it prevents neural tube defects.","Club foot is linked to oligohydramnios, not thalidomide.","Polydactyly is an extra digit and isn't the classic thalidomide defect.","Syndactyly is fused digits, and folic acid prevents rather than causes defects.","Correct — amelia, with thalidomide as the classic cause."]},"e46":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Symphalangia = fused joints (failure of apoptosis at interphalangeal joints).</p><p>Learn the \"sym/syn\" family by the root: syn- = together. Syndactyly (dactyl = digit) = fused fingers/toes. Symphalangia (phalanx) = fusion of interphalangeal joints, so the finger can't bend. Synophthalmia (ophthalmos = eye) = fused eyes (cyclopia, holoprosencephaly). Poly- = many: polydactyly = extra digits; polythelia = extra nipples. A- = without: amelia = no limbs; athelia = no nipple; amastia = no breast.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Synophthalmia — extra finger</b> — Synophthalmia is fused eyes, not an extra finger.</li><li><b>Syndactyly — extra finger</b> — Syndactyly is fused fingers, not an extra finger.</li><li><b>Amelia — fused eyes</b> — Amelia is absence of limbs, not fused eyes.</li><li><b>Polydactyly — fused fingers</b> — Polydactyly is an extra finger, not fused fingers.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Syn- = fused; poly- = extra; a- = absent.</p>","w":["Correct — symphalangia means fused joints.","Synophthalmia is fused eyes, not an extra finger.","Syndactyly is fused fingers, not an extra finger.","Amelia is absence of limbs, not fused eyes.","Polydactyly is an extra finger, not fused fingers."]},"e47":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Congenital talipes equinovarus (club foot): the foot is inverted, adducted and plantarflexed; associated with oligohydramnios.</p><p>\"Equino\" = plantarflexed like a horse's hoof; \"varus\" = inverted (sole facing medially). Oligohydramnios restricts fetal movement and compresses the limbs, contributing to the deformity; there are also genetic and neuromuscular causes (e.g., spina bifida). Boys are affected about twice as often. Treatment is serial casting (Ponseti method), often with Achilles tenotomy. Oligohydramnios also causes Potter sequence (pulmonary hypoplasia, flat face, limb deformities).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Amelia</b> — Amelia is absence of limbs.</li><li><b>Symphalangia</b> — Symphalangia is fused finger joints.</li><li><b>Plagiocephaly</b> — Plagiocephaly is a lopsided skull.</li><li><b>Syndactyly</b> — Syndactyly is fused digits.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Oligohydramnios (renal agenesis, ROM) → Potter sequence, club foot, pulmonary hypoplasia.</p>","w":["Amelia is absence of limbs.","Correct — club foot (talipes equinovarus).","Symphalangia is fused finger joints.","Plagiocephaly is a lopsided skull.","Syndactyly is fused digits."]},"e48":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Each somite divides into sclerotome (vertebrae, ribs), dermatome (dermis of the back) and myotome (skeletal muscle).</p><p>Somites form from paraxial mesoderm on either side of the neural tube. The ventromedial sclerotome migrates around the notochord and neural tube to form vertebrae and ribs. The dermomyotome splits into dermatome (dermis of the dorsal skin) and myotome (muscle). Each somite carries its own spinal nerve, which is why each dermatome and myotome has a specific segmental nerve supply — the basis of dermatome maps and reflex testing.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Epiphysis, metaphysis and diaphysis</b> — Epiphysis, metaphysis and diaphysis are parts of a long bone.</li><li><b>Neurocranium, viscerocranium and mandible</b> — Neurocranium, viscerocranium and mandible relate to the skull.</li><li><b>Perichondrium, chondroblasts and chondrocytes</b> — Perichondrium, chondroblasts and chondrocytes are stages of cartilage formation.</li><li><b>Ectoderm, mesoderm and endoderm</b> — Ectoderm, mesoderm and endoderm are the germ layers.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Sclerotome → vertebrae; dermatome → dermis; myotome → muscle.</p>","w":["Epiphysis, metaphysis and diaphysis are parts of a long bone.","Neurocranium, viscerocranium and mandible relate to the skull.","Correct — sclerotome, dermatome and myotome.","Perichondrium, chondroblasts and chondrocytes are stages of cartilage formation.","Ectoderm, mesoderm and endoderm are the germ layers."]},"e49":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Dorsomedial myotome (epimere) → deep (intrinsic) back muscles, supplied by dorsal rami.</p><p>The myotome divides into a dorsomedial part (epimere) and a ventrolateral part (hypomere). The epimere forms the deep back muscles — erector spinae, transversospinalis — innervated by the dorsal primary rami of spinal nerves. The hypomere forms the body wall and limb muscles, innervated by ventral rami. This explains why the back's intrinsic muscles are supplied by dorsal rami while the superficial back muscles (trapezius, latissimus) — which migrated from the limb or head — are supplied by ventral rami or cranial nerves.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Apical ectodermal ridge</b> — The AER drives limb outgrowth; it isn't part of the myotome.</li><li><b>Ventrolateral part</b> — The ventrolateral part forms body wall and limb muscles.</li><li><b>Sclerotome</b> — The sclerotome forms vertebrae and ribs.</li><li><b>Dermatome</b> — The dermatome forms the dermis of the skin.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Epimere → dorsal rami (deep back); hypomere → ventral rami (body wall, limbs).</p>","w":["The AER drives limb outgrowth; it isn't part of the myotome.","The ventrolateral part forms body wall and limb muscles.","The sclerotome forms vertebrae and ribs.","Correct — the dorsomedial part forms the back muscles.","The dermatome forms the dermis of the skin."]},"e50":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Ventrolateral myotome (hypomere) → anterior abdominal wall, intercostal and limb muscles, supplied by ventral rami.</p><p>The hypomere splits into layers that mirror each other in the thorax and abdomen: external, internal and innermost intercostals correspond to external oblique, internal oblique and transversus abdominis. A ventral column forms rectus abdominis. Ventral rami (intercostal and subcostal nerves, plus T12–L1) supply these. Failure of these muscles to develop underlies prune belly syndrome.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Dorsomedial part</b> — The dorsomedial part forms back muscles.</li><li><b>Sclerotome</b> — The sclerotome forms bone and cartilage.</li><li><b>Dermatome</b> — The dermatome forms the dermis.</li><li><b>Neural crest</b> — Neural crest doesn't form trunk skeletal muscles.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Three layers: external, internal, innermost/transversus in both thorax and abdomen.</p>","w":["The dorsomedial part forms back muscles.","The sclerotome forms bone and cartilage.","The dermatome forms the dermis.","Neural crest doesn't form trunk skeletal muscles.","Correct — the ventrolateral part forms these muscles."]},"e51":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Poland syndrome: unilateral absence or hypoplasia of the pectoralis major (sternal head), often with ipsilateral hand anomalies.</p><p>Poland syndrome is thought to result from interrupted blood flow in the subclavian artery around week 6. Features: flat chest on one side, absent anterior axillary fold, often hypoplastic breast/nipple, and ipsilateral syndactyly or short fingers (brachysyndactyly). It's usually sporadic and more common on the right side in males. Treatment is reconstructive surgery if needed.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Chondro-osteodystrophy</b> — Chondro-osteodystrophy shortens the vertebral column.</li><li><b>Prune belly syndrome</b> — Prune belly syndrome affects the abdominal muscles.</li><li><b>Amelia</b> — Amelia is absence of limbs.</li><li><b>Athelia</b> — Athelia is absence of the nipple.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Poland: absent pectoralis + ipsilateral syndactyly.</p>","w":["Correct — Poland syndrome.","Chondro-osteodystrophy shortens the vertebral column.","Prune belly syndrome affects the abdominal muscles.","Amelia is absence of limbs.","Athelia is absence of the nipple."]},"e52":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Prune belly (Eagle–Barrett) syndrome: absent/deficient abdominal muscles → wrinkled abdomen; classically with urinary tract dilation and undescended testes in boys.</p><p>The classic triad is (1) deficient abdominal wall musculature, (2) urinary tract anomalies (dilated bladder, megaureters, hydronephrosis, often from early urethral obstruction), and (3) bilateral cryptorchidism. It's almost exclusively in males. Urinary obstruction can cause oligohydramnios and pulmonary hypoplasia (Potter sequence).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Polythelia</b> — Polythelia is extra nipples.</li><li><b>Osteopetrosis</b> — Osteopetrosis is a bone disorder with defective osteoclasts.</li><li><b>Poland syndrome</b> — Poland syndrome affects the pectoral muscles.</li><li><b>Spina bifida</b> — Spina bifida is a vertebral arch defect.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Prune belly triad: abdominal muscle deficiency + urinary tract dilation + cryptorchidism.</p>","w":["Polythelia is extra nipples.","Correct — prune belly syndrome.","Osteopetrosis is a bone disorder with defective osteoclasts.","Poland syndrome affects the pectoral muscles.","Spina bifida is a vertebral arch defect."]},"e53":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Epidermis comes from surface ectoderm; dermis comes from mesoderm (and neural crest in the face).</p><p>Surface ectoderm forms the epidermis and its appendages (hair, nails, sweat, sebaceous and mammary glands). The dermis develops from dermatome (back), lateral plate mesoderm (limbs and body wall), and neural crest (face and neck). Melanocytes migrate into the epidermis from neural crest. Langerhans cells come from bone marrow.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Epidermis from mesoderm; dermis from ectoderm</b> — This is reversed.</li><li><b>Both from ectoderm</b> — The dermis comes from mesoderm, not ectoderm.</li><li><b>Both from mesoderm</b> — The epidermis comes from ectoderm, not mesoderm.</li><li><b>Epidermis from endoderm; dermis from mesoderm</b> — Endoderm lines the gut and respiratory tract, not the skin.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Melanocytes = neural crest; Langerhans cells = bone marrow.</p>","w":["This is reversed.","The dermis comes from mesoderm, not ectoderm.","Correct — epidermis from ectoderm, dermis from mesoderm.","The epidermis comes from ectoderm, not mesoderm.","Endoderm lines the gut and respiratory tract, not the skin."]},"e54":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Epidermal layers superficial → deep: corneum, lucidum, granulosum, spinosum, basale (\"Come, Let's Get Sun Burned\").</p><p>Basale (germinativum): single layer of dividing stem cells, melanocytes, Merkel cells. Spinosum: keratinocytes joined by desmosomes (\"prickle cells\"), Langerhans cells. Granulosum: keratohyalin granules and lamellar bodies (lipids for the water barrier). Lucidum: clear layer present only in thick skin (palms, soles). Corneum: dead, flattened, keratin-filled cells that shed. Keratinocytes take about 4 weeks to move from basale to corneum.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Corneum → granulosum → lucidum → spinosum → basale</b> — Lucidum lies above (superficial to) granulosum.</li><li><b>Lucidum → corneum → spinosum → granulosum → basale</b> — Corneum, not lucidum, is the most superficial layer.</li><li><b>Corneum → spinosum → granulosum → lucidum → basale</b> — Granulosum lies above spinosum, and lucidum is immediately beneath corneum.</li><li><b>Basale → spinosum → granulosum → lucidum → corneum</b> — This order runs deep to superficial, the reverse of what was asked.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> \"Come, Let's Get Sun Burned\" (superficial → deep).</p>","w":["Lucidum lies above (superficial to) granulosum.","Corneum, not lucidum, is the most superficial layer.","Granulosum lies above spinosum, and lucidum is immediately beneath corneum.","Correct — corneum, lucidum, granulosum, spinosum, basale.","This order runs deep to superficial, the reverse of what was asked."]},"e55":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Stratum basale = stratum germinativum: the deepest, mitotically active layer resting on the basement membrane over the dermal papillae.</p><p>\"Germinativum\" means it generates new cells: stem cells divide continuously to replenish the epidermis. During development, downgrowths of this layer into the dermis form hair follicles, sweat glands and (via follicles) sebaceous glands. Hemidesmosomes anchor basal cells to the basement membrane. Basal cell carcinoma arises from this layer.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Papillary dermis</b> — The papillary dermis belongs to the dermis, not the epidermis.</li><li><b>Stratum corneum</b> — The stratum corneum is the most superficial layer.</li><li><b>Stratum lucidum</b> — The stratum lucidum lies just beneath the corneum in thick skin.</li><li><b>Stratum granulosum</b> — The stratum granulosum lies in the middle of the epidermis.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Basale: stem cells, melanocytes, Merkel cells; origin of hair follicles and sweat glands.</p>","w":["The papillary dermis belongs to the dermis, not the epidermis.","The stratum corneum is the most superficial layer.","The stratum lucidum lies just beneath the corneum in thick skin.","The stratum granulosum lies in the middle of the epidermis.","Correct — the stratum germinativum."]},"e56":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Per the notes, nails develop in the stratum lucidum; as the nail migrates to the distal phalanx, it drags palmar-side nerve supply to the dorsum.</p><p>Nail fields first appear on the tips of the digits, then migrate dorsally onto the distal phalanx. The nerves migrate with them, which explains a clinically useful fact: the skin and nail bed over the dorsum of the distal fingertips are supplied by palmar (volar) digital nerves (e.g., median nerve for the dorsal tips of the lateral 3½ fingers), not dorsal nerves. This matters for digital nerve blocks.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>In the stratum basale; they take nerves from the dermis</b> — The notes describe nails developing in the stratum lucidum, not the stratum basale, and nerves aren't taken from the dermis.</li><li><b>In the dermis; they have no nerve supply</b> — Nails form from the epidermis and do have a nerve supply.</li><li><b>In the stratum corneum; the dorsal nerve supply moves to the palm</b> — The nerve supply moves from the palmar side to the dorsum, not the reverse.</li><li><b>In the hair follicle; nerves come from the sebaceous gland</b> — Nails don't develop from hair follicles, and nerves don't come from sebaceous glands.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Dorsal fingertip and nail bed = palmar digital nerve supply.</p>","w":["Correct — per the notes, nails form in the stratum lucidum and drag palmar nerve supply dorsally.","The notes describe nails developing in the stratum lucidum, not the stratum basale, and nerves aren't taken from the dermis.","Nails form from the epidermis and do have a nerve supply.","The nerve supply moves from the palmar side to the dorsum, not the reverse.","Nails don't develop from hair follicles, and nerves don't come from sebaceous glands."]},"e57":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hair develops when the stratum basale grows down into the dermis; keratinized cells form the hair root; the first fine fetal hair is lanugo.</p><p>Epidermal buds (hair germs) push into the dermis, and their base is indented by a dermal papilla containing capillaries and nerves. Cells over the papilla (hair matrix) proliferate and keratinize to form the hair shaft. Lanugo appears around months 3–4, covers the fetus, and is mostly shed before birth, replaced by vellus and terminal hairs. Persistent lanugo in adults may be seen in anorexia nervosa. Sebaceous glands bud from the side of the follicle.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Sebaceous glands produce hair; primitive hair is vernix</b> — Sebaceous glands arise from hair follicles rather than producing hair, and vernix is a skin coating, not hair.</li><li><b>The stratum corneum folds inward; primitive hair is lanugo</b> — The hair root comes from the stratum basale, not the stratum corneum.</li><li><b>Sweat glands form the hair root; primitive hair is terminal hair</b> — Sweat glands don't form hair roots, and terminal hair isn't the primitive form.</li><li><b>The dermis grows up into the epidermis; primitive hair is vellus</b> — The epidermis grows down into the dermis (not the reverse), and vellus hair isn't the primitive fetal hair.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Lanugo = fine fetal hair, shed before or soon after birth.</p>","w":["Sebaceous glands arise from hair follicles rather than producing hair, and vernix is a skin coating, not hair.","Correct — basale downgrowth forms the root; lanugo is primitive hair.","The hair root comes from the stratum basale, not the stratum corneum.","Sweat glands don't form hair roots, and terminal hair isn't the primitive form.","The epidermis grows down into the dermis (not the reverse), and vellus hair isn't the primitive fetal hair."]},"e58":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Sebaceous glands bud from the walls of hair follicles and secrete sebum (holocrine).</p><p>Sebaceous glands develop as outgrowths from the side of the developing hair follicle, so most open into follicles (pilosebaceous units). They secrete sebum by holocrine secretion. In the fetus, sebum mixes with shed cells to form vernix caseosa. At puberty, androgens enlarge the glands, contributing to acne. Free sebaceous glands (not associated with hair) exist in lips, eyelids (meibomian) and areola (Montgomery).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>From the dermis; milk</b> — Sebaceous glands don't arise from the dermis, and milk is secreted by mammary glands.</li><li><b>From the stratum lucidum; keratin</b> — They don't originate from the stratum lucidum, and they don't secrete keratin.</li><li><b>From mammary ducts; sebum</b> — They don't develop from mammary ducts.</li><li><b>From the stratum corneum; sweat</b> — They don't originate from the stratum corneum, and sweat comes from sweat glands.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Sebaceous = from follicles; sweat glands = from stratum basale.</p>","w":["Sebaceous glands don't arise from the dermis, and milk is secreted by mammary glands.","They don't originate from the stratum lucidum, and they don't secrete keratin.","Correct — sebaceous glands develop from hair follicles and secrete sebum.","They don't develop from mammary ducts.","They don't originate from the stratum corneum, and sweat comes from sweat glands."]},"e59":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Sweat glands develop as solid downgrowths of the stratum basale (germinativum) into the dermis.</p><p>The downgrowth elongates and coils at its deep end to form the secretory portion; the central cells degenerate to form a lumen. Eccrine glands are widespread and open directly onto the skin surface; apocrine glands (axilla, groin, areola) develop from hair follicles and open into them. Peripheral cells form myoepithelial cells.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Stratum spinosum</b> — The stratum spinosum isn't the source of glands.</li><li><b>Stratum corneum</b> — The stratum corneum consists of dead cells and doesn't form glands.</li><li><b>Stratum lucidum</b> — The stratum lucidum doesn't form sweat glands.</li><li><b>Stratum granulosum</b> — The stratum granulosum isn't the source of glands.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Eccrine glands open onto skin; apocrine glands open into hair follicles.</p>","w":["The stratum spinosum isn't the source of glands.","The stratum corneum consists of dead cells and doesn't form glands.","The stratum lucidum doesn't form sweat glands.","Correct — sweat glands develop from the stratum basale.","The stratum granulosum isn't the source of glands."]},"e60":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Mammary glands are modified sweat glands that grow down from epidermis into the dermis, with branching ducts; at puberty, estrogen causes gland hypertrophy.</p><p>Mammary ridges (milk lines) form along the ventral body wall from axilla to groin around week 4–6; most regresses except in the pectoral region. Epidermal cords grow down into the dermis and branch into lactiferous ducts, which canalize late in fetal life and open into a pit that becomes the nipple. At puberty, estrogen stimulates duct growth and fat deposition (with progesterone stimulating lobular development), forming the breast bud. Supernumerary nipples (polythelia) can occur anywhere along the milk line.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>They develop from the stratum corneum; at puberty the ducts close</b> — Mammary glands don't develop from the stratum corneum, and ducts don't close at puberty.</li><li><b>They form only at puberty from hair follicles</b> — They begin forming before birth, not only at puberty, and not from hair follicles.</li><li><b>They grow up from the muscle layer; at puberty testosterone causes atrophy</b> — They don't arise from muscle, and estrogen drives their growth at puberty.</li><li><b>They develop from sebaceous glands; at puberty they stop growing</b> — They are separate from sebaceous glands and continue to grow at puberty.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Milk line: axilla → groin; polythelia is common along it.</p>","w":["Mammary glands don't develop from the stratum corneum, and ducts don't close at puberty.","They begin forming before birth, not only at puberty, and not from hair follicles.","They don't arise from muscle, and estrogen drives their growth at puberty.","They are separate from sebaceous glands and continue to grow at puberty.","Correct — ducts grow down and branch; estrogen causes hypertrophy at puberty."]},"e61":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Epidermolysis bullosa: inherited fragility of the epidermal–dermal junction → blisters from minor friction.</p><p>Different structural proteins can be affected. EB simplex: keratin 5/14 mutations, split within basal keratinocytes (mildest). Junctional EB: laminin-332, type XVII collagen or integrin α6β4 defects, split in the lamina lucida. Dystrophic EB: type VII collagen (anchoring fibrils) defects, split below the basement membrane, with scarring and risk of squamous cell carcinoma. Newborns develop bullae at sites of handling; care focuses on avoiding friction and treating wounds.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Hypertrichosis</b> — Hypertrichosis is excessive hair growth.</li><li><b>Polythelia</b> — Polythelia is the presence of extra nipples.</li><li><b>Sebaceous cyst</b> — A sebaceous cyst results from a blocked pore.</li><li><b>Vernix caseosa</b> — Vernix caseosa is a normal cheesy coating on newborns.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> EB simplex (keratin), junctional (laminin/BP180), dystrophic (collagen VII).</p>","w":["Correct — epidermolysis bullosa.","Hypertrichosis is excessive hair growth.","Polythelia is the presence of extra nipples.","A sebaceous cyst results from a blocked pore.","Vernix caseosa is a normal cheesy coating on newborns."]},"e62":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Vernix caseosa: the white, cheesy coating on newborns — dead skin cells, amniotic fluid components and sebum.</p><p>Fetal sebaceous glands become active in the third trimester. Sebum mixes with desquamated epidermal cells and lanugo hairs to form vernix, which waterproofs the skin against amniotic fluid, aids thermoregulation, has antimicrobial properties and lubricates the baby during birth. It is most abundant around 36–38 weeks and thinner in post-term babies, who may have dry, peeling skin.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Sebaceous cyst; trapped sebum</b> — A sebaceous cyst is a blocked, inflamed gland, not a newborn coating.</li><li><b>Trichobezoar; swallowed hair</b> — A trichobezoar is a hairball in the stomach.</li><li><b>Lanugo; fine hair only</b> — Lanugo is fine primitive hair, not a cheesy coating (although it's mixed into vernix).</li><li><b>Epidermolysis bullosa; blister fluid</b> — Epidermolysis bullosa causes blisters.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Vernix is protective; it isn't routinely scrubbed off immediately.</p>","w":["A sebaceous cyst is a blocked, inflamed gland, not a newborn coating.","Correct — vernix caseosa.","A trichobezoar is a hairball in the stomach.","Lanugo is fine primitive hair, not a cheesy coating (although it's mixed into vernix).","Epidermolysis bullosa causes blisters."]},"e63":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Trichobezoar = mass of swallowed hair in the stomach, associated with trichophagia (hair eating), often with trichotillomania.</p><p>Hair is indigestible and resists peristalsis, so it accumulates in the stomach as a hairball, typically in young women with psychiatric conditions. Symptoms: abdominal pain, early satiety, vomiting, weight loss, palpable epigastric mass. Rapunzel syndrome is when the trichobezoar's tail extends into the small bowel. Treatment: removal (endoscopic or surgical) plus psychiatric care.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Sebaceous cyst — excessive hair growth</b> — Excessive hair growth is hypertrichosis, not a sebaceous cyst.</li><li><b>Hypertrichosis — blocked pores with inflamed sebaceous glands</b> — Blocked pores with inflamed sebaceous glands describe a sebaceous cyst, not hypertrichosis.</li><li><b>Vernix caseosa — bullae at the epidermo-dermal junction</b> — Bullae at the epidermo-dermal junction describe epidermolysis bullosa.</li><li><b>Epidermolysis bullosa — cheesy newborn coating</b> — The cheesy newborn coating is vernix caseosa.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Trichotillomania + trichophagia → trichobezoar.</p>","w":["Excessive hair growth is hypertrichosis, not a sebaceous cyst.","Blocked pores with inflamed sebaceous glands describe a sebaceous cyst, not hypertrichosis.","Correct — trichobezoar is hair in the stomach associated with trichophagia.","Bullae at the epidermo-dermal junction describe epidermolysis bullosa.","The cheesy newborn coating is vernix caseosa."]},"e64":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Polythelia = extra nipple(s) along the embryonic mammary line (axilla to groin).</p><p>The mammary ridges normally regress except at the pectoral region. If segments persist, accessory nipples (polythelia) or, more rarely, accessory breast tissue (polymastia) develop anywhere along the line — most often just below the normal breast or in the axilla. Accessory breast tissue can respond to hormones, swell in pregnancy, and rarely develop disease, including cancer. They're often mistaken for moles.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Poland syndrome</b> — Poland syndrome is absence of the pectoral muscles.</li><li><b>Athelia</b> — Athelia is absence of the nipple.</li><li><b>Amastia</b> — Amastia is absence of mammary glands.</li><li><b>Hypertrichosis</b> — Hypertrichosis is excessive hair.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Polythelia = extra nipple; polymastia = extra breast tissue.</p>","w":["Poland syndrome is absence of the pectoral muscles.","Athelia is absence of the nipple.","Amastia is absence of mammary glands.","Correct — polythelia.","Hypertrichosis is excessive hair."]},"e65":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Athelia = no nipple; amastia = no mammary gland; polythelia = extra nipple(s).</p><p>Use roots: \"thel-\" = nipple; \"mast-\" = breast. The prefix \"a-\" means absent and \"poly-\" means many. Athelia and amastia are rare and may occur with Poland syndrome or ectodermal dysplasias. Amazia is absence of breast glandular tissue with the nipple present.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Polythelia — no mammary glands; Athelia — extra nipples</b> — Polythelia means extra nipples (not absent glands), and athelia means no nipple (not extra nipples).</li><li><b>Amastia — extra glands; Polythelia — no nipple</b> — Amastia means absent glands (not extra), and polythelia means extra nipples (not absent).</li><li><b>Athelia — no mammary glands; Amastia — no nipple</b> — This is reversed: athelia = no nipple; amastia = no glands.</li><li><b>Athelia — extra nipples; Amastia — no nipple</b> — Athelia means no nipple, and amastia means no glands.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Thel = nipple; mast = breast.</p>","w":["Polythelia means extra nipples (not absent glands), and athelia means no nipple (not extra nipples).","Amastia means absent glands (not extra), and polythelia means extra nipples (not absent).","This is reversed: athelia = no nipple; amastia = no glands.","Athelia means no nipple, and amastia means no glands.","Correct — athelia is no nipple; amastia is no mammary gland."]},"p1":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> A motor unit is one alpha motor neuron plus all the muscle fibers it innervates; each fiber belongs to only one motor unit.</p><p>When the alpha motor neuron fires, every fiber in its unit contracts. Force is graded by recruiting more units (small, fatigue-resistant ones first — the size principle) and by raising their firing rate. In this patient, normal conduction velocity means the nerves are healthy, while a small CMAP that falls further with repetitive stimulation points to failure at the neuromuscular junction — classically myasthenia gravis, where fewer acetylcholine receptors make each successive end-plate potential less likely to reach threshold.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>A single muscle fiber and all the neurons that synapse onto it</b> — This is backwards — each skeletal muscle fiber is normally innervated by only one motor neuron.</li><li><b>The neuromuscular junction and its associated synaptic vesicles</b> — The NMJ is only the synapse between the neuron and one fiber; it's part of the motor unit, not the whole unit.</li><li><b>A group of muscle fibers that contract in response to a single stimulus</b> — This describes the result of activating a motor unit but omits the neuron that defines it.</li><li><b>A collection of motor neurons that innervate the same muscle</b> — A group of motor neurons supplying one muscle is a motor neuron pool, made of many motor units.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Decremental response on repetitive stimulation = postsynaptic NMJ disease (myasthenia); incremental response = presynaptic (Lambert–Eaton).</p>","w":["Correct — a motor unit is a single motor neuron and all the muscle fibers it innervates.","This is backwards — each skeletal muscle fiber is normally innervated by only one motor neuron.","The NMJ is only the synapse between the neuron and one fiber; it's part of the motor unit, not the whole unit.","This describes the result of activating a motor unit but omits the neuron that defines it.","A group of motor neurons supplying one muscle is a motor neuron pool, made of many motor units."]},"p2":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> A stronger electrical stimulus to a nerve recruits more axons (motor units) until all are activated — then the response plateaus.</p><p>Axons in a nerve have different thresholds (large axons have lower thresholds to external stimulation). As stimulus intensity rises, more axons reach threshold and more motor units contribute, so the compound muscle action potential grows. Once every axon is firing (supramaximal stimulus), increasing the intensity can't add more units, so the amplitude stops rising. Each individual muscle fiber action potential is all-or-none and doesn't get bigger.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Increased firing frequency of individual motor neurons</b> — A single stimulus produces one action potential per axon; a stronger stimulus doesn't make each neuron fire more often.</li><li><b>Increased amplitude of individual muscle fiber action potentials</b> — Muscle fiber action potentials are all-or-none; their amplitude doesn't increase with stimulus strength.</li><li><b>Increased release of acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction</b> — ACh release per impulse is normally more than enough (safety factor) and isn't raised by stimulus intensity.</li><li><b>Increased conduction velocity of nerve action potentials</b> — Conduction velocity depends on axon diameter and myelination, not stimulus intensity.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Graded force = recruitment (number of units) + rate coding (firing frequency).</p>","w":["A single stimulus produces one action potential per axon; a stronger stimulus doesn't make each neuron fire more often.","Correct — increasing stimulus intensity recruits more motor units until all are active.","Muscle fiber action potentials are all-or-none; their amplitude doesn't increase with stimulus strength.","ACh release per impulse is normally more than enough (safety factor) and isn't raised by stimulus intensity.","Conduction velocity depends on axon diameter and myelination, not stimulus intensity."]},"p3":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Myasthenia gravis: antibodies against postsynaptic nicotinic ACh receptors → fewer receptors for ACh to bind → fatigable weakness improved by AChE inhibitors.</p><p>Normal transmission: an action potential opens presynaptic voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels, Ca²⁺ triggers vesicle fusion, ACh diffuses across the cleft and binds nicotinic receptors, opening cation channels that produce the end-plate potential; acetylcholinesterase then breaks ACh down. In MG, antibodies block, cross-link and destroy receptors, so the end-plate potential barely reaches threshold, and as ACh release falls with repeated use, transmission fails — weakness worsens through the day and affects eye and bulbar muscles first. Edrophonium briefly blocks AChE, letting ACh linger and bind the remaining receptors.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Depolarization of the presynaptic neuron</b> — Presynaptic depolarization is normal in MG; the nerve itself works.</li><li><b>Vesicular release of acetylcholine into the synaptic cleft</b> — Reduced ACh release is Lambert–Eaton syndrome (antibodies to presynaptic Ca²⁺ channels), which improves with repeated effort.</li><li><b>Reuptake of acetylcholine into the presynaptic neuron</b> — ACh isn't reuptaken intact — AChE hydrolyzes it, and only choline is recycled.</li><li><b>Propagation of action potentials along the sarcolemma</b> — Conduction along the sarcolemma is normal; the problem is reaching threshold at the end plate.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> MG: ptosis, diplopia, worse with use, thymoma association, anti-AChR antibodies.</p>","w":["Presynaptic depolarization is normal in MG; the nerve itself works.","Reduced ACh release is Lambert–Eaton syndrome (antibodies to presynaptic Ca²⁺ channels), which improves with repeated effort.","Correct — MG impairs binding of ACh to postsynaptic nicotinic receptors.","ACh isn't reuptaken intact — AChE hydrolyzes it, and only choline is recycled.","Conduction along the sarcolemma is normal; the problem is reaching threshold at the end plate."]},"p4":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Botulinum toxin cleaves SNARE proteins in the motor nerve terminal, preventing ACh vesicle exocytosis → flaccid paralysis.</p><p>Botulinum toxin (from Clostridium botulinum, via contaminated canned food, wounds, or honey in infants) is taken up into cholinergic nerve terminals. Its light chain is a protease that cleaves SNAP-25, synaptobrevin or syntaxin — the proteins that dock vesicles to the membrane. Calcium may still enter, but vesicles can't fuse, so ACh isn't released. Result: descending flaccid paralysis (cranial nerves first — diplopia, ptosis, dysphagia), plus autonomic signs (dry mouth, dilated pupils). Of the offered choices, \"blockade of presynaptic Ca²⁺ influx\" is the closest description of failed release, though the true mechanism is downstream of Ca²⁺ entry. Therapeutic uses include strabismus, dystonia, spasticity and cosmetic wrinkles.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Inhibition of voltage-gated sodium channels in the presynaptic neuron</b> — Blocking voltage-gated Na⁺ channels is tetrodotoxin's mechanism; with botulinum the nerve action potential is normal.</li><li><b>Inhibition of acetylcholinesterase in the synaptic cleft</b> — AChE inhibitors (neostigmine, organophosphates) increase ACh, causing cholinergic excess, not flaccid paralysis.</li><li><b>Competitive antagonism of nicotinic acetylcholine receptors</b> — Competitive nicotinic receptor blockade is curare (tubocurarine).</li><li><b>Hyperactivation of acetylcholine release causing receptor desensitization</b> — Botulinum decreases ACh release; it doesn't overstimulate.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Botulinum = presynaptic release failure (SNARE cleavage); tetanus toxin blocks inhibitory interneuron release (spastic paralysis).</p>","w":["Blocking voltage-gated Na⁺ channels is tetrodotoxin's mechanism; with botulinum the nerve action potential is normal.","AChE inhibitors (neostigmine, organophosphates) increase ACh, causing cholinergic excess, not flaccid paralysis.","Marked correct (closest choice) — botulinum toxin stops presynaptic ACh release.","Competitive nicotinic receptor blockade is curare (tubocurarine).","Botulinum decreases ACh release; it doesn't overstimulate."]},"p5":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hierarchy: muscle (epimysium) → fascicles (perimysium) → myofibers/cells (endomysium) → myofibrils → sarcomeres → myofilaments (actin, myosin).</p><p>A myofiber is a single multinucleated muscle cell, packed with hundreds of myofibrils and surrounded by its endomysium. Each myofibril is a chain of sarcomeres, the basic contractile units (Z disc to Z disc). Each sarcomere is built from overlapping thin (actin) and thick (myosin) myofilaments. Fibers group into fascicles wrapped in perimysium, and fascicles make up the muscle, wrapped in epimysium.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Myofilament – A bundle of myofibrils surrounded by endomysium</b> — Myofilaments are single protein filaments (actin, myosin), much smaller than myofibrils.</li><li><b>Myofibril – A bundle of fascicles contained within the perimysium</b> — A myofibril is a chain of sarcomeres inside one cell; bundles of fibers in perimysium are fascicles.</li><li><b>Fascicle – A bundle of myofilaments forming the basic contractile unit</b> — A fascicle is a bundle of muscle fibers; the basic contractile unit is the sarcomere.</li><li><b>Sarcomere – A bundle of fascicles that functions as a contractile unit</b> — A sarcomere is the segment between two Z discs, not a bundle of fascicles.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Muscle &gt; fascicle &gt; fiber &gt; myofibril &gt; sarcomere &gt; myofilament.</p>","w":["Correct — a myofiber is a muscle cell full of myofibrils, wrapped in endomysium.","Myofilaments are single protein filaments (actin, myosin), much smaller than myofibrils.","A myofibril is a chain of sarcomeres inside one cell; bundles of fibers in perimysium are fascicles.","A fascicle is a bundle of muscle fibers; the basic contractile unit is the sarcomere.","A sarcomere is the segment between two Z discs, not a bundle of fascicles."]},"p6":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> T-tubules carry the action potential deep into the muscle fiber, where DHPR–RyR1 coupling triggers SR Ca²⁺ release.</p><p>Muscle fibers are wide, so diffusion of a signal from the surface would be too slow to activate central myofibrils together. T-tubules are invaginations of the sarcolemma that penetrate the fiber at every A–I junction, conducting the action potential inward. Their dihydropyridine receptors (L-type Ca²⁺ channels acting as voltage sensors) are mechanically linked to ryanodine receptors (RyR1) on the adjacent terminal cisternae of the SR. Depolarization changes DHPR shape, pulling RyR1 open; Ca²⁺ floods out, binds troponin C, and all myofibrils contract synchronously.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Direct activation of myosin heads for cross-bridge cycling</b> — Myosin heads are activated by ATP hydrolysis and binding to exposed actin sites, not by T-tubules.</li><li><b>Release of calcium directly from the T-tubules into the sarcoplasm</b> — Ca²⁺ is released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum via RyR1, not from the T-tubules.</li><li><b>Uptake of calcium from the sarcoplasm into the sarcoplasmic reticulum</b> — Ca²⁺ reuptake into the SR is done by the SERCA pump during relaxation.</li><li><b>Inhibition of excessive calcium release to prevent muscle fatigue</b> — T-tubules trigger Ca²⁺ release; they don't limit it.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Excitation–contraction coupling: AP → T-tubule → DHPR → RyR1 → Ca²⁺ → troponin C → cross-bridges.</p>","w":["Myosin heads are activated by ATP hydrolysis and binding to exposed actin sites, not by T-tubules.","Correct — T-tubules propagate the action potential into the interior of the fiber.","Ca²⁺ is released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum via RyR1, not from the T-tubules.","Ca²⁺ reuptake into the SR is done by the SERCA pump during relaxation.","T-tubules trigger Ca²⁺ release; they don't limit it."]},"p7":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Striations come from the regular alignment of thick (myosin) and thin (actin) myofilaments into A and I bands within sarcomeres.</p><p>In skeletal and cardiac muscle, sarcomeres in adjacent myofibrils line up in register, so their bands form continuous stripes across the whole fiber. The A band (dark) corresponds to the thick filaments with some overlapping actin; the I band (light) contains only thin filaments and is bisected by the Z disc; the H zone is the myosin-only center of the A band. Smooth muscle lacks this ordered arrangement and so has no striations.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Fascicles and myofibers</b> — Fascicles and myofibers are bundles visible at low power; they don't create banding.</li><li><b>Myofibers and myofibrils</b> — Myofibers and myofibrils are larger structural levels; the bands come from what's inside the myofibrils.</li><li><b>Endomysium and perimysium</b> — Endomysium and perimysium are connective tissue sheaths, not the source of striations.</li><li><b>Sarcolemma and sarcoplasm</b> — The sarcolemma (membrane) and sarcoplasm (cytoplasm) aren't banded.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Striated = skeletal + cardiac (sarcomeres); non-striated = smooth.</p>","w":["Fascicles and myofibers are bundles visible at low power; they don't create banding.","Myofibers and myofibrils are larger structural levels; the bands come from what's inside the myofibrils.","Correct — the bands reflect the arrangement of actin and myosin myofilaments.","Endomysium and perimysium are connective tissue sheaths, not the source of striations.","The sarcolemma (membrane) and sarcoplasm (cytoplasm) aren't banded."]},"p8":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Malignant hyperthermia is usually caused by a mutant ryanodine receptor (RyR1) that releases too much SR Ca²⁺ when triggered by volatile anesthetics or succinylcholine.</p><p>Triggering agents make the mutant RyR1 channel stay open, flooding the sarcoplasm with Ca²⁺. Muscle contracts continuously (rigidity, masseter spasm), SERCA pumps and cross-bridges consume enormous ATP, generating heat (hyperthermia), CO₂ (rising end-tidal CO₂ is an early sign), lactic acidosis, and rhabdomyolysis with hyperkalemia and myoglobinuria. It's autosomal dominant. Treatment: stop the trigger and give dantrolene, which binds RyR1 and blocks Ca²⁺ release.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Dihydropyridine receptor (DHPR)</b> — DHPR is the T-tubule voltage sensor that signals RyR1; it isn't the release channel (DHPR mutations are a rarer cause).</li><li><b>Sodium-potassium ATPase (Na⁺/K⁺ pump)</b> — The Na⁺/K⁺ pump maintains ion gradients; it isn't the SR Ca²⁺ release channel.</li><li><b>Tropomyosin</b> — Tropomyosin regulates actin–myosin binding; it doesn't release Ca²⁺.</li><li><b>Myosin light-chain kinase</b> — MLCK regulates smooth muscle contraction, not skeletal Ca²⁺ release.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> MH: rising end-tidal CO₂, rigidity, hyperthermia after anesthesia → dantrolene.</p>","w":["DHPR is the T-tubule voltage sensor that signals RyR1; it isn't the release channel (DHPR mutations are a rarer cause).","Correct — mutant RyR1 causes excessive SR calcium release in malignant hyperthermia.","The Na⁺/K⁺ pump maintains ion gradients; it isn't the SR Ca²⁺ release channel.","Tropomyosin regulates actin–myosin binding; it doesn't release Ca²⁺.","MLCK regulates smooth muscle contraction, not skeletal Ca²⁺ release."]},"p9":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Dystrophin links the actin cytoskeleton to the sarcolemma and extracellular matrix via the dystrophin–glycoprotein complex; its loss causes Duchenne/Becker muscular dystrophy.</p><p>During contraction, force must be transmitted from myofibrils to the cell membrane and surrounding basal lamina. Dystrophin (one of the largest genes, X-linked) binds cortical actin inside the cell and the dystroglycan complex in the membrane, which binds laminin outside. Without it, the sarcolemma tears with repeated contractions, Ca²⁺ leaks in, and fibers degenerate and are replaced by fat and fibrosis (pseudohypertrophy of calves). Duchenne (frameshift, no dystrophin) starts in early childhood with Gowers sign; Becker (in-frame, reduced/abnormal dystrophin) is milder and later. Creatine kinase is very high.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Titin</b> — Titin spans Z disc to M line, anchoring myosin and providing elasticity, not actin-to-membrane anchoring.</li><li><b>Nebulin</b> — Nebulin sets thin filament length.</li><li><b>Myosin light chain</b> — Myosin light chains regulate the myosin head.</li><li><b>Troponin C</b> — Troponin C binds Ca²⁺ to regulate contraction.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Duchenne: X-linked, Gowers sign, calf pseudohypertrophy, high CK, cardiomyopathy.</p>","w":["Titin spans Z disc to M line, anchoring myosin and providing elasticity, not actin-to-membrane anchoring.","Nebulin sets thin filament length.","Correct — dystrophin anchors actin to the sarcolemma; its mutation causes muscular dystrophy.","Myosin light chains regulate the myosin head.","Troponin C binds Ca²⁺ to regulate contraction."]},"p10":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> If Ca²⁺ can't bind troponin C, tropomyosin stays over actin's myosin-binding sites and myosin can't bind actin — no contraction.</p><p>At rest, tropomyosin lies in the groove of the actin filament, blocking myosin-binding sites, held there by troponin I. When Ca²⁺ binds troponin C, the troponin complex changes shape and rolls tropomyosin deeper into the groove, exposing the sites. Blocking Ca²⁺–TnC binding leaves the \"off\" state locked in place regardless of how much Ca²⁺ the SR releases, so cross-bridges can't form, ATP use by myosin stays low, and no force is generated.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Increased ATP hydrolysis by myosin heads</b> — Without actin binding, cross-bridge cycling and its ATP hydrolysis don't increase.</li><li><b>Increased cross-bridge cycling and force generation</b> — Cycling and force fall, not rise.</li><li><b>Unregulated myosin-actin interaction leading to sustained contraction</b> — The binding sites remain blocked; there's no unregulated interaction.</li><li><b>Enhanced calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum</b> — SR Ca²⁺ release is upstream of troponin and isn't enhanced by this drug.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Troponin C = Ca²⁺ sensor; troponin I = inhibitor; troponin T = tropomyosin anchor.</p>","w":["Without actin binding, cross-bridge cycling and its ATP hydrolysis don't increase.","Correct — myosin can't bind actin because tropomyosin still covers the binding sites.","Cycling and force fall, not rise.","The binding sites remain blocked; there's no unregulated interaction.","SR Ca²⁺ release is upstream of troponin and isn't enhanced by this drug."]},"p11":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Titin, the largest protein, spans from the Z disc to the M line, anchoring thick filaments and acting as a spring that provides passive tension.</p><p>Each titin molecule runs half a sarcomere. Its Z-disc end is fixed, its A-band portion binds the thick filament (keeping it centered), and its I-band portion contains elastic, spring-like segments. When a muscle is stretched, titin's springs extend and generate passive tension, then recoil to restore resting length. Titin isoforms explain differences in stiffness between muscles, and titin mutations are a common cause of dilated cardiomyopathy.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Nebulin</b> — Nebulin runs along the thin filament, setting its length.</li><li><b>Dystrophin</b> — Dystrophin anchors actin to the sarcolemma.</li><li><b>Tropomyosin</b> — Tropomyosin lies along actin, covering myosin-binding sites.</li><li><b>Myosin-binding protein C</b> — Myosin-binding protein C sits on the thick filament (C-zone) and modulates cross-bridges; it doesn't span Z to M.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Titin = passive tension/elasticity; nebulin = thin filament ruler.</p>","w":["Nebulin runs along the thin filament, setting its length.","Dystrophin anchors actin to the sarcolemma.","Tropomyosin lies along actin, covering myosin-binding sites.","Correct — titin extends from Z disc to M line, giving passive tension and structural support.","Myosin-binding protein C sits on the thick filament (C-zone) and modulates cross-bridges; it doesn't span Z to M."]},"p12":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Nebulin is the \"molecular ruler\" that sets and stabilizes thin filament length; mutations cause nemaline myopathy.</p><p>Nebulin runs along each actin filament from the Z disc, with its length matching that of the thin filament, and binds many actin monomers, helping to set filament length and stability. In nemaline myopathy (often NEB mutations), thin filaments are short and disorganized, and rod-like \"nemaline bodies\" (Z-disc material) accumulate in fibers, seen with Gomori trichrome stain. Children have hypotonia, facial and proximal weakness, and sometimes respiratory difficulty, because short thin filaments overlap poorly with myosin and generate less force.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Titin</b> — Titin relates to the thick filament and elasticity.</li><li><b>Troponin C</b> — Troponin C binds Ca²⁺; it doesn't determine filament length.</li><li><b>Desmin</b> — Desmin is an intermediate filament linking myofibrils at the Z discs.</li><li><b>Myosin heavy chain</b> — Myosin heavy chain is the thick filament motor.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Nemaline rods on Gomori trichrome → nebulin (or actin) mutation.</p>","w":["Titin relates to the thick filament and elasticity.","Correct — nebulin determines thin filament length; its mutation shortens actin filaments.","Troponin C binds Ca²⁺; it doesn't determine filament length.","Desmin is an intermediate filament linking myofibrils at the Z discs.","Myosin heavy chain is the thick filament motor."]},"p13":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> After Ca²⁺ binds troponin C, tropomyosin is displaced, exposing myosin-binding sites on actin.</p><p>Sequence of contraction: motor nerve AP → ACh → end-plate potential → muscle AP → T-tubules → DHPR → RyR1 → Ca²⁺ release → Ca²⁺ binds troponin C → tropomyosin moves → myosin (already \"cocked\" by ATP hydrolysis) binds actin → Pi release triggers the power stroke → ADP released → ATP binds and myosin detaches → ATP hydrolysis re-cocks the head. Relaxation begins when SERCA pumps Ca²⁺ back into the SR.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Detachment of myosin from actin</b> — Detachment of myosin from actin requires ATP binding and comes later in the cycle.</li><li><b>Hydrolysis of ATP to reset the myosin head</b> — ATP hydrolysis cocks the myosin head, but binding sites must be exposed first.</li><li><b>Depolarization of the sarcolemma via sodium influx</b> — Sarcolemmal depolarization happens before Ca²⁺ is released.</li><li><b>Reuptake of calcium into the sarcoplasmic reticulum</b> — Ca²⁺ reuptake into the SR ends contraction (relaxation).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Ca²⁺ → troponin C → tropomyosin moves → cross-bridges form.</p>","w":["Detachment of myosin from actin requires ATP binding and comes later in the cycle.","ATP hydrolysis cocks the myosin head, but binding sites must be exposed first.","Correct — tropomyosin is displaced to expose myosin-binding sites on actin.","Sarcolemmal depolarization happens before Ca²⁺ is released.","Ca²⁺ reuptake into the SR ends contraction (relaxation)."]},"p14":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The myosin heavy chain head contains the ATPase and actin-binding site that drive the power stroke.</p><p>Myosin II has two heavy chains (each with a globular head, neck and long tail) and two pairs of light chains. ATP binds the head and is hydrolyzed to ADP + Pi, cocking the head. When the head binds actin, Pi release triggers the conformational change (power stroke) that pulls actin toward the M line about 10 nm; ADP then leaves, and a new ATP binding releases the head. The speed of the heavy chain's ATPase determines whether a fiber is fast or slow twitch.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Actin</b> — Actin is the track myosin pulls on; it doesn't hydrolyze ATP for the power stroke.</li><li><b>Troponin T</b> — Troponin T anchors the troponin complex to tropomyosin.</li><li><b>Tropomyosin</b> — Tropomyosin regulates access to binding sites.</li><li><b>Nebulin</b> — Nebulin sets thin filament length.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Myosin ATPase speed = fast vs. slow twitch; mutations in β-myosin heavy chain cause hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.</p>","w":["Actin is the track myosin pulls on; it doesn't hydrolyze ATP for the power stroke.","Correct — the myosin heavy chain head hydrolyzes ATP and generates the power stroke.","Troponin T anchors the troponin complex to tropomyosin.","Tropomyosin regulates access to binding sites.","Nebulin sets thin filament length."]},"p15":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> An acetylcholinesterase inhibitor (neostigmine, pyridostigmine) raises ACh in the cleft, helping it reach the remaining receptors in myasthenia gravis.</p><p>In myasthenia, the problem is too few functional nicotinic receptors. Inhibiting acetylcholinesterase prolongs ACh's presence and lets each molecule bind receptors repeatedly, enlarging the end-plate potential so it reaches threshold more often. Pyridostigmine is the usual oral drug; neostigmine and edrophonium (short-acting, diagnostic) work the same way. Too much can cause cholinergic crisis (muscarinic side effects and depolarizing weakness). Drugs that reduce ACh release or block channels would worsen weakness.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>ω-Conotoxin</b> — ω-Conotoxin blocks presynaptic N-type Ca²⁺ channels, reducing ACh release — worse weakness.</li><li><b>Dendrotoxin</b> — Dendrotoxin blocks K⁺ channels, causing hyperexcitability, but doesn't fix receptor loss.</li><li><b>Botulinum toxin</b> — Botulinum toxin prevents ACh release, causing paralysis — contraindicated.</li><li><b>Tetrodotoxin (TTX)</b> — Tetrodotoxin blocks Na⁺ channels, stopping action potentials — worse weakness.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> MG treatment: pyridostigmine (symptomatic), immunosuppression, thymectomy; myasthenic crisis: IVIG/plasmapheresis.</p>","w":["ω-Conotoxin blocks presynaptic N-type Ca²⁺ channels, reducing ACh release — worse weakness.","Dendrotoxin blocks K⁺ channels, causing hyperexcitability, but doesn't fix receptor loss.","Correct — neostigmine inhibits AChE, increasing ACh at the NMJ.","Botulinum toxin prevents ACh release, causing paralysis — contraindicated.","Tetrodotoxin blocks Na⁺ channels, stopping action potentials — worse weakness."]},"p16":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Botulinum toxin acts presynaptically, blocking vesicle fusion and ACh release; curare, bungarotoxin and nicotine act at postsynaptic receptors.</p><p>Classify NMJ drugs by site. Presynaptic release blockers: botulinum toxin (SNARE cleavage), ω-conotoxin (Ca²⁺ channels), hemicholinium (choline uptake). Postsynaptic receptor antagonists: curare (competitive), α-bungarotoxin (nearly irreversible). Postsynaptic agonists/depolarizers: nicotine, succinylcholine. In the cleft: AChE inhibitors (neostigmine) increase ACh. Only botulinum toxin among the options interferes with release itself.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Curare</b> — Curare competitively blocks postsynaptic nicotinic receptors.</li><li><b>α-Bungarotoxin</b> — α-Bungarotoxin binds postsynaptic nicotinic receptors almost irreversibly.</li><li><b>Neostigmine</b> — Neostigmine inhibits acetylcholinesterase in the cleft, increasing ACh.</li><li><b>Nicotine</b> — Nicotine is an agonist at nicotinic receptors.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Presynaptic: botulinum, ω-conotoxin; postsynaptic: curare, bungarotoxin; cleft: AChE inhibitors.</p>","w":["Curare competitively blocks postsynaptic nicotinic receptors.","α-Bungarotoxin binds postsynaptic nicotinic receptors almost irreversibly.","Correct — botulinum toxin prevents ACh release from the presynaptic terminal.","Neostigmine inhibits acetylcholinesterase in the cleft, increasing ACh.","Nicotine is an agonist at nicotinic receptors."]},"p17":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Paralysis with normal ACh release means a postsynaptic block — nicotinic receptor antagonism (like curare).</p><p>If the nerve releases ACh normally but the muscle doesn't respond, the defect must be at or after the receptor. Competitive nondepolarizing blockers (tubocurarine, rocuronium, vecuronium) occupy nicotinic receptors without opening them, so the end-plate potential stays below threshold. They are used to relax muscles during surgery and are reversed by AChE inhibitors (neostigmine) or sugammadex (for rocuronium/vecuronium).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Inhibition of acetylcholinesterase</b> — AChE inhibition increases ACh, enhancing (or with large doses depolarizing) rather than producing this block.</li><li><b>Blockade of voltage-gated Ca++ channels in the presynaptic neuron</b> — Blocking presynaptic Ca²⁺ channels would prevent ACh release — excluded by the stem.</li><li><b>Enhancement of acetylcholine release</b> — More ACh release would enhance transmission, not paralyze.</li><li><b>Blockade of K+ channels in the presynaptic neuron</b> — Blocking presynaptic K⁺ channels prolongs the presynaptic AP and increases release.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Nondepolarizing blockers: competitive, reversed by neostigmine; depolarizing (succinylcholine): persistent depolarization, worsened by neostigmine in phase I.</p>","w":["AChE inhibition increases ACh, enhancing (or with large doses depolarizing) rather than producing this block.","Blocking presynaptic Ca²⁺ channels would prevent ACh release — excluded by the stem.","Correct — the drug blocks postsynaptic nicotinic receptors while release is normal.","More ACh release would enhance transmission, not paralyze.","Blocking presynaptic K⁺ channels prolongs the presynaptic AP and increases release."]},"p18":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Curare (d-tubocurarine) is a competitive antagonist at postsynaptic nicotinic ACh receptors.</p><p>Curare binds the ACh-binding sites on the nicotinic receptor without opening the channel. Fewer receptors respond, the end-plate potential shrinks below threshold, and the muscle is flaccidly paralyzed (respiratory muscles included, so ventilation is needed). Because the block is competitive, raising ACh with neostigmine can overcome it. It's the prototype for modern nondepolarizing neuromuscular blockers used in anesthesia.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It prevents the release of acetylcholine from the presynaptic neuron.</b> — Preventing ACh release is botulinum toxin (or Ca²⁺ channel blockers).</li><li><b>It inhibits the breakdown of acetylcholine in the synaptic cleft.</b> — Inhibiting ACh breakdown is an AChE inhibitor such as neostigmine.</li><li><b>It enhances the release of acetylcholine from the presynaptic neuron.</b> — Curare doesn't affect ACh release.</li><li><b>It increases the sensitivity of the postsynaptic cell to acetylcholine.</b> — Curare decreases, not increases, the end plate's response to ACh.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Competitive antagonist → reversible by more agonist (neostigmine raises ACh).</p>","w":["Preventing ACh release is botulinum toxin (or Ca²⁺ channel blockers).","Correct — curare blocks postsynaptic nicotinic ACh receptors.","Inhibiting ACh breakdown is an AChE inhibitor such as neostigmine.","Curare doesn't affect ACh release.","Curare decreases, not increases, the end plate's response to ACh."]},"p19":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Acetylcholinesterase in the synaptic cleft hydrolyzes ACh into choline and acetate, ending each signal.</p><p>AChE is anchored to the basal lamina of the cleft and is extremely fast, so each ACh molecule acts only briefly, allowing the end plate to repolarize between impulses. The choline produced is taken back into the nerve terminal by a high-affinity Na⁺-choline transporter and combined with acetyl-CoA by choline acetyltransferase to make new ACh, which is packaged into vesicles. Organophosphate poisoning (irreversible AChE inhibition) causes cholinergic crisis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Acetylcholine receptor</b> — The ACh receptor binds ACh to open its channel; it doesn't degrade it.</li><li><b>Choline transporter</b> — The choline transporter recycles choline after breakdown.</li><li><b>Synaptic vesicle</b> — Synaptic vesicles store and release ACh.</li><li><b>Voltage-gated Ca++ channel</b> — Voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels trigger release.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Organophosphate poisoning: \"DUMBBELSS\" muscarinic signs + muscle fasciculations/weakness; treat with atropine + pralidoxime.</p>","w":["The ACh receptor binds ACh to open its channel; it doesn't degrade it.","Correct — acetylcholinesterase breaks down ACh in the synaptic cleft.","The choline transporter recycles choline after breakdown.","Synaptic vesicles store and release ACh.","Voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels trigger release."]},"p20":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Isometric contraction generates tension without a change in muscle length.</p><p>Muscle contractions are described by what happens to length. Isometric: force equals load, so length is constant (holding a weight still, pushing a wall). Isotonic concentric: muscle shortens while lifting a load. Isotonic eccentric: muscle lengthens while controlling a load (lowering a weight, walking downhill) — this produces the most force per fiber and the most muscle soreness. In the lab, holding the muscle at fixed length and measuring tension tests isometric properties such as the length–tension relationship.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Isotonic concentric contraction</b> — Concentric contraction means shortening under load.</li><li><b>Isotonic eccentric contraction</b> — Eccentric contraction means lengthening under load.</li><li><b>Tetanic contraction</b> — Tetanic describes stimulation frequency (fused summation), not whether length changes.</li><li><b>Fibrillation</b> — Fibrillation is uncoordinated spontaneous contraction of individual fibers (e.g., after denervation).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Isometric = same length; isotonic = same load (concentric shortens, eccentric lengthens).</p>","w":["Concentric contraction means shortening under load.","Eccentric contraction means lengthening under load.","Correct — tension without shortening is an isometric contraction.","Tetanic describes stimulation frequency (fused summation), not whether length changes.","Fibrillation is uncoordinated spontaneous contraction of individual fibers (e.g., after denervation)."]},"p21":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Eccentric contraction = the muscle actively generates force while lengthening under a load it is controlling.</p><p>When lowering a dumbbell, the external load (gravity on the weight) exceeds the force the biceps produces, so the muscle lengthens, but its cross-bridges are still active, braking the descent. Eccentric work can generate more force than concentric work (cross-bridges are forcibly pulled apart, and passive elements help) and uses less ATP per unit force, but it causes more microscopic damage — the main cause of delayed-onset muscle soreness 24–72 hours later. Walking downhill and landing from jumps are eccentric for the quadriceps.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Isometric contraction</b> — Isometric contraction has no change in length (holding the dumbbell still).</li><li><b>Isotonic concentric contraction</b> — Concentric contraction is shortening — lifting the dumbbell up.</li><li><b>Fused tetanic contraction</b> — Fused tetany describes stimulation frequency, not the direction of length change.</li><li><b>Isokinetic contraction</b> — Isokinetic contraction is at constant velocity, usually on a special dynamometer.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Eccentric contractions → most force and most soreness (DOMS).</p>","w":["Isometric contraction has no change in length (holding the dumbbell still).","Concentric contraction is shortening — lifting the dumbbell up.","Correct — controlled lowering is an isotonic eccentric contraction.","Fused tetany describes stimulation frequency, not the direction of length change.","Isokinetic contraction is at constant velocity, usually on a special dynamometer."]},"p22":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The force–velocity relationship is inverse: the slower a muscle shortens, the more force it can generate (maximum at zero velocity, i.e., isometric).</p><p>Force depends on how many cross-bridges are attached at any moment. When a muscle shortens quickly, filaments slide past each other fast, so each cross-bridge spends less time attached and fewer are bound simultaneously; force falls. As velocity approaches zero, the maximum number of cross-bridges can attach, giving maximal force. At the other extreme, maximal shortening velocity is reached only with no load. That's why the heaviest lifts move slowly. Eccentric contractions extend the curve beyond isometric force.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The force generated by the muscle increases as the velocity of contraction increases.</b> — This reverses the relationship — force falls as velocity rises.</li><li><b>The muscle generates the most force when it contracts at maximal velocity.</b> — At maximal velocity the load (force) is near zero.</li><li><b>At higher velocities of contraction, the muscle is able to generate more force due to the greater recruitment of motor units.</b> — Recruitment raises force at any given velocity but doesn't reverse the curve; higher velocity still means less force.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Heavy load = slow movement; light load = fast movement (hyperbolic Hill curve).</p>","w":["This reverses the relationship — force falls as velocity rises.","At maximal velocity the load (force) is near zero.","Correct — at low contraction velocities, the muscle can generate more force.","Recruitment raises force at any given velocity but doesn't reverse the curve; higher velocity still means less force."]},"p23":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> During concentric contraction, force decreases as shortening velocity increases (hyperbolic force–velocity curve).</p><p>Same principle as the previous question. Plotting force against velocity gives a curve starting at maximum force (isometric, zero velocity) and falling to zero force at maximum velocity. Power (force × velocity) is therefore greatest at intermediate speeds, around one-third of maximal velocity, which is why athletes use moderate loads for power training. Fast-twitch fibers have a higher maximum velocity than slow-twitch ones, shifting the curve to the right.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>As velocity increases, the force generated by the muscle increases.</b> — This reverses the relationship.</li><li><b>The force generated by the muscle is independent of contraction velocity.</b> — Force clearly depends on velocity — that's the definition of the force–velocity curve.</li><li><b>Maximum force is produced at the highest contraction velocity.</b> — Maximum force occurs at zero velocity (isometric); maximum velocity occurs at zero load.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Peak power at ~1/3 of max velocity.</p>","w":["This reverses the relationship.","Force clearly depends on velocity — that's the definition of the force–velocity curve.","Maximum force occurs at zero velocity (isometric); maximum velocity occurs at zero load.","Correct — force decreases as velocity of contraction increases."]},"p24":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Passive tension comes from stretching the muscle's elastic elements (titin and connective tissue), not from cross-bridges.</p><p>When a relaxed muscle is stretched beyond its resting length, it resists like a rubber band. Inside the fiber, titin's spring regions extend; outside, endomysium, perimysium, epimysium and tendon stretch. Passive tension is negligible at short lengths and rises steeply with stretch, preventing overstretching and helping the muscle recoil. Total tension measured in a stretched, stimulated muscle = active (cross-bridge) tension + passive tension.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Passive tension results from the active cross-bridge cycling between actin and myosin filaments.</b> — Cross-bridge cycling produces active tension.</li><li><b>Passive tension is maximal when the muscle is at its shortest length.</b> — Passive tension is near zero at short lengths and rises with stretch.</li><li><b>Passive tension is generated when the muscle is contracted against a load.</b> — Contracting against a load generates active tension.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Total tension = active + passive; passive dominates at long lengths.</p>","w":["Cross-bridge cycling produces active tension.","Correct — passive tension comes from stretching the muscle's elastic structures.","Passive tension is near zero at short lengths and rises with stretch.","Contracting against a load generates active tension."]},"p25":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Active tension peaks at optimal sarcomere length (best actin–myosin overlap, ~2.0–2.2 µm); passive tension rises as the muscle is stretched.</p><p>The length–tension curve: at very short lengths, thin filaments overlap each other and thick filaments hit the Z discs, so active tension is low. At optimal length, every myosin head can reach actin without interference, so active tension is maximal. When stretched further, overlap decreases and active tension falls, reaching zero when filaments no longer overlap. Meanwhile passive tension, from titin and connective tissue, is negligible until stretch and then rises steeply. In the body, most skeletal muscles operate near optimal length; in the heart, the same principle underlies the Frank–Starling mechanism.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Active tension is greatest when the muscle is at its maximal length, while passive tension is minimal at this point.</b> — At maximal length, filament overlap is poor (low active tension) and passive tension is highest — the reverse.</li><li><b>Passive tension and active tension are the same at all muscle lengths.</b> — Active and passive tension follow completely different curves.</li><li><b>Active tension increases as the muscle is stretched, while passive tension decreases.</b> — Beyond optimal length, active tension falls while passive tension rises.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Length–tension relationship explains Frank–Starling: more preload → more force (up to a point).</p>","w":["At maximal length, filament overlap is poor (low active tension) and passive tension is highest — the reverse.","Correct — active tension is maximal at optimal length, and passive tension rises with stretch.","Active and passive tension follow completely different curves.","Beyond optimal length, active tension falls while passive tension rises."]},"p26":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Energy systems in sequence: stored ATP (seconds) → creatine phosphate (~10 s) → glycolysis of muscle glycogen (main source as activity continues) → increasing oxidative use of fat over long durations.</p><p>Muscle holds only a few seconds of ATP, buffered by creatine phosphate via creatine kinase. Once these are used, ATP comes from glycolysis and oxidative phosphorylation. Muscle glycogen is the most readily available carbohydrate and, per the slides, the main fuel once ATP and CP are depleted; blood glucose (from liver glycogen) and fatty acids contribute more as exercise continues and as intensity falls. In a marathon, glycogen depletion (\"hitting the wall\") occurs around 2 hours, which is why carbohydrate loading helps.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Creatine phosphate</b> — Creatine phosphate lasts only seconds.</li><li><b>Blood glucose</b> — Blood glucose contributes but less than muscle glycogen early in sustained activity.</li><li><b>Fatty acids</b> — Fatty acids become more important in prolonged, lower-intensity work.</li><li><b>Amino acids</b> — Amino acids are a minor fuel during exercise.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hitting the wall = muscle glycogen depletion.</p>","w":["Creatine phosphate lasts only seconds.","Correct (per the slides) — stored muscle glycogen is the main fuel after ATP and CP are depleted.","Blood glucose contributes but less than muscle glycogen early in sustained activity.","Fatty acids become more important in prolonged, lower-intensity work.","Amino acids are a minor fuel during exercise."]},"p27":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Ca²⁺ release from the SR is what turns on cross-bridge cycling, so it is directly coupled to ATP hydrolysis by myosin ATPase.</p><p>The question asks which energy step depends on Ca²⁺ entering the sarcoplasm. Calcium binds troponin C, exposing actin's binding sites, allowing myosin to cycle and hydrolyze ATP (step 1 in the slide figure). Calcium also later drives ATP use by SERCA for reuptake. The other steps (creatine kinase, glycolysis, oxidative phosphorylation, lactate formation) are ATP-regenerating pathways that respond to falling ATP, not directly to Ca²⁺ movement.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>ATP production from glycolysis (Step 3c)</b> — Glycolysis regenerates ATP; it isn't directly coupled to Ca²⁺ release.</li><li><b>Creatine phosphate breakdown by creatine kinase (Step 3a)</b> — Creatine kinase rebuilds ATP from creatine phosphate.</li><li><b>Oxidative phosphorylation (Step 3b)</b> — Oxidative phosphorylation supplies ATP in mitochondria.</li><li><b>Conversion of pyruvate to lactate</b> — Pyruvate → lactate regenerates NAD⁺ to keep anaerobic glycolysis running.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> ATP uses in muscle: myosin ATPase (contraction), SERCA (relaxation), Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase (membrane potential).</p>","w":["Marked correct — Ca²⁺ release triggers myosin ATPase activity and ATP hydrolysis.","Glycolysis regenerates ATP; it isn't directly coupled to Ca²⁺ release.","Creatine kinase rebuilds ATP from creatine phosphate.","Oxidative phosphorylation supplies ATP in mitochondria.","Pyruvate → lactate regenerates NAD⁺ to keep anaerobic glycolysis running."]},"p28":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Slow-twitch (type I) fibers: high fatigue resistance, many mitochondria, rich capillary supply, myoglobin, aerobic metabolism — but low force.</p><p>Type I fibers are recruited first and used for posture and endurance. Their slow myosin ATPase produces slower contraction and lower force, but abundant mitochondria and oxidative enzymes let them regenerate ATP efficiently from fats and carbohydrates for hours. Endurance training increases their mitochondrial density and capillaries. Fast-twitch fibers trade endurance for speed and power.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>High fatigue resistance and high force production.</b> — Half right — type I fibers resist fatigue but produce LOW force.</li><li><b>Rapid contraction and low fatigue resistance.</b> — Rapid contraction with low fatigue resistance describes fast-twitch fibers.</li><li><b>Low fatigue resistance and high power output.</b> — Low fatigue resistance with high power describes fast-twitch glycolytic fibers.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Marathon = type I; sprint/lift = type II.</p>","w":["Half right — type I fibers resist fatigue but produce LOW force.","Rapid contraction with low fatigue resistance describes fast-twitch fibers.","Low fatigue resistance with high power describes fast-twitch glycolytic fibers.","Correct — slow-twitch fibers have high fatigue resistance and many mitochondria."]},"p29":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Fast-twitch (type II) fibers contract rapidly and forcefully but fatigue quickly, relying on anaerobic glycolysis.</p><p>Type IIx/IIb fibers have fast myosin ATPase, large diameter, extensive SR for rapid Ca²⁺ cycling, big glycogen stores and few mitochondria. They produce large, quick forces for brief efforts like lifting and sprinting, but lactate and H⁺ accumulate and phosphocreatine runs out, so they tire within seconds to a minute. Resistance training mainly enlarges these fibers (hypertrophy).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Low power output and low force generation.</b> — Fast-twitch fibers have HIGH power and force.</li><li><b>High number of mitochondria and high aerobic capacity.</b> — Many mitochondria and high aerobic capacity describe slow-twitch (type I) fibers.</li><li><b>Slow contraction speed with a high resistance to fatigue.</b> — Slow contraction with high fatigue resistance describes slow-twitch fibers.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Type II fibers hypertrophy most with strength training and are lost first with aging (sarcopenia).</p>","w":["Fast-twitch fibers have HIGH power and force.","Correct — fast-twitch fibers produce high force rapidly but fatigue easily.","Many mitochondria and high aerobic capacity describe slow-twitch (type I) fibers.","Slow contraction with high fatigue resistance describes slow-twitch fibers."]},"p30":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The latent period is the short delay after the action potential before tension develops, while excitation–contraction coupling (Ca²⁺ release, troponin activation) takes place.</p><p>A single twitch has three phases. Latent period (a few ms): the AP spreads through T-tubules, Ca²⁺ is released, binds troponin, and cross-bridges begin to attach — tension isn't yet measurable. Contraction phase: cross-bridges cycle, tension rises to a peak and the muscle shortens. Relaxation phase: SERCA pumps Ca²⁺ back into the SR, cross-bridges detach and tension falls. The twitch lasts much longer than the muscle action potential, which is why stimuli can summate.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Relaxation phase</b> — The relaxation phase is when tension falls after the peak.</li><li><b>Contraction phase</b> — The contraction phase is when tension rises to its peak and the muscle shortens.</li><li><b>Summation phase</b> — Summation is the build-up of force from repeated stimuli, not a phase of a single twitch.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Twitch = latent → contraction → relaxation; AP is much shorter than the twitch.</p>","w":["The relaxation phase is when tension falls after the peak.","Correct — the latent period is the delay for excitation–contraction coupling.","The contraction phase is when tension rises to its peak and the muscle shortens.","Summation is the build-up of force from repeated stimuli, not a phase of a single twitch."]},"p31":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Peak tension develops during the contraction phase, when cross-bridges cycle most actively.</p><p>After the latent period, Ca²⁺ concentration around the myofilaments is high, cross-bridges form rapidly, and tension climbs to its peak (and the muscle shortens if the load allows). As SERCA removes Ca²⁺, fewer cross-bridges form and the relaxation phase begins. Fast fibers have shorter contraction and relaxation phases than slow fibers because their SR pumps Ca²⁺ faster and their myosin cycles faster.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Latent period</b> — During the latent period there's no measurable tension yet.</li><li><b>Relaxation phase</b> — During the relaxation phase, tension is declining as Ca²⁺ is pumped back.</li><li><b>Refractory period</b> — The refractory period is a brief electrical property of the membrane, not part of the tension curve.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Skeletal muscle refractory period is short relative to the twitch → summation and tetanus are possible (unlike cardiac muscle).</p>","w":["During the latent period there's no measurable tension yet.","During the relaxation phase, tension is declining as Ca²⁺ is pumped back.","Correct — peak tension is generated in the contraction phase.","The refractory period is a brief electrical property of the membrane, not part of the tension curve."]},"p32":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Wave (temporal) summation: a new action potential arrives before the muscle fully relaxes, so tension builds on the previous twitch.</p><p>Because a skeletal muscle action potential lasts only a few ms while the twitch lasts tens of ms, a second stimulus can arrive while Ca²⁺ is still elevated and some cross-bridges are attached. Additional Ca²⁺ release adds to the existing contraction, so peak tension is higher than a single twitch. As frequency rises further, the twitches merge into unfused tetanus (wavy) and then fused tetanus (smooth plateau). Cardiac muscle can't do this because its long refractory period prevents summation.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Single twitch</b> — A twitch is the response to a single action potential.</li><li><b>Fused tetany</b> — Fused tetany is the smooth maximal plateau with no relaxation at all.</li><li><b>Unfused tetany</b> — Unfused tetany is a sustained, wavy contraction at higher frequency — the step beyond basic summation.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Twitch → summation → unfused tetanus → fused tetanus with increasing frequency.</p>","w":["A twitch is the response to a single action potential.","Correct — this is wave summation.","Fused tetany is the smooth maximal plateau with no relaxation at all.","Unfused tetany is a sustained, wavy contraction at higher frequency — the step beyond basic summation."]},"p33":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Fused tetanus is the smooth, maximal plateau reached when stimulation is so frequent that the muscle can't relax at all.</p><p>At high stimulation frequencies, Ca²⁺ stays elevated continuously, troponin remains saturated, and cross-bridges cycle without interruption. The twitches fuse into a steady, maximal force. Raising the frequency further can't add Ca²⁺ effect or cross-bridges, so force plateaus. Voluntary strong contractions use near-tetanic firing rates, combined with recruitment of all motor units.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Single twitch</b> — A single twitch produces far less than maximal force.</li><li><b>Wave summation</b> — During summation, tension still rises with frequency; it hasn't plateaued.</li><li><b>Unfused tetany</b> — Unfused tetany still has partial relaxation and can rise with more frequency.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Tetanus (physiological) ≠ tetanus (disease from Clostridium tetani toxin).</p>","w":["A single twitch produces far less than maximal force.","During summation, tension still rises with frequency; it hasn't plateaued.","Correct — the plateau of maximal contraction is fused tetany.","Unfused tetany still has partial relaxation and can rise with more frequency."]},"p34":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Exercise raises cardiac output by increasing both heart rate and stroke volume, while total peripheral resistance falls.</p><p>Sympathetic stimulation increases heart rate and contractility. Venous return rises because the skeletal muscle pump squeezes veins, deeper breathing aids the thoracic pump, and venoconstriction shifts blood centrally, increasing preload and stroke volume (Frank–Starling). Cardiac output can rise 4–6-fold. Local metabolites (adenosine, K⁺, H⁺, CO₂, NO) dilate arterioles in working muscle, lowering total peripheral resistance, so mean arterial pressure rises only modestly while systolic pressure rises more.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Decreased cardiac output and stroke volume.</b> — Both cardiac output and stroke volume rise during exercise.</li><li><b>Decreased venous return and peripheral resistance.</b> — Venous return increases; peripheral resistance does fall, but venous return doesn't.</li><li><b>Increased systemic vascular resistance and heart rate.</b> — Systemic vascular resistance falls because exercising muscle vasodilates.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Exercise: ↑HR, ↑SV, ↑CO, ↓TPR, ↑systolic BP, diastolic roughly unchanged.</p>","w":["Both cardiac output and stroke volume rise during exercise.","Correct — heart rate and stroke volume both increase.","Venous return increases; peripheral resistance does fall, but venous return doesn't.","Systemic vascular resistance falls because exercising muscle vasodilates."]},"p35":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Minute ventilation = tidal volume × respiratory rate; both increase during exercise.</p><p>At the onset of exercise, ventilation rises quickly through neural signals (central command and joint/muscle receptors). Tidal volume increases first, then respiratory rate rises as intensity increases further. Arterial PO₂ and PCO₂ stay close to normal in moderate exercise because ventilation matches metabolic demand; at very high intensity, lactic acid stimulates further hyperventilation (beyond the ventilatory threshold).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Decreased tidal volume and increased respiratory rate.</b> — Tidal volume increases, not decreases.</li><li><b>Increased tidal volume and decreased respiratory rate.</b> — Respiratory rate increases, not decreases.</li><li><b>Decreased tidal volume and respiratory rate.</b> — Both increase.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> In moderate exercise arterial PO₂ and PCO₂ remain normal despite greatly increased ventilation.</p>","w":["Tidal volume increases, not decreases.","Respiratory rate increases, not decreases.","Correct — tidal volume and respiratory rate both increase.","Both increase."]},"p36":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> EPOC (excess post-exercise oxygen consumption) is mainly the O₂ needed to restore ATP and creatine phosphate, refill O₂ stores, clear lactate and support raised temperature, HR and breathing.</p><p>During intense exercise, ATP demand outstrips aerobic supply, so stores of ATP, creatine phosphate and O₂ (on myoglobin and hemoglobin) are drawn down and lactate accumulates. After exercise, oxygen consumption stays above resting levels while these are restored: the fast component (minutes) rebuilds ATP–CP and O₂ stores; the slow component (longer) handles lactate conversion, elevated body temperature, catecholamine effects and ongoing cardiorespiratory work.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>EPOC results from the direct re-synthesis of glycogen in the liver and muscles.</b> — Glycogen resynthesis takes hours to days and isn't the main cause of EPOC.</li><li><b>EPOC is caused by increased carbon dioxide production during muscle recovery.</b> — CO₂ is a product of metabolism; it doesn't cause the extra O₂ uptake.</li><li><b>EPOC is due to decreased oxygen availability in the bloodstream following exercise.</b> — Blood O₂ availability isn't decreased after exercise.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> EPOC fast component = ATP/CP + O₂ stores; slow component = lactate, temperature, hormones.</p>","w":["Correct — EPOC is driven largely by replenishing ATP and creatine phosphate.","Glycogen resynthesis takes hours to days and isn't the main cause of EPOC.","CO₂ is a product of metabolism; it doesn't cause the extra O₂ uptake.","Blood O₂ availability isn't decreased after exercise."]},"p37":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> After intense anaerobic exercise, EPOC is large, partly because oxygen is needed to clear accumulated lactate (oxidation or gluconeogenesis via the Cori cycle).</p><p>Anaerobic glycolysis produces lactate and H⁺. During recovery, lactate is either oxidized to CO₂ (in heart, slow muscle fibers) or carried to the liver and converted back to glucose (Cori cycle, which costs ATP generated aerobically). Both require extra oxygen. Together with restoration of ATP–CP and O₂ stores, this produces a larger, longer EPOC after anaerobic work than after steady aerobic exercise.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>EPOC is minimal because anaerobic exercise does not require a significant increase in oxygen consumption post-exercise.</b> — EPOC is larger, not minimal, after anaerobic work.</li><li><b>EPOC results from the immediate replenishment of oxygen stores in the blood and muscle tissues.</b> — Refilling myoglobin/blood O₂ is a small, fast part of EPOC, not its main role here.</li><li><b>EPOC is primarily related to the restoration of the oxygen debt accumulated during aerobic exercise.</b> — The workout was anaerobic; the oxygen debt mainly reflects that anaerobic work.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cori cycle: muscle lactate → liver gluconeogenesis → glucose back to muscle (net cost 4 ATP).</p>","w":["EPOC is larger, not minimal, after anaerobic work.","Correct — EPOC after anaerobic exercise includes the O₂ needed to clear lactate.","Refilling myoglobin/blood O₂ is a small, fast part of EPOC, not its main role here.","The workout was anaerobic; the oxygen debt mainly reflects that anaerobic work."]},"p38":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Dense bodies anchor actin filaments in smooth muscle and transmit force — the functional equivalent of Z discs.</p><p>Smooth muscle lacks sarcomeres; instead, actin filaments attach to cytoplasmic dense bodies (containing α-actinin) and membrane-associated dense plaques. Desmin and vimentin intermediate filaments link dense bodies into a 3D network. When myosin pulls on actin, dense bodies are drawn together and the force is transmitted to the cell membrane and on to neighboring cells via adherens junctions, so the cell shortens and twists. The lattice arrangement allows smooth muscle to shorten much more than skeletal muscle.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Dense bodies serve as storage sites for calcium ions during muscle contraction.</b> — Ca²⁺ is stored in the sarcoplasmic reticulum, not dense bodies.</li><li><b>Dense bodies store glycogen for energy production during contraction.</b> — Glycogen is stored as cytoplasmic granules.</li><li><b>Dense bodies are responsible for the release of acetylcholine in smooth muscle.</b> — ACh is released from autonomic nerve varicosities, not dense bodies.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Dense bodies = Z-disc equivalent (α-actinin).</p>","w":["Correct — dense bodies anchor actin filaments and transmit force.","Ca²⁺ is stored in the sarcoplasmic reticulum, not dense bodies.","Glycogen is stored as cytoplasmic granules.","ACh is released from autonomic nerve varicosities, not dense bodies."]},"p39":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Many dense bodies (dense plaques) anchor actin directly to the plasma membrane; Z discs link thin filaments of adjacent sarcomeres in a regular array.</p><p>In skeletal muscle, Z discs sit within myofibrils, joining the thin filaments of neighboring sarcomeres in perfect alignment, and the whole myofibril connects to the membrane at its ends (myotendinous junction) and via costameres. In smooth muscle there are no sarcomeres: cytoplasmic dense bodies scatter throughout the cell, and dense plaques on the inner membrane attach actin directly, so contraction pulls on the membrane at many points and the cell changes shape as a whole. That direct membrane anchoring is the key distinction per this key.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Dense bodies are located in the sarcoplasmic reticulum, while Z-discs are found in the plasma membrane.</b> — Neither sits in the SR; Z discs are within myofibrils, not the plasma membrane.</li><li><b>Dense bodies contain myosin filaments, whereas Z-discs anchor only actin filaments.</b> — Dense bodies anchor actin, not myosin.</li><li><b>Dense bodies are involved in calcium storage, whereas Z-discs are not.</b> — Neither stores Ca²⁺; the SR does.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Smooth muscle: dense plaques on the membrane + dense bodies in cytoplasm.</p>","w":["Neither sits in the SR; Z discs are within myofibrils, not the plasma membrane.","Correct (per key) — dense bodies/plaques tie actin to the membrane, while Z discs join actin filaments of adjacent sarcomeres.","Dense bodies anchor actin, not myosin.","Neither stores Ca²⁺; the SR does."]},"p40":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The latch state: dephosphorylated myosin stays attached to actin and cycles very slowly, so smooth muscle maintains tension with very little ATP.</p><p>Smooth muscle contraction starts when Ca²⁺–calmodulin activates MLCK to phosphorylate myosin light chains. If myosin light-chain phosphatase dephosphorylates myosin while it's still bound to actin, the head detaches extremely slowly, forming a \"latch bridge.\" Tension is maintained even as Ca²⁺ and MLCK activity fall, with ATP use reduced several-fold. This is ideal for sustained tone in blood vessels and sphincters. Drugs that relax vascular smooth muscle (e.g., nitrates raising cGMP, calcium channel blockers) reduce tone partly by favoring dephosphorylation and relaxation.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The latch state is characterized by rapid detachment of myosin heads from actin filaments, allowing for sustained contraction.</b> — The latch state involves slow, not rapid, detachment.</li><li><b>The latch state is associated with an increased rate of ATP hydrolysis, resulting in smooth muscle relaxation.</b> — Latch lowers ATP use and maintains tone rather than relaxing the muscle.</li><li><b>The latch state occurs during the initial phase of muscle contraction, contributing to rapid shortening.</b> — Latch is a sustained, later phase, not the initial rapid shortening.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Smooth muscle: slow, sustained, economical contraction (latch); skeletal: fast, ATP-hungry.</p>","w":["Correct — in the latch state, attached cross-bridges maintain tension with little ATP use.","The latch state involves slow, not rapid, detachment.","Latch lowers ATP use and maintains tone rather than relaxing the muscle.","Latch is a sustained, later phase, not the initial rapid shortening."]},"p41":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Multi-unit smooth muscle: each fiber is separately innervated, with few or no gap junctions, so fibers contract independently (fine, graded control).</p><p>Think of multi-unit smooth muscle as working like skeletal motor units: force is graded by how many fibers the autonomic nerves activate. Because there is little electrical coupling, it rarely shows spontaneous pacemaker activity or stretch-induced contraction. Examples: iris (pupil size), ciliary muscle (lens accommodation), arrector pili, vas deferens, and large arteries and airways. Single-unit (visceral) muscle is the opposite: gap junctions make a sheet behave as one unit.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Multi-unit smooth muscle fibers are electrically coupled via gap junctions and contract as a single unit.</b> — Gap-junction coupling with contraction as one unit defines single-unit (visceral) smooth muscle.</li><li><b>Multi-unit smooth muscle is primarily involved in sustained contractions, such as peristalsis in the gastrointestinal tract.</b> — Peristalsis in the gut is a function of single-unit smooth muscle.</li><li><b>Multi-unit smooth muscle fibers have a low degree of innervation, which allows for coordinated contraction.</b> — Multi-unit smooth muscle is densely innervated; dense innervation, not low innervation, is what controls it.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Multi-unit = iris, ciliary body, arrector pili, vas deferens. Single-unit = gut, uterus, bladder, small vessels.</p>","w":["Correct — multi-unit fibers are separately innervated and contract independently.","Gap-junction coupling with contraction as one unit defines single-unit (visceral) smooth muscle.","Peristalsis in the gut is a function of single-unit smooth muscle.","Multi-unit smooth muscle is densely innervated; dense innervation, not low innervation, is what controls it."]},"p42":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Single-unit (visceral) smooth muscle cells are coupled by gap junctions, so the whole sheet depolarizes and contracts together.</p><p>Many single-unit muscles contain pacemaker cells (e.g., interstitial cells of Cajal in the gut) that generate slow waves; electrical activity spreads through gap junctions, producing rhythmic, coordinated contraction such as peristalsis. Single-unit muscle also contracts in response to stretch (the myogenic response) and is strongly modulated by hormones — oxytocin increases gap junctions and contraction in the uterus near term.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Single-unit smooth muscle fibers contract independently, with each fiber having its own nerve supply.</b> — Independent fibers each with their own nerve supply describes multi-unit smooth muscle.</li><li><b>Single-unit smooth muscle is typically found in muscles that require fine, discrete movements, such as the iris.</b> — The iris needs fine, discrete control and is a multi-unit example.</li><li><b>Single-unit smooth muscle fibers do not respond to hormonal regulation.</b> — Single-unit smooth muscle responds strongly to hormones and local factors (oxytocin, stretch).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Labor: estrogen ↑ gap junctions (connexin 43) and oxytocin receptors in the myometrium.</p>","w":["Independent fibers each with their own nerve supply describes multi-unit smooth muscle.","Correct — gap junctions synchronize contraction in single-unit smooth muscle.","The iris needs fine, discrete control and is a multi-unit example.","Single-unit smooth muscle responds strongly to hormones and local factors (oxytocin, stretch)."]},"p43":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Smooth muscle contraction starts with a rise in cytosolic Ca²⁺, which binds calmodulin — every later step depends on this.</p><p>Sequence: stimulus (stretch, nerve, hormone) → Ca²⁺ enters through L-type channels and is released from the SR (IP₃ receptors, Ca²⁺-induced release) → Ca²⁺ binds calmodulin → Ca²⁺–calmodulin activates myosin light-chain kinase → MLCK phosphorylates the regulatory light chain → myosin ATPase activity rises and cross-bridges cycle. Relaxation: Ca²⁺ is removed and myosin light-chain phosphatase dephosphorylates myosin. Note smooth muscle has no troponin; regulation is on the myosin side.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Myosin light-chain kinase (MLCK) activation and phosphorylation of myosin.</b> — MLCK activation is the second step, after Ca²⁺ binds calmodulin.</li><li><b>Activation of the sodium-potassium pump to increase intracellular calcium.</b> — The Na⁺/K⁺ pump doesn't raise intracellular Ca²⁺.</li><li><b>Activation of actin filaments to generate force.</b> — Smooth muscle has no troponin; actin isn't \"activated\" first — myosin phosphorylation is the switch.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Skeletal: Ca²⁺ → troponin C (thin filament). Smooth: Ca²⁺ → calmodulin → MLCK (thick filament).</p>","w":["MLCK activation is the second step, after Ca²⁺ binds calmodulin.","Correct — Ca²⁺ release and binding to calmodulin initiate contraction.","The Na⁺/K⁺ pump doesn't raise intracellular Ca²⁺.","Smooth muscle has no troponin; actin isn't \"activated\" first — myosin phosphorylation is the switch."]},"p44":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The latch state lets smooth muscle hold tension for long periods while using very little ATP.</p><p>When myosin light-chain phosphatase dephosphorylates a myosin head while it's still attached to actin, the head detaches extremely slowly (\"latch bridge\"). Tension persists although Ca²⁺ and MLCK activity have fallen, and ATP use drops several-fold. This explains how vascular smooth muscle and sphincters maintain tone all day with minimal energy cost.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The activation of the sodium-potassium ATPase pump to maintain ion gradients.</b> — The Na⁺/K⁺ pump consumes ATP and maintains ion gradients; it doesn't hold tension.</li><li><b>The rapid dissociation of myosin heads from actin, conserving energy.</b> — Rapid detachment would let tension fall, not maintain it.</li><li><b>The continuous cycling of actin and myosin cross-bridges, allowing for constant force generation.</b> — Continuous cross-bridge cycling consumes one ATP per stroke — the opposite of energy saving.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Latch = sustained tone, low ATP (vessels, sphincters).</p>","w":["The Na⁺/K⁺ pump consumes ATP and maintains ion gradients; it doesn't hold tension.","Correct — the latch state maintains tension with minimal ATP.","Rapid detachment would let tension fall, not maintain it.","Continuous cross-bridge cycling consumes one ATP per stroke — the opposite of energy saving."]},"p45":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> L-type voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels open with depolarization and let extracellular Ca²⁺ in, a major activator source in smooth muscle.</p><p>In smooth muscle, depolarization (from slow waves, stretch, or receptor-operated channels) opens L-type channels; Ca²⁺ entry directly raises cytosolic Ca²⁺ and also triggers Ca²⁺-induced Ca²⁺ release from the SR. This contrasts with skeletal muscle, where the L-type channel (DHPR) acts mainly as a voltage sensor mechanically coupled to RyR1 and extracellular Ca²⁺ entry isn't needed. That's why dihydropyridine Ca²⁺ channel blockers (amlodipine, nifedipine) relax vascular smooth muscle and lower blood pressure but don't weaken skeletal muscle.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Voltage-gated calcium channels are activated by the binding of neurotransmitters and allow calcium influx, which directly triggers muscle contraction.</b> — Channels opened by neurotransmitter binding are ligand-gated (receptor-operated), not voltage-gated.</li><li><b>Voltage-gated calcium channels are responsible for maintaining the resting membrane potential by regulating potassium efflux.</b> — Resting membrane potential is set mainly by K⁺ channels and the Na⁺/K⁺ pump.</li><li><b>Voltage-gated calcium channels are found exclusively in skeletal muscle, where they play a role in initiating action potentials.</b> — Voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels are present in smooth and cardiac muscle and in neurons, not only skeletal muscle.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Dihydropyridines (amlodipine) → vascular smooth muscle; non-dihydropyridines (verapamil, diltiazem) → heart.</p>","w":["Channels opened by neurotransmitter binding are ligand-gated (receptor-operated), not voltage-gated.","Correct — depolarization opens them and Ca²⁺ entry drives contraction.","Resting membrane potential is set mainly by K⁺ channels and the Na⁺/K⁺ pump.","Voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels are present in smooth and cardiac muscle and in neurons, not only skeletal muscle."]},"p46":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Blocking Ca²⁺ channels reduces Ca²⁺ entry → less neurotransmitter release from nerve terminals and less smooth muscle contraction.</p><p>Ca²⁺ entry is essential in two places here. At nerve terminals, Ca²⁺ influx triggers synaptic vesicle fusion (via synaptotagmin), so reducing it decreases transmitter release. In smooth muscle, Ca²⁺ influx supplies activator Ca²⁺ for calmodulin–MLCK, so reducing it inhibits contraction. Skeletal muscle is relatively unaffected because its contraction uses Ca²⁺ stored in the SR, released through mechanical DHPR–RyR1 coupling without needing extracellular Ca²⁺. Cardiac muscle does depend on Ca²⁺ entry, which is why Ca²⁺ channel blockers treat arrhythmias.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Decreased calcium influx leading to reduced muscle contraction in smooth and skeletal muscle.</b> — Skeletal muscle contraction isn't reduced, because it uses SR Ca²⁺ released by DHPR–RyR1 coupling.</li><li><b>Increased calcium influx, resulting in enhanced neurotransmitter release and muscle relaxation.</b> — Channel blockers decrease Ca²⁺ influx, and reduced (not enhanced) release would follow.</li><li><b>Increased calcium influx and excessive muscle contraction in both smooth and skeletal muscle.</b> — Ca²⁺ influx decreases; contraction would not be excessive.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Lambert–Eaton syndrome (antibodies to presynaptic voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels) → reduced ACh release → weakness that improves with repeated use.</p>","w":["Skeletal muscle contraction isn't reduced, because it uses SR Ca²⁺ released by DHPR–RyR1 coupling.","Channel blockers decrease Ca²⁺ influx, and reduced (not enhanced) release would follow.","Correct — less Ca²⁺ influx reduces transmitter release and smooth muscle contraction.","Ca²⁺ influx decreases; contraction would not be excessive."]},"p47":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Active tension requires overlap of thick and thin filaments; at ~3.65 µm there is no overlap, so no cross-bridges can form.</p><p>Thick filaments are ~1.6 µm long and each thin filament ~1.0 µm. Once the sarcomere is stretched to roughly 1.6 + 2 × 1.0 ≈ 3.6 µm, thin filaments are pulled completely past the ends of the thick filaments. Even with a normal action potential, normal Ca²⁺ release and normal troponin binding, myosin heads have nothing to grab, so active tension is zero. Passive tension (titin) is high at this length. At short lengths, tension also falls, but for different reasons: thin filaments overlap each other and thick filaments abut the Z discs.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The Z-disks compress the thick filaments and prevent cross-bridge detachment.</b> — Z disc compression of thick filaments occurs at very SHORT lengths, not long ones.</li><li><b>The titin molecules become fully relaxed and prevent calcium from binding to troponin.</b> — Titin is stretched (not relaxed) at long lengths and doesn't control Ca²⁺ binding to troponin.</li><li><b>The sarcoplasmic reticulum becomes unable to release calcium at extended sarcomere lengths.</b> — The stem states excitation is normal; SR Ca²⁺ release doesn't fail with stretch.</li><li><b>The actin filaments overlap excessively, obstructing the movement of myosin heads.</b> — Excessive actin overlap reduces tension at short lengths, not at 3.65 µm.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Optimal sarcomere length ~2.0–2.2 µm; zero active tension ~3.6 µm.</p>","w":["Z disc compression of thick filaments occurs at very SHORT lengths, not long ones.","Correct — with no filament overlap, myosin heads cannot reach actin.","Titin is stretched (not relaxed) at long lengths and doesn't control Ca²⁺ binding to troponin.","The stem states excitation is normal; SR Ca²⁺ release doesn't fail with stretch.","Excessive actin overlap reduces tension at short lengths, not at 3.65 µm."]},"p48":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Total tension = active (cross-bridge) + passive (elastic) tension; beyond optimal length, passive rises faster than active falls.</p><p>As a muscle is stretched past optimal length, filament overlap drops and active tension declines. At the same time, titin and connective tissue (endomysium, perimysium, epimysium) are stretched and resist like springs, with steeply increasing passive tension. The sum keeps rising, so total measured force increases even though the contractile machinery is producing less. Passive tension requires no ATP and no Ca²⁺.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Increased calcium release raises active tension despite reduced filament overlap.</b> — Ca²⁺ release isn't increased, and active tension is falling.</li><li><b>Increased ATP hydrolysis directly increases the passive resistance of the muscle.</b> — Passive tension is purely elastic and doesn't depend on ATP hydrolysis.</li><li><b>Excessive actin overlap increases passive tension while preserving maximal cross-bridge formation.</b> — Stretching decreases filament overlap; excessive overlap occurs only at short lengths.</li><li><b>Reduced myosin ATPase activity causes connective tissue to generate additional active tension.</b> — Connective tissue generates passive tension only — never active tension.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Titin = the main intracellular source of passive tension; also centers the thick filament.</p>","w":["Ca²⁺ release isn't increased, and active tension is falling.","Passive tension is purely elastic and doesn't depend on ATP hydrolysis.","Correct — elastic elements resist stretch more as active tension falls.","Stretching decreases filament overlap; excessive overlap occurs only at short lengths.","Connective tissue generates passive tension only — never active tension."]},"p49":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Isometric contraction: force without movement → external work is zero, but ATP is still consumed continuously by cross-bridge cycling and Ca²⁺ pumping.</p><p>Mechanical work = force × distance. Holding a dumbbell still involves no displacement, so no external work is done. However, each cross-bridge must detach (needing ATP binding) and reattach to keep up force, and the SR keeps releasing Ca²⁺ while SERCA pumps it back (also ATP-dependent). All the chemical energy is converted to heat, which is why holding a weight still feels tiring and makes you warm.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Cross-bridge cycling ceases completely once muscle length becomes constant.</b> — Cross-bridges keep cycling during an isometric contraction; that's how force is maintained.</li><li><b>ATP consumption stops because calcium is no longer released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum.</b> — The EMG shows ongoing activation, so Ca²⁺ release continues.</li><li><b>The muscle performs maximal external work because tension is maintained over time.</b> — Without displacement, external work is zero, regardless of how long tension is held.</li><li><b>The muscle shortens internally at a constant velocity despite the fixed elbow angle.</b> — The muscle as a whole does not keep shortening; only small stretch of elastic elements occurs at the onset.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Isometric = constant length; isotonic = constant load (concentric or eccentric).</p>","w":["Cross-bridges keep cycling during an isometric contraction; that's how force is maintained.","Correct — force is generated but external work is zero because there's no displacement.","The EMG shows ongoing activation, so Ca²⁺ release continues.","Without displacement, external work is zero, regardless of how long tension is held.","The muscle as a whole does not keep shortening; only small stretch of elastic elements occurs at the onset."]},"p50":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Force–velocity: lighter load → faster shortening, approaching Vmax at zero load (Vmax depends on myosin ATPase rate).</p><p>The Hill curve is hyperbolic: maximum isometric force (P₀) occurs at zero velocity; maximal shortening velocity occurs at zero load. Reducing the load lets each cross-bridge cycle with less resistance, so filaments slide faster. Vmax itself is set by intrinsic myosin ATPase activity (fast fibers &gt; slow fibers), and P₀ depends on cross-sectional area and the number of cross-bridges — neither changes when you simply change the load.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Shortening velocity would remain zero because the muscle must first increase its sarcomere length.</b> — The muscle can shorten immediately from its current length; no extra stretch is required.</li><li><b>Shortening velocity would decrease because fewer cross-bridges are required to support the load.</b> — Less load means faster, not slower, shortening.</li><li><b>Maximum isometric force would immediately decrease in direct proportion to the reduction in load.</b> — Maximum isometric force is intrinsic to the muscle and doesn't change with the external load.</li><li><b>Shortening velocity would increase only if stimulation frequency were reduced to a single twitch.</b> — Velocity is set by the load and myosin ATPase; reducing stimulation to a twitch would lower force, not raise velocity.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Vmax ∝ myosin ATPase activity; P₀ ∝ cross-sectional area.</p>","w":["Correct — reducing the load increases velocity toward the unloaded maximum.","The muscle can shorten immediately from its current length; no extra stretch is required.","Less load means faster, not slower, shortening.","Maximum isometric force is intrinsic to the muscle and doesn't change with the external load.","Velocity is set by the load and myosin ATPase; reducing stimulation to a twitch would lower force, not raise velocity."]},"p51":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Relaxation requires SERCA, an ATP-dependent pump, to move Ca²⁺ from the sarcoplasm back into the SR.</p><p>When stimulation stops, Ca²⁺ release stops, but Ca²⁺ already in the sarcoplasm must be actively removed. SERCA (sarco/endoplasmic reticulum Ca²⁺-ATPase) pumps it into the SR, where calsequestrin stores it. As Ca²⁺ falls, it dissociates from troponin C, tropomyosin re-covers actin's myosin-binding sites, and the muscle relaxes. If SERCA is impaired, Ca²⁺ stays high and relaxation is slow — Brody disease (SERCA1 deficiency) causes exercise-induced stiffness and cramping. Phospholamban regulates SERCA2 in the heart.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>ATP binding to myosin heads, preventing their detachment from actin.</b> — ATP binding to myosin is needed for detachment, and ATP availability is normal; the stem says Ca²⁺ stays elevated.</li><li><b>Calcium binding to troponin C, preventing tropomyosin from moving away from actin.</b> — Ca²⁺ binding to troponin C starts contraction; a defect there would weaken contraction, not delay relaxation.</li><li><b>Acetylcholine binding to postsynaptic nicotinic receptors at the motor end plate.</b> — ACh binding is upstream of excitation; stimulation has already stopped.</li><li><b>Sodium influx through voltage-gated channels during the muscle action potential.</b> — Na⁺ influx generates the action potential; it isn't involved in relaxation.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Two ATP-dependent steps in a contraction cycle: cross-bridge detachment (myosin) and Ca²⁺ reuptake (SERCA).</p>","w":["ATP binding to myosin is needed for detachment, and ATP availability is normal; the stem says Ca²⁺ stays elevated.","Ca²⁺ binding to troponin C starts contraction; a defect there would weaken contraction, not delay relaxation.","Correct — impaired SERCA Ca²⁺ reuptake delays relaxation.","ACh binding is upstream of excitation; stimulation has already stopped.","Na⁺ influx generates the action potential; it isn't involved in relaxation."]},"p52":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Rigor mortis: without ATP, myosin heads cannot detach from actin, so cross-bridges lock in place.</p><p>In the cross-bridge cycle, ATP binding to the myosin head is what releases it from actin. After death, the SR leaks Ca²⁺ (SERCA stops), exposing binding sites, so myosin binds actin; as ATP is depleted, the heads remain stuck in the rigor (strongly bound) state. Rigidity begins a few hours after death, peaks around 12 hours and resolves after 24–48 hours as proteins break down (autolysis).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Persistent acetylcholine release causes continuous stimulation of nicotinic receptors.</b> — Nerve activity and ACh release stop after death; there's no ongoing stimulation.</li><li><b>Excess ATP hydrolysis maintains the myosin heads in a permanently detached state.</b> — There is no ATP left; ATP binding detaches heads — it doesn't keep them permanently detached when absent.</li><li><b>Calcium depletion prevents tropomyosin from covering actin-binding sites.</b> — Ca²⁺ isn't depleted; it leaks from the SR, allowing binding sites to be exposed.</li><li><b>Increased phosphocreatine synthesis produces sustained high-energy phosphate availability.</b> — Phosphocreatine is depleted too, so no high-energy phosphate remains.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> ATP roles: detach myosin from actin, re-cock the head (hydrolysis), power SERCA.</p>","w":["Nerve activity and ACh release stop after death; there's no ongoing stimulation.","There is no ATP left; ATP binding detaches heads — it doesn't keep them permanently detached when absent.","Ca²⁺ isn't depleted; it leaks from the SR, allowing binding sites to be exposed.","Correct — ATP is required for myosin heads to detach from actin.","Phosphocreatine is depleted too, so no high-energy phosphate remains."]},"p53":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Maximal effort energy sequence: stored ATP (~2 s) → phosphocreatine (~10 s) → anaerobic glycolysis (~10 s to 2 min) → oxidative metabolism.</p><p>Phosphocreatine regenerates ATP fastest (creatine kinase, a single reaction), but stores are small and largely exhausted within ~10 s. Anaerobic glycolysis then becomes the main ATP source in a 40-second sprint; it is fast but yields only 2 ATP per glucose and generates lactate and H⁺, contributing to fatigue near the end of the bout. Oxidative phosphorylation is highly efficient but slow to ramp up, so it dominates in longer, submaximal exercise.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Oxidative phosphorylation initially provides all ATP, but mitochondrial ATP production stops after several seconds.</b> — Oxidative phosphorylation is too slow to supply all ATP at the start of maximal effort, and it doesn't stop after a few seconds.</li><li><b>Anaerobic glycolysis requires oxygen and therefore becomes dominant when muscle oxygen availability increases.</b> — Anaerobic glycolysis does not require oxygen.</li><li><b>Stored ATP can sustain maximal contraction for several minutes, making phosphocreatine unnecessary.</b> — Stored ATP lasts only a couple of seconds of maximal contraction.</li><li><b>Fat oxidation produces ATP faster than phosphocreatine cleavage during brief maximal exercise.</b> — Fat oxidation is the slowest ATP source, not faster than phosphocreatine.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> 400-m sprint ≈ glycolytic; 100-m ≈ phosphagen; marathon ≈ oxidative.</p>","w":["Oxidative phosphorylation is too slow to supply all ATP at the start of maximal effort, and it doesn't stop after a few seconds.","Correct — limited phosphocreatine stores shift ATP supply to glycolysis.","Anaerobic glycolysis does not require oxygen.","Stored ATP lasts only a couple of seconds of maximal contraction.","Fat oxidation is the slowest ATP source, not faster than phosphocreatine."]},"p54":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> EPOC = extra O₂ used after exercise to restore ATP–phosphocreatine, refill O₂ stores, clear lactate, and support elevated metabolism.</p><p>Recovery oxygen consumption has a fast component (first few minutes) that resynthesizes phosphocreatine and ATP and reloads myoglobin and hemoglobin with O₂, and a slower component that oxidizes lactate or converts it to glucose (Cori cycle), and supports elevated body temperature, catecholamines, heart rate and breathing. The more intense the exercise, the larger the EPOC.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Continued maximal recruitment of all skeletal muscle motor units.</b> — Exercise has stopped; motor units aren't being recruited.</li><li><b>Persistent shortening of all active muscle fibers at maximal unloaded velocity.</b> — The muscles are no longer contracting.</li><li><b>Complete cessation of oxidative metabolism until lactate is removed.</b> — Oxidative metabolism increases during recovery; it is what clears lactate.</li><li><b>Conversion of all muscle lactate directly into ATP within skeletal muscle without additional metabolism.</b> — Lactate must be oxidized via pyruvate or converted to glucose in the liver, both requiring O₂ and additional metabolism.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> EPOC is larger after high-intensity than after low-intensity exercise.</p>","w":["Exercise has stopped; motor units aren't being recruited.","The muscles are no longer contracting.","Oxidative metabolism increases during recovery; it is what clears lactate.","Correct — restoring energy stores and O₂ reserves (plus other recovery processes) drives EPOC.","Lactate must be oxidized via pyruvate or converted to glucose in the liver, both requiring O₂ and additional metabolism."]},"p55":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Henneman's size principle: small motor neurons (low threshold) are recruited first, then progressively larger ones as force demand rises.</p><p>A small motor neuron has a small surface area and high input resistance, so a given synaptic current produces a larger depolarization (V = IR) and reaches threshold first. Small neurons supply slow, fatigue-resistant (type I) motor units with few fibers, enabling fine, graded force — ideal for delicate piano playing. As the passage gets louder, larger motor neurons controlling fast, powerful (type II) units are added. Force is also increased by raising firing frequency (rate coding).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Large motor neurons are recruited first because they have the lowest activation thresholds.</b> — Large motor neurons have the highest thresholds and are recruited last.</li><li><b>All motor units are activated simultaneously to ensure uniform muscle force.</b> — Recruitment is orderly and progressive, not all at once.</li><li><b>Fast glycolytic motor units are always recruited before slow oxidative motor units, regardless of task.</b> — Slow oxidative units are recruited before fast glycolytic units.</li><li><b>Increasing muscle force depends exclusively on increasing action-potential amplitude in individual muscle fibers.</b> — Action potentials are all-or-none; force increases through recruitment and firing rate, not amplitude.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Fine control muscles (eye, fingers) have small motor units (few fibers per neuron).</p>","w":["Large motor neurons have the highest thresholds and are recruited last.","Recruitment is orderly and progressive, not all at once.","Correct — small, excitable motor neurons are recruited first.","Slow oxidative units are recruited before fast glycolytic units.","Action potentials are all-or-none; force increases through recruitment and firing rate, not amplitude."]},"p56":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Fused tetanus occurs when stimuli are so frequent that cytosolic Ca²⁺ never falls back to baseline.</p><p>The skeletal muscle action potential lasts a few ms, while the twitch lasts 10–100 ms. Each new action potential releases more Ca²⁺ before SERCA has cleared the last pulse, so Ca²⁺ remains high, troponin stays saturated, and cross-bridges cycle continuously. Series elastic elements remain stretched, so force summates to a smooth plateau about 3–4 times greater than a single twitch.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Repeated stimulation depletes all intracellular calcium, causing a sustained contraction without cross-bridge cycling.</b> — Ca²⁺ is sustained, not depleted, and cross-bridges keep cycling.</li><li><b>Increased stimulation frequency converts slow oxidative fibers into fast glycolytic fibers within seconds.</b> — Fiber types don't convert within seconds.</li><li><b>Each action potential produces a progressively larger action-potential amplitude in the muscle membrane.</b> — Action potentials are all-or-none; their amplitude doesn't grow.</li><li><b>Increased stimulation frequency removes the need for ATP during cross-bridge cycling.</b> — Cross-bridge cycling always requires ATP.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Tetanus possible in skeletal muscle (short refractory period), impossible in cardiac muscle (long refractory period).</p>","w":["Correct — Ca²⁺ stays elevated, sustaining cross-bridge activation.","Ca²⁺ is sustained, not depleted, and cross-bridges keep cycling.","Fiber types don't convert within seconds.","Action potentials are all-or-none; their amplitude doesn't grow.","Cross-bridge cycling always requires ATP."]},"p57":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Muscle mass reflects the balance of protein synthesis vs. degradation: training → hypertrophy (more myofibrils per fiber); disuse → atrophy.</p><p>Resistance training activates mTOR signaling and satellite cells, increasing synthesis of actin, myosin and other proteins, so each fiber gains myofibrils and enlarges; fiber number changes little in humans. Immobilization, denervation, fasting or cachexia shifts the balance toward degradation via the ubiquitin–proteasome system (atrogenes like MuRF1 and atrogin-1) and autophagy, so fibers shrink. Atrophy appears within days of immobilization.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Training increases muscle mass mainly by converting all slow fibers into fast fibers, whereas immobilization increases fiber number.</b> — Hypertrophy is mainly enlargement of existing fibers, not fiber-type conversion; immobilization doesn't increase fiber number.</li><li><b>Training suppresses contractile-protein synthesis, whereas immobilization increases actin and myosin production.</b> — This reverses the actual changes.</li><li><b>Training causes permanent calcium accumulation within muscle fibers, whereas immobilization prevents calcium release.</b> — Neither training nor disuse causes permanent Ca²⁺ accumulation or loss of Ca²⁺ release.</li><li><b>Both changes are caused exclusively by alterations in motor-neuron action-potential amplitude.</b> — Motor neuron action potential amplitude is all-or-none and doesn't change.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hypertrophy = bigger cells; hyperplasia = more cells. Skeletal muscle adapts mainly by hypertrophy.</p>","w":["Hypertrophy is mainly enlargement of existing fibers, not fiber-type conversion; immobilization doesn't increase fiber number.","This reverses the actual changes.","Neither training nor disuse causes permanent Ca²⁺ accumulation or loss of Ca²⁺ release.","Motor neuron action potential amplitude is all-or-none and doesn't change.","Correct — training increases contractile protein accumulation; disuse increases degradation."]},"p58":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Myasthenia gravis: antibodies against postsynaptic nicotinic AChRs → smaller end-plate potentials → fatigable weakness.</p><p>Normally each impulse produces an end-plate potential well above threshold (the safety factor). Antibodies block receptors, cross-link them for internalization, and trigger complement damage to the end plate, so fewer receptors are available. With repeated firing, ACh released per impulse normally declines slightly; in myasthenia the EPP then falls below threshold in more fibers, so weakness worsens with use and improves with rest. Ocular muscles (ptosis, diplopia) are often affected first. Diagnosis: anti-AChR antibodies, decremental response on repetitive nerve stimulation; treatment includes acetylcholinesterase inhibitors (pyridostigmine) and thymectomy (associated thymoma).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Excessive calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum prevents myosin detachment.</b> — SR Ca²⁺ handling is normal in myasthenia.</li><li><b>Failure of acetylcholine synthesis prevents all acetylcholine release from the motor neuron.</b> — ACh synthesis and release are normal; the problem is postsynaptic.</li><li><b>Increased dystrophin synthesis disrupts the connection between actin and the extracellular matrix.</b> — Dystrophin is unaffected (it's absent, not increased, in Duchenne dystrophy).</li><li><b>ATP depletion prevents every myosin head from binding to actin during the first contraction.</b> — ATP is not depleted.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Myasthenia (postsynaptic) worsens with use; Lambert–Eaton (presynaptic) improves with use.</p>","w":["SR Ca²⁺ handling is normal in myasthenia.","Correct — fewer receptors reduce the end-plate potential below threshold.","ACh synthesis and release are normal; the problem is postsynaptic.","Dystrophin is unaffected (it's absent, not increased, in Duchenne dystrophy).","ATP is not depleted."]},"p59":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Malignant hyperthermia: mutant RyR1 releases Ca²⁺ uncontrollably when exposed to volatile anesthetics or succinylcholine.</p><p>Uncontrolled SR Ca²⁺ release causes sustained contraction (rigidity, including masseter spasm). Myosin ATPase and SERCA burn huge amounts of ATP, generating heat (hyperthermia), CO₂ (rising end-tidal CO₂ is often the earliest sign) and lactic acid (metabolic acidosis). As ATP runs out, fibers break down (rhabdomyolysis), releasing K⁺ (hyperkalemia, arrhythmias) and myoglobin. It's autosomal dominant, hence the family history. Treatment: stop the trigger, give dantrolene (blocks RyR1 Ca²⁺ release), cool the patient and correct electrolytes.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Autoantibodies block postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors, preventing end-plate depolarization.</b> — Antibodies against AChRs describe myasthenia gravis, which causes weakness, not rigidity or hyperthermia.</li><li><b>Dystrophin deficiency causes chronic membrane injury without altering sarcoplasmic-reticulum calcium release.</b> — Dystrophin deficiency describes Duchenne muscular dystrophy, a chronic disease.</li><li><b>ATP depletion immediately prevents all actin–myosin interactions, causing flaccid paralysis and reduced heat production.</b> — Malignant hyperthermia causes rigidity, not flaccid paralysis, and greatly increases heat production.</li><li><b>Reduced calcium entry into the sarcoplasm prevents cross-bridge cycling and produces compensatory hyperthermia.</b> — Ca²⁺ in the sarcoplasm is markedly increased, not reduced.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Earliest sign = rising end-tidal CO₂; treatment = dantrolene.</p>","w":["Antibodies against AChRs describe myasthenia gravis, which causes weakness, not rigidity or hyperthermia.","Dystrophin deficiency describes Duchenne muscular dystrophy, a chronic disease.","Correct — unregulated SR Ca²⁺ release drives sustained contraction and hypermetabolism.","Malignant hyperthermia causes rigidity, not flaccid paralysis, and greatly increases heat production.","Ca²⁺ in the sarcoplasm is markedly increased, not reduced."]},"p60":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Duchenne muscular dystrophy: absent dystrophin destabilizes the sarcolemma → contraction-induced tears, Ca²⁺ influx, fiber necrosis.</p><p>Dystrophin links actin inside the fiber to the dystrophin–glycoprotein complex in the membrane and to laminin in the extracellular matrix, spreading the force of contraction. Without it, the sarcolemma tears with repeated contraction; Ca²⁺ floods in and activates proteases (calpains), causing necrosis. Repeated degeneration exhausts regeneration, and fibers are replaced by fat and fibrous tissue (calf pseudohypertrophy). Proximal weakness causes the Gowers sign. CK is markedly elevated. Caused by frameshift deletions in the DMD gene (X-linked); Becker dystrophy is a milder in-frame form with partially functional dystrophin. Death often results from cardiomyopathy or respiratory failure.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Increased dystrophin production prevents myosin heads from detaching from actin during relaxation.</b> — Dystrophin is absent, not increased, and it doesn't affect myosin detachment.</li><li><b>Autoimmune destruction of postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors reduces the end-plate potential.</b> — Antibodies to AChRs describe myasthenia gravis.</li><li><b>A constitutively open RyR1 channel causes acute anesthetic-triggered hypermetabolism.</b> — A RyR1 mutation with anesthetic-triggered hypermetabolism describes malignant hyperthermia.</li><li><b>Increased titin elasticity prevents passive tension from developing during muscle stretch.</b> — Titin isn't involved in Duchenne muscular dystrophy.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Duchenne = frameshift, no dystrophin; Becker = in-frame, some dystrophin, milder.</p>","w":["Correct — loss of dystrophin destabilizes the membrane, allowing injury and Ca²⁺ entry.","Dystrophin is absent, not increased, and it doesn't affect myosin detachment.","Antibodies to AChRs describe myasthenia gravis.","A RyR1 mutation with anesthetic-triggered hypermetabolism describes malignant hyperthermia.","Titin isn't involved in Duchenne muscular dystrophy."]},"p61":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Muscle fibers remodel the number of sarcomeres in series so each sarcomere stays near optimal length at the muscle's habitual resting length.</p><p>A muscle held shortened (cast in a shortened position, contracture) loses sarcomeres in series; a muscle held lengthened, or growing with bone during childhood, adds them at the fiber ends. This keeps filament overlap optimal for the new working length. The flip side: the shortened muscle now has less excursion, so contractures resist stretching and require prolonged, sustained stretch (or serial casting) to reverse.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Addition of sarcomeres in series to restore the muscle's original resting length despite continued shortening.</b> — Sarcomeres are added when a muscle is chronically lengthened, not shortened.</li><li><b>Complete conversion of all contractile proteins into extracellular collagen.</b> — Contractile proteins aren't converted into collagen, although fibrosis may increase.</li><li><b>Permanent elevation of cytosolic calcium, causing continuous cross-bridge cycling.</b> — Cytosolic Ca²⁺ doesn't stay elevated; the change is structural.</li><li><b>Immediate conversion of slow oxidative fibers into fast glycolytic fibers without structural remodeling.</b> — Fiber-type conversion is slow, requires remodeling, and isn't the length-adaptation mechanism.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Shortened → lose sarcomeres in series; lengthened → add sarcomeres in series.</p>","w":["Sarcomeres are added when a muscle is chronically lengthened, not shortened.","Contractile proteins aren't converted into collagen, although fibrosis may increase.","Cytosolic Ca²⁺ doesn't stay elevated; the change is structural.","Correct — sarcomeres in series are lost to fit the shortened length.","Fiber-type conversion is slow, requires remodeling, and isn't the length-adaptation mechanism."]},"p62":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> In smooth muscle, Ca²⁺ only triggers contraction by way of MLCK; blocking MLCK relaxes the muscle even if Ca²⁺ remains high.</p><p>The chain is Ca²⁺ → calmodulin → MLCK → phosphorylation of the myosin regulatory light chain → myosin ATPase on → cross-bridge cycling. Each link is necessary. With MLCK blocked, phosphorylation falls, myosin ATPase activity drops, cross-bridges stop cycling, arterioles relax, and vascular resistance (and blood pressure) falls. This is why the drug works \"downstream\" of Ca²⁺.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Calcium binds troponin C, moving tropomyosin away from actin-binding sites.</b> — Smooth muscle has no troponin; regulation is on the myosin side.</li><li><b>Calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum increases, directly inhibiting actin–myosin interactions.</b> — SR Ca²⁺ release promotes, not inhibits, actin–myosin interaction.</li><li><b>Myosin light-chain phosphatase is inhibited, preventing the formation of force-generating cross-bridges.</b> — Inhibiting MLCP would increase phosphorylation and contraction — and the drug targets MLCK, not MLCP.</li><li><b>Calcium binds calmodulin, directly activating myosin ATPase independently of phosphorylation.</b> — Ca²⁺–calmodulin acts through MLCK; it doesn't activate myosin ATPase directly.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Contraction ∝ MLCK/MLCP balance, not just Ca²⁺.</p>","w":["Smooth muscle has no troponin; regulation is on the myosin side.","Correct — less light-chain phosphorylation reduces myosin ATPase activity and cycling.","SR Ca²⁺ release promotes, not inhibits, actin–myosin interaction.","Inhibiting MLCP would increase phosphorylation and contraction — and the drug targets MLCK, not MLCP.","Ca²⁺–calmodulin acts through MLCK; it doesn't activate myosin ATPase directly."]},"p63":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Gap junctions decide whether excitation spreads: abundant → single-unit (syncytium); scarce → multi-unit (each fiber separately controlled).</p><p>Preparation X shows cell-to-cell spread of excitation, typical of single-unit (visceral) smooth muscle in the gut, uterus, bladder and small vessels. Preparation Y stays local, typical of multi-unit smooth muscle in the iris, ciliary muscle, vas deferens, piloerector muscles and large airways/arteries. Both share the same contractile machinery (calmodulin–MLCK, dense bodies); only electrical coupling differs.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Preparation X contains troponin, whereas preparation Y contains calmodulin.</b> — Neither type of smooth muscle contains troponin; both use calmodulin.</li><li><b>Preparation X relies exclusively on autonomic nerve stimulation, whereas preparation Y contracts spontaneously.</b> — This is reversed: single-unit muscle often contracts spontaneously, while multi-unit depends on nerve input.</li><li><b>Preparation X has a well-developed sarcoplasmic reticulum, whereas preparation Y depends entirely on extracellular calcium.</b> — SR development doesn't determine spread between cells.</li><li><b>Preparation X uses rapid cross-bridge cycling, whereas preparation Y uses the latch mechanism exclusively.</b> — Cross-bridge kinetics (fast cycling vs. latch) don't determine electrical spread.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Single-unit = functional syncytium via gap junctions.</p>","w":["Neither type of smooth muscle contains troponin; both use calmodulin.","This is reversed: single-unit muscle often contracts spontaneously, while multi-unit depends on nerve input.","Correct — abundant gap junctions explain spread in X; limited coupling explains Y.","SR development doesn't determine spread between cells.","Cross-bridge kinetics (fast cycling vs. latch) don't determine electrical spread."]},"p64":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Smooth muscle relies heavily on extracellular Ca²⁺ entering through L-type channels on depolarization (electromechanical coupling).</p><p>Compared with skeletal muscle, smooth muscle has a modest SR, so Ca²⁺ influx is a major source of activator Ca²⁺. Depolarization opens L-type voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels; Ca²⁺ enters, binds calmodulin and activates MLCK. Removing extracellular Ca²⁺ reduces this influx, so even a strong depolarization produces weak contraction. Skeletal muscle, in contrast, keeps contracting in Ca²⁺-free solution for a while because it uses SR stores via DHPR–RyR1 coupling.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Reduced extracellular Ca²⁺ increases myosin light-chain phosphorylation by activating MLCP.</b> — MLCP dephosphorylates myosin, and low Ca²⁺ doesn't increase myosin phosphorylation.</li><li><b>Reduced extracellular Ca²⁺ prevents calcium from binding to troponin, leaving tropomyosin over the myosin-binding sites.</b> — Smooth muscle has no troponin.</li><li><b>Reduced extracellular Ca²⁺ directly increases myosin ATPase activity, accelerating cross-bridge detachment.</b> — Myosin ATPase activity falls, not rises, when Ca²⁺ is low.</li><li><b>Reduced extracellular Ca²⁺ increases IP₃ production, depleting actin filaments of regulatory proteins.</b> — IP₃ isn't increased, and it doesn't deplete actin regulatory proteins.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Calcium channel blockers exploit this dependence to relax arterioles.</p>","w":["Correct — less Ca²⁺ influx limits Ca²⁺–calmodulin activation of MLCK.","MLCP dephosphorylates myosin, and low Ca²⁺ doesn't increase myosin phosphorylation.","Smooth muscle has no troponin.","Myosin ATPase activity falls, not rises, when Ca²⁺ is low.","IP₃ isn't increased, and it doesn't deplete actin regulatory proteins."]},"p65":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Pharmacomechanical coupling: Gq → PLC → IP₃ → SR Ca²⁺ release → calmodulin–MLCK → contraction, without major depolarization.</p><p>Agonists like acetylcholine (M₃ in gut), norepinephrine (α₁ in vessels) and angiotensin II act through Gq. Phospholipase C cleaves PIP₂ into IP₃ (releases SR Ca²⁺ via IP₃ receptors) and DAG (activates PKC, which can sensitize the contractile apparatus to Ca²⁺ via MLCP inhibition). Smooth muscle can therefore contract with little change in membrane potential, unlike skeletal muscle, which always requires an action potential.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Gq activation → increased cAMP → activation of MLCP → myosin phosphorylation.</b> — cAMP comes from Gs signaling and promotes relaxation; MLCP dephosphorylates (not phosphorylates) myosin.</li><li><b>Gq activation → inhibition of phospholipase C → reduced IP₃ → calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum.</b> — Gq activates phospholipase C; it doesn't inhibit it.</li><li><b>Gq activation → direct activation of myosin ATPase → cross-bridge cycling without calcium signaling.</b> — Contraction still requires Ca²⁺ signaling; Gq doesn't directly activate myosin ATPase.</li><li><b>Gq activation → troponin C activation → movement of tropomyosin → smooth muscle contraction.</b> — Smooth muscle has no troponin.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Gq: α₁, M₁/M₃, H₁, V₁, AT₁ → PLC → IP₃/DAG.</p>","w":["cAMP comes from Gs signaling and promotes relaxation; MLCP dephosphorylates (not phosphorylates) myosin.","Gq activates phospholipase C; it doesn't inhibit it.","Contraction still requires Ca²⁺ signaling; Gq doesn't directly activate myosin ATPase.","Correct — this is the Gq–PLC–IP₃–Ca²⁺–MLCK pathway.","Smooth muscle has no troponin."]},"p66":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> In uterine (and ureteral) smooth muscle, action potentials with a long plateau keep L-type Ca²⁺ channels open, sustaining contraction.</p><p>During the plateau, Ca²⁺ continues to enter while repolarization is delayed. Cytosolic Ca²⁺ stays high, so Ca²⁺–calmodulin keeps MLCK active and myosin stays phosphorylated. The contraction therefore lasts as long as the plateau (here ~800 ms). Near term, estrogen and oxytocin increase excitability, gap junctions and oxytocin receptors to support strong, coordinated labor contractions.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Persistent fast sodium influx maintains depolarization and directly activates skeletal-type troponin.</b> — The plateau is carried mainly by Ca²⁺, not fast Na⁺, and smooth muscle has no troponin.</li><li><b>Rapid potassium efflux shortens the action potential but maintains maximal calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum.</b> — Rapid K⁺ efflux would end the plateau and shorten the action potential.</li><li><b>Inhibition of all calcium channels prevents relaxation by permanently locking myosin to actin.</b> — Blocking Ca²⁺ channels would cause relaxation, not prevent it.</li><li><b>Prolonged membrane hyperpolarization increases voltage-gated calcium channel opening and accelerates relaxation.</b> — Hyperpolarization closes voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels and wouldn't sustain contraction.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Tocolytics (e.g., nifedipine) block L-type channels to stop preterm contractions.</p>","w":["The plateau is carried mainly by Ca²⁺, not fast Na⁺, and smooth muscle has no troponin.","Correct — delayed repolarization prolongs Ca²⁺ entry and myosin phosphorylation.","Rapid K⁺ efflux would end the plateau and shorten the action potential.","Blocking Ca²⁺ channels would cause relaxation, not prevent it.","Hyperpolarization closes voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels and wouldn't sustain contraction."]},"p67":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Latch state: after stimulation falls, dephosphorylated myosin heads remain attached and cycle very slowly, maintaining tension at low ATP cost.</p><p>Force in smooth muscle can persist even when MLCK activity and Ca²⁺ fall, because MLCP dephosphorylates heads that are still bound to actin, and these \"latch bridges\" detach slowly. Cycling rate (and ATP use) drops far more than force does. This economical tone maintenance is essential for blood vessels, sphincters and other organs that must stay contracted for long periods.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Persistent maximal MLCK activity maintains rapid cross-bridge cycling despite reduced stimulation.</b> — MLCK activity and cycling decline as stimulation falls; they aren't maintained at maximal levels.</li><li><b>Increased calcium influx continuously accelerates myosin ATPase activity, preserving force at high energy cost.</b> — ATP consumption decreases, not increases.</li><li><b>Troponin remains bound to calcium, maintaining actin activation after cytosolic calcium falls.</b> — Smooth muscle has no troponin.</li><li><b>Complete inhibition of myosin phosphorylation prevents cross-bridge attachment while maintaining active force.</b> — Phosphorylation isn't completely blocked; some attached cross-bridges carry the force.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Latch = high force, low cycling, low ATP.</p>","w":["MLCK activity and cycling decline as stimulation falls; they aren't maintained at maximal levels.","ATP consumption decreases, not increases.","Correct — slowly cycling attached cross-bridges hold tension with little ATP.","Smooth muscle has no troponin.","Phosphorylation isn't completely blocked; some attached cross-bridges carry the force."]},"p68":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Stress-relaxation (plasticity): after a sudden stretch, smooth muscle tension rises and then gradually falls as it adapts to the new length.</p><p>When the bladder is filled quickly, pressure spikes because the wall is stretched. Over the next 30–60 s, smooth muscle cells rearrange their cross-bridges and filament lattice to the new length, so tension — and thus pressure (Laplace: P = 2T/r) — falls back toward baseline even though volume stays higher. This lets hollow organs (bladder, stomach, rectum) hold larger volumes without high pressure. The reverse (stress-relaxation reversal) occurs when volume is removed.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The bladder empties partially through the urethra, reducing volume and restoring baseline pressure.</b> — The outlet is closed, so volume can't fall.</li><li><b>Parasympathetic stimulation progressively increases detrusor contraction, lowering intravesical pressure.</b> — Detrusor contraction would raise, not lower, intravesical pressure.</li><li><b>Increased bladder volume permanently inhibits calcium–calmodulin binding, abolishing smooth muscle contractility.</b> — Contractility isn't abolished; the bladder can still contract to void.</li><li><b>Stretch causes immediate and sustained myosin phosphorylation, continuously increasing bladder wall tension.</b> — Sustained increasing tension would keep pressure high, which is the opposite of what was observed.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Stress-relaxation = smooth muscle's ability to accommodate volume (receptive relaxation is a neural reflex).</p>","w":["Correct — stress-relaxation reduces tension after the initial rise.","The outlet is closed, so volume can't fall.","Detrusor contraction would raise, not lower, intravesical pressure.","Contractility isn't abolished; the bladder can still contract to void.","Sustained increasing tension would keep pressure high, which is the opposite of what was observed."]},"p69":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> NO → soluble guanylyl cyclase → ↑cGMP → PKG → lower Ca²⁺ and activation of MLCP → myosin dephosphorylation → vasodilation.</p><p>Nitrates (nitroglycerin, isosorbide) release NO, mostly dilating veins (reducing preload) and also coronary vessels, relieving angina. PKG reduces Ca²⁺ entry, increases SR uptake and extrusion, and activates myosin light-chain phosphatase, so cross-bridge activity falls. PDE-5 inhibitors (sildenafil) prolong the cGMP signal — never combine them with nitrates (severe hypotension).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>cGMP activates MLCK, increasing myosin phosphorylation and promoting sustained contraction.</b> — cGMP decreases myosin phosphorylation and promotes relaxation, not contraction.</li><li><b>cGMP increases cytosolic calcium through IP₃ receptors, accelerating actin–myosin cycling.</b> — cGMP lowers cytosolic Ca²⁺; it doesn't increase IP₃-mediated release.</li><li><b>cGMP activates troponin C, removing tropomyosin from actin and initiating relaxation.</b> — Smooth muscle has no troponin.</li><li><b>cGMP inhibits calcium extrusion, maintaining a high cytosolic calcium concentration while decreasing vascular resistance.</b> — cGMP promotes Ca²⁺ removal, lowering cytosolic Ca²⁺.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Nitrates + PDE-5 inhibitors = dangerous hypotension.</p>","w":["cGMP decreases myosin phosphorylation and promotes relaxation, not contraction.","cGMP lowers cytosolic Ca²⁺; it doesn't increase IP₃-mediated release.","Smooth muscle has no troponin.","Correct — cGMP reduces Ca²⁺-dependent activation and promotes dephosphorylation.","cGMP promotes Ca²⁺ removal, lowering cytosolic Ca²⁺."]},"p70":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Active hyperemia: metabolites from working muscle (↓O₂, ↑CO₂, H⁺, K⁺, adenosine, lactate) relax local arterioles, increasing flow.</p><p>These vasodilator metabolites act directly on arteriolar smooth muscle — for example, by opening K⁺ channels (K-ATP, K-IR), hyperpolarizing the cells and closing L-type Ca²⁺ channels — so Ca²⁺ falls and MLCK activity drops. Resistance falls only in the active tissue, matching flow to metabolic demand. In exercising muscle, local metabolic control overrides sympathetic vasoconstriction (functional sympatholysis).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Local metabolites increase MLCK activity in arteriolar smooth muscle, increasing resistance and diverting blood toward the exercising tissue.</b> — Increased MLCK activity would constrict arterioles and reduce flow.</li><li><b>Local metabolites inhibit calcium removal from smooth muscle, maintaining contraction while increasing vessel diameter.</b> — Maintained contraction cannot increase vessel diameter.</li><li><b>Increased tissue oxygen consumption directly stimulates troponin-mediated relaxation of arteriolar smooth muscle.</b> — Smooth muscle has no troponin, and O₂ consumption doesn't act through troponin.</li><li><b>Reduced local oxygen increases sympathetic norepinephrine release, producing selective dilation through α₁-adrenergic receptors.</b> — α₁ receptors cause vasoconstriction, not dilation.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Exercise blood flow is regulated mainly by local metabolites.</p>","w":["Increased MLCK activity would constrict arterioles and reduce flow.","Correct — local metabolic signals relax arteriolar smooth muscle.","Maintained contraction cannot increase vessel diameter.","Smooth muscle has no troponin, and O₂ consumption doesn't act through troponin.","α₁ receptors cause vasoconstriction, not dilation."]},"p71":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The same transmitter can contract one smooth muscle and relax another — the response depends on the receptor subtype and its signaling pathway.</p><p>Norepinephrine on vascular α₁ receptors activates Gq → IP₃ → Ca²⁺ → contraction, raising systemic vascular resistance. In the intestine, norepinephrine acts on β₂ (Gs → cAMP → relaxation) and α₂ receptors (including presynaptic inhibition of ACh release from enteric neurons), reducing motility. This fits the sympathetic \"fight-or-flight\" pattern: raise blood pressure, suppress digestion.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Norepinephrine is always excitatory, but intestinal smooth muscle lacks myosin regulatory light chains.</b> — Norepinephrine isn't always excitatory, and intestinal smooth muscle has regulatory light chains.</li><li><b>Vascular smooth muscle uses troponin, whereas intestinal smooth muscle uses calmodulin.</b> — Neither vascular nor intestinal smooth muscle uses troponin.</li><li><b>Norepinephrine enters vascular cells but cannot enter intestinal cells, producing different intracellular calcium concentrations.</b> — Norepinephrine acts on surface receptors; it doesn't need to enter the cells.</li><li><b>Intestinal smooth muscle is electrically uncoupled, whereas vascular smooth muscle must be electrically coupled to respond to norepinephrine.</b> — Intestinal smooth muscle is largely single-unit (coupled), and coupling doesn't explain opposite responses.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> α₁ = Gq (contract); α₂ = Gi; β₁/β₂ = Gs (β₂ relaxes smooth muscle).</p>","w":["Norepinephrine isn't always excitatory, and intestinal smooth muscle has regulatory light chains.","Neither vascular nor intestinal smooth muscle uses troponin.","Norepinephrine acts on surface receptors; it doesn't need to enter the cells.","Intestinal smooth muscle is largely single-unit (coupled), and coupling doesn't explain opposite responses.","Correct — different receptor subtypes and pathways explain the opposite effects."]},"p72":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Inhibiting MLCP keeps myosin phosphorylated as Ca²⁺ falls, prolonging tension (\"Ca²⁺ sensitization\").</p><p>Smooth muscle tension reflects the balance of MLCK (on) and MLCP (off). Normally, as Ca²⁺ falls, MLCK becomes less active and MLCP dephosphorylates myosin, so the muscle relaxes. Blocking MLCP means phosphorylated myosin persists and cross-bridges keep cycling even at near-baseline Ca²⁺. Physiologically, agonists such as angiotensin II, endothelin and norepinephrine activate RhoA/Rho-kinase, which inhibits MLCP — a mechanism implicated in hypertension and vasospasm (Rho-kinase inhibitors like fasudil relax vessels).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Inhibition of MLCP accelerates myosin dephosphorylation, reducing ATP consumption and prolonging contraction.</b> — Inhibiting MLCP slows, rather than speeds, dephosphorylation.</li><li><b>Inhibition of MLCP prevents calcium from binding to calmodulin, directly increasing myosin ATPase activity.</b> — MLCP has no effect on Ca²⁺–calmodulin binding.</li><li><b>Inhibition of MLCP increases calcium extrusion from the cell, which directly phosphorylates myosin regulatory light chains.</b> — Ca²⁺ extrusion doesn't phosphorylate myosin.</li><li><b>Inhibition of MLCP activates troponin I, preventing tropomyosin from covering actin-binding sites.</b> — Smooth muscle has no troponin.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Rho-kinase → inhibits MLCP → Ca²⁺ sensitization → sustained contraction.</p>","w":["Correct — slowed dephosphorylation keeps cross-bridges active despite falling Ca²⁺.","Inhibiting MLCP slows, rather than speeds, dephosphorylation.","MLCP has no effect on Ca²⁺–calmodulin binding.","Ca²⁺ extrusion doesn't phosphorylate myosin.","Smooth muscle has no troponin."]},"p73":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Slow waves (from interstitial cells of Cajal) set the rhythm; contraction occurs only when a slow wave crosses threshold and triggers Ca²⁺ spike potentials.</p><p>Slow waves are rhythmic depolarizations, not action potentials, and by themselves cause little contraction. When their peak reaches threshold (about −40 to −35 mV), spike potentials fire, carried mainly by Ca²⁺ through L-type channels, and Ca²⁺–calmodulin–MLCK causes contraction. The more spikes per slow wave, the stronger the contraction. Parasympathetic input (ACh) and stretch raise the baseline toward threshold; sympathetic input lowers it. Slow-wave frequency sets the maximum contraction frequency (stomach ~3/min, duodenum ~12/min).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Every slow wave is an action potential that necessarily produces a maximal contraction.</b> — Slow waves aren't action potentials and don't always cause contraction.</li><li><b>Slow waves arise from skeletal-type motor neuron discharges that directly activate troponin.</b> — Slow waves arise from interstitial cells of Cajal, not motor neurons, and smooth muscle has no troponin.</li><li><b>Slow waves inhibit voltage-gated calcium channels, and contraction occurs only when the membrane becomes more negative.</b> — Depolarization (less negative), not hyperpolarization, opens voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels.</li><li><b>Slow waves directly phosphorylate myosin without altering intracellular calcium.</b> — Contraction requires a rise in Ca²⁺; slow waves don't phosphorylate myosin directly.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Slow-wave frequency = maximal frequency of GI contraction.</p>","w":["Slow waves aren't action potentials and don't always cause contraction.","Slow waves arise from interstitial cells of Cajal, not motor neurons, and smooth muscle has no troponin.","Correct — contraction occurs when slow waves reach threshold and trigger Ca²⁺-dependent spikes.","Depolarization (less negative), not hyperpolarization, opens voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels.","Contraction requires a rise in Ca²⁺; slow waves don't phosphorylate myosin directly."]},"p74":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Side-polar myosin filaments pull actin in opposite directions along their whole length, letting smooth muscle shorten far more than skeletal muscle.</p><p>Skeletal muscle myosin is bipolar with a bare central zone, and sarcomere shortening is limited when thick filaments hit the Z discs. In smooth muscle, myosin heads on opposite faces point in opposite directions along the entire filament, there are no Z discs, and actin filaments anchor to scattered dense bodies. Actin can therefore slide along the full length of the thick filament, allowing shortening to a much smaller fraction of resting length — useful for hollow organs that must empty almost completely.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Troponin-mediated movement of tropomyosin allows actin filaments to slide farther than in skeletal muscle.</b> — Smooth muscle has no troponin.</li><li><b>Dense bodies function as calcium storage sites that release calcium continuously during contraction.</b> — Dense bodies anchor actin filaments; they don't store Ca²⁺.</li><li><b>The absence of ATP hydrolysis allows actin and myosin to remain permanently attached while the fiber shortens.</b> — Contraction always requires ATP hydrolysis.</li><li><b>A highly developed sarcoplasmic reticulum generates sequential contractions along repeating sarcomeres.</b> — Smooth muscle has a modest SR and no repeating sarcomeres.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Smooth muscle can shorten up to ~80% of its length vs. ~30% for skeletal muscle.</p>","w":["Smooth muscle has no troponin.","Correct — oppositely oriented cross-bridges permit extensive shortening without sarcomeres.","Dense bodies anchor actin filaments; they don't store Ca²⁺.","Contraction always requires ATP hydrolysis.","Smooth muscle has a modest SR and no repeating sarcomeres."]},"p75":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The iris and ciliary muscle are multi-unit smooth muscle: separately innervated fibers with few gap junctions, permitting precise, localized control.</p><p>Because electrical coupling is minimal, stimulating one group of fibers produces a local response that doesn't propagate through the sheet. Force is graded by how many fibers the autonomic nerves activate, like recruitment in skeletal muscle. Parasympathetic fibers (CN III via ciliary ganglion) constrict the pupil and contract the ciliary muscle for near vision; sympathetic fibers dilate the pupil.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Extensive gap junctions synchronize every fiber, producing a single propagating action potential throughout the tissue.</b> — Extensive gap junctions and propagation describe single-unit smooth muscle.</li><li><b>Spontaneous pacemaker activity initiates rhythmic contraction independently of neural input.</b> — Spontaneous pacemaker activity is a single-unit feature; multi-unit muscle depends on nerve input.</li><li><b>Calcium binds troponin C, allowing neighboring fibers to contract as a functional syncytium.</b> — Smooth muscle has no troponin.</li><li><b>Dense bodies are absent, so force cannot be transmitted between actin and myosin.</b> — Dense bodies are present in all smooth muscle and transmit force.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Multi-unit = precise control (eye, arrector pili, vas deferens).</p>","w":["Extensive gap junctions and propagation describe single-unit smooth muscle.","Spontaneous pacemaker activity is a single-unit feature; multi-unit muscle depends on nerve input.","Smooth muscle has no troponin.","Correct — independently innervated fibers allow precise local control.","Dense bodies are present in all smooth muscle and transmit force."]},"p76":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> β₂ agonists (Gs → ↑cAMP → PKA) relax airway smooth muscle by lowering Ca²⁺ and inhibiting MLCK.</p><p>Albuterol binds β₂ receptors on bronchial smooth muscle. Gs activates adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP and activating protein kinase A. PKA reduces cytosolic Ca²⁺ (enhanced SERCA uptake and extrusion, reduced release) and phosphorylates MLCK, lowering its affinity for Ca²⁺–calmodulin. Myosin phosphorylation falls, the muscle relaxes, and the airways widen. Side effects reflect β₂ effects elsewhere: tremor (skeletal muscle), tachycardia and hypokalemia (K⁺ shift into cells).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>β₂-receptor activation stimulates Gq and increases IP₃-mediated calcium release, producing sustained airway contraction.</b> — β₂ receptors are Gs-coupled, not Gq, and they cause relaxation, not contraction.</li><li><b>β₂-receptor activation increases MLCK activity, promoting myosin phosphorylation and narrowing the bronchi.</b> — PKA inhibits MLCK, reducing myosin phosphorylation and widening the bronchi.</li><li><b>β₂-receptor activation increases troponin C binding, preventing calcium from entering the sarcoplasm.</b> — Smooth muscle has no troponin.</li><li><b>β₂-receptor activation inhibits all myosin dephosphorylation, causing persistent cross-bridge attachment and increased airway caliber.</b> — Persistent cross-bridge attachment would cause contraction, not wider airways.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Gs → cAMP relaxes smooth muscle; in the heart, Gs → cAMP increases contractility.</p>","w":["Correct — increased cAMP reduces Ca²⁺-dependent activation and favors relaxation.","β₂ receptors are Gs-coupled, not Gq, and they cause relaxation, not contraction.","PKA inhibits MLCK, reducing myosin phosphorylation and widening the bronchi.","Smooth muscle has no troponin.","Persistent cross-bridge attachment would cause contraction, not wider airways."]},"q1":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Thin (actin) filaments are anchored at the Z line by α-actinin; the sarcomere runs from one Z line to the next.</p><p>On an EM of skeletal muscle, the dark A band is the length of the thick myosin filaments, bisected by the M line; the pale I bands on either side contain only thin filaments and are bisected by the dense Z line. Each actin filament's barbed (plus) end is embedded in the Z disc and cross-linked there by α-actinin, capped by CapZ, with nebulin running along it as a ruler. An antibody that prevents actin from binding its anchor therefore targets the Z line. During contraction, myosin heads pull the free (minus) ends of actin toward the M line, so the Z lines are drawn together.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>B</b> — The A band spans the thick filaments (myosin plus overlapping actin); actin isn't anchored there.</li><li><b>C</b> — The M line cross-links thick filaments with myomesin; it anchors myosin, not actin.</li><li><b>D</b> — The edge of the A band marks the ends of the thick filaments, not an actin attachment.</li><li><b>E</b> — The I band contains thin filaments only; their anchor is the Z line in its center, not the band itself.</li><li><b>F</b> — This is part of the neighbouring sarcomere's A band — thick filaments, not the actin anchor.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Z line proteins: α-actinin, CapZ, desmin (links Z discs to each other and the membrane). M line: myomesin. Titin spans Z line to M line.</p>","w":["Correct — the Z line is where actin filaments are anchored by α-actinin.","The A band spans the thick filaments (myosin plus overlapping actin); actin isn't anchored there.","The M line cross-links thick filaments with myomesin; it anchors myosin, not actin.","The edge of the A band marks the ends of the thick filaments, not an actin attachment.","The I band contains thin filaments only; their anchor is the Z line in its center, not the band itself.","This is part of the neighbouring sarcomere's A band — thick filaments, not the actin anchor."]},"q2":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Connexins (e.g., connexin-43) build gap junctions, which electrically couple myometrial cells so labor contractions spread and synchronize.</p><p>Six connexin subunits form a hemichannel (connexon); connexons in adjacent membranes dock to form a ~1.5–2 nm channel through which ions and small molecules (&lt;1 kDa, e.g., cAMP, Ca²⁺) pass. Late in pregnancy, rising estrogen (relative to progesterone) increases connexin-43 and oxytocin receptor expression, turning the myometrium into a functional syncytium: an action potential in one cell spreads to its neighbors, and oxytocin triggers strong coordinated contractions. Progesterone has the opposite effect, keeping the uterus quiet.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Adherens junction</b> — Adherens junctions (cadherins linked to actin via catenins) provide adhesion, not electrical coupling.</li><li><b>Desmosomes</b> — Desmosomes (desmogleins/desmocollins to keratin filaments) resist mechanical stress; they don't pass ions.</li><li><b>Hemidesmosomes</b> — Hemidesmosomes (integrin α6β4) anchor cells to the basal lamina.</li><li><b>Tight junction</b> — Tight junctions (claudins, occludin) seal the paracellular space; they don't connect cytoplasm.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Gap junctions couple cardiac muscle, smooth muscle (labor), osteocytes and some neurons (electrical synapses).</p>","w":["Adherens junctions (cadherins linked to actin via catenins) provide adhesion, not electrical coupling.","Desmosomes (desmogleins/desmocollins to keratin filaments) resist mechanical stress; they don't pass ions.","Correct — connexin-43 forms gap junctions that electrically couple myometrial cells.","Hemidesmosomes (integrin α6β4) anchor cells to the basal lamina.","Tight junctions (claudins, occludin) seal the paracellular space; they don't connect cytoplasm."]},"q3":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The blood–brain barrier is formed by tight junctions between brain capillary endothelial cells; polar dopamine can't cross it.</p><p>Brain capillaries are continuous (non-fenestrated), joined by complex tight junctions (claudin-5, occludin), have very few pinocytotic vesicles, and are surrounded by a basal lamina, pericytes and astrocyte end-feet that induce and maintain the barrier. Polar or charged molecules like dopamine can't diffuse between the cells and have no transporter, so IV dopamine acts only peripherally (heart, vessels). Levodopa is used for Parkinson disease because it is carried across by the large neutral amino acid transporter (LAT1) and converted to dopamine inside the brain; carbidopa blocks peripheral conversion.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Gap junction</b> — Gap junctions let small molecules pass between adjacent cells; they don't form the BBB seal.</li><li><b>Fenestrations</b> — Fenestrations are pores that INCREASE permeability; BBB capillaries lack them.</li><li><b>Desmosomes</b> — Desmosomes provide mechanical strength, not a permeability barrier.</li><li><b>Hemidesmosomes</b> — Hemidesmosomes anchor cells to the basal lamina.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Areas without a BBB (circumventricular organs, e.g., area postrema, posterior pituitary) have fenestrated capillaries.</p>","w":["Gap junctions let small molecules pass between adjacent cells; they don't form the BBB seal.","Correct — endothelial tight junctions form the blood–brain barrier that excludes dopamine.","Fenestrations are pores that INCREASE permeability; BBB capillaries lack them.","Desmosomes provide mechanical strength, not a permeability barrier.","Hemidesmosomes anchor cells to the basal lamina."]},"q4":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Osteocytes communicate with each other through gap junctions between their processes inside canaliculi.</p><p>When an osteoblast becomes surrounded by its own mineralized matrix, it becomes an osteocyte sitting in a lacuna. It keeps dozens of thin processes running through canaliculi, touching processes of neighboring osteocytes and the bone-lining cells at the surface. Gap junctions (connexin-43) at these contacts let nutrients, ions and signals pass, so the osteocyte network acts as the bone's mechanosensor and directs remodeling (e.g., via sclerostin and RANKL). Because nutrients travel through this chain, osteocytes must be within ~0.2 mm of a blood vessel.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Tight junction</b> — Tight junctions seal epithelial/endothelial layers; osteocytes aren't sealed together.</li><li><b>Fenestrations</b> — Fenestrations are pores in capillary endothelium, not connections between cells.</li><li><b>Desmosomes</b> — Desmosomes give mechanical adhesion in epithelia and cardiac muscle, not communication.</li><li><b>Hemidesmosomes</b> — Hemidesmosomes attach cells to a basal lamina.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Osteocytes = most abundant bone cells; gap-junction network through canaliculi; mechanosensors.</p>","w":["Correct — osteocyte processes are joined by gap junctions inside canaliculi.","Tight junctions seal epithelial/endothelial layers; osteocytes aren't sealed together.","Fenestrations are pores in capillary endothelium, not connections between cells.","Desmosomes give mechanical adhesion in epithelia and cardiac muscle, not communication.","Hemidesmosomes attach cells to a basal lamina."]},"q5":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Tendon = dense regular connective tissue packed with parallel type I collagen bundles.</p><p>Type I collagen is the most abundant collagen and the strongest in tension. Fibroblasts (tenocytes) in tendon line up between thick parallel bundles all oriented along the line of pull, which is why tendons resist stretching so well but heal slowly (few cells, poor blood supply). Collagen types: I — bone, skin, tendon, ligament, late scar; II — cartilage, vitreous, nucleus pulposus; III — reticular fibers, vessels, early granulation tissue; IV — basement membrane.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Type II</b> — Type II collagen is in hyaline and elastic cartilage, vitreous and nucleus pulposus.</li><li><b>Type III</b> — Type III collagen forms reticular fibers (lymphoid organs, liver, vessels, early wounds).</li><li><b>Type IV</b> — Type IV collagen forms the sheet-like network of the basement membrane.</li><li><b>Type VII</b> — Type VII collagen forms anchoring fibrils under the epidermis (defective in dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> \"Be (So Totally) Cool, Read Books\": type I bone, skin, tendon; II cartilage; III reticulin; IV basement membrane.</p>","w":["Correct — tendon is made of type I collagen.","Type II collagen is in hyaline and elastic cartilage, vitreous and nucleus pulposus.","Type III collagen forms reticular fibers (lymphoid organs, liver, vessels, early wounds).","Type IV collagen forms the sheet-like network of the basement membrane.","Type VII collagen forms anchoring fibrils under the epidermis (defective in dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa)."]},"q6":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Melanocytes come from neural crest cells that migrate into the stratum basale of the epidermis.</p><p>During the 4th–5th weeks, neural crest cells delaminate from the edges of the neural folds and migrate widely. Those destined to be melanocytes reach the basal layer of the epidermis (and hair follicles, uvea, inner ear). There they produce melanin in melanosomes using tyrosinase and pass the granules to ~30–40 neighboring keratinocytes through dendritic processes. Melanoma, their malignant tumor, is recognized by ABCDE changes (asymmetry, border irregularity, color variation, diameter &gt;6 mm, evolution) — this patient's lesion fits.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Mesoderm</b> — Mesoderm forms dermis, muscle, bone and vessels, not melanocytes.</li><li><b>Endoderm</b> — Endoderm forms the lining of the gut and respiratory tract and their glands.</li><li><b>Ectoderm</b> — Surface ectoderm forms keratinocytes, hair and nails; neural crest arises from ectoderm, but the specific origin is neural crest.</li><li><b>Notochord</b> — The notochord persists only as the nucleus pulposus.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Neural crest derivatives: melanocytes, PNS ganglia, Schwann cells, adrenal medulla, odontoblasts, aorticopulmonary septum, craniofacial bones.</p>","w":["Correct — melanocytes are derived from neural crest cells.","Mesoderm forms dermis, muscle, bone and vessels, not melanocytes.","Endoderm forms the lining of the gut and respiratory tract and their glands.","Surface ectoderm forms keratinocytes, hair and nails; neural crest arises from ectoderm, but the specific origin is neural crest.","The notochord persists only as the nucleus pulposus."]},"q7":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cartilage is avascular, so damaged cartilage heals poorly and tends to break down after injury or inflammation.</p><p>Chondrocytes depend on diffusion through the matrix — from perichondrial vessels in most cartilage, or from synovial fluid for articular cartilage, which has no perichondrium. There is no blood supply to bring in inflammatory cells, stem cells or nutrients for repair, and mature chondrocytes divide very little. After a knee injury with an inflamed joint (here possibly infection or hemarthrosis), enzymes in the synovial fluid degrade the matrix while the cartilage can't regenerate, so it is progressively destroyed; any repair that occurs is by fibrocartilage from the perichondrium or subchondral bone.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Cartilage contains collagen</b> — Type II collagen gives cartilage tensile strength; it doesn't explain its poor repair.</li><li><b>Cartilage contains chondroitin sulphate</b> — Chondroitin sulfate holds water for compression resistance; it isn't why cartilage breaks down.</li><li><b>Cartilage is not innervated</b> — Lack of innervation explains why cartilage damage is painless, not why it heals poorly.</li><li><b>Cartilage tends to calcify</b> — Calcification happens in endochondral ossification and aging, not as the mechanism of injury-related destruction.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cartilage: avascular, aneural, alymphatic — nutrition by diffusion, so poor healing.</p>","w":["Type II collagen gives cartilage tensile strength; it doesn't explain its poor repair.","Chondroitin sulfate holds water for compression resistance; it isn't why cartilage breaks down.","Lack of innervation explains why cartilage damage is painless, not why it heals poorly.","Correct — cartilage is avascular, so it repairs poorly after injury.","Calcification happens in endochondral ossification and aging, not as the mechanism of injury-related destruction."]},"q8":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The osteogenic (cambium) layer of the periosteum supplies most of the cells that form the fracture callus.</p><p>Fracture healing proceeds through hematoma → inflammation → soft (cartilaginous/fibrous) callus → hard (bony) callus → remodeling. Osteoprogenitor cells of the inner periosteum (and to a lesser extent the endosteum and marrow stromal cells) proliferate; near the fracture, where oxygen is low, they form cartilage, and farther away, where vessels are intact, they form woven bone directly. The cartilage is then replaced by bone (endochondral ossification), and woven bone is remodeled into lamellar bone. Children's thick, highly cellular periosteum is why their fractures heal fast and remodel well.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Cartilage</b> — Cartilage appears temporarily in the soft callus but is replaced by bone; it isn't the source.</li><li><b>Marrow</b> — Marrow/endosteum contributes some osteoprogenitors, but less than the periosteum.</li><li><b>Compact bone</b> — Compact bone is the tissue being repaired, not the producer of new bone.</li><li><b>Cancellous bone</b> — Cancellous bone is existing bone; it doesn't generate the callus.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Periosteum: outer fibrous layer + inner osteogenic layer; anchored by Sharpey's fibers.</p>","w":["Correct — the periosteum's osteoprogenitor cells make most of the new bone.","Cartilage appears temporarily in the soft callus but is replaced by bone; it isn't the source.","Marrow/endosteum contributes some osteoprogenitors, but less than the periosteum.","Compact bone is the tissue being repaired, not the producer of new bone.","Cancellous bone is existing bone; it doesn't generate the callus."]},"q9":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> In the sliding filament model the A band (length of the thick filaments) stays constant; the I band and H zone shorten.</p><p>During contraction, myosin heads pull actin filaments toward the M line, so the filaments overlap more but neither shortens. Consequences: the A band (thick filament length) is unchanged; the I band (thin filaments only) shrinks because more actin slides into the A band; the H zone (thick filaments only) shrinks and may disappear; the Z lines approach each other, so each sarcomere shortens, and therefore each myofibril and the whole fiber shorten.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>I band</b> — The I band shortens as actin slides into the A band.</li><li><b>Sarcomere</b> — The sarcomere (Z to Z) shortens.</li><li><b>Myofibril</b> — A myofibril is a chain of sarcomeres, so it shortens.</li><li><b>Myofiber</b> — The myofiber shortens because its myofibrils do.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> \"A band always stays the same; HI shortens (H zone and I band).\"</p>","w":["Correct — the A band (length of the myosin filaments) maintains its length.","The I band shortens as actin slides into the A band.","The sarcomere (Z to Z) shortens.","A myofibril is a chain of sarcomeres, so it shortens.","The myofiber shortens because its myofibrils do."]},"q10":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Recurrent sinopulmonary/ear infections + infertility with normal sperm count = primary ciliary dyskinesia from defective axonemal dynein.</p><p>Motile cilia and sperm flagella share the 9+2 axoneme: nine outer microtubule doublets around a central pair. Dynein arms on each doublet use ATP to walk along the neighboring doublet; nexin links convert this sliding into bending. When dynein arms are missing or defective, respiratory cilia can't clear mucus (chronic sinusitis, otitis, bronchiectasis) and sperm can't swim, so the man is infertile despite a normal count. If embryonic nodal cilia are affected, left–right asymmetry is random and half have situs inversus (Kartagener syndrome). Women may have subfertility from immotile tubal cilia.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Desmin</b> — Desmin is the intermediate filament of muscle (Z disc linkage), not a ciliary motor.</li><li><b>Kinesin</b> — Kinesin moves cargo anterogradely along microtubules within cells, not ciliary beating.</li><li><b>Alpha-nexin</b> — Nexin links neighboring doublets; the classic defect is in dynein.</li><li><b>Titin</b> — Titin is the elastic sarcomere protein in muscle.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Primary ciliary dyskinesia: low nasal nitric oxide, immotile sperm, situs inversus in ~50%.</p>","w":["Desmin is the intermediate filament of muscle (Z disc linkage), not a ciliary motor.","Correct — defective dynein arms leave cilia and sperm immotile.","Kinesin moves cargo anterogradely along microtubules within cells, not ciliary beating.","Nexin links neighboring doublets; the classic defect is in dynein.","Titin is the elastic sarcomere protein in muscle."]},"q11":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Myasthenia gravis = autoantibodies against nicotinic ACh receptors at the neuromuscular junction → fatigable weakness, especially ocular and bulbar.</p><p>Anti-AChR antibodies block receptors, cross-link them so they're internalized, and activate complement that damages the junctional folds. With fewer receptors, the end-plate potential barely reaches threshold, and repeated stimulation (as ACh release falls with each impulse) causes failure — weakness that worsens with use and improves with rest. Ptosis and diplopia are common first signs. It's associated with thymic hyperplasia or thymoma; treatment includes acetylcholinesterase inhibitors (pyridostigmine), immunosuppression and thymectomy.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Demyelinating disease</b> — Demyelinating diseases (MS, Guillain–Barré) slow nerve conduction; they don't involve AChR antibodies.</li><li><b>Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis</b> — ALS is degeneration of upper and lower motor neurons with fasciculations, no autoantibodies.</li><li><b>Duchenne muscular dystrophy</b> — Duchenne muscular dystrophy is X-linked dystrophin deficiency in young boys with proximal weakness.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Myasthenia: postsynaptic, worsens with use; Lambert–Eaton: presynaptic Ca²⁺ channel antibodies, improves with use.</p>","w":["Correct — antibodies to acetylcholine receptors cause myasthenia gravis.","Demyelinating diseases (MS, Guillain–Barré) slow nerve conduction; they don't involve AChR antibodies.","ALS is degeneration of upper and lower motor neurons with fasciculations, no autoantibodies.","Duchenne muscular dystrophy is X-linked dystrophin deficiency in young boys with proximal weakness."]},"q12":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The primary ossification center (diaphysis) forms prenatally: hypertrophic chondrocytes die, the matrix calcifies, and osteoblasts lay osteoid on the cartilage remnants.</p><p>In endochondral ossification a hyaline cartilage model is replaced by bone. First a periosteal bone collar forms around the mid-shaft; chondrocytes inside hypertrophy, secrete alkaline phosphatase, calcify their matrix and die. A periosteal bud (vessels plus osteoprogenitor cells) invades the hollowed cartilage, and osteoblasts deposit osteoid on the calcified cartilage spicules. So this primary center contains degenerating chondrocytes and new osteoid. Secondary centers appear in the epiphyses mostly after birth, and cartilage left between them and the diaphysis becomes the epiphyseal (growth) plate.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Secondary centre</b> — Secondary centers form in the epiphyses, mostly after birth.</li><li><b>Epiphysis</b> — Prenatally the epiphysis is still cartilage; its secondary center appears later.</li><li><b>Epiphyseal plate</b> — The epiphyseal plate is defined only after secondary centers form, between epiphysis and diaphysis.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Primary center = diaphysis, before birth; secondary centers = epiphyses, mostly after birth.</p>","w":["Correct — the primary ossification center contains osteoid and degenerating chondrocytes before birth.","Secondary centers form in the epiphyses, mostly after birth.","Prenatally the epiphysis is still cartilage; its secondary center appears later.","The epiphyseal plate is defined only after secondary centers form, between epiphysis and diaphysis."]},"q13":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hemidesmosomes anchor basal epithelial cells to the basal lamina (integrin α6β4 + BP180 binding laminin; plectin/BP230 linking keratin).</p><p>A hemidesmosome looks like half a desmosome but connects a cell to the extracellular matrix rather than to another cell. Inside, plaque proteins (plectin, BP230) attach keratin intermediate filaments; transmembrane integrin α6β4 and type XVII collagen (BP180) bind laminin-332 in the basal lamina, which is in turn anchored to the dermis by type VII collagen fibrils. Autoantibodies against BP180/BP230 cause bullous pemphigoid (tense subepidermal blisters), while antibodies to desmogleins cause pemphigus vulgaris (flaccid intraepidermal blisters).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Desmosomes</b> — Desmosomes join cells to each other, not to the basal lamina.</li><li><b>Tight junction</b> — Tight junctions seal the apical paracellular space.</li><li><b>Collagen fibres</b> — Collagen fibers are in the connective tissue below; the cell attaches through hemidesmosomes.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Pemphigus = desmosomes (intraepidermal); pemphigoid = hemidesmosomes (subepidermal).</p>","w":["Correct — hemidesmosomes anchor basal cells to the basal lamina.","Desmosomes join cells to each other, not to the basal lamina.","Tight junctions seal the apical paracellular space.","Collagen fibers are in the connective tissue below; the cell attaches through hemidesmosomes."]},"q14":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Osteoblasts synthesize osteoid (type I collagen + matrix proteins) and initiate its mineralization; deficient osteoid and mineralization point to them.</p><p>Osteoblasts line forming bone surfaces, secreting type I collagen, osteocalcin, osteonectin and alkaline phosphatase. Matrix vesicles budding from them concentrate calcium and phosphate to start hydroxyapatite crystal formation. If calcium, phosphate or vitamin D is lacking (rickets in children, osteomalacia in adults), osteoid is laid down but poorly mineralized; if osteoblast function itself is impaired, less osteoid forms. Either way the bones are soft and weak, bowing under load.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Osteoclast</b> — Osteoclasts (multinucleated, monocyte-derived) resorb bone; they don't make osteoid.</li><li><b>Osteocyte</b> — Osteocytes maintain the matrix and sense mechanical load; they aren't the main osteoid producers.</li><li><b>Mast cell</b> — Mast cells release histamine and heparin; they don't form bone.</li><li><b>Lack of blood supply</b> — Loss of blood supply causes avascular necrosis (dead bone), not reduced osteoid and mineralization.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Alkaline phosphatase is a marker of osteoblast activity (raised in rickets, Paget disease, healing fractures).</p>","w":["Osteoclasts (multinucleated, monocyte-derived) resorb bone; they don't make osteoid.","Correct — osteoblasts make and mineralize osteoid.","Osteocytes maintain the matrix and sense mechanical load; they aren't the main osteoid producers.","Mast cells release histamine and heparin; they don't form bone.","Loss of blood supply causes avascular necrosis (dead bone), not reduced osteoid and mineralization."]},"q15":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Kartagener syndrome is caused by defective dynein arms of the ciliary axoneme.</p><p>Kartagener syndrome = primary ciliary dyskinesia + situs inversus, usually autosomal recessive. Dynein arms on the outer microtubule doublets generate the sliding force for ciliary beating. Without them: chronic sinusitis, recurrent bronchitis and bronchiectasis from stagnant mucus; infertility from immotile sperm and tubal cilia; and random left–right patterning because nodal cilia in the embryo can't create the leftward fluid flow, so ~half have situs inversus (dextrocardia).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Defect in kinesin</b> — Kinesin is an anterograde intracellular transport motor, not the ciliary motor.</li><li><b>Defect in titin</b> — Titin is a sarcomere protein in muscle.</li><li><b>Defect in nexin</b> — Nexin links adjacent doublets; it isn't the classic defect in Kartagener syndrome.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Kartagener triad: situs inversus, chronic sinusitis, bronchiectasis.</p>","w":["Correct — defective dynein causes the ciliary dysfunction of Kartagener syndrome.","Kinesin is an anterograde intracellular transport motor, not the ciliary motor.","Titin is a sarcomere protein in muscle.","Nexin links adjacent doublets; it isn't the classic defect in Kartagener syndrome."]},"q16":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> A proteoglycan is a core protein with many covalently attached glycosaminoglycan (GAG) chains.</p><p>GAGs are long unbranched chains of repeating disaccharides (an amino sugar plus a uronic acid), usually sulfated and strongly negative, which attract cations and water. Except for hyaluronan (which is not sulfated and has no core protein), GAGs such as chondroitin, keratan, dermatan and heparan sulfate are attached to core proteins to make proteoglycans. In cartilage, aggrecan proteoglycans attach via link proteins to a hyaluronan backbone, forming huge hydrated aggregates that resist compression.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>They consist of a core of fibrous protein covalently bound to glycoproteins.</b> — The side chains are GAGs, not glycoproteins.</li><li><b>They are attached to ribonucleic acid.</b> — Proteoglycans are extracellular matrix molecules; they're not attached to RNA.</li><li><b>They are binding sites for deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA).</b> — They don't serve as DNA-binding sites.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hyaluronan is the only GAG that is not sulfated and not bound to a core protein.</p>","w":["The side chains are GAGs, not glycoproteins.","Proteoglycans are extracellular matrix molecules; they're not attached to RNA.","They don't serve as DNA-binding sites.","Correct — proteoglycans are a protein core with covalently attached glycosaminoglycans."]},"q17":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Langerhans cells = dendritic antigen-presenting cells of the epidermis with tennis-racket Birbeck granules (CD1a, langerin), derived from myeloid precursors.</p><p>Langerhans cells sit mainly in the stratum spinosum and look like clear, star-shaped cells on H&amp;E. They capture antigens from the skin, then migrate through lymphatics to regional lymph nodes, where they present them to T cells (on MHC class II and CD1a for lipid antigens). Their Birbeck granules (rod-shaped with a bulbous end) are formed by langerin. Proliferations of these cells cause Langerhans cell histiocytosis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Melanocytes</b> — Melanocytes are dendritic but contain melanosomes and come from the neural crest; they don't present antigen.</li><li><b>Kupffer cell</b> — Kupffer cells are macrophages in liver sinusoids.</li><li><b>Mesangial cell</b> — Mesangial cells support glomerular capillaries in the kidney.</li><li><b>Merkel cell</b> — Merkel cells are mechanoreceptor cells in the stratum basale with dense neuroendocrine granules.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Epidermal cells: keratinocytes, melanocytes (neural crest), Langerhans cells (bone marrow), Merkel cells (touch).</p>","w":["Correct — Langerhans cells have Birbeck (tennis-racket) granules and present antigen to T cells.","Melanocytes are dendritic but contain melanosomes and come from the neural crest; they don't present antigen.","Kupffer cells are macrophages in liver sinusoids.","Mesangial cells support glomerular capillaries in the kidney.","Merkel cells are mechanoreceptor cells in the stratum basale with dense neuroendocrine granules."]},"q18":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Kinesin carries cargo toward the microtubule plus end (anterograde, cell body → axon terminal); dynein carries it toward the minus end (retrograde).</p><p>In axons, microtubules are oriented with their plus ends toward the terminal. Herpes simplex and varicella-zoster viruses first enter sensory nerve endings in the skin and are carried retrograde by dynein to the ganglion, where they become latent. On reactivation (stress, immunosuppression, UV light), new virions are carried anterograde by kinesin down the axon to the skin, producing recurrent cold sores or shingles in that dermatome.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Dynein</b> — Dynein mediates the retrograde trip from skin to ganglion during initial infection.</li><li><b>Lamin</b> — Lamins are intermediate filaments of the nuclear lamina.</li><li><b>Selectin</b> — Selectins mediate leukocyte rolling on endothelium.</li><li><b>Spectrin</b> — Spectrin is a membrane skeleton protein (e.g., in red cells), not a motor.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Kinesin = \"kin-go\" outward (anterograde); dynein = \"dyne-in\" back to the cell body (retrograde). Rabies and tetanus toxin travel retrograde.</p>","w":["Dynein mediates the retrograde trip from skin to ganglion during initial infection.","Correct — kinesin carries the reactivated virus anterogradely down the axon to the skin.","Lamins are intermediate filaments of the nuclear lamina.","Selectins mediate leukocyte rolling on endothelium.","Spectrin is a membrane skeleton protein (e.g., in red cells), not a motor."]},"q19":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Osteogenesis imperfecta is caused by defective type I collagen (COL1A1/COL1A2 mutations).</p><p>Type I collagen is a triple helix of two α1 and one α2 chains, requiring glycine at every third position. Most OI mutations substitute glycine or reduce collagen production, giving weak, brittle bones. Findings: multiple fractures with minimal trauma (sometimes mistaken for child abuse), blue sclerae (thin collagen lets the choroid show through), dentinogenesis imperfecta (opalescent teeth), hearing loss (ossicle fractures/otosclerosis), and short stature in severe types. Most forms are autosomal dominant.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Collagen type 2</b> — Type II defects affect cartilage and vitreous (some chondrodysplasias, Stickler syndrome).</li><li><b>Collagen type 3</b> — Type III defects cause vascular Ehlers–Danlos syndrome (arterial and bowel rupture).</li><li><b>Collagen type 4</b> — Type IV defects cause Alport syndrome (kidney, ear, eye basement membranes).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> OI = \"can't BITE\": Bones, I (eye — blue sclerae), Teeth, Ears (hearing loss).</p>","w":["Correct — osteogenesis imperfecta is a defect of type I collagen.","Type II defects affect cartilage and vitreous (some chondrodysplasias, Stickler syndrome).","Type III defects cause vascular Ehlers–Danlos syndrome (arterial and bowel rupture).","Type IV defects cause Alport syndrome (kidney, ear, eye basement membranes)."]},"q20":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The menisci are fibrocartilage within a synovial joint; only their outer rim is vascular, and the inner part depends on synovial fluid.</p><p>Each meniscus has a vascular outer third (red zone) supplied by capillaries from the capsule and an avascular inner part (white zone) nourished by diffusion from synovial fluid, aided by joint movement. Tears in the red zone can heal or be repaired; tears in the white zone usually can't and may need trimming. Articular cartilage likewise relies on synovial fluid because it lacks perichondrium.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Sutures</b> — Sutures are fibrous joints with no cavity or synovial fluid.</li><li><b>Acromioclavicular joint</b> — The acromioclavicular joint is synovial (with a small disc), but it isn't the classic example of cartilage dependent on synovial fluid for nutrition.</li><li><b>Vertebral joints</b> — Intervertebral discs are cartilaginous joints nourished through the vertebral end plates, not synovial fluid.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Meniscus red zone (outer, vascular) heals; white zone (inner, avascular) doesn't.</p>","w":["Correct — the (inner) lateral meniscus depends on synovial fluid for nutrition.","Sutures are fibrous joints with no cavity or synovial fluid.","The acromioclavicular joint is synovial (with a small disc), but it isn't the classic example of cartilage dependent on synovial fluid for nutrition.","Intervertebral discs are cartilaginous joints nourished through the vertebral end plates, not synovial fluid."]},"q21":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hyaline cartilage covers the articular ends of long bones; it is avascular, made of type II collagen, and grows both appositionally and interstitially.</p><p>Hyaline cartilage is the most common cartilage: articular surfaces, costal cartilages, nose, larynx, trachea and bronchi, epiphyseal plates and the fetal skeleton. Its glassy matrix is type II collagen fibrils in a proteoglycan (aggrecan)-rich ground substance, with chondrocytes in lacunae, often in isogenous groups. It has a perichondrium everywhere except at articular surfaces and growth plates. It grows appositionally (perichondrial chondroblasts add matrix to the surface) and interstitially (chondrocytes divide and secrete matrix from within) — interstitial growth is what lengthens bones at the growth plate.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It is vascular.</b> — Cartilage is avascular; nutrients diffuse from the perichondrium or synovial fluid.</li><li><b>It contains type IV collagen.</b> — Hyaline cartilage contains type II collagen; type IV is in basal laminae.</li><li><b>It undergoes appositional growth only.</b> — It grows both appositionally and interstitially (bone is the tissue that grows appositionally only).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Articular cartilage has no perichondrium, so it can't regenerate well.</p>","w":["Cartilage is avascular; nutrients diffuse from the perichondrium or synovial fluid.","Hyaline cartilage contains type II collagen; type IV is in basal laminae.","It grows both appositionally and interstitially (bone is the tissue that grows appositionally only).","Correct — the articular ends of long bones are covered by hyaline cartilage."]},"q24":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Osteocytes are mature bone cells, one per lacuna, whose processes in canaliculi are linked by gap junctions.</p><p>Osteocytes form when osteoblasts become trapped in mineralized matrix. They shrink their synthetic machinery (less RER and Golgi), keep long processes in canaliculi, and communicate through gap junctions with each other and with surface lining cells. This syncytial network senses mechanical strain and fluid flow and signals osteoblasts and osteoclasts (e.g., sclerostin inhibits bone formation; RANKL promotes resorption). They also help regulate calcium and phosphate (FGF23 secretion).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>They contain large amounts of rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER).</b> — Abundant RER marks osteoblasts, which actively secrete collagen; osteocytes have much less.</li><li><b>They are immature bone cells.</b> — Osteocytes are mature cells; immature cells are osteoprogenitors and osteoblasts.</li><li><b>They are housed as isogenous groups in lacunae.</b> — Isogenous groups (clusters from one dividing cell) are typical of chondrocytes in cartilage.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> One cell per lacuna in bone; isogenous groups in cartilage.</p>","w":["Correct — osteocytes communicate via gap junctions between their processes.","Abundant RER marks osteoblasts, which actively secrete collagen; osteocytes have much less.","Osteocytes are mature cells; immature cells are osteoprogenitors and osteoblasts.","Isogenous groups (clusters from one dividing cell) are typical of chondrocytes in cartilage."]},"q25":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Osteoclasts are large multinucleated cells from the monocyte–macrophage lineage that resorb bone, sitting in pits called Howship lacunae.</p><p>Osteoclast precursors (from hematopoietic monocytes) fuse under the influence of M-CSF and RANKL (from osteoblasts and osteocytes), forming multinucleated cells. An active osteoclast seals onto bone with a ring of integrin-based attachment (clear zone) and forms a ruffled border; it pumps H⁺ (via carbonic anhydrase and a proton ATPase) to dissolve hydroxyapatite and secretes cathepsin K and MMPs to digest collagen. The resulting shallow resorption pit is a Howship lacuna. Osteoprotegerin (OPG) blocks RANKL and inhibits osteoclast formation; calcitonin inhibits them directly.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>They are enucleated cells.</b> — Osteoclasts are multinucleated, not enucleated.</li><li><b>They produce collagen.</b> — Osteoblasts produce collagen; osteoclasts digest it.</li><li><b>They are derived from osteoprogenitor cells.</b> — They come from hematopoietic (monocyte) precursors, not mesenchymal osteoprogenitor cells.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> RANKL (from osteoblasts) → osteoclast formation; OPG is the decoy that blocks it; denosumab is an anti-RANKL antibody.</p>","w":["Osteoclasts are multinucleated, not enucleated.","Osteoblasts produce collagen; osteoclasts digest it.","Correct — osteoclasts occupy Howship (resorption) lacunae.","They come from hematopoietic (monocyte) precursors, not mesenchymal osteoprogenitor cells."]},"q27":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Acromegaly = excess growth hormone after the growth plates close → bones thicken (appositional growth) rather than lengthen.</p><p>Growth hormone acts largely through IGF-1 from the liver. Before epiphyseal closure, excess GH stimulates the growth plates and causes gigantism (excessive height). After closure, bones can only grow by apposition, so they become thicker and coarser: enlarged hands and feet (rings and shoes stop fitting), prognathism, frontal bossing, widened teeth gaps, plus soft tissue overgrowth, organomegaly, hypertension and glucose intolerance. The usual cause is a GH-secreting pituitary adenoma; diagnosis is elevated IGF-1 and failure of GH suppression after an oral glucose load.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Hypervitaminosis A</b> — Hypervitaminosis A increases bone resorption and fragility and can close growth plates early; it doesn't thicken bones.</li><li><b>Hypovitaminosis A</b> — Vitamin A deficiency impairs normal remodeling and growth.</li><li><b>Hypervitaminosis D</b> — Hypervitaminosis D causes hypercalcemia and bone resorption, not thick bones.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> GH excess before plate closure = gigantism; after = acromegaly. Screen with IGF-1.</p>","w":["Hypervitaminosis A increases bone resorption and fragility and can close growth plates early; it doesn't thicken bones.","Correct — acromegaly is caused by excess growth hormone after growth-plate closure.","Vitamin A deficiency impairs normal remodeling and growth.","Hypervitaminosis D causes hypercalcemia and bone resorption, not thick bones."]},"q28":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Pemphigus vulgaris = IgG against desmoglein 3 (± 1) in desmosomes → acantholysis → fragile intraepidermal blisters and oral erosions.</p><p>Desmosomes are spot welds between keratinocytes; their transmembrane cadherins (desmogleins and desmocollins) bind each other across cells and connect via plakoglobin and desmoplakin to keratin filaments. Autoantibodies against desmoglein separate keratinocytes from one another (acantholysis) just above the basal layer, so the blister roof is very thin and ruptures easily, leaving painful erosions — the mouth is often involved first. Direct immunofluorescence shows a net-like (intercellular) IgG pattern, and gentle pressure extends the blister (Nikolsky sign positive).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Collagen</b> — Antibodies to collagen (type XVII in pemphigoid, type VII in epidermolysis bullosa acquisita) produce SUBepidermal blisters.</li><li><b>Connexin</b> — Connexins form gap junctions; they aren't the pemphigus target.</li><li><b>Occludin</b> — Occludin is a tight junction protein.</li><li><b>Integrins</b> — Integrins/hemidesmosome components are targets in subepidermal disease (bullous pemphigoid).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Pemphigus: intraepidermal, flaccid, Nikolsky +, mucosa involved. Pemphigoid: subepidermal, tense, Nikolsky −, usually no oral lesions.</p>","w":["Antibodies to collagen (type XVII in pemphigoid, type VII in epidermolysis bullosa acquisita) produce SUBepidermal blisters.","Connexins form gap junctions; they aren't the pemphigus target.","Correct — pemphigus antibodies target desmoglein in desmosomes.","Occludin is a tight junction protein.","Integrins/hemidesmosome components are targets in subepidermal disease (bullous pemphigoid)."]},"q29":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> A microvillus is held up by a core bundle of actin filaments (cross-linked by villin and fimbrin) anchored in the terminal web.</p><p>Each microvillus contains 20–30 parallel actin filaments with their plus ends at the tip. Villin and fimbrin bundle them, and myosin I connects them laterally to the membrane. At the base, the bundle roots into the terminal web (spectrin, myosin II, intermediate filaments). The brush border greatly increases absorptive surface and carries digestive enzymes. Congenital microvillus inclusion disease (MYO5B mutations) causes intractable newborn diarrhea because the brush border can't be formed or maintained — the structural core that fails is actin.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Dynein</b> — Dynein is a microtubule motor; microvilli contain no microtubules.</li><li><b>Kinesin</b> — Kinesin is also a microtubule motor.</li><li><b>Myosin</b> — Myosin I links the actin core to the membrane, but the core itself is actin.</li><li><b>Spectrin</b> — Spectrin is part of the terminal web below, not the microvillus core.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Microvilli = actin core; cilia = microtubule (9+2) axoneme; stereocilia = long actin-based microvilli.</p>","w":["Dynein is a microtubule motor; microvilli contain no microtubules.","Kinesin is also a microtubule motor.","Correct — microvilli are supported by a core of actin filaments.","Myosin I links the actin core to the membrane, but the core itself is actin.","Spectrin is part of the terminal web below, not the microvillus core."]},"q31":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Laminin in the basal lamina binds integrins on the basal cell surface, anchoring the epithelium.</p><p>The basal lamina (lamina lucida + lamina densa) is made by the epithelium and contains laminin, type IV collagen, nidogen (entactin) and perlecan (a heparan sulfate proteoglycan). Laminin is a cross-shaped glycoprotein whose arms bind cell-surface integrins and dystroglycan on one side and type IV collagen and nidogen on the other, so it's the main link from cell to basal lamina. Below, the lamina reticularis (type III collagen) and anchoring fibrils (type VII) attach the whole basement membrane to the connective tissue.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Glycosaminoglycans</b> — GAGs form the hydrated ground substance; they aren't the adhesion molecule.</li><li><b>Glycoprotein</b> — Laminin is a glycoprotein, but \"glycoprotein\" is too general; the specific adhesive molecule is laminin.</li><li><b>Collagen type 1</b> — Type I collagen is in the underlying connective tissue; the basal lamina contains type IV.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Basal lamina components: laminin, type IV collagen, nidogen, perlecan.</p>","w":["Correct — laminin links basal cell integrins to the basal lamina.","GAGs form the hydrated ground substance; they aren't the adhesion molecule.","Laminin is a glycoprotein, but \"glycoprotein\" is too general; the specific adhesive molecule is laminin.","Type I collagen is in the underlying connective tissue; the basal lamina contains type IV."]},"q33":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Type II collagen is the collagen of hyaline (articular) cartilage; its appearance in synovial fluid means cartilage is being eroded.</p><p>In rheumatoid arthritis, inflamed synovium forms a pannus that grows over and erodes articular cartilage and subchondral bone, releasing matrix fragments into the joint fluid. Because each tissue has a characteristic collagen, the type found identifies what is being destroyed: type II = articular cartilage; type I = bone, tendon, capsule; type III/IV = vessels and basement membranes. Type II collagen fragments (and markers like CTX-II) are used as indicators of cartilage breakdown.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Vascular endothelium</b> — Endothelium rests on a basal lamina of type IV collagen.</li><li><b>Compact bone</b> — Compact bone is made mainly of type I collagen.</li><li><b>Vascular smooth muscle</b> — Vascular smooth muscle is surrounded mainly by type III (reticular) and IV/V collagen.</li><li><b>Synovial membrane</b> — The synovial membrane contains types I and III collagen.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cartilage (type II) erosion → joint space narrowing on X-ray.</p>","w":["Endothelium rests on a basal lamina of type IV collagen.","Compact bone is made mainly of type I collagen.","Vascular smooth muscle is surrounded mainly by type III (reticular) and IV/V collagen.","Correct — type II collagen in synovial fluid indicates articular cartilage erosion.","The synovial membrane contains types I and III collagen."]},"q34":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Eosinophils are granulocytes made in bone marrow from myeloid stem cells; the other choices are resident cells derived from mesenchyme.</p><p>Connexive tissue cells fall into two groups. Resident (fixed) cells — fibroblasts, adipocytes, pericytes, osteoblasts, chondroblasts — arise from mesenchymal stem cells. Transient (wandering) cells — eosinophils, neutrophils, basophils, monocytes/macrophages, lymphocytes, plasma cells — arise from hematopoietic stem cells and enter the tissue from blood; granulocytes and monocytes come from the myeloid line. Eosinophils (bilobed nucleus, orange granules with major basic protein) increase in parasitic infection and allergy.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Pericytes</b> — Pericytes arise from mesenchymal cells surrounding capillaries.</li><li><b>Fibroblasts</b> — Fibroblasts arise from mesenchymal cells.</li><li><b>Osteoblasts</b> — Osteoblasts arise from mesenchymal osteoprogenitor cells.</li><li><b>Adipocytes</b> — Adipocytes arise from mesenchymal cells.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Mast cells and macrophages are also hematopoietic in origin, even though they live in connective tissue.</p>","w":["Pericytes arise from mesenchymal cells surrounding capillaries.","Correct — eosinophils arise from myeloid stem cells.","Fibroblasts arise from mesenchymal cells.","Osteoblasts arise from mesenchymal osteoprogenitor cells.","Adipocytes arise from mesenchymal cells."]},"q36":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The periosteum has an outer fibrous layer and an inner osteogenic layer containing osteoprogenitor cells.</p><p>The outer fibrous layer is dense irregular connective tissue rich in blood vessels and nerves, anchored to bone by Sharpey (perforating) fibers and continuous with tendons and ligaments. The inner (cambium) layer contains osteoprogenitor cells that become osteoblasts, providing appositional growth in width and fracture repair. Periosteum is highly vascular and very pain-sensitive — the reason bone fractures and periosteal stretching hurt.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It is devoid of a blood supply.</b> — The periosteum is well supplied with blood vessels and nerves.</li><li><b>It produces osteoclasts.</b> — Osteoclasts come from blood monocytes, not the periosteum.</li><li><b>It is responsible for interstitial bone growth.</b> — Bone can't grow interstitially; periosteum supports appositional growth.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Periosteal elevation (Codman triangle, \"onion skin\") on X-ray signals tumor or infection lifting the periosteum.</p>","w":["The periosteum is well supplied with blood vessels and nerves.","Osteoclasts come from blood monocytes, not the periosteum.","Bone can't grow interstitially; periosteum supports appositional growth.","Correct — the inner layer of the periosteum contains osteoprogenitor cells."]},"q37":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Unmineralized osteoid covering bone surfaces + low vitamin D, calcium and phosphate + high alkaline phosphatase = osteomalacia.</p><p>Osteomalacia (rickets in children) is failure to mineralize newly formed osteoid. Here chronic diarrhea indicates malabsorption of vitamin D (fat-soluble) and calcium; low 1,25-(OH)₂D lowers gut absorption of calcium and phosphate, secondary hyperparathyroidism further wastes phosphate, and there isn't enough mineral to calcify osteoid. Osteoblasts keep working (high alkaline phosphatase) but their osteoid stays soft, so bones bend and develop painful pseudofractures (Looser zones). The key to diagnosis is the NON-decalcified biopsy, which shows wide osteoid seams; decalcification would hide the difference.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Osteoporosis</b> — Osteoporosis is reduced bone mass with NORMAL mineralization and normal serum Ca, PO₄, vitamin D and ALP.</li><li><b>Scurvy</b> — Scurvy impairs collagen hydroxylation (poor osteoid formation), not mineralization of existing osteoid.</li><li><b>Paget disease</b> — Paget disease shows chaotic remodeling (mosaic bone) with high ALP but normal Ca, PO₄ and vitamin D.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Osteomalacia: low Ca, low PO₄, high ALP, high PTH. Osteoporosis: all normal.</p>","w":["Correct — unmineralized osteoid with low vitamin D/Ca/PO₄ and high ALP indicates osteomalacia.","Osteoporosis is reduced bone mass with NORMAL mineralization and normal serum Ca, PO₄, vitamin D and ALP.","Scurvy impairs collagen hydroxylation (poor osteoid formation), not mineralization of existing osteoid.","Paget disease shows chaotic remodeling (mosaic bone) with high ALP but normal Ca, PO₄ and vitamin D."]},"q38":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Bone mineral is calcium hydroxyapatite; inadequate dietary calcium (or vitamin D) gives weak, poorly mineralized bones.</p><p>About two-thirds of bone's dry weight is inorganic, mainly hydroxyapatite [Ca₁₀(PO₄)₆(OH)₂], which gives hardness and resistance to compression; type I collagen gives tensile strength. Growing children need plenty of calcium to mineralize new bone; deficiency (or vitamin D deficiency, which impairs calcium absorption) leaves bone undermineralized (rickets-like changes) and prone to fracture with minor falls. Dietary history is therefore key in a child with a low-energy fracture.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Potassium</b> — Potassium isn't a structural component of bone mineral.</li><li><b>Iron</b> — Iron deficiency causes anemia, not weak bones.</li><li><b>Carbohydrates</b> — Carbohydrates supply energy; they don't mineralize bone.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Bone: ~65% inorganic (hydroxyapatite), ~35% organic (mostly type I collagen).</p>","w":["Potassium isn't a structural component of bone mineral.","Correct — calcium deficiency weakens bone mineralization in a growing child.","Iron deficiency causes anemia, not weak bones.","Carbohydrates supply energy; they don't mineralize bone."]},"q39":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Gap junctions are connexon channels that let ions and small molecules pass directly between adjacent cells.</p><p>Each connexon is a ring of six connexins; connexons from two cells align end-to-end, creating a channel ~1.5–2 nm wide spanning a narrow 2–3 nm gap. Ions, second messengers (cAMP, IP₃, Ca²⁺), and small metabolites (&lt;1 kDa) pass through, giving electrical and metabolic coupling. Channels close when intracellular Ca²⁺ rises or pH falls, isolating damaged cells. They're abundant in cardiac muscle (intercalated discs), smooth muscle, osteocytes, and some neurons.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It extends as a zone around the apical perimeter of adjacent cells.</b> — An apical belt describes the zonula occludens (tight junction) or zonula adherens.</li><li><b>It possesses dense plaques composed in part of desmoplakins.</b> — Dense plaques with desmoplakins are desmosome features.</li><li><b>Its adhesion is dependent upon calcium ions.</b> — Calcium-dependent adhesion is the property of cadherins in adherens junctions and desmosomes.</li><li><b>It possesses transmembrane linker glycoproteins.</b> — Transmembrane linker glycoproteins (cadherins) belong to adherens junctions and desmosomes.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Junction checklist: tight = seal (claudin/occludin); adherens = actin (E-cadherin); desmosome = keratin (desmoglein); gap = communication (connexin); hemidesmosome = basal lamina (integrin).</p>","w":["An apical belt describes the zonula occludens (tight junction) or zonula adherens.","Dense plaques with desmoplakins are desmosome features.","Correct — gap junctions permit ion passage between adjacent cells.","Calcium-dependent adhesion is the property of cadherins in adherens junctions and desmosomes.","Transmembrane linker glycoproteins (cadherins) belong to adherens junctions and desmosomes."]},"q40":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Anaphylaxis is a type I hypersensitivity reaction triggered by IgE-sensitized mast cells (and basophils) degranulating.</p><p>On first exposure, allergen-specific Th2 help drives plasma cells to make IgE, which binds FcεRI on mast cells. On re-exposure, the allergen cross-links adjacent IgE molecules, triggering degranulation within minutes: preformed histamine and tryptase plus newly made leukotrienes, prostaglandins and platelet-activating factor. Systemically these cause vasodilation and capillary leak (hypotension, shock), bronchospasm and laryngeal edema. Treatment is intramuscular epinephrine. Mast cells are identified by metachromatic granules (purple with toluidine blue) and tryptase.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Fibroblasts</b> — Fibroblasts make fibers and ground substance; they don't mediate allergy.</li><li><b>Eosinophils</b> — Eosinophils are recruited later (late-phase reaction) and help limit it; they don't trigger anaphylaxis.</li><li><b>Pericytes</b> — Pericytes are contractile cells around capillaries.</li><li><b>Macrophages</b> — Macrophages phagocytose and present antigen; they aren't the trigger of type I hypersensitivity.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Serum tryptase is raised after anaphylaxis (marker of mast cell degranulation).</p>","w":["Fibroblasts make fibers and ground substance; they don't mediate allergy.","Eosinophils are recruited later (late-phase reaction) and help limit it; they don't trigger anaphylaxis.","Pericytes are contractile cells around capillaries.","Correct — IgE-sensitized mast cells degranulate to cause anaphylaxis.","Macrophages phagocytose and present antigen; they aren't the trigger of type I hypersensitivity."]},"q41":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Immotile cilia are classically caused by defective axonemal dynein arms (primary ciliary dyskinesia).</p><p>The modified structures on the apical surface of a respiratory epithelium are cilia, each with a 9+2 axoneme rooted in a basal body. Dynein arms on the doublets hydrolyze ATP and slide neighboring doublets, which nexin links convert into a whip-like beat that moves mucus upward (mucociliary clearance). With immotile cilia, mucus stagnates, causing chronic sinusitis, bronchitis and bronchiectasis; sperm flagella share the defect, causing infertility, and embryonic nodal cilia failure gives situs inversus in about half (Kartagener syndrome).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Defect in kinesin</b> — Kinesin is a cytoplasmic transport motor, not the ciliary motor.</li><li><b>Defect in titin</b> — Titin is a muscle sarcomere protein.</li><li><b>Defect in nexin</b> — Nexin links doublets but isn't the classic cause of immotile cilia.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cilia: 9+2 axoneme, basal body with 9 triplets; immotile cilia → think dynein.</p>","w":["Correct — defective dynein is the cause of immotile cilia.","Kinesin is a cytoplasmic transport motor, not the ciliary motor.","Titin is a muscle sarcomere protein.","Nexin links doublets but isn't the classic cause of immotile cilia."]},"q42":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Haversian canals run along the center of each osteon; Volkmann (perforating) canals run across and connect them to each other and to the surfaces.</p><p>Compact bone is built of osteons: concentric lamellae of type I collagen and hydroxyapatite around a central Haversian canal that carries vessels and nerves. Osteocytes in lacunae between lamellae connect through canaliculi to the canal. Volkmann canals pierce obliquely or transversely, linking Haversian canals together and to the periosteal and endosteal blood supply. Between osteons lie interstitial lamellae (remnants of old osteons), and circumferential lamellae line the outer and inner surfaces.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Bone matrix contains primarily type II collagen.</b> — Bone matrix is mainly type I collagen; type II is in cartilage.</li><li><b>About 65% of the dry weight of bone is organic.</b> — About 65% of bone's dry weight is INorganic (hydroxyapatite); ~35% is organic.</li><li><b>Bone growth occurs via interstitial growth only.</b> — Bone grows only appositionally, because its rigid mineralized matrix can't expand from within.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Haversian = longitudinal (center of osteon); Volkmann = transverse (connectors).</p>","w":["Bone matrix is mainly type I collagen; type II is in cartilage.","About 65% of bone's dry weight is INorganic (hydroxyapatite); ~35% is organic.","Correct — Haversian canals are interconnected by Volkmann canals.","Bone grows only appositionally, because its rigid mineralized matrix can't expand from within."]},"q43":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> During contraction the sarcomere shortens (Z lines move together); the filaments themselves don't change length.</p><p>Contraction follows the cross-bridge cycle: Ca²⁺ binds troponin C, tropomyosin shifts, myosin heads bind actin, release ADP + Pi in the power stroke that pulls actin toward the M line, then bind ATP to detach and re-cock. Because both thick and thin filaments keep their lengths, the zones that shrink are those defined by overlap: the I band and H zone narrow, Z lines approach, and each sarcomere shortens. The A band and M line stay the same.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>A band</b> — The A band (thick filament length) stays constant.</li><li><b>M line</b> — The M line is a line in the center of the A band; it doesn't shorten.</li><li><b>Myosin</b> — Myosin filaments keep their length.</li><li><b>Actin</b> — Actin filaments keep their length; they slide.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Rigor mortis: no ATP → myosin can't detach from actin.</p>","w":["The A band (thick filament length) stays constant.","Correct — the sarcomere (Z line to Z line) shortens during contraction.","The M line is a line in the center of the A band; it doesn't shorten.","Myosin filaments keep their length.","Actin filaments keep their length; they slide."]},"q44":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The skeletal muscle thin filament = F-actin + tropomyosin + the troponin complex (TnT, TnI, TnC).</p><p>At rest, tropomyosin lies along the actin groove covering myosin-binding sites, held in place by troponin: TnT binds tropomyosin, TnI inhibits actin–myosin interaction, TnC binds calcium. When Ca²⁺ released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum binds TnC, the complex changes shape, tropomyosin rolls away, and myosin heads can bind actin. Cardiac troponins (cTnI, cTnT) are released when heart muscle is damaged and are the key lab test for myocardial infarction.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Calmodulin</b> — Calmodulin is the Ca²⁺-binding regulator in smooth muscle, which lacks troponin.</li><li><b>Myosin</b> — Myosin forms the thick filament.</li><li><b>Calcium</b> — Calcium is a regulatory ion, not a filament protein.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Troponin = skeletal and cardiac muscle; calmodulin–MLCK = smooth muscle.</p>","w":["Correct — troponin is the third protein of the skeletal muscle thin filament.","Calmodulin is the Ca²⁺-binding regulator in smooth muscle, which lacks troponin.","Myosin forms the thick filament.","Calcium is a regulatory ion, not a filament protein."]},"q45":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> In smooth muscle, calmodulin is the Ca²⁺ sensor (the role troponin C plays in skeletal muscle); Ca²⁺–calmodulin activates myosin light chain kinase.</p><p>Smooth muscle regulation is thick-filament (myosin) based. When cytosolic Ca²⁺ rises (from extracellular entry and SR release), 4 Ca²⁺ bind calmodulin, and the complex activates MLCK, which phosphorylates the regulatory light chain of myosin II. Phosphorylated myosin can cycle with actin and contract. Myosin light chain phosphatase reverses this, producing relaxation. The latch state lets smooth muscle hold tension with little ATP use.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Tropomyosin</b> — Tropomyosin is present but doesn't bind Ca²⁺.</li><li><b>Myosin light chain kinase</b> — MLCK is the enzyme activated by Ca²⁺–calmodulin — the next step, not the sensor.</li><li><b>Troponin I</b> — Troponin I is part of skeletal/cardiac troponin; smooth muscle has none.</li><li><b>Caldesmon</b> — Caldesmon is an actin-binding modulator, not the Ca²⁺ sensor.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Skeletal: Ca²⁺ → troponin C (actin-based regulation). Smooth: Ca²⁺ → calmodulin → MLCK (myosin-based regulation).</p>","w":["Correct — calmodulin plays the calcium-sensing role of troponin C in smooth muscle.","Tropomyosin is present but doesn't bind Ca²⁺.","MLCK is the enzyme activated by Ca²⁺–calmodulin — the next step, not the sensor.","Troponin I is part of skeletal/cardiac troponin; smooth muscle has none.","Caldesmon is an actin-binding modulator, not the Ca²⁺ sensor."]},"q46":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Actin is a myofilament — the thin filament of the sarcomere.</p><p>Muscle organization from large to small: muscle → fascicle → muscle fiber (one multinucleated cell) → myofibrils (long chains of sarcomeres filling the cell) → myofilaments (actin thin filaments and myosin thick filaments). Actin filaments are double-helical polymers of G-actin with tropomyosin and troponin; myosin filaments are bundles of myosin II molecules with heads projecting toward the thin filaments.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Myosin</b> — Myosin is the other (thick) myofilament, a different protein.</li><li><b>Muscle fibers</b> — A muscle fiber is a whole muscle cell.</li><li><b>Myofibrils</b> — A myofibril is a bundle of myofilaments inside the cell.</li><li><b>Myocardium</b> — Myocardium is the muscle layer of the heart.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Fiber (cell) &gt; myofibril &gt; myofilament (actin, myosin).</p>","w":["Correct — actin is a myofilament (the thin filament).","Myosin is the other (thick) myofilament, a different protein.","A muscle fiber is a whole muscle cell.","A myofibril is a bundle of myofilaments inside the cell.","Myocardium is the muscle layer of the heart."]},"q47":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Fast-twitch muscle is built from white (type II) fibers: large, glycolytic, low myoglobin, few mitochondria, fast but fatigable.</p><p>Fiber types differ in myosin ATPase speed and metabolism. Type I (red, slow-oxidative) fibers are small, packed with mitochondria and myoglobin, surrounded by many capillaries, and resist fatigue — postural muscles like soleus. Type IIb/IIx (white, fast-glycolytic) fibers are large, store glycogen, rely on anaerobic glycolysis, contract quickly and powerfully but tire fast — sprinting and jumping. Type IIa (intermediate) fibers are fast but oxidative. The neuron determines the fiber type: cross-innervation can convert one type to another.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Red fibers</b> — Red (type I) fibers make up slow-twitch muscle.</li><li><b>Intermediate fibers</b> — Intermediate (type IIa) fibers have mixed properties and aren't the defining fast-twitch white fiber.</li><li><b>All of the above</b> — Red and intermediate fibers aren't the fast-twitch type, so not \"all.\"</li><li><b>None of the above</b> — White fibers do fit, so \"none\" is wrong.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> \"One slow red ox\": type I = slow, red, oxidative. Dark meat (chicken legs) = red; white meat (breast) = white.</p>","w":["Red (type I) fibers make up slow-twitch muscle.","Correct — white (type II) fibers make up fast-twitch muscle.","Intermediate (type IIa) fibers have mixed properties and aren't the defining fast-twitch white fiber.","Red and intermediate fibers aren't the fast-twitch type, so not \"all.\"","White fibers do fit, so \"none\" is wrong."]},"q48":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The dark A band is bisected by the paler H band (zone), which in turn is bisected by the M line.</p><p>In the A band, the region where actin and myosin overlap is darkest; the central region containing only thick filaments is lighter and is called the H band (H zone, from German \"hell,\" bright). The M line, made of myomesin and creatine kinase cross-linking the thick filaments, runs through the middle of the H band. The light I band contains only thin filaments and is bisected by the Z line. During contraction the H band and I band shorten while the A band stays constant.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>A band</b> — The A band is the dark band itself, not the line bisecting it.</li><li><b>I band</b> — The I band is the light band of thin filaments only.</li><li><b>Z line</b> — The Z line bisects the I band.</li><li><b>M line</b> — The M line runs through the center of the H band (some sources call it the line bisecting the A band; this question's key uses the H band).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Z bisects I; H (and its M line) bisects A.</p>","w":["The A band is the dark band itself, not the line bisecting it.","The I band is the light band of thin filaments only.","The Z line bisects the I band.","Correct — the H band divides the A band into two halves.","The M line runs through the center of the H band (some sources call it the line bisecting the A band; this question's key uses the H band)."]},"q49":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Smooth muscle cells are spindle-shaped (fusiform) with one central nucleus and no striations.</p><p>Smooth muscle cells are tapered at both ends and pack tightly in sheets, their thick middles nestled against neighbors' thin ends. Actin and myosin are arranged in a lattice anchored to dense bodies (α-actinin, the Z-line equivalent) rather than in sarcomeres, which is why there are no striations. When they contract, the nucleus may look corkscrew-shaped. They're found in vessel walls, gut, airways, uterus, bladder and the iris.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Skeletal muscle</b> — Skeletal muscle cells are long cylinders with many peripheral nuclei and striations.</li><li><b>Cardiac muscle</b> — Cardiac muscle cells are branched with one or two central nuclei, striations and intercalated discs.</li><li><b>Both A and B</b> — Neither skeletal nor cardiac cells are spindle-shaped.</li><li><b>A, B and C</b> — Only smooth muscle is spindle-shaped.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Striated + peripheral nuclei = skeletal; striated + central nucleus + intercalated discs = cardiac; non-striated spindle = smooth.</p>","w":["Skeletal muscle cells are long cylinders with many peripheral nuclei and striations.","Cardiac muscle cells are branched with one or two central nuclei, striations and intercalated discs.","Correct — smooth muscle is composed of spindle-shaped cells.","Neither skeletal nor cardiac cells are spindle-shaped.","Only smooth muscle is spindle-shaped."]},"q50":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> A motor unit = one motor neuron + all the muscle fibers it innervates.</p><p>When a motor neuron fires, all its fibers contract together (all-or-none at the unit level). Muscles needing fine control have small motor units (a few fibers each, e.g., extraocular and hand muscles); muscles needing power have large units (hundreds to thousands of fibers, e.g., gastrocnemius). Force is graded by recruitment — small units first, then larger ones (size principle) — and by firing rate. In denervation and reinnervation, surviving neurons sprout to adopt orphaned fibers, creating giant motor units seen on EMG.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Motor neuron</b> — A motor neuron is only the nerve cell, without its muscle fibers.</li><li><b>Motor end plate</b> — The motor end plate is the junction where nerve meets muscle.</li><li><b>Neuromuscular spindle</b> — A neuromuscular spindle is a stretch receptor (intrafusal fibers).</li><li><b>Neurotransmitter</b> — A neurotransmitter is the chemical messenger (acetylcholine) at the junction.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Size principle: small motor units recruited first.</p>","w":["Correct — a single neuron plus the muscle fibers it innervates is a motor unit.","A motor neuron is only the nerve cell, without its muscle fibers.","The motor end plate is the junction where nerve meets muscle.","A neuromuscular spindle is a stretch receptor (intrafusal fibers).","A neurotransmitter is the chemical messenger (acetylcholine) at the junction."]},"q51":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Thick filaments are made of myosin II; thin filaments are actin + tropomyosin + troponin.</p><p>Each myosin II molecule has two heavy chains (long tail + globular head) and two pairs of light chains. About 300 molecules bundle tail-to-tail into a bipolar thick filament with heads projecting outward from both ends and a bare zone in the middle (at the M line). The heads contain the actin-binding site and ATPase that form cross-bridges and generate force. Titin anchors each thick filament to the Z discs and keeps it centered.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Myofilaments</b> — Myofilament is the general term for both actin and myosin filaments, not the specific protein.</li><li><b>Muscle fibers</b> — A muscle fiber is the whole cell.</li><li><b>Myofibrils</b> — A myofibril is a bundle of myofilaments.</li><li><b>Myocardium</b> — Myocardium is heart muscle tissue.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Myosin head = actin-binding site + ATPase; the bare zone of the thick filament lies in the H zone.</p>","w":["Myofilament is the general term for both actin and myosin filaments, not the specific protein.","Correct — thick filaments are composed of myosin.","A muscle fiber is the whole cell.","A myofibril is a bundle of myofilaments.","Myocardium is heart muscle tissue."]},"q52":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Slow-twitch muscle is made of red (type I) fibers: oxidative, myoglobin-rich, fatigue-resistant.</p><p>Type I fibers rely on aerobic metabolism with many mitochondria and capillaries; myoglobin (an O₂-storing heme protein) gives them their red color. They contract slowly with modest force but can keep going for long periods, ideal for posture (back muscles, soleus) and endurance. Endurance training increases mitochondria and capillaries; it doesn't usually convert fiber types outright.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>White fibers</b> — White fibers are fast-twitch.</li><li><b>Intermediate fibers</b> — Intermediate fibers aren't the defining slow-twitch type.</li><li><b>All of the above</b> — White and intermediate fibers aren't slow-twitch.</li><li><b>None of the above</b> — Red fibers fit, so \"none\" is wrong.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Type I = slow, red, oxidative, fatigue-resistant; type II = fast, white, glycolytic.</p>","w":["Correct — red (type I) fibers make up slow-twitch muscle.","White fibers are fast-twitch.","Intermediate fibers aren't the defining slow-twitch type.","White and intermediate fibers aren't slow-twitch.","Red fibers fit, so \"none\" is wrong."]},"q53":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Intercalated discs are unique to cardiac muscle, joining cells with fascia adherens, desmosomes and gap junctions.</p><p>Cardiac myocytes are branched cells joined end-to-end at intercalated discs, which look like dark step-shaped lines on light microscopy. The transverse portion carries fascia adherens (anchoring actin of the terminal sarcomeres) and desmosomes (anchoring desmin intermediate filaments), holding cells together against the force of contraction. The lateral portion carries gap junctions (connexin-43), letting depolarization spread quickly so the myocardium contracts as a functional syncytium.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Skeletal muscle</b> — Skeletal muscle fibers are single long multinucleated cells without intercalated discs.</li><li><b>Smooth muscle</b> — Smooth muscle cells connect through gap junctions but don't have intercalated discs.</li><li><b>Both A and B</b> — Skeletal muscle doesn't have them.</li><li><b>A, B and C</b> — Only cardiac muscle has them.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Intercalated disc = fascia adherens + desmosome (transverse) + gap junction (lateral).</p>","w":["Skeletal muscle fibers are single long multinucleated cells without intercalated discs.","Correct — intercalated discs are found only in cardiac muscle.","Smooth muscle cells connect through gap junctions but don't have intercalated discs.","Skeletal muscle doesn't have them.","Only cardiac muscle has them."]},"q54":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Epimysium wraps the whole muscle; perimysium wraps fascicles; endomysium wraps each fiber.</p><p>These connective tissue sheaths are continuous with each other and with the tendon, transmitting force from the fibers to bone. Epimysium is dense irregular connective tissue around the entire muscle. Perimysium divides the muscle into fascicles and carries the larger vessels and nerves. Endomysium is a delicate layer of reticular fibers (type III collagen) and basal lamina around each individual muscle fiber, containing capillaries and nerve branches. The satellite (stem) cells sit between the sarcolemma and the basal lamina.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Sarcoplasm</b> — Sarcoplasm is the cytoplasm of a muscle cell.</li><li><b>Perimysium</b> — Perimysium surrounds fascicles.</li><li><b>Sarcolemma</b> — Sarcolemma is the plasma membrane of a muscle cell.</li><li><b>Endomysium</b> — Endomysium surrounds individual muscle fibers.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Epi- (upon) = whole muscle; peri- (around) = fascicle; endo- (within) = fiber.</p>","w":["Correct — the epimysium is the outer connective tissue covering of a muscle.","Sarcoplasm is the cytoplasm of a muscle cell.","Perimysium surrounds fascicles.","Sarcolemma is the plasma membrane of a muscle cell.","Endomysium surrounds individual muscle fibers."]},"q55":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Keratohyalin granules (profilaggrin, loricrin) define the stratum granulosum.</p><p>As keratinocytes move up from the stratum spinosum, they enter the stratum granulosum, where they accumulate dark basophilic keratohyalin granules. Profilaggrin is processed to filaggrin, which aggregates keratin filaments into dense bundles. Lamellar (Odland) granules release lipids into the extracellular space, forming the skin's water barrier. Cells then lose their nuclei and organelles and become the flattened, dead squames of the stratum corneum. Loss-of-function filaggrin mutations are linked to ichthyosis vulgaris and atopic dermatitis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Stratum basale</b> — The stratum basale is the single layer of dividing stem cells.</li><li><b>Stratum spinosum</b> — The stratum spinosum has desmosome \"spines\" and keratin bundles but no keratohyalin granules.</li><li><b>Stratum lucidum</b> — The stratum lucidum is a clear layer (thick skin only) with eleidin, no granules.</li><li><b>Stratum corneum</b> — The stratum corneum consists of dead, flattened keratinized cells.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Epidermis from surface to base: \"Come, Let's Get Sun Burned\" — Corneum, Lucidum, Granulosum, Spinosum, Basale.</p>","w":["The stratum basale is the single layer of dividing stem cells.","The stratum spinosum has desmosome \"spines\" and keratin bundles but no keratohyalin granules.","Correct — keratohyalin granules are found in the stratum granulosum.","The stratum lucidum is a clear layer (thick skin only) with eleidin, no granules.","The stratum corneum consists of dead, flattened keratinized cells."]},"q56":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Langerhans cells are the bone marrow–derived antigen-presenting (macrophage-lineage) cells of the epidermis.</p><p>Tissue-specific macrophages of the mononuclear phagocyte system: Kupffer cells (liver), alveolar \"dust cells\" (lung), microglia (CNS), osteoclasts (bone), histiocytes (connective tissue), and Langerhans cells (epidermis). Langerhans cells capture antigen in the skin and migrate to lymph nodes to present it to T cells; they contain Birbeck granules and express CD1a and langerin. They're key in contact dermatitis (type IV hypersensitivity).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Kupffer cells</b> — Kupffer cells are the macrophages of the liver sinusoids.</li><li><b>Histiocyte</b> — Histiocyte is a general term for connective tissue macrophages.</li><li><b>Dust cell</b> — Dust cells are alveolar macrophages in the lung.</li><li><b>Microglia</b> — Microglia are the macrophages of the central nervous system.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Tissue macrophages: Kupffer (liver), dust cells (lung), microglia (CNS), osteoclasts (bone), Langerhans (skin).</p>","w":["Kupffer cells are the macrophages of the liver sinusoids.","Histiocyte is a general term for connective tissue macrophages.","Dust cells are alveolar macrophages in the lung.","Correct — Langerhans cells are the macrophage-type antigen-presenting cells of the skin.","Microglia are the macrophages of the central nervous system."]},"q57":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The lunula is the white crescent at the base of the nail — the visible distal part of the nail matrix.</p><p>The nail plate is hard keratin produced by the nail matrix, most of which lies hidden under the proximal nail fold. The matrix epithelium is thick, so it masks the pink color of the underlying capillaries, making its visible edge white (the lunula). Distal to the lunula, the nail plate sits on the thin nail bed, which lets the vessels show through as pink. The eponychium (cuticle) covers the junction between the proximal fold and the plate.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Eponychium</b> — The eponychium is the cuticle — the skin fold over the nail root.</li><li><b>Matrix</b> — The matrix is the growing region; only its visible tip forms the lunula.</li><li><b>Nail bed</b> — The nail bed is the epithelium under the plate; it looks pink.</li><li><b>Root</b> — The root is the proximal part of the nail hidden under the skin fold.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Damage to the nail matrix causes permanent nail deformity; damage to the bed usually doesn't.</p>","w":["Correct — the white half-moon at the base of the nail is the lunula.","The eponychium is the cuticle — the skin fold over the nail root.","The matrix is the growing region; only its visible tip forms the lunula.","The nail bed is the epithelium under the plate; it looks pink.","The root is the proximal part of the nail hidden under the skin fold."]},"q58":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Free nerve endings are the most abundant skin receptors, sensing pain, temperature, itch and crude touch.</p><p>Free nerve endings are unencapsulated terminals of thinly myelinated Aδ and unmyelinated C fibers that penetrate even into the epidermis. Encapsulated receptors are fewer and more specialized: Meissner corpuscles (light touch, dermal papillae of glabrous skin), Pacinian corpuscles (vibration and deep pressure, onion-like layers in the deep dermis/hypodermis), Ruffini endings (sustained stretch), and Merkel cell–neurite complexes (sustained light touch, shape/texture). Krause end bulbs occur in mucocutaneous regions.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Ruffini's corpuscles</b> — Ruffini corpuscles sense sustained stretch in the dermis.</li><li><b>Pacinian corpuscles</b> — Pacinian corpuscles sense vibration and deep pressure.</li><li><b>Krause's end bulbs</b> — Krause end bulbs lie mainly in mucocutaneous areas.</li><li><b>Meissner's corpuscles</b> — Meissner corpuscles sense light touch in the dermal papillae of hairless skin.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Pacinian = onion-like, vibration; Meissner = dermal papillae, light touch (fingertips).</p>","w":["Correct — free nerve endings are the most numerous sensory receptors in skin.","Ruffini corpuscles sense sustained stretch in the dermis.","Pacinian corpuscles sense vibration and deep pressure.","Krause end bulbs lie mainly in mucocutaneous areas.","Meissner corpuscles sense light touch in the dermal papillae of hairless skin."]},"q59":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The stratum lucidum (clear layer) is found only in thick skin of the palms and soles.</p><p>Thick skin has all five epidermal layers, with an especially thick stratum corneum and a stratum lucidum: a thin, clear, eosinophilic band of flattened anucleate cells containing eleidin, lying between the granulosum and corneum. Thin skin (most of the body) lacks a distinct stratum lucidum, has a thinner corneum, but has hair follicles and sebaceous glands, which thick skin lacks.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Stratum basale</b> — The stratum basale is present in all skin.</li><li><b>Stratum spinosum</b> — The stratum spinosum is present in all skin.</li><li><b>Stratum granulosum</b> — The stratum granulosum is present in all skin (thinner in thin skin).</li><li><b>Stratum corneum</b> — The stratum corneum is present in all skin.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Thick skin = palms and soles: stratum lucidum, no hair follicles, many sweat glands.</p>","w":["The stratum basale is present in all skin.","The stratum spinosum is present in all skin.","The stratum granulosum is present in all skin (thinner in thin skin).","Correct — the stratum lucidum is present only in thick skin.","The stratum corneum is present in all skin."]},"q60":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Huxley's layer is the middle layer of the inner root sheath of a hair follicle (between Henle's layer and the inner sheath cuticle).</p><p>The hair follicle has, from outside in: the connective tissue sheath, the glassy membrane (basement membrane), the outer root sheath (continuous with the epidermis), the inner root sheath (Henle's layer, Huxley's layer, cuticle of the inner root sheath), and the hair shaft (cuticle, cortex, medulla). The inner root sheath disintegrates at the level of the sebaceous gland duct. All the other options are layers or cells of the retina — a reminder to classify the answer by organ first.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Pigment epithelium</b> — Pigment epithelium is the outermost layer of the retina.</li><li><b>Müller cells</b> — Müller cells are the supporting glial cells of the retina.</li><li><b>Horizontal cells</b> — Horizontal cells are retinal interneurons in the outer plexiform layer.</li><li><b>Cones</b> — Cones are retinal photoreceptors for color vision.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Inner root sheath: Henle (outer), Huxley (middle), cuticle (inner) — \"HHC.\"</p>","w":["Pigment epithelium is the outermost layer of the retina.","Müller cells are the supporting glial cells of the retina.","Correct — Huxley's layer is part of the hair follicle's inner root sheath.","Horizontal cells are retinal interneurons in the outer plexiform layer.","Cones are retinal photoreceptors for color vision."]},"q61":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Osteoid is the newly secreted, unmineralized organic bone matrix (mostly type I collagen) made by osteoblasts.</p><p>Bone formation happens in two steps: osteoblasts secrete osteoid (type I collagen, osteocalcin, osteonectin, proteoglycans), then mineralization follows over days as hydroxyapatite crystals nucleate in gaps between collagen fibrils (helped by matrix vesicles and alkaline phosphatase). A thin osteoid seam normally lines forming surfaces; wide seams mean defective mineralization (osteomalacia/rickets).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Osteoclast</b> — An osteoclast is the bone-resorbing cell.</li><li><b>Osteon</b> — An osteon (Haversian system) is the structural unit of compact bone.</li><li><b>Osteocyte</b> — An osteocyte is a mature bone cell in a lacuna.</li><li><b>Osteoblast</b> — An osteoblast makes osteoid but isn't the matrix itself.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Wide osteoid seams on a non-decalcified biopsy = osteomalacia.</p>","w":["An osteoclast is the bone-resorbing cell.","An osteon (Haversian system) is the structural unit of compact bone.","An osteocyte is a mature bone cell in a lacuna.","An osteoblast makes osteoid but isn't the matrix itself.","Correct — osteoid is unmineralized bone matrix."]},"q62":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Skin makes vitamin D, prevents water loss, provides sensation and regulates temperature — all are true functions.</p><p>Ultraviolet B light converts 7-dehydrocholesterol in keratinocytes to previtamin D₃ (cholecalciferol), later hydroxylated in the liver and kidney. The stratum corneum and lipids from lamellar granules form a water-impermeable barrier (major fluid loss occurs in large burns). Receptors (free endings, Meissner, Pacinian, Merkel, Ruffini) provide touch, pain, temperature, vibration and pressure. Eccrine sweating and dermal blood flow adjustments regulate heat. The skin also provides immune defense (Langerhans cells), physical protection and UV protection (melanin).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Vitamin D production</b> — Skin does produce vitamin D with UV light.</li><li><b>Protection from water loss</b> — Skin does prevent water loss.</li><li><b>Sensory reception</b> — Skin does provide sensory reception.</li><li><b>Heat regulation</b> — Skin does regulate body heat.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Burns → loss of the water barrier and thermoregulation → fluid loss and hypothermia.</p>","w":["Skin does produce vitamin D with UV light.","Skin does prevent water loss.","Skin does provide sensory reception.","Skin does regulate body heat.","Correct — all of these are functions of skin."]},"q63":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The stratum basale (germinativum) contains the dividing stem cells that renew the epidermis.</p><p>The stratum basale is a single layer of cuboidal-to-columnar keratinocytes attached to the basement membrane by hemidesmosomes. Its stem cells divide; daughter cells move upward, differentiating through the spinosum, granulosum and corneum over about 4 weeks before being shed. Melanocytes and Merkel cells also sit in this layer. Basal cell carcinoma arises from these basal keratinocytes.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Stratum spinosum</b> — The stratum spinosum has only occasional mitoses in its deepest part.</li><li><b>Stratum granulosum</b> — Stratum granulosum cells are differentiating and no longer divide.</li><li><b>Stratum lucidum</b> — Stratum lucidum cells have lost their nuclei.</li><li><b>Stratum corneum</b> — Stratum corneum cells are dead.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Epidermal turnover ≈ 4 weeks; psoriasis speeds this up dramatically.</p>","w":["Correct — the stratum basale contains the dividing cells.","The stratum spinosum has only occasional mitoses in its deepest part.","Stratum granulosum cells are differentiating and no longer divide.","Stratum lucidum cells have lost their nuclei.","Stratum corneum cells are dead."]},"q64":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The zonula occludens (tight junction) — the most apical part of the junctional complex — seals the paracellular space with claudins and occludin.</p><p>In the junctional complex of intestinal epithelium, from apical to basal: tight junction (zonula occludens), adherens junction (zonula adherens), desmosome (macula adherens). At the tight junction, strands of claudins and occludin fuse the outer membrane leaflets so solutes can't pass between cells; it also separates apical and basolateral membrane proteins (cell polarity). Leaky vs. tight epithelia differ mainly in claudin type. Some bacterial toxins (e.g., Clostridium perfringens enterotoxin) target claudins.</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Tight junction = barrier + fence; it's the most apical junction.</p>","w":["A — microvilli on the apical surface.","Correct — B is the tight junction (zonula occludens), which forms the seal.","C — zonula adherens, a belt of E-cadherin linked to actin; adhesion, not a seal.","D — desmosome (macula adherens), a spot weld anchoring keratin filaments.","E — the terminal web, the actin network the microvilli root into."]},"q65":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The desmosome (macula adherens) anchors keratin intermediate filaments via desmoglein/desmocollin and dense plaques (desmoplakin, plakoglobin).</p><p>Desmosomes are spot welds between cells. Their cadherins (desmogleins and desmocollins) bind across the intercellular space, and inside each cell dense plaques of plakoglobin and desmoplakin tether keratin (or desmin in cardiac muscle) intermediate filaments, distributing mechanical stress across the tissue. Pemphigus vulgaris antibodies target desmoglein. By contrast, the zonula adherens links E-cadherin to actin through catenins.</p><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Desmosome → intermediate filaments; adherens junction → actin.</p>","w":["Microvilli contain actin cores, not intermediate filaments.","The tight junction is linked to actin and seals the space.","The zonula adherens links E-cadherin to actin, not intermediate filaments.","Correct — D is the desmosome, which anchors keratin intermediate filaments.","The terminal web is mostly actin (with some intermediate filaments), and it isn't a junction."]},"q66":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Apical to basal: zonula occludens → zonula adherens → macula adherens (desmosome).</p><p>The junctional complex, visible as the terminal bar on light microscopy, consists of three components in a fixed order. The tight junction is at the very top, sealing the cells. Just below it the adherens belt (E-cadherin to actin via catenins) encircles the cell and is linked to the terminal web. Below that, desmosomes form discontinuous spot welds anchoring intermediate filaments. Gap junctions are scattered along the lateral membranes, and hemidesmosomes anchor the basal surface to the basal lamina — they aren't part of the classic complex.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Zonula adherens → zonula occludens → macula adherens</b> — The tight junction is always the most apical, above the adherens belt.</li><li><b>Macula adherens → zonula occludens → zonula adherens</b> — The desmosome lies below both belts, not on top.</li><li><b>Zonula occludens → macula adherens → zonula adherens</b> — The adherens belt lies directly below the tight junction, above the desmosomes.</li><li><b>Gap junction → zonula occludens → hemidesmosome</b> — Gap junctions and hemidesmosomes aren't part of the classic junctional complex.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Order: \"Tight, Adherens, Desmosome\" — \"TAD\" from the top.</p>","w":["The tight junction is always the most apical, above the adherens belt.","Correct — zonula occludens, then zonula adherens, then macula adherens.","The desmosome lies below both belts, not on top.","The adherens belt lies directly below the tight junction, above the desmosomes.","Gap junctions and hemidesmosomes aren't part of the classic junctional complex."]},"q67":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The zonula adherens is a Ca²⁺-dependent E-cadherin belt linked to actin; losing E-cadherin lets carcinoma cells detach and invade.</p><p>E-cadherin molecules on neighboring cells bind each other in the presence of calcium; inside the cell, catenins (β-catenin, α-catenin, p120) connect them to a ring of actin filaments that runs around the apex of the cell and into the terminal web. Loss of E-cadherin is a hallmark of epithelial–mesenchymal transition: cells loosen, become motile and invade. In diffuse gastric carcinoma and lobular breast carcinoma, CDH1 mutations cause single \"signet-ring\" or single-file invasive cells. Freed β-catenin can also drive proliferation (Wnt pathway, APC loss in colon cancer).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Tight junction</b> — Tight junctions use claudins and occludin to seal the paracellular space; not cadherin–actin belts.</li><li><b>Desmosome</b> — Desmosomes also use cadherins, but they're spot welds anchored to keratin, not an apical actin belt.</li><li><b>Gap junction</b> — Gap junctions are connexin channels for communication.</li><li><b>Hemidesmosome</b> — Hemidesmosomes use integrins to bind the basal lamina.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> E-cadherin loss = invasion; seen in lobular breast carcinoma and diffuse gastric cancer.</p>","w":["Tight junctions use claudins and occludin to seal the paracellular space; not cadherin–actin belts.","Correct — the adherens junction is an E-cadherin belt linked to actin.","Desmosomes also use cadherins, but they're spot welds anchored to keratin, not an apical actin belt.","Gap junctions are connexin channels for communication.","Hemidesmosomes use integrins to bind the basal lamina."]},"q68":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Tight junctions also act as a \"fence\" that stops apical and basolateral membrane proteins from mixing, keeping the cell polarized.</p><p>Epithelial cells have two membrane domains with different proteins: apical (e.g., brush-border enzymes, Na⁺/glucose cotransporter SGLT1) and basolateral (Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase, GLUT2). Transcellular transport depends on this separation — e.g., glucose enters apically and leaves basally. The tight junction strand network prevents lateral diffusion of these proteins and lipids in the outer leaflet. Its \"gate\" function (paracellular seal) and \"fence\" function (polarity) both rely on claudins and occludin.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>They anchor keratin filaments to the cell membrane</b> — Anchoring keratin to the membrane is the desmosome's job.</li><li><b>They allow ions to pass directly between neighbouring cells</b> — Passing ions directly between cells is the gap junction's job.</li><li><b>They attach cells to the basal lamina</b> — Attaching to the basal lamina is the hemidesmosome's job.</li><li><b>They transmit contractile force between cells</b> — Transmitting force between cells is done by adherens junctions and desmosomes.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Tight junction = gate (paracellular barrier) + fence (polarity).</p>","w":["Anchoring keratin to the membrane is the desmosome's job.","Correct — tight junctions keep apical and basolateral membrane proteins separate.","Passing ions directly between cells is the gap junction's job.","Attaching to the basal lamina is the hemidesmosome's job.","Transmitting force between cells is done by adherens junctions and desmosomes."]},"q69":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Bullous pemphigoid = autoantibodies against hemidesmosome proteins (BP180/BP230) → tense subepidermal blisters with linear IgG/C3 at the basement membrane.</p><p>Bullous pemphigoid typically affects older adults, with intensely itchy, tense, fluid-filled blisters on flexural skin, trunk and limbs; oral involvement is uncommon. Because the split is beneath the whole epidermis, the blister roof is thick and blisters stay intact (Nikolsky sign negative). Biopsy shows a subepidermal split with eosinophils; direct immunofluorescence shows a linear band at the dermal–epidermal junction. Treatment is with corticosteroids.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Desmosomes</b> — Antibodies to desmosomes (desmoglein) cause pemphigus vulgaris — flaccid intraepidermal blisters, net-like IF, oral erosions.</li><li><b>Tight junctions</b> — Tight junctions aren't the target of blistering diseases.</li><li><b>Gap junctions</b> — Gap junctions aren't targets in blistering disease.</li><li><b>Adherens junctions</b> — Adherens junctions aren't the pemphigoid target.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Pemphigoid: tense, subepidermal, linear IF, elderly, mucosa usually spared. Pemphigus: flaccid, intraepidermal, net-like IF, oral lesions.</p>","w":["Antibodies to desmosomes (desmoglein) cause pemphigus vulgaris — flaccid intraepidermal blisters, net-like IF, oral erosions.","Correct — bullous pemphigoid antibodies target hemidesmosomes.","Tight junctions aren't the target of blistering diseases.","Gap junctions aren't targets in blistering disease.","Adherens junctions aren't the pemphigoid target."]},"q70":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> A gap junction channel (connexon) is a hexamer of 6 connexins; ions and small molecules (&lt;~1 kDa) pass through.</p><p>On EM, gap junctions appear as close appositions of two membranes separated by a 2–3 nm gap, and freeze-fracture shows packed arrays of particles (connexons). Two connexons — one from each cell — dock to form a complete channel. They allow ions (electrical coupling), second messengers like cAMP and Ca²⁺, and small metabolites to pass but exclude proteins and nucleic acids. They close in response to high Ca²⁺ or low pH, sealing off injured cells.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>4 claudins; water only</b> — Claudins form tight junctions; they don't make channel units of four that pass water.</li><li><b>6 cadherins; proteins and RNA</b> — Cadherins form adherens junctions and desmosomes; large molecules like proteins and RNA can't pass gap junctions.</li><li><b>12 integrins; ions only</b> — Integrins form hemidesmosomes and focal adhesions, not channels.</li><li><b>2 occludins; no molecules pass</b> — Occludin is a tight junction protein.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Connexon = 6 connexins; channel = 2 connexons.</p>","w":["Claudins form tight junctions; they don't make channel units of four that pass water.","Correct — each connexon is 6 connexins and passes ions and small molecules.","Cadherins form adherens junctions and desmosomes; large molecules like proteins and RNA can't pass gap junctions.","Integrins form hemidesmosomes and focal adhesions, not channels.","Occludin is a tight junction protein."]},"q71":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Heparan sulfate proteoglycan (perlecan) gives the basal lamina its strong negative charge and acts as a charge barrier.</p><p>Sulfated GAG chains on perlecan carry dense negative charges, attracting cations and water and repelling negatively charged molecules. In the glomerular basement membrane this charge, together with the size barrier of type IV collagen and the podocyte slit diaphragm, keeps albumin (which is negatively charged) out of the urine. Loss of this charge contributes to proteinuria in minimal change disease.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Type IV collagen</b> — Type IV collagen provides the structural network (size barrier), not the charge.</li><li><b>Laminin</b> — Laminin is the adhesive bridge between cell integrins and the lamina.</li><li><b>Entactin (nidogen)</b> — Entactin (nidogen) links laminin to type IV collagen.</li><li><b>Fibronectin</b> — Fibronectin links cells to matrix collagen; it isn't the charge barrier.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Glomerular filtration barrier: fenestrated endothelium → GBM (type IV collagen + heparan sulfate) → podocyte slit diaphragm (nephrin).</p>","w":["Type IV collagen provides the structural network (size barrier), not the charge.","Laminin is the adhesive bridge between cell integrins and the lamina.","Correct — heparan sulfate proteoglycan gives the basal lamina its negative charge.","Entactin (nidogen) links laminin to type IV collagen.","Fibronectin links cells to matrix collagen; it isn't the charge barrier."]},"q72":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Basal lamina = lamina lucida + lamina densa (made by epithelium); the reticular lamina beneath is made by connective tissue fibroblasts.</p><p>On light microscopy the \"basement membrane\" (PAS-positive) is the combination of the basal lamina and the reticular lamina. On EM, the lamina lucida (electron-lucent, with laminin and integrins) sits next to the cell, the lamina densa (type IV collagen network) below it, and then the reticular lamina of type III reticular fibers and type VII anchoring fibrils from the underlying connective tissue. Anchoring fibrils loop through the lamina densa to fasten it to the dermis; their defect causes dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Lamina lucida</b> — The lamina lucida is part of the basal lamina (epithelial product).</li><li><b>Lamina densa</b> — The lamina densa is part of the basal lamina.</li><li><b>Plasma membrane of the basal cell</b> — The basal cell's plasma membrane isn't a layer of the basement membrane.</li><li><b>Hemidesmosome plaque</b> — The hemidesmosome plaque is a cell junction, not a basement membrane layer.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Basement membrane (light microscopy) = basal lamina (epithelial) + reticular lamina (connective tissue).</p>","w":["The lamina lucida is part of the basal lamina (epithelial product).","The lamina densa is part of the basal lamina.","Correct — the reticular lamina is made by the connective tissue, not the epithelium.","The basal cell's plasma membrane isn't a layer of the basement membrane.","The hemidesmosome plaque is a cell junction, not a basement membrane layer."]},"q73":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Reticular fibers are thin type III collagen fibers that form the supporting mesh of lymphoid organs, liver and bone marrow; they stain black with silver.</p><p>Reticular fibers are produced by reticular cells (specialized fibroblasts) in lymph nodes, spleen and bone marrow, by hepatic stellate cells in the liver, and by smooth muscle and Schwann cells elsewhere. They're too thin to see on H&amp;E but are argyrophilic (silver stain) and PAS-positive because of their glycosylation. They form a flexible scaffold that holds free cells (lymphocytes, hematopoietic cells, hepatocytes) in place. Type III collagen also predominates in early granulation tissue and vessel walls.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Type I</b> — Type I collagen forms thick, strong fibers in bone, tendon and dermis.</li><li><b>Type II</b> — Type II collagen is in cartilage and vitreous.</li><li><b>Type IV</b> — Type IV collagen forms sheet-like basement membrane networks, not fibers.</li><li><b>Type VII</b> — Type VII collagen forms anchoring fibrils under the epidermis.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Silver stain → reticular fibers (type III collagen).</p>","w":["Type I collagen forms thick, strong fibers in bone, tendon and dermis.","Type II collagen is in cartilage and vitreous.","Correct — reticular fibers are made of type III collagen.","Type IV collagen forms sheet-like basement membrane networks, not fibers.","Type VII collagen forms anchoring fibrils under the epidermis."]},"q74":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hematuria + progressive renal failure + sensorineural hearing loss + anterior lenticonus = Alport syndrome (type IV collagen, usually X-linked COL4A5).</p><p>Specialized basement membranes in the glomerulus, cochlea and lens capsule use the α3–α4–α5 chains of type IV collagen. When α5 is defective, the glomerular basement membrane becomes thin, split and laminated (\"basket-weave\" on EM), causing hematuria, proteinuria and end-stage renal disease in young men; affected uncles on the mother's side fit X-linked inheritance. The cochlear basement membrane defect causes high-frequency hearing loss, and the thin lens capsule bulges forward (anterior lenticonus).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Type I collagen</b> — Type I collagen defects cause osteogenesis imperfecta.</li><li><b>Type II collagen</b> — Type II defects affect cartilage and vitreous.</li><li><b>Type III collagen</b> — Type III defects cause vascular Ehlers–Danlos syndrome.</li><li><b>Fibrillin-1</b> — Fibrillin-1 defects cause Marfan syndrome, with lens DISLOCATION (ectopia lentis), not lenticonus.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Alport: \"can't see, can't pee, can't hear high C\" — type IV collagen. Goodpasture antibodies target the α3 chain of the same collagen.</p>","w":["Type I collagen defects cause osteogenesis imperfecta.","Type II defects affect cartilage and vitreous.","Type III defects cause vascular Ehlers–Danlos syndrome.","Correct — Alport syndrome is a type IV collagen defect.","Fibrillin-1 defects cause Marfan syndrome, with lens DISLOCATION (ectopia lentis), not lenticonus."]},"q75":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Thin translucent skin, easy bruising and arterial rupture without joint hypermobility = vascular Ehlers–Danlos syndrome (type III collagen, COL3A1).</p><p>Type III collagen is abundant in the walls of arteries, hollow organs (bowel, uterus) and skin. In vascular EDS (autosomal dominant), defective type III collagen leaves these structures fragile: spontaneous rupture of medium-sized arteries (splenic, renal, iliac), bowel perforation, uterine rupture in pregnancy, and visible veins through thin skin. Joint hypermobility is usually limited to the small joints, unlike classic EDS. It's the most life-threatening form of EDS.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Type I</b> — Type I collagen defects cause osteogenesis imperfecta (fractures, blue sclerae).</li><li><b>Type II</b> — Type II collagen is in cartilage and vitreous.</li><li><b>Type IV</b> — Type IV defects cause Alport syndrome.</li><li><b>Type V</b> — Type V collagen is involved in classic EDS — hyperextensible skin and hypermobile joints, not arterial rupture.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Vascular EDS = type III collagen = arterial/organ rupture; classic EDS = type V = stretchy skin + loose joints.</p>","w":["Type I collagen defects cause osteogenesis imperfecta (fractures, blue sclerae).","Type II collagen is in cartilage and vitreous.","Correct — vascular EDS is caused by mutations in type III collagen.","Type IV defects cause Alport syndrome.","Type V collagen is involved in classic EDS — hyperextensible skin and hypermobile joints, not arterial rupture."]},"q76":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Ehlers–Danlos syndromes are disorders of collagen synthesis or processing: hyperextensible skin, hypermobile joints, easy bruising and poor (\"cigarette paper\") scars.</p><p>Collagen synthesis involves translation of pro-α chains, hydroxylation of proline and lysine (needs vitamin C), glycosylation, triple-helix formation, secretion, cleavage of propeptides, and cross-linking by lysyl oxidase. Defects at different steps or in different collagens (mainly type V in classic EDS, type III in vascular EDS, lysyl hydroxylase in kyphoscoliotic EDS, procollagen peptidase in dermatosparaxis) produce the EDS family. Weak collagen makes skin stretch and tear, joints dislocate, and wounds heal with thin, wide scars.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Deficiency of lysosomal enzymes that degrade GAGs</b> — Deficiency of lysosomal enzymes that degrade GAGs describes the mucopolysaccharidoses (e.g., Hurler, Hunter).</li><li><b>Mutation of fibrillin-1</b> — Fibrillin-1 mutation causes Marfan syndrome (tall, long limbs, lens dislocation, aortic root dilation).</li><li><b>Deficiency of α1-antitrypsin</b> — α1-antitrypsin deficiency causes panacinar emphysema and liver disease.</li><li><b>Autoantibodies to hemidesmosomes</b> — Autoantibodies to hemidesmosomes cause bullous pemphigoid.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> EDS = collagen; Marfan = fibrillin-1 (elastic fiber scaffold); OI = type I collagen.</p>","w":["Correct — Ehlers–Danlos syndromes result from defective collagen synthesis or processing.","Deficiency of lysosomal enzymes that degrade GAGs describes the mucopolysaccharidoses (e.g., Hurler, Hunter).","Fibrillin-1 mutation causes Marfan syndrome (tall, long limbs, lens dislocation, aortic root dilation).","α1-antitrypsin deficiency causes panacinar emphysema and liver disease.","Autoantibodies to hemidesmosomes cause bullous pemphigoid."]},"q77":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> In osteogenesis imperfecta the type I collagen–rich sclera is thin and translucent, so the dark choroid shows through as blue.</p><p>The sclera is dense connective tissue built mostly of type I collagen. When type I collagen is defective or deficient, the sclera thins, and the pigmented, vascular choroid beneath lends it a bluish tint. Other type I collagen tissues show the same weakness: bone (fragility, bowing deformities), dentin (opalescent, brownish teeth — dentinogenesis imperfecta), ligaments (laxity) and the middle ear ossicles (hearing loss).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Deposition of a blue pigment in the sclera</b> — No pigment is deposited in the sclera.</li><li><b>Increased melanin in the conjunctiva</b> — Melanin in the conjunctiva isn't increased.</li><li><b>Accumulation of glycosaminoglycans in the cornea</b> — GAG accumulation (e.g., Hurler syndrome) causes corneal clouding, not blue sclerae.</li><li><b>Hemorrhage beneath the sclera</b> — Subscleral hemorrhage would look red and patchy.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Blue sclerae also occur in normal infants and in iron deficiency, because their sclerae are thin.</p>","w":["No pigment is deposited in the sclera.","Correct — the thin sclera lets the underlying choroid show through.","Melanin in the conjunctiva isn't increased.","GAG accumulation (e.g., Hurler syndrome) causes corneal clouding, not blue sclerae.","Subscleral hemorrhage would look red and patchy."]},"q78":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Fibronectin links cells (via integrins) to collagen and other matrix molecules in connective tissue and guides cell migration in wound healing.</p><p>Fibronectin is a large dimeric glycoprotein with separate binding domains for integrins (via the RGD sequence), collagen, fibrin and heparin. Fibroblasts and endothelial cells use it as a track to crawl into a wound, and plasma fibronectin incorporated into a blood clot helps cells enter it. It also directs embryonic cell migration (e.g., neural crest). Laminin plays the equivalent role in the basal lamina.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Laminin</b> — Laminin is the adhesive glycoprotein of the basal lamina, not the general connective tissue matrix.</li><li><b>Hyaluronic acid</b> — Hyaluronic acid is a GAG that hydrates the matrix; it isn't the cell–collagen linker.</li><li><b>Elastin</b> — Elastin is a fibrous protein giving elastic recoil.</li><li><b>Aggrecan</b> — Aggrecan is the large proteoglycan of cartilage.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Integrin ligands: fibronectin (connective tissue), laminin (basal lamina).</p>","w":["Laminin is the adhesive glycoprotein of the basal lamina, not the general connective tissue matrix.","Correct — fibronectin links cells to collagen and supports migration in wound healing.","Hyaluronic acid is a GAG that hydrates the matrix; it isn't the cell–collagen linker.","Elastin is a fibrous protein giving elastic recoil.","Aggrecan is the large proteoglycan of cartilage."]},"q79":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Keratan sulfate keeps corneal collagen fibrils evenly spaced, which is essential for transparency; it's also found in cartilage and bone.</p><p>The corneal stroma consists of layers of thin type I collagen fibrils, each layer running at an angle to the next. Proteoglycans carrying keratan sulfate (lumican, keratocan) bind the fibrils and hold them at a precise, uniform spacing smaller than the wavelength of light, so scattered light cancels out and the cornea stays clear. Corneal swelling or GAG accumulation disrupts this spacing, causing clouding (e.g., in mucopolysaccharidoses).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Hyaluronic acid</b> — Hyaluronic acid is unsulfated and found in synovial fluid and vitreous.</li><li><b>Dermatan sulfate</b> — Dermatan sulfate is found in skin, blood vessels and heart valves.</li><li><b>Heparin</b> — Heparin is stored in mast cell granules.</li><li><b>Heparan sulfate</b> — Heparan sulfate is found in basement membranes and on cell surfaces.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> GAG locations: hyaluronan — joints/vitreous; chondroitin — cartilage; keratan — cornea; dermatan — skin; heparan — basement membrane; heparin — mast cells.</p>","w":["Hyaluronic acid is unsulfated and found in synovial fluid and vitreous.","Correct — keratan sulfate maintains collagen spacing in the cornea.","Dermatan sulfate is found in skin, blood vessels and heart valves.","Heparin is stored in mast cell granules.","Heparan sulfate is found in basement membranes and on cell surfaces."]},"q80":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Plasma cells — terminally differentiated B lymphocytes — secrete antibodies; they have basophilic cytoplasm (RER), a perinuclear Golgi \"hof\" and a clock-face nucleus.</p><p>When activated by antigen (with T-cell help), B cells proliferate and differentiate into plasma cells, which are protein-secreting factories: abundant rough ER makes the cytoplasm intensely basophilic, the large Golgi appears as a pale area beside the nucleus, and coarse heterochromatin blocks form the \"cartwheel\" pattern. They're common in chronic inflammation and in lamina propria of the gut. Excess immunoglobulin can accumulate as Russell bodies. Malignant plasma cells cause multiple myeloma.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Monocytes; cytokines</b> — Monocytes become macrophages, which have pale foamy or vacuolated cytoplasm and secrete cytokines.</li><li><b>T lymphocytes; perforin</b> — T lymphocytes are small round cells with scant cytoplasm; they don't have this morphology.</li><li><b>Fibroblasts; collagen</b> — Fibroblasts are spindle-shaped with elongated nuclei and secrete collagen.</li><li><b>Mast cells; histamine</b> — Mast cells are packed with metachromatic granules and release histamine.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Plasma cell: eccentric clock-face nucleus + perinuclear hof + basophilic cytoplasm; CD138 positive.</p>","w":["Monocytes become macrophages, which have pale foamy or vacuolated cytoplasm and secrete cytokines.","Correct — these are plasma cells, derived from B lymphocytes, secreting antibodies.","T lymphocytes are small round cells with scant cytoplasm; they don't have this morphology.","Fibroblasts are spindle-shaped with elongated nuclei and secrete collagen.","Mast cells are packed with metachromatic granules and release histamine."]},"q81":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Fibroblasts are the resident cells that make collagen, elastic fibers and ground substance.</p><p>Active fibroblasts have abundant RER and Golgi; quiescent fibrocytes are smaller and darker. They synthesize procollagen, elastin, fibrillin, GAGs, proteoglycans and adhesive glycoproteins, and they remodel the matrix with metalloproteinases. In wound healing, some differentiate into myofibroblasts (with smooth muscle actin) that contract the wound. Other fixed cells (adipocytes, pericytes) and wandering cells (macrophages, mast cells, plasma cells) have different roles.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Macrophage</b> — Macrophages phagocytose and present antigen.</li><li><b>Mast cell</b> — Mast cells release histamine and heparin in allergic reactions.</li><li><b>Plasma cell</b> — Plasma cells secrete antibodies.</li><li><b>Adipocyte</b> — Adipocytes store triglyceride.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Myofibroblasts (from fibroblasts) cause wound contraction and, in excess, contractures.</p>","w":["Macrophages phagocytose and present antigen.","Mast cells release histamine and heparin in allergic reactions.","Correct — fibroblasts synthesize collagen, elastin and ground substance.","Plasma cells secrete antibodies.","Adipocytes store triglyceride."]},"q82":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Elastic cartilage (epiglottis, external ear, auditory tube) = type II collagen + abundant elastic fibers, with a perichondrium.</p><p>Elastic cartilage looks like hyaline cartilage packed with a dense network of branching elastic fibers (best seen with elastic stains such as Verhoeff or orcein). The fibers let it bend and spring back — the epiglottis folds over the larynx during swallowing, and the ear can be bent without breaking. It has chondrocytes in lacunae, often singly or in small groups, and a perichondrium for growth. It doesn't calcify with age as hyaline cartilage can.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Hyaline; type II collagen only</b> — Hyaline cartilage has type II collagen but no elastic fibers.</li><li><b>Fibrocartilage; type I and II collagen</b> — Fibrocartilage shows thick bundles of type I collagen with rows of chondrocytes, not branching elastic fibers.</li><li><b>Fibrocartilage; elastic fibres only</b> — Fibrocartilage isn't elastic; it contains type I and II collagen.</li><li><b>Hyaline; type I collagen</b> — Hyaline cartilage contains type II collagen, not type I.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Elastic cartilage sites: \"EEA\" — Ear, Epiglottis, Auditory (Eustachian) tube.</p>","w":["Hyaline cartilage has type II collagen but no elastic fibers.","Correct — the epiglottis is elastic cartilage: type II collagen plus elastic fibers.","Fibrocartilage shows thick bundles of type I collagen with rows of chondrocytes, not branching elastic fibers.","Fibrocartilage isn't elastic; it contains type I and II collagen.","Hyaline cartilage contains type II collagen, not type I."]},"q83":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Fibrocartilage = rows of chondrocytes between thick type I collagen bundles, no perichondrium; in IV discs (annulus), pubic symphysis, menisci.</p><p>Fibrocartilage is found where tissue must resist both tension and compression. Its thick type I collagen bundles resist tearing, and the chondrocytes and small amounts of type II collagen/aggrecan resist squeezing. Chondrocytes sit in rows or pairs between the bundles. It lacks a perichondrium and blends with dense connective tissue. Examples: annulus fibrosus, pubic symphysis, knee menisci, TMJ disc, and tendon/ligament insertions into bone.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Hyaline cartilage</b> — Hyaline cartilage has a glassy type II collagen matrix and a perichondrium (except articular).</li><li><b>Elastic cartilage</b> — Elastic cartilage has elastic fibers and a perichondrium.</li><li><b>Articular cartilage</b> — Articular cartilage is hyaline cartilage.</li><li><b>Growth plate cartilage</b> — Growth plate cartilage is hyaline cartilage.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Fibrocartilage = types I + II collagen, no perichondrium.</p>","w":["Hyaline cartilage has a glassy type II collagen matrix and a perichondrium (except articular).","Elastic cartilage has elastic fibers and a perichondrium.","Correct — fibrocartilage fits this description.","Articular cartilage is hyaline cartilage.","Growth plate cartilage is hyaline cartilage."]},"q84":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Articular cartilage lacks a perichondrium (no source of new chondroblasts) and is avascular, so defects don't regenerate.</p><p>Most hyaline cartilage is covered by a perichondrium whose inner chondrogenic layer can supply new chondroblasts for appositional growth and limited repair. Articular cartilage has no perichondrium and no blood vessels, nourished only by diffusion from synovial fluid. Mature chondrocytes divide very little and are trapped in their lacunae. Injury therefore isn't repaired by new hyaline cartilage; if subchondral bone is breached, marrow cells may form weaker fibrocartilage instead (the basis of microfracture surgery).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It contains type I collagen</b> — Articular cartilage contains type II collagen, not type I.</li><li><b>Its chondrocytes have no lacunae</b> — Its chondrocytes do sit in lacunae.</li><li><b>It is calcified</b> — Articular cartilage isn't calcified except in its deepest zone next to bone.</li><li><b>It is nourished only by blood vessels</b> — It is nourished by synovial fluid diffusion, not blood vessels.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Microfracture surgery: drilling into subchondral bone lets marrow cells make fibrocartilage repair tissue.</p>","w":["Articular cartilage contains type II collagen, not type I.","Correct — articular cartilage lacks a perichondrium, so it can't regenerate.","Its chondrocytes do sit in lacunae.","Articular cartilage isn't calcified except in its deepest zone next to bone.","It is nourished by synovial fluid diffusion, not blood vessels."]},"q85":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> In rheumatoid arthritis, inflamed synovium forms a pannus that grows over and erodes articular cartilage and subchondral bone.</p><p>Rheumatoid arthritis is an autoimmune disease in which T cells, B cells and macrophages infiltrate the synovium, which proliferates into a thick, vascular granulation tissue (pannus). The pannus releases cytokines (TNF-α, IL-1, IL-6) and enzymes that destroy cartilage, and activates osteoclasts that erode bone at the joint margins. Typical features: symmetric small-joint polyarthritis (MCPs, PIPs), morning stiffness &gt;1 hour, rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP antibodies, and eventually deformities (ulnar deviation, swan neck).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Perichondrium</b> — Articular cartilage has no perichondrium.</li><li><b>Periosteum</b> — The periosteum isn't the tissue that becomes inflamed in RA.</li><li><b>Epiphyseal plate</b> — The epiphyseal plate is closed in adults.</li><li><b>Joint capsule ligaments</b> — Capsular ligaments are stretched later but don't erode cartilage.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> RA spares the DIP joints; osteoarthritis involves DIPs (Heberden nodes).</p>","w":["Articular cartilage has no perichondrium.","The periosteum isn't the tissue that becomes inflamed in RA.","Correct — the inflamed synovial membrane (pannus) erodes the articular cartilage.","The epiphyseal plate is closed in adults.","Capsular ligaments are stretched later but don't erode cartilage."]},"q86":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Long bones lengthen by interstitial growth of growth-plate cartilage — chondrocytes dividing within the matrix.</p><p>The epiphyseal plate has zones: reserve (resting) cartilage → proliferation (chondrocytes dividing into columns) → hypertrophy (cells enlarge) → calcification (matrix calcifies, cells die) → ossification (osteoblasts lay bone on calcified cartilage). Cartilage is added at the epiphyseal side by interstitial growth and replaced by bone at the diaphyseal side, so the plate stays about the same thickness while the bone lengthens. Achondroplasia (FGFR3 activation) disrupts the proliferative zone, causing short limbs.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Appositional growth from the perichondrium</b> — Appositional growth from the perichondrium widens cartilage, not lengthens the bone.</li><li><b>Appositional growth by osteoblasts</b> — Osteoblast apposition adds bone at surfaces (width), not cartilage length.</li><li><b>Interstitial growth of bone matrix</b> — Bone matrix can't grow interstitially because it's mineralized.</li><li><b>Hypertrophy of osteocytes</b> — Osteocytes don't drive growth.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Growth plate zones: \"Real People Have Courage Often\" — Reserve, Proliferative, Hypertrophic, Calcification, Ossification.</p>","w":["Appositional growth from the perichondrium widens cartilage, not lengthens the bone.","Correct — interstitial growth by chondrocyte mitosis lengthens the growth plate.","Osteoblast apposition adds bone at surfaces (width), not cartilage length.","Bone matrix can't grow interstitially because it's mineralized.","Osteocytes don't drive growth."]},"q87":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The metaphysis is the flared, highly vascular region between the diaphysis and the growth plate — a favorite site for childhood osteomyelitis and bone tumors.</p><p>Long bone regions: diaphysis (shaft, compact bone around the medullary cavity), metaphysis (where the shaft widens toward the end and new bone is actively formed from the growth plate), epiphyseal plate (growth cartilage), and epiphysis (the end, with a secondary ossification center under the articular cartilage). In children, metaphyseal capillary loops turn sharply and blood flows slowly, letting blood-borne bacteria settle (hematogenous osteomyelitis). The rapidly dividing cells there also explain why osteosarcoma typically arises in the metaphysis around the knee.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Diaphysis</b> — The diaphysis is the shaft.</li><li><b>Epiphysis</b> — The epiphysis is the end of the bone, beyond the growth plate.</li><li><b>Medullary cavity</b> — The medullary cavity is the marrow space inside the shaft.</li><li><b>Articular cartilage</b> — Articular cartilage covers the joint surface of the epiphysis.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Metaphysis: osteosarcoma, osteomyelitis (children). Epiphysis: giant cell tumor (adults). Diaphysis: Ewing sarcoma.</p>","w":["The diaphysis is the shaft.","The epiphysis is the end of the bone, beyond the growth plate.","Correct — the metaphysis is the flared part between the shaft and the growth plate.","The medullary cavity is the marrow space inside the shaft.","Articular cartilage covers the joint surface of the epiphysis."]},"q88":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The proliferative zone of the growth plate has the highest mitotic index, with chondrocytes in columns like stacks of coins.</p><p>From the epiphysis toward the diaphysis, the growth plate zones are: resting/reserve (small, scattered chondrocytes — stem cells), proliferative (rapid mitosis, flattened cells stacked in columns along the bone's long axis), hypertrophic (cells enlarge and store glycogen), calcified cartilage (matrix calcifies, cells die), and ossification (osteoblasts lay bone on calcified spicules). Growth hormone (via IGF-1) and thyroid hormone stimulate proliferation; FGFR3 overactivity (achondroplasia) inhibits it.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>A</b> — Zone A is the resting zone: small scattered chondrocytes with little division.</li><li><b>C</b> — Zone C is the hypertrophic zone: cells enlarge rather than divide.</li><li><b>D</b> — Zone D is calcification/ossification: chondrocytes die and bone is laid down.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Proliferative zone = columns, mitosis; hypertrophic = large cells, glycogen; then calcification and ossification.</p>","w":["Zone A is the resting zone: small scattered chondrocytes with little division.","Correct — B is the proliferative zone, with the highest mitotic index.","Zone C is the hypertrophic zone: cells enlarge rather than divide.","Zone D is calcification/ossification: chondrocytes die and bone is laid down."]},"q89":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> In the hypertrophic zone, chondrocytes swell, store glycogen and prepare the matrix for calcification.</p><p>Hypertrophic chondrocytes enlarge up to tenfold, accumulate glycogen, and secrete type X collagen, alkaline phosphatase and VEGF. Alkaline phosphatase and matrix vesicles start calcification of the longitudinal septa between cell columns; VEGF attracts blood vessels from the metaphysis. The cells then die (apoptosis), leaving calcified cartilage spicules on which osteoblasts deposit bone. The sequence ensures cartilage is replaced by bone at the same rate it's produced.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>A</b> — Zone A is the resting zone.</li><li><b>B</b> — Zone B is the proliferative zone — cells divide but stay small and flat.</li><li><b>D</b> — Zone D is the calcified/ossification zone, where cells have died.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Type X collagen is a marker of hypertrophic chondrocytes.</p>","w":["Zone A is the resting zone.","Zone B is the proliferative zone — cells divide but stay small and flat.","Correct — C is the hypertrophic zone, where chondrocytes enlarge and store glycogen.","Zone D is the calcified/ossification zone, where cells have died."]},"q90":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> In the final (ossification) zone, vessels invade and osteoblasts lay down bone on remnants of calcified cartilage.</p><p>Endochondral ossification at the growth plate ends with capillaries and osteoprogenitor cells invading the spaces left by dead hypertrophic chondrocytes. Osteoblasts deposit osteoid on the calcified cartilage septa, forming mixed spicules (bone covering a cartilage core — blue cartilage core with pink bone on H&amp;E). Osteoclasts later remodel these primary trabeculae into mature bone. This continuous replacement at the diaphyseal side of the plate produces longitudinal growth.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Chondrocytes divide rapidly in columns</b> — Rapid columnar division describes the proliferative zone.</li><li><b>Chondrocytes enlarge and store glycogen</b> — Enlarging glycogen-rich chondrocytes describe the hypertrophic zone.</li><li><b>Small chondrocytes rest in a hyaline matrix</b> — Small resting chondrocytes in hyaline matrix describe the resting (reserve) zone.</li><li><b>Osteoclasts form new cartilage</b> — Osteoclasts resorb bone and calcified cartilage; they don't make cartilage.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Mixed spicule = calcified cartilage core + bone covering → hallmark of endochondral ossification.</p>","w":["Rapid columnar division describes the proliferative zone.","Enlarging glycogen-rich chondrocytes describe the hypertrophic zone.","Correct — in the ossification zone, osteoblasts lay bone on calcified cartilage remnants.","Small resting chondrocytes in hyaline matrix describe the resting (reserve) zone.","Osteoclasts resorb bone and calcified cartilage; they don't make cartilage."]},"q91":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Osteoblasts form a row of cuboidal cells on forming bone surfaces and secrete osteoid toward the bone.</p><p>Active osteoblasts are plump and cuboidal with basophilic cytoplasm (abundant RER for collagen synthesis) and a prominent Golgi. They line up like an epithelium on the bone surface, connected by gap junctions. They secrete osteoid (type I collagen, osteocalcin) toward the existing bone; a pale osteoid seam lies between them and the mineralized bone. Some become trapped as osteocytes; others flatten into inactive bone-lining cells. Osteoblasts also control osteoclasts via RANKL and OPG.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Osteocytes; hydroxyapatite</b> — Osteocytes sit inside lacunae within the matrix, not in a row on the surface; hydroxyapatite isn't secreted as such.</li><li><b>Osteoclasts; acid and proteases</b> — Osteoclasts are large multinucleated cells in Howship lacunae that secrete acid and proteases.</li><li><b>Chondroblasts; type II collagen</b> — Chondroblasts make cartilage matrix (type II collagen), not bone.</li><li><b>Fibroblasts; type III collagen</b> — Fibroblasts are spindle-shaped connective tissue cells.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Row of cuboidal cells on bone = osteoblasts; big multinucleated cell in a pit = osteoclast.</p>","w":["Osteocytes sit inside lacunae within the matrix, not in a row on the surface; hydroxyapatite isn't secreted as such.","Correct — these are osteoblasts, secreting osteoid.","Osteoclasts are large multinucleated cells in Howship lacunae that secrete acid and proteases.","Chondroblasts make cartilage matrix (type II collagen), not bone.","Fibroblasts are spindle-shaped connective tissue cells."]},"q92":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Osteoblasts are polarized: they secrete matrix from the side facing the existing bone, adding layers of osteoid on that surface.</p><p>Like secretory epithelial cells, osteoblasts have an apical (secretory) side and a basal side. Collagen and other matrix proteins are released toward the underlying bone, so new osteoid is laid on the surface beneath them while the cell layer moves outward. Matrix vesicles bud from the same side to start mineralization. As layers accumulate, some osteoblasts become buried and turn into osteocytes.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>They secrete matrix in all directions equally</b> — Secretion is directional, not equal in all directions.</li><li><b>They secrete matrix only into the marrow cavity</b> — Matrix is laid on bone surfaces, not into the marrow cavity.</li><li><b>They release enzymes from a ruffled border</b> — A ruffled border belongs to osteoclasts.</li><li><b>They secrete matrix only after becoming osteocytes</b> — Osteocytes maintain matrix; osteoblasts produce it.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Osteoblasts → polarized secretion toward bone; osteoclasts → ruffled border facing bone.</p>","w":["Secretion is directional, not equal in all directions.","Correct — osteoblasts secrete matrix toward the bone surface they rest on.","Matrix is laid on bone surfaces, not into the marrow cavity.","A ruffled border belongs to osteoclasts.","Osteocytes maintain matrix; osteoblasts produce it."]},"q93":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Secondary ossification centers appear in the epiphyses, mostly after birth; the primary center is in the diaphysis before birth.</p><p>Endochondral ossification begins in the diaphysis: a periosteal bone collar forms, central chondrocytes hypertrophy and die, and a periosteal bud brings in vessels and osteoprogenitor cells (primary center). Later, usually after birth, vessels enter the epiphyses and the same process creates secondary centers. Cartilage is spared only at the articular surface and at the epiphyseal plate between the two centers. When the plates close (ossify) after puberty, longitudinal growth stops.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>In the diaphysis</b> — The diaphysis holds the primary center.</li><li><b>In the metaphysis</b> — The metaphysis is the transition zone next to the plate, not an ossification center.</li><li><b>In the periosteal collar</b> — The periosteal collar forms around the diaphysis by intramembranous ossification.</li><li><b>In the medullary cavity</b> — The medullary cavity forms by osteoclast resorption.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Primary center: diaphysis, prenatal. Secondary centers: epiphyses, mostly postnatal. Growth plate between them.</p>","w":["The diaphysis holds the primary center.","Correct — the secondary ossification center appears in the epiphysis.","The metaphysis is the transition zone next to the plate, not an ossification center.","The periosteal collar forms around the diaphysis by intramembranous ossification.","The medullary cavity forms by osteoclast resorption."]},"q94":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Calcified bone matrix = type I collagen (organic) + hydroxyapatite (inorganic calcium phosphate).</p><p>About one-third of bone's dry weight is organic, mainly type I collagen with smaller amounts of osteocalcin, osteonectin, osteopontin and proteoglycans; about two-thirds is hydroxyapatite crystals. The collagen gives tensile strength and some flexibility (bone without collagen is brittle — osteogenesis imperfecta); the mineral gives hardness and compressive strength (bone without mineral is rubbery — osteomalacia). Bone also stores most of the body's calcium and phosphate.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Type II collagen and chondroitin sulfate</b> — Type II collagen and chondroitin sulfate describe cartilage matrix.</li><li><b>Type IV collagen and laminin</b> — Type IV collagen and laminin describe the basal lamina.</li><li><b>Elastin and calcium carbonate</b> — Bone isn't made of elastin or calcium carbonate.</li><li><b>Type III collagen and calcium phosphate</b> — Type III collagen isn't the bone collagen.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Remove mineral (acid) → bone bends like rubber; remove collagen (burning) → bone crumbles.</p>","w":["Type II collagen and chondroitin sulfate describe cartilage matrix.","Correct — bone matrix is type I collagen mineralized with hydroxyapatite.","Type IV collagen and laminin describe the basal lamina.","Bone isn't made of elastin or calcium carbonate.","Type III collagen isn't the bone collagen."]},"q95":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Skeletal muscle regenerates from satellite cells — stem cells between the sarcolemma and the basal lamina.</p><p>Mature skeletal muscle fibers are multinucleated and can't divide. Satellite cells are quiescent, flattened cells under each fiber's basal lamina. After injury they are activated (Pax7 → MyoD, myogenin), proliferate, and fuse with each other or with damaged fibers to rebuild the muscle, while some return to quiescence to replenish the pool. Regeneration works best when the basal lamina scaffold is intact. Large defects heal partly with scar tissue from fibroblasts.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Fibroblasts</b> — Fibroblasts make scar tissue, not new muscle fibers.</li><li><b>Pericytes</b> — Pericytes support capillaries (with limited stem cell potential).</li><li><b>Myoepithelial cells</b> — Myoepithelial cells surround gland acini (e.g., sweat, mammary).</li><li><b>Mature myocytes dividing by mitosis</b> — Mature skeletal muscle fibers are post-mitotic.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Regeneration: skeletal muscle (satellite cells) — limited; cardiac — none (scar); smooth — good (mitosis).</p>","w":["Fibroblasts make scar tissue, not new muscle fibers.","Correct — satellite cells regenerate skeletal muscle fibers.","Pericytes support capillaries (with limited stem cell potential).","Myoepithelial cells surround gland acini (e.g., sweat, mammary).","Mature skeletal muscle fibers are post-mitotic."]},"q96":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Infarcted myocardium is replaced by fibrous scar tissue because adult cardiomyocytes barely divide and cardiac muscle lacks satellite cells.</p><p>After an MI, dead myocytes provoke inflammation (neutrophils at 1–3 days, macrophages at 3–7 days), then granulation tissue forms (1–2 weeks), and finally a collagenous scar (type I collagen) by about 2 months. Because the scar can't contract or conduct normally, the heart may develop regional wall motion abnormalities, aneurysm, or arrhythmias; the remaining myocytes hypertrophy to compensate. The weak, healing wall around days 3–14 is when rupture is most likely.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>By satellite cell regeneration</b> — Cardiac muscle lacks satellite cells.</li><li><b>By mitosis of surviving cardiomyocytes, restoring the muscle</b> — Adult cardiomyocytes have very little mitotic capacity and don't restore lost muscle.</li><li><b>By smooth muscle cells</b> — Smooth muscle doesn't replace heart muscle.</li><li><b>By hypertrophy of the dead cells</b> — Dead cells can't hypertrophy; only surviving cells can.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> MI timeline: coagulative necrosis → neutrophils → macrophages (rupture risk 3–14 days) → granulation tissue → scar.</p>","w":["Cardiac muscle lacks satellite cells.","Adult cardiomyocytes have very little mitotic capacity and don't restore lost muscle.","Correct — dead cardiac muscle is replaced by fibrous scar.","Smooth muscle doesn't replace heart muscle.","Dead cells can't hypertrophy; only surviving cells can."]},"q97":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Rigor mortis: no ATP → myosin heads can't detach from actin, so cross-bridges lock.</p><p>In the cross-bridge cycle, ATP binding to the myosin head is what releases it from actin; ATP hydrolysis then re-cocks the head. After death, ATP production stops, while Ca²⁺ leaks from the sarcoplasmic reticulum (and can't be pumped back without ATP), exposing actin binding sites. Myosin heads bind actin and stay bound, so muscles become stiff. Rigor begins a few hours after death and passes after 1–2 days as proteins break down.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Excess ATP keeps myosin heads cycling</b> — ATP is depleted, not in excess; excess ATP would let myosin detach and relax.</li><li><b>Calcium is pumped back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum</b> — Ca²⁺ leaks out of the SR; pumping it back requires ATP, which is gone.</li><li><b>Troponin binds tropomyosin permanently</b> — Troponin doesn't permanently bind tropomyosin; that isn't the mechanism.</li><li><b>Acetylcholine accumulates at the neuromuscular junction</b> — Acetylcholine accumulation would cause twitching and then paralysis, not rigor.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> ATP is needed for both detachment (myosin) and relaxation (SERCA pumping Ca²⁺ back).</p>","w":["ATP is depleted, not in excess; excess ATP would let myosin detach and relax.","Correct — without ATP, myosin heads can't detach from actin.","Ca²⁺ leaks out of the SR; pumping it back requires ATP, which is gone.","Troponin doesn't permanently bind tropomyosin; that isn't the mechanism.","Acetylcholine accumulation would cause twitching and then paralysis, not rigor."]},"q98":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> A triad = one T-tubule flanked by two terminal cisternae of the sarcoplasmic reticulum (at the A–I junction in skeletal muscle).</p><p>The action potential spreads from the sarcolemma down the T-tubules into the fiber. Voltage-sensing dihydropyridine receptors (DHPR, L-type Ca²⁺ channels) in the T-tubule membrane are mechanically coupled to ryanodine receptors (RyR1) in the adjacent SR terminal cisternae; depolarization opens RyR1 and Ca²⁺ floods the cytoplasm, triggering contraction. Cardiac muscle has dyads (one T-tubule + one cisterna) at the Z line, and its RyR2 opens by Ca²⁺-induced Ca²⁺ release. Mutations in RyR1 cause malignant hyperthermia.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Two T-tubules and one terminal cisterna</b> — The arrangement is reversed: one T-tubule between two cisternae.</li><li><b>One Z disc and two M lines</b> — Z discs and M lines are sarcomere landmarks, not the triad.</li><li><b>Actin, myosin and titin</b> — Actin, myosin and titin are sarcomere proteins.</li><li><b>A gap junction, desmosome and fascia adherens</b> — Gap junctions, desmosomes and fascia adherens make up the cardiac intercalated disc.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Skeletal: triads at A–I junction, mechanical DHPR–RyR1 coupling. Cardiac: dyads at Z line, Ca²⁺-induced Ca²⁺ release.</p>","w":["Correct — a triad is one T-tubule and two terminal cisternae.","The arrangement is reversed: one T-tubule between two cisternae.","Z discs and M lines are sarcomere landmarks, not the triad.","Actin, myosin and titin are sarcomere proteins.","Gap junctions, desmosomes and fascia adherens make up the cardiac intercalated disc."]},"q99":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The Z disc bisects the light I band and marks the boundary of each sarcomere.</p><p>In longitudinal EM of skeletal muscle, the repeating pattern is: Z line — I band (light, actin only) — A band (dark, myosin + overlapping actin, with the paler H band and central M line) — I band — Z line. The Z disc is a dense lattice of α-actinin and other proteins where actin filaments from adjacent sarcomeres overlap and are anchored. Desmin intermediate filaments connect neighboring Z discs to each other and to the sarcolemma, keeping myofibrils in register.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>M line</b> — The M line lies in the middle of the A band (dark band), not the light band.</li><li><b>H band</b> — The H band is the pale zone in the middle of the A band.</li><li><b>A band</b> — The A band is the dark band itself, not a line.</li><li><b>T-tubule</b> — T-tubules sit at the A–I junction and don't form continuous dark lines across the fiber.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Z line bisects I band; M line bisects A band (via the H band).</p>","w":["The M line lies in the middle of the A band (dark band), not the light band.","Correct — the Z disc bisects each light I band.","The H band is the pale zone in the middle of the A band.","The A band is the dark band itself, not a line.","T-tubules sit at the A–I junction and don't form continuous dark lines across the fiber."]},"q100":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Dense bodies of smooth muscle are the equivalent of Z lines: both anchor actin filaments with α-actinin.</p><p>Smooth muscle has no sarcomeres. Instead, actin filaments attach to cytoplasmic dense bodies and to membrane-associated dense plaques, both rich in α-actinin. Myosin filaments interdigitate with the actin in a criss-cross lattice; when they pull, dense bodies move closer together and the cell shortens and twists. The Z line performs this anchoring role in the ordered sarcomeres of skeletal and cardiac muscle.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>A</b> — Label A is SR/T-tubule membrane, not an actin anchor.</li><li><b>C</b> — Label C is the I band (actin only), not the anchoring structure.</li><li><b>D</b> — Label D is a mitochondrion.</li><li><b>F</b> — Label F is the A band (actin + myosin).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Smooth muscle dense bodies = Z-line equivalents (α-actinin).</p>","w":["Label A is SR/T-tubule membrane, not an actin anchor.","Correct — B is the Z line, the equivalent of smooth muscle dense bodies.","Label C is the I band (actin only), not the anchoring structure.","Label D is a mitochondrion.","Label F is the A band (actin + myosin)."]},"q101":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The I band (light band) contains only thin (actin) filaments; it's bisected by the Z line and shortens during contraction.</p><p>Each I band spans the ends of two adjacent sarcomeres — the portion of thin filaments not overlapped by thick filaments. It appears light (\"isotropic\" under polarized light) because it lacks the dense myosin. As contraction pulls actin deeper into the A band, the I band narrows. The A band (anisotropic, dark) contains myosin with overlapping actin and keeps its length.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>A</b> — Label A is SR/T-tubule membrane.</li><li><b>B</b> — Label B is the Z line, which anchors actin; it's a line, not a band.</li><li><b>D</b> — Label D is a mitochondrion.</li><li><b>F</b> — Label F is the A band, containing myosin plus actin.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> I = Isotropic = light = actin only; A = Anisotropic = dark = myosin.</p>","w":["Label A is SR/T-tubule membrane.","Label B is the Z line, which anchors actin; it's a line, not a band.","Correct — C is the I band, containing only thin filaments.","Label D is a mitochondrion.","Label F is the A band, containing myosin plus actin."]},"q102":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> \"HZI shrink, A stays\": the H band and I band shorten; the A band and filaments don't.</p><p>As thin filaments slide toward the center of the sarcomere, the overlap with thick filaments increases. The H band (thick filaments only) narrows because actin moves into it; the I band (thin filaments only) narrows because more actin is pulled into the A band; and the Z lines approach each other. The A band reflects the fixed length of the myosin filaments, so it stays constant. Neither type of filament changes length.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>A band and H band</b> — The A band doesn't shorten.</li><li><b>A band and I band</b> — The A band doesn't shorten.</li><li><b>A band and M line</b> — The A band and M line don't shorten.</li><li><b>Thick and thin filaments</b> — Filaments slide past each other; they don't shorten.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Shorten: H, I, sarcomere. Constant: A band, filament lengths.</p>","w":["The A band doesn't shorten.","Correct — the H band and I band shorten.","The A band doesn't shorten.","The A band and M line don't shorten.","Filaments slide past each other; they don't shorten."]},"q103":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Smooth muscle: spindle-shaped cells with a single central cigar-shaped nucleus, tapered overlapping ends, no striations.</p><p>Identification tips: in longitudinal section smooth muscle cells look like tightly packed spindles with elongated central nuclei (sometimes corkscrew-shaped when contracted) and pink homogeneous cytoplasm. In cross-section you see circles of varying size, only some containing a nucleus (cut through the middle of the cell). Compare dense regular connective tissue, where wavy nuclei are thinner and sit between acellular collagen bundles, and peripheral nerve, which is wavier with Schwann cell nuclei and a frothy myelin look.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Skeletal muscle</b> — Skeletal muscle has cross-striations and many peripheral nuclei.</li><li><b>Cardiac muscle</b> — Cardiac muscle is striated and branched, with central nuclei and intercalated discs.</li><li><b>Dense regular connective tissue</b> — Dense regular connective tissue has thin flattened fibroblast nuclei between thick acellular collagen bundles.</li><li><b>Peripheral nerve</b> — Peripheral nerve has wavy fibers with Schwann cell nuclei and a frothy or \"herringbone\" myelin appearance.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Smooth muscle vs. nerve: nerve is wavier and paler with Schwann nuclei; smooth muscle has fat cigar-shaped central nuclei.</p>","w":["Skeletal muscle has cross-striations and many peripheral nuclei.","Cardiac muscle is striated and branched, with central nuclei and intercalated discs.","Correct — this is smooth muscle.","Dense regular connective tissue has thin flattened fibroblast nuclei between thick acellular collagen bundles.","Peripheral nerve has wavy fibers with Schwann cell nuclei and a frothy or \"herringbone\" myelin appearance."]},"q104":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The dark step-like transverse bands in cardiac muscle are intercalated discs; their gap junctions let the heart contract as one unit.</p><p>Intercalated discs join cardiac myocytes end to end. Their transverse portion holds fascia adherens (anchoring actin) and desmosomes (anchoring desmin), withstanding mechanical pull. Their lateral (longitudinal) portion holds gap junctions (connexin-43), which pass ions directly from cell to cell so an action potential spreads rapidly through the myocardium, making it a functional syncytium. Disorders of desmosome proteins cause arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Z discs; titin</b> — Z discs are thinner, regularly repeating lines within each sarcomere; titin is an elastic protein, not a coupling channel.</li><li><b>Intercalated discs; tight junctions</b> — Tight junctions aren't present in intercalated discs.</li><li><b>Triads; ryanodine receptors</b> — Triads are skeletal muscle EM structures; ryanodine receptors release SR calcium.</li><li><b>Dense bodies; α-actinin</b> — Dense bodies with α-actinin are features of smooth muscle.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Intercalated disc: fascia adherens + desmosome + gap junction.</p>","w":["Z discs are thinner, regularly repeating lines within each sarcomere; titin is an elastic protein, not a coupling channel.","Correct — these are intercalated discs, whose gap junctions couple the cells electrically.","Tight junctions aren't present in intercalated discs.","Triads are skeletal muscle EM structures; ryanodine receptors release SR calcium.","Dense bodies with α-actinin are features of smooth muscle."]},"q105":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> G marks the stratum granulosum — cells with dark keratohyalin granules beneath the thick stratum corneum of sole skin.</p><p>Thick skin from deep to surface: stratum basale (single row of dividing cells on the basement membrane), stratum spinosum (polygonal \"prickle\" cells joined by desmosomes), stratum granulosum (flattened cells with dark basophilic keratohyalin granules), stratum lucidum (thin clear band, thick skin only), and stratum corneum (thick layer of dead keratin squames). Thick skin covers palms and soles and lacks hair follicles and sebaceous glands.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>C</b> — C labels the stratum corneum (very thick here).</li><li><b>S</b> — S labels the stratum spinosum.</li><li><b>B</b> — B labels the stratum basale.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Thick skin = 5 layers including stratum lucidum; no hair.</p>","w":["C labels the stratum corneum (very thick here).","Correct — G is the stratum granulosum.","S labels the stratum spinosum.","B labels the stratum basale."]},"q106":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Langerhans cells sit mainly in the stratum spinosum.</p><p>In the numbered section, 1 is the stratum corneum, 2 the stratum granulosum, 3 the stratum spinosum, 4 the stratum basale, and 5 the papillary dermis. Langerhans cells (bone marrow–derived dendritic cells with Birbeck granules) are scattered among the keratinocytes of the stratum spinosum, extending dendrites between them to sample antigens. Melanocytes and Merkel cells, by contrast, sit in the stratum basale.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>1</b> — Layer 1 is the stratum corneum — dead keratinocytes.</li><li><b>2</b> — Layer 2 is the stratum granulosum.</li><li><b>4</b> — Layer 4 is the stratum basale, home of melanocytes and Merkel cells rather than most Langerhans cells.</li><li><b>5</b> — Layer 5 is the dermis.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Stratum basale: melanocytes, Merkel cells. Stratum spinosum: Langerhans cells.</p>","w":["Layer 1 is the stratum corneum — dead keratinocytes.","Layer 2 is the stratum granulosum.","Correct — layer 3 (stratum spinosum) contains most Langerhans cells.","Layer 4 is the stratum basale, home of melanocytes and Merkel cells rather than most Langerhans cells.","Layer 5 is the dermis."]},"q107":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Basal keratinocytes are anchored to the basement membrane by hemidesmosomes (integrin α6β4 and BP180 → laminin; plectin/BP230 → keratin).</p><p>The dermo-epidermal junction must resist shear. Hemidesmosomes on the basal surface of stratum basale cells connect the internal keratin cytoskeleton through transmembrane proteins to laminin-332 in the basal lamina, which is fixed to the dermis by type VII collagen anchoring fibrils. Failure anywhere in this chain causes subepidermal blistering: autoantibodies against BP180/BP230 (bullous pemphigoid), mutations in laminin-332 or plectin (junctional epidermolysis bullosa), or type VII collagen (dystrophic EB).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Desmosomes</b> — Desmosomes join keratinocytes to each other, not to the basement membrane.</li><li><b>Tight junctions</b> — Tight junctions aren't the anchoring junction at the basement membrane.</li><li><b>Gap junctions</b> — Gap junctions couple cells for communication.</li><li><b>Adherens junctions</b> — Adherens junctions join neighboring cells.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Basal cell → basement membrane: hemidesmosome; cell → cell: desmosome.</p>","w":["Desmosomes join keratinocytes to each other, not to the basement membrane.","Correct — hemidesmosomes anchor stratum basale cells to the basement membrane.","Tight junctions aren't the anchoring junction at the basement membrane.","Gap junctions couple cells for communication.","Adherens junctions join neighboring cells."]},"q108":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Merkel cells in the stratum basale, with their nerve endings (Merkel discs), are slow-adapting touch receptors for fine touch and two-point discrimination.</p><p>Merkel cells are clear cells in the basal layer containing dense-core neuroendocrine granules and keratin 20. Each contacts a disc-shaped expansion of a myelinated sensory axon; sustained pressure triggers a continuous discharge (slow adaptation), conveying shape, edges and texture. They are densest in fingertips, lips and other areas of high tactile acuity. Merkel cell carcinoma is an aggressive skin cancer often linked to a polyomavirus.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Melanocyte</b> — Melanocytes produce melanin; they aren't mechanoreceptors.</li><li><b>Langerhans cell</b> — Langerhans cells are antigen-presenting cells in the stratum spinosum.</li><li><b>Keratinocyte</b> — Keratinocytes produce keratin and form the barrier.</li><li><b>Meissner corpuscle</b> — Meissner corpuscles are encapsulated, rapidly adapting receptors in the dermal papillae, not epidermal cells.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Slow-adapting: Merkel (texture), Ruffini (stretch). Rapid-adapting: Meissner (light touch/flutter), Pacinian (vibration).</p>","w":["Melanocytes produce melanin; they aren't mechanoreceptors.","Langerhans cells are antigen-presenting cells in the stratum spinosum.","Correct — Merkel cells are slow-adapting mechanoreceptors for fine touch.","Keratinocytes produce keratin and form the barrier.","Meissner corpuscles are encapsulated, rapidly adapting receptors in the dermal papillae, not epidermal cells."]},"q109":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Sebaceous glands use holocrine secretion: whole cells fill with lipid, die and become the secretion (sebum).</p><p>Sebaceous glands are usually attached to hair follicles (pilosebaceous unit). Basal cells at the periphery divide; daughter cells move toward the center, accumulate lipid droplets, their nuclei shrink, and they rupture, releasing sebum into the follicle. Sebum lubricates hair and skin and has antimicrobial effects. Androgens stimulate the glands at puberty, which, together with follicular plugging and Cutibacterium acnes, drives acne.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Merocrine</b> — Merocrine secretion is exocytosis without cell loss (eccrine sweat glands, salivary glands).</li><li><b>Apocrine</b> — Apocrine secretion releases part of the apical cytoplasm (axillary apocrine glands, mammary lipid).</li><li><b>Endocrine</b> — Endocrine secretion goes into the blood.</li><li><b>Paracrine</b> — Paracrine signaling acts on neighboring cells.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Holocrine = whole cell (sebaceous); apocrine = apex (apocrine sweat, mammary lipid); merocrine = exocytosis (eccrine sweat).</p>","w":["Merocrine secretion is exocytosis without cell loss (eccrine sweat glands, salivary glands).","Apocrine secretion releases part of the apical cytoplasm (axillary apocrine glands, mammary lipid).","Correct — sebum is released by holocrine secretion.","Endocrine secretion goes into the blood.","Paracrine signaling acts on neighboring cells."]},"q110":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Albinism = normal number of melanocytes but absent/defective tyrosinase (or melanin transport), so no pigment and high skin cancer risk.</p><p>Melanin synthesis: tyrosine → DOPA → dopaquinone → melanin, with tyrosinase catalyzing the first steps inside melanosomes. In oculocutaneous albinism (autosomal recessive), tyrosinase (or another melanosome protein) is missing, so melanocytes are present but can't make pigment. Without melanin, the skin has no protection against UV-induced DNA damage, raising the risk of squamous and basal cell carcinoma and melanoma. Eye involvement causes reduced vision, nystagmus and photophobia.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Autoimmune destruction of melanocytes</b> — Autoimmune destruction of melanocytes is vitiligo — patchy, acquired, melanocytes absent.</li><li><b>Failure of neural crest migration</b> — Failure of neural crest migration would leave melanocytes absent; here they're present (as in Waardenburg syndrome or piebaldism, they're missing).</li><li><b>Excess ACTH</b> — Excess ACTH causes HYPERpigmentation (Addison disease).</li><li><b>Malignant transformation of melanocytes</b> — Malignant transformation of melanocytes is melanoma.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Albinism: melanocytes present, no pigment. Vitiligo: melanocytes absent.</p>","w":["Autoimmune destruction of melanocytes is vitiligo — patchy, acquired, melanocytes absent.","Correct — albinism results from absent or defective tyrosinase.","Failure of neural crest migration would leave melanocytes absent; here they're present (as in Waardenburg syndrome or piebaldism, they're missing).","Excess ACTH causes HYPERpigmentation (Addison disease).","Malignant transformation of melanocytes is melanoma."]},"q111":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Vitiligo = acquired (usually autoimmune) loss of melanocytes → sharply demarcated depigmented patches.</p><p>In vitiligo, CD8⁺ T cells and autoantibodies destroy melanocytes, often around the mouth, eyes, hands, genitals and sites of trauma (Koebner phenomenon). Biopsy shows absence of melanocytes in affected skin. It is associated with other autoimmune diseases (thyroid disease, type 1 diabetes, pernicious anemia, Addison disease). Lesions glow brightly under a Wood's lamp. In contrast, albinism is congenital and diffuse with melanocytes present.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Albinism</b> — Albinism is congenital and diffuse, with melanocytes present but lacking pigment.</li><li><b>Addison disease</b> — Addison disease causes diffuse HYPERpigmentation.</li><li><b>Melasma</b> — Melasma is patchy facial hyperpigmentation (pregnancy, oral contraceptives).</li><li><b>Melanoma</b> — Melanoma is a malignant pigmented tumor of melanocytes.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Vitiligo is associated with autoimmune thyroid disease — check TSH.</p>","w":["Albinism is congenital and diffuse, with melanocytes present but lacking pigment.","Correct — depigmented patches with no melanocytes indicate vitiligo.","Addison disease causes diffuse HYPERpigmentation.","Melasma is patchy facial hyperpigmentation (pregnancy, oral contraceptives).","Melanoma is a malignant pigmented tumor of melanocytes."]},"q112":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> In Addison disease, low cortisol raises pituitary POMC; ACTH and α-MSH (both from POMC) stimulate melanocortin-1 receptors on melanocytes → hyperpigmentation.</p><p>Primary adrenal insufficiency (often autoimmune) causes low cortisol and aldosterone: fatigue, weight loss, hypotension, hyponatremia, hyperkalemia and salt craving. Without cortisol's negative feedback, the pituitary overproduces POMC, which is cleaved into ACTH and α-MSH. Both bind MC1R on melanocytes, increasing melanin synthesis; pigment shows especially in sun-exposed areas, palmar creases, scars, knuckles and the buccal mucosa. Secondary adrenal insufficiency (low ACTH) does not cause hyperpigmentation.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Increased number of melanocytes from neural crest</b> — The number of melanocytes isn't increased; their activity is.</li><li><b>Excess cortisol stimulates melanin production</b> — Cortisol is low in Addison disease, and cortisol doesn't directly stimulate melanin.</li><li><b>Tyrosinase deficiency</b> — Tyrosinase deficiency causes albinism (no pigment).</li><li><b>Iron deposition in the dermis</b> — Iron deposition causes the bronze skin of hemochromatosis.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Primary adrenal insufficiency → hyperpigmentation; secondary (pituitary) → no hyperpigmentation.</p>","w":["The number of melanocytes isn't increased; their activity is.","Correct — high ACTH (with MSH-like activity) stimulates melanocytes.","Cortisol is low in Addison disease, and cortisol doesn't directly stimulate melanin.","Tyrosinase deficiency causes albinism (no pigment).","Iron deposition causes the bronze skin of hemochromatosis."]},"q113":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Epidermal stem cells in the bulge region of hair follicles regenerate epidermis after partial-thickness burns.</p><p>The hair follicle bulge, near the insertion of the arrector pili muscle, houses multipotent stem cells. When the surface epidermis is destroyed but deeper dermis with follicles (and sweat glands) survives, cells migrate up from these appendages and spread across the wound as islands that merge. Full-thickness burns destroy all appendages, so no islands can form and healing only occurs from the wound edges — grafting is needed.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Sebaceous glands</b> — Sebaceous glands secrete sebum; they aren't the main stem cell reservoir.</li><li><b>Arrector pili muscles</b> — The arrector pili is smooth muscle that raises hairs.</li><li><b>Meissner corpuscles</b> — Meissner corpuscles are touch receptors.</li><li><b>Dermal papillae</b> — Dermal papillae are connective tissue, not a source of epidermal stem cells.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Partial-thickness burns heal from follicles/glands; full-thickness need grafts.</p>","w":["Sebaceous glands secrete sebum; they aren't the main stem cell reservoir.","Correct — hair follicle (bulge) stem cells re-epithelialize the wound.","The arrector pili is smooth muscle that raises hairs.","Meissner corpuscles are touch receptors.","Dermal papillae are connective tissue, not a source of epidermal stem cells."]},"q114":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Keratinocytes are the most numerous epidermal cells and are joined by abundant desmosomes (the \"prickles\" of the stratum spinosum).</p><p>Keratinocytes (~90% of epidermal cells) produce keratin intermediate filaments that insert into desmosomes, forming a continuous, mechanically tough network across the epidermis. On H&amp;E the stratum spinosum shows spiny projections where cell shrinkage during processing pulls cells apart except at desmosomes. The other epidermal cells are fewer: melanocytes (pigment), Langerhans cells (antigen presentation) and Merkel cells (touch).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Melanocytes; gap junctions</b> — Melanocytes are a minor population and don't form desmosomes with keratinocytes.</li><li><b>Langerhans cells; tight junctions</b> — Langerhans cells are scattered APCs, not the main population.</li><li><b>Merkel cells; hemidesmosomes</b> — Merkel cells are few touch receptors.</li><li><b>Keratinocytes; adherens junctions only</b> — Keratinocytes do have adherens junctions, but desmosomes are their defining connections.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Keratin mutations (e.g., K5/K14) cause epidermolysis bullosa simplex — blistering within the basal cells.</p>","w":["Melanocytes are a minor population and don't form desmosomes with keratinocytes.","Correct — keratinocytes are most numerous and joined by desmosomes.","Langerhans cells are scattered APCs, not the main population.","Merkel cells are few touch receptors.","Keratinocytes do have adherens junctions, but desmosomes are their defining connections."]}});
